const DAYS = {"1":{"day":1,"week":"01","themeRu":"Five styles, one language","themeEn":"Сно́ва вме́сте: пять стиле́й ре́чи","intro":"Welcome to mastery. C1 isn't about new grammar — you already build participles, turn a sentence passive, argue with «поско́льку». It's about <b>register</b>: saying the right thing in the right <b>style</b>. The same idea can be scientific, official, journalistic, plain-spoken or literary — and a native hears the difference instantly. Эмма edits literary translations now; today you start hearing what she hears. Five styles, one language.","introAudio":"Welcome to mastery. C1 isn't about new grammar — you already build participles, turn a sentence passive, argue with 'poskolku'. It's about register: saying the right thing in the right style. The same idea can be scientific, official, journalistic, plain-spoken or literary — and a native hears the difference instantly. Emma edits literary translations now; today you start hearing what she hears. Five styles, one language.","goals":["Name the <b>five functional styles</b>: <b>нау́чный, официа́льно-делово́й, публицисти́ческий, разгово́рный, худо́жественный</b>","Place a word on the <b>register scale</b>: <b>кни́жное — нейтра́льное — разгово́рное</b>","Recognise a style from its markers (a term, a cliché, a particle, an image)","Grasp the C1 shift: not just <b>correct</b> grammar, but the <b>right register</b>"],"learned":["Named the five functional styles","Placed words on the register scale (кни́жное / нейтра́льное / разгово́рное)","Recognised a style from its markers","Understood that C1 is about choosing the right register, not just correct grammar"],"review":{"intro":"One minute on the B2 muscles — a participle, a case a verb demands, a connector — and then we open the lens that runs through all of C1: style.","introAudio":"One minute on the B2 muscles — a participle, a case a verb demands, a connector — and then we open the lens that runs through all of C1: style.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. This is B2 — an old friend.","q":"“an article written by a professor” (passive, past) —","opts":["статья́, пи́шущая профе́ссором","статья́, напи́санная профе́ссором","статья́, писа́вшая профе́ссора"],"answer":1,"explain":"Passive past → <b>напи́санная</b>, and the author goes in the instrumental: профе́ссором. The others are active."},{"type":"choice","q":"“The result depends on many factors.” —","opts":["Результа́т зави́сит от мно́гих фа́кторов.","Результа́т зави́сит мно́гими фа́кторами.","Результа́т зави́сит на мно́гие фа́кторы."],"answer":0,"explain":"зави́сеть governs <b>от + genitive</b>: от мно́гих фа́кторов. The verb picks the case."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“Although it was difficult, I finished.”</b>","want":"Хотя́ бы́ло тру́дно, я зако́нчил","answer":"Хотя́ бы́ло тру́дно, я зако́нчил"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The five styles","title":"One language, <em>five</em> ways to speak it","paras":["Russian sorts its speech into <b>five functional styles</b> (функциона́льные сти́ли), each living in its own world. <b>Нау́чный</b> (scientific) — textbooks, articles, lectures: precise, term-heavy, impersonal. <b>Официа́льно-делово́й</b> (official) — laws, documents, applications: fixed clichés and formulas. <b>Публицисти́ческий</b> (journalistic) — newspapers, TV, speeches: expressive yet standard, made to persuade.","And two more you already live in: <b>разгово́рный</b> (conversational) — friends, home, chat: short, lively, full of particles; and <b>худо́жественный</b> (literary) — fiction: images, and free to bend every other style for effect.","The point of C1 isn't a new tense. It's knowing which of these five you're in — because the same thought needs different words in each."],"audio":"Russian sorts its speech into five functional styles, each living in its own world. Nauchny, scientific — textbooks, articles, lectures: precise, term-heavy, impersonal. Ofitsialno-delovoy, official — laws, documents, applications: fixed clichés and formulas. Publitsistichesky, journalistic — newspapers, TV, speeches: expressive yet standard, made to persuade. And two you already live in: razgovorny, conversational — friends, home, chat: short, lively, full of particles; and khudozhestvenny, literary — fiction: images, free to bend every other style for effect. C1 isn't a new tense. It's knowing which of these five you're in.","table":{"rows":[["нау́чный <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"научный стиль\">🔊</button>","scientific — articles, lectures (terms, impersonal)"],["официа́льно-делово́й <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"официально-деловой стиль\">🔊</button>","official — laws, documents (clichés, formulas)"],["публицисти́ческий <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"публицистический стиль\">🔊</button>","journalistic — press, TV (expressive, persuasive)"],["разгово́рный <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"разговорный стиль\">🔊</button>","conversational — friends, chat (particles, short)"],["худо́жественный <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"художественный стиль\">🔊</button>","literary — fiction (imagery, mixes styles)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"The scientific style prefers the impersonal.","en":"Нау́чный стиль <b>предпочита́ет</b> безли́чность.","say":"Научный стиль предпочитает безличность."},{"ru":"In conversation we speak more simply and freely.","en":"В разгово́ре мы говори́м про́ще и свобо́днее.","say":"В разговоре мы говорим проще и свободнее."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Прошу́ предоста́вить…» (to a friend)","right":"«Дай, пожа́луйста…» (to a friend)","why":"«Прошу́ предоста́вить» is the <b>official</b> style — correct grammar, wrong world. To a friend it sounds absurdly stiff. Register, not grammar, is the C1 error."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"Which style lives in laws, applications and official letters?","opts":["разгово́рный","официа́льно-делово́й","худо́жественный"],"answer":1,"explain":"Documents and laws → the <b>официа́льно-делово́й</b> style: fixed clichés and formulas."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"The style of newspapers, TV and speeches is the … one. Type it:","accept":["публицистический","публицистический стиль"],"placeholder":"публиц…","explain":"The press style is <b>публицисти́ческий</b> — expressive but standard, built to persuade."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The register scale","title":"<em>Кни́жное — нейтра́льное — разгово́рное</em>: one meaning, many words","paras":["Inside and across those styles runs a scale of <b>register</b> — how elevated or plain a word is. Dictionaries mark it: <b>высо́кое</b> (lofty), <b>кни́жное</b> (bookish), <b>нейтра́льное</b> (neutral — safe anywhere), <b>разгово́рное</b> (colloquial), <b>просторе́чное</b> (low, rough).","The trap for English is that one English word often hides a whole Russian column. “Face” is <b>лик</b> (lofty), <b>лицо́</b> (neutral), <b>физионо́мия</b> (colloquial, jokey). “To die” is <b>сконча́ться</b> (official/bookish), <b>умере́ть</b> (neutral), <b>помере́ть</b> (colloquial). Same meaning — different worlds.","The neutral word is your safe default. The marked ones are tools: pick one on purpose, and never by accident."],"audio":"Inside and across those styles runs a scale of register — how elevated or plain a word is. Dictionaries mark it: vysokoye, lofty; knizhnoye, bookish; neytralnoye, neutral, safe anywhere; razgovornoye, colloquial; prostorechnoye, low and rough. One English word often hides a whole Russian column. 'Face' is lik, lofty; litso, neutral; fizionomiya, colloquial and jokey. 'To die' is skonchatsya, official; umeret, neutral; pomeret, colloquial. Same meaning, different worlds. The neutral word is your safe default; the marked ones are tools — pick one on purpose, never by accident.","table":{"rows":[["лик — лицо́ — физионо́мия <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"лик, лицо, физиономия\">🔊</button>","face: lofty — neutral — colloquial"],["сконча́ться — умере́ть — помере́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"скончаться, умереть, помереть\">🔊</button>","to die: official — neutral — colloquial"],["де́нежные сре́дства — де́ньги — деньжа́та <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"денежные средства, деньги, деньжата\">🔊</button>","money: official — neutral — colloquial"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"In a document: “Funds have been transferred.”","en":"В докуме́нте: «<b>Де́нежные сре́дства</b> перечи́слены».","say":"В документе: денежные средства перечислены."},{"ru":"To a friend: “Have you got any money?”","en":"Дру́гу: «У тебя́ <b>де́ньги</b> есть?»","say":"Другу: у тебя деньги есть?"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"На конфере́нции: «У профе́ссора у́мная физионо́мия».","right":"На конфере́нции: «У профе́ссора у́мное лицо́».","why":"<b>физионо́мия</b> is colloquial and slightly mocking — fine with friends, wrong at a conference. The neutral <b>лицо́</b> fits. Wrong register, not wrong word."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"You're writing an official report. “Money” should be…","opts":["деньжа́та","де́нежные сре́дства","ба́бки"],"answer":1,"explain":"Official register → <b>де́нежные сре́дства</b>. деньжа́та is colloquial, ба́бки is slang."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word is <b>neutral</b> (safe in any style)?","opts":["лик","лицо́","физионо́мия"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>лицо́</b> is neutral. лик is lofty, физионо́мия is colloquial."},{"type":"type","q":"The neutral word for “to die” (not сконча́ться, not помере́ть). Type it:","accept":["умереть"],"placeholder":"нейтра́льное сло́во…","explain":"<b>умере́ть</b> — the neutral middle of the scale."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Reading the markers","title":"How to <em>tell</em> which style you're in","paras":["You don't need a label on a text to know its style — the words give it away. <b>Terms + nominal phrases + impersonal verbs</b> («иссле́дование пока́зывает, что…», «представля́ется целесообра́зным») → scientific. <b>Fixed clichés</b> («в связи́ с, на основа́нии, настоя́щим уведомля́ю») → official.","<b>Vivid words next to standard phrases</b>, questions to the reader, evaluation («как изве́стно», «о́стрый вопро́с») → journalistic. <b>Particles, short sentences, everyday words</b> («ну, вот, да ла́дно, что́-то уста́л») → conversational. And <b>imagery</b> — a metaphor, an epithet — points to the literary.","Train your ear on the markers, and any text announces its own register within a line or two."],"audio":"You don't need a label to know a text's style — the words give it away. Terms, nominal phrases and impersonal verbs — issledovaniye pokazyvayet chto, predstavlyayetsya tselesoobraznym — mean scientific. Fixed clichés — v svyazi s, na osnovanii, nastoyashchim uvedomlyayu — mean official. Vivid words beside standard phrases, questions to the reader, evaluation — kak izvestno, ostry vopros — mean journalistic. Particles, short sentences, everyday words — nu, vot, da ladno, chto-to ustal — mean conversational. And imagery, a metaphor or an epithet, points to the literary. Train your ear on the markers.","examples":[{"ru":"Marker of science: “It is deemed appropriate to note…”","en":"При́знак нау́ки: «<b>Представля́ется целесообра́зным</b> отме́тить…»","say":"Признак науки: представляется целесообразным отметить."},{"ru":"Marker of conversation: “Well, I'm kind of tired.”","en":"При́знак разгово́ра: «<b>Ну, я что́-то уста́л</b>».","say":"Признак разговора: ну, я что-то устал."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Настоя́щим уведомля́ю, что я о́чень уста́л, ну про́сто жесть».","right":"«Настоя́щим уведомля́ю, что чу́вствую себя́ нехорошо́».","why":"The official opener «настоя́щим уведомля́ю» needs an official continuation; the slangy «ну про́сто жесть» collides with it. Mixing styles by accident is the giveaway of a non-native — pick one register and hold it."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«В связи́ с приня́тием зако́на…» — which style?","opts":["разгово́рный","официа́льно-делово́й","худо́жественный"],"answer":1,"explain":"The cliché <b>в связи́ с</b> + a nominalization is a classic <b>official</b> marker."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Да ла́дно, вот э́то да!» — which style?","opts":["разгово́рный","нау́чный","официа́льно-делово́й"],"answer":0,"explain":"Particles and everyday exclamations → the <b>разгово́рный</b> (conversational) style."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Иссле́дование пока́зывает, что вопро́с остаётся откры́тым.","sub":"a scientific-style sentence — read it evenly, like a lecture","want":"исследование показывает что вопрос остается открытым"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Why it matters — and the week ahead","title":"At C1, <em>correct</em> isn't enough — it has to <em>fit</em>","paras":["Here's the shift that defines this level. Until now, a sentence was right if its grammar was right. From today, it also has to be <b>right for the situation</b>: the same correct Russian can be perfect in a lecture and ridiculous in a chat. C1 is the ear that hears the difference — and the control to choose.","So every task this month asks two questions, not one: is it <b>correct</b>, and is it <b>appropriate</b>? A grammatically flawless sentence in the wrong register is now a mistake.","This week we install the system. Today: the five styles and the register scale. Then: choosing among <b>synonyms</b> by shade, <b>suffixes</b> that carry attitude (до́мик vs доми́ще), the <b>scientific</b> and <b>official</b> styles up close, and <b>bookish condensation</b> — saying more in fewer words. By Sunday you'll hear register everywhere."],"audio":"Here's the shift that defines this level. Until now, a sentence was right if its grammar was right. From today, it also has to be right for the situation: the same correct Russian can be perfect in a lecture and ridiculous in a chat. C1 is the ear that hears the difference, and the control to choose. So every task this month asks two questions: is it correct, and is it appropriate? A flawless sentence in the wrong register is now a mistake. This week we install the system. Today: the five styles and the register scale. Then: choosing among synonyms by shade, suffixes that carry attitude, domik versus domishche, the scientific and official styles up close, and bookish condensation — saying more in fewer words.","examples":[{"ru":"Right in a lecture: “The data confirm the hypothesis.”","en":"Уме́стно на ле́кции: «Да́нные <b>подтвержда́ют</b> гипо́тезу».","say":"Уместно на лекции: данные подтверждают гипотезу."},{"ru":"Right in a chat: “Yeah, turns out it's true!”","en":"Уме́стно в ча́те: «Ага́, <b>и пра́вда</b> так!»","say":"Уместно в чате: ага, и правда так!"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"A sentence is grammatically perfect but sounds absurd in its situation. At C1 that is…","opts":["fine — grammar is all that matters","a mistake — wrong register","impossible"],"answer":1,"explain":"At C1 register counts: correct grammar in the wrong style is a <b>mistake</b>."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which pair means the same but differs only in <b>register</b>?","opts":["лицо́ / физионо́мия","лицо́ / рука́","лицо́ / лица́"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>лицо́ / физионо́мия</b> — same meaning “face”, different register (neutral vs colloquial). рука́ is another word; ли́ца is just the plural."},{"type":"type","q":"Two questions rule C1 now. One is “is it correct?” Type the other in one English word — is it …?","accept":["appropriate","appropriate?","fitting","suitable"],"placeholder":"is it …?","explain":"Correct <b>and</b> appropriate — does it fit the style and situation?"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Эмма is at Лена's café with an editing job open on her laptop. Ива́н drops by. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Над чем сиди́шь? У тебя́ тако́е сосредото́ченное лицо́.","ru":"What are you working on? You've got such a focused face."},{"who":"A","en":"Реда́ктирую перево́д. Оди́н и тот же смысл прихо́дится говори́ть по-ра́зному.","ru":"Editing a translation. The same meaning has to be said differently each time."},{"who":"B","en":"Как э́то по-ра́зному?","ru":"Differently how?"},{"who":"A","en":"В догово́ре — «сто́роны обя́зуются». В статье́ — «нельзя́ не согласи́ться». А до́ма мы бы сказа́ли про́сто «договори́лись».","ru":"In a contract — “the parties undertake.” In an article — “one cannot but agree.” At home we'd just say “deal.”"},{"who":"B","en":"Одно́ и то же?!","ru":"The same thing?!"},{"who":"A","en":"По смы́слу — да. По сти́лю — три ра́зных ми́ра.","ru":"In meaning — yes. In style — three different worlds."},{"who":"B","en":"Тепе́рь я то́же бу́ду слы́шать э́то ве́зде.","ru":"Now I'll hear it everywhere too."},{"who":"A","en":"Вот и́менно. Э́то и есть настоя́щий язы́к.","ru":"Exactly. That's what the real language is."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for talking about style itself — the toolkit of C1. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"стиль","ru":"style, register","ex":"У ка́ждого те́кста свой стиль."},{"en":"нау́чный","ru":"scientific (style)","ex":"Нау́чный стиль лю́бит то́чность."},{"en":"официа́льно-делово́й","ru":"official-business (style)","ex":"Заявле́ние пи́шут в официа́льно-делово́м сти́ле."},{"en":"публицисти́ческий","ru":"journalistic (style)","ex":"Газе́та — э́то публицисти́ческий стиль."},{"en":"разгово́рный","ru":"conversational (style)","ex":"С друзья́ми мы в разгово́рном сти́ле."},{"en":"худо́жественный","ru":"literary, artistic (style)","ex":"Худо́жественный стиль по́лон о́бразов."},{"en":"нейтра́льный","ru":"neutral (register)","ex":"Нейтра́льное сло́во уме́стно везде́."},{"en":"кни́жный","ru":"bookish (register)","ex":"«Ны́не» — кни́жное сло́во."},{"en":"разгово́рное","ru":"colloquial (register)","ex":"«Деньжа́та» — разгово́рное сло́во."},{"en":"уме́стный","ru":"appropriate, fitting","ex":"Ва́жно вы́брать уме́стное сло́во."},{"en":"отте́нок","ru":"shade, nuance","ex":"У сино́нимов ра́зные отте́нки."},{"en":"реги́стр","ru":"register (level of speech)","ex":"Держи́ оди́н реги́стр в те́ксте."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each phrase to its style.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its style — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["На основа́нии зако́на…","official-business"],["Иссле́дование пока́зывает…","scientific"],["Ну что, договори́лись?","conversational"],["Как изве́стно, э́то о́стрый вопро́с.","journalistic"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"The style of laws and applications is…","opts":["разгово́рный","официа́льно-делово́й","худо́жественный"],"answer":1},{"q":"Which word is <b>neutral</b>?","opts":["лицо́","лик","физионо́мия"],"answer":0},{"q":"«Иссле́дование пока́зывает, что…» belongs to the … style.","opts":["разгово́рный","нау́чный","официа́льно-делово́й"],"answer":1},{"q":"For an official report, “money” is…","opts":["деньжа́та","ба́бки","де́нежные сре́дства"],"answer":2},{"q":"At C1, a sentence must be correct <b>and</b>…","opts":["appropriate to the style","as long as possible","full of terms"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"One meaning, three styles — read each aloud and hear the register change. (Same idea: an agreement was reached.)","title":"Оди́н смысл, три сти́ля","sentences":["Официа́льно: Сто́роны пришли́ к соглаше́нию и подписа́ли догово́р.","В газе́те: Наконе́ц-то сто́роны договори́лись — до́лгий спор позади́.","До́ма: Ну всё, договори́лись, по рука́м!","Смысл оди́н, но стиль — от зако́на до ку́хни.","Слы́шать э́ту ра́зницу — и есть владе́ние языко́м."],"translation":"Official: The parties reached an agreement and signed a contract. In the paper: The parties have finally come to terms — the long dispute is over. At home: Right, it's a deal, shake on it! One meaning, but the style runs from the law to the kitchen. Hearing that difference is what command of the language is."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — say one thing two ways.","prompt":"Take one simple idea (e.g. “the meeting is postponed”, “I can't come”, “the project is finished”) and write it in Russian TWICE: once in the <b>official</b> style and once in the <b>conversational</b> style. Label each. 2–3 sentences total.","hint":"Official leans on clichés (в связи́ с, вы́нужден сообщи́ть); conversational leans on particles and short words (слу́шай, не смогу́, всё, гото́во). Same meaning, two worlds 🙂","example":"Официа́льно: В связи́ с обстоя́тельствами встре́ча перено́сится на сле́дующую неде́лю. По-дру́жески: Слу́шай, дава́й перенесём на сле́дующую неде́лю, не получа́ется сейча́с."}},"2":{"day":2,"week":"01","themeRu":"The right shade of a word","themeEn":"Отте́нки сло́ва: сино́нимы","intro":"Эмма has one English line to translate — «he looked at her» — and four Russian verbs open on her screen: <b>смотре́л, гляде́л, взира́л, пя́лился</b>. All four mean “looked.” None are interchangeable: one is neutral, one is warm and folksy, one is lofty, one is rude. Yesterday you met the five styles; today you meet the choice they force on every single word. At C1 a concept isn't one word — it's a little family of synonyms, and you pick the member by its <b>shade</b> (отте́нок).","introAudio":"Emma has one English line to translate — 'he looked at her' — and four Russian verbs open on her screen: smotrel, glyadel, vziral, pyalilsya. All four mean 'looked.' None are interchangeable: one is neutral, one is warm and folksy, one is lofty, one is rude. Yesterday you met the five styles; today you meet the choice they force on every single word. At C1 a concept isn't one word — it's a little family of synonyms, and you pick the member by its shade.","goals":["Choose among <b>synonyms</b> (сино́нимы) by register: <b>смотре́ть / гляде́ть / взира́ть / пя́литься</b>","Feel <b>intensity</b> in a set: <b>большо́й — огро́мный — грома́дный</b>","Hear the <b>connotation</b> (коннота́ция — the attitude a word carries): approval, irony, contempt","Reach for the right <b>antonym</b> (анто́ним) for the register you're in"],"learned":["Chose synonyms by register (смотре́ть / гляде́ть / взира́ть / пя́литься)","Ranked a synonym set by intensity (большо́й — огро́мный — грома́дный)","Read the connotation a word carries (warm, ironic, contemptuous)","Matched an antonym to the register of the surrounding text"],"review":{"intro":"Before new shades, a look back at yesterday's map — the five styles and the register scale — plus one B2 reflex: a verb that demands its own case.","introAudio":"Before new shades, a look back at yesterday's map — the five styles and the register scale — plus one B2 reflex: a verb that demands its own case.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. Yesterday's map.","q":"«Настоя́щим уведомля́ю, что…» announces which style?","opts":["разгово́рный","официа́льно-делово́й","худо́жественный"],"answer":1,"explain":"The frozen cliché <b>настоя́щим уведомля́ю</b> is a pure <b>официа́льно-делово́й</b> (official) marker."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word for “money” is <b>neutral</b> — safe in any style?","opts":["деньжа́та","де́ньги","де́нежные сре́дства"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>де́ньги</b> is the neutral middle. деньжа́та is colloquial, де́нежные сре́дства is official."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order. A B2 reflex — the verb picks the case.","q":"Build: <b>“Emma is interested in literature.”</b> (интересова́ться + instrumental)","want":"Э́мма интересу́ется литерату́рой","answer":"Э́мма интересу́ется литерату́рой"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Synonyms by register","title":"One meaning, a whole <em>family</em> of words","paras":["English hands you one verb — “to look.” Russian hands you a whole family, sorted by register and attitude. <b>Смотре́ть</b> is neutral, safe anywhere. <b>Гляде́ть</b> is warm, folksy, a touch old-fashioned or poetic. <b>Взира́ть</b> is lofty (высо́кое) — you взира́ешь on something grand, or with solemn detachment. <b>Пя́литься</b> is rude and colloquial — to gawk, to stare stupidly.","So there is no such thing as “the Russian for <i>look</i>.” There is only the right member of the family for <i>this</i> style and <i>this</i> attitude. Pick смотре́ть in a report, гляде́ть in a folk tale, взира́ть in a solemn passage, and пя́литься only where you actually mean rudeness.","This is the everyday work of C1, and exactly what Эмма does when she edits: not “is this word correct?” but “is this the right member of the set?”"],"audio":"English hands you one verb — 'to look.' Russian hands you a whole family, sorted by register and attitude. Smotret is neutral, safe anywhere. Glyadet is warm, folksy, a touch old-fashioned or poetic. Vzirat is lofty — you behold something grand, or with solemn detachment. Pyalitsya is rude and colloquial — to gawk, to stare stupidly. So there is no such thing as 'the Russian for look.' There is only the right member of the family for this style and this attitude. This is the everyday work of C1, and exactly what Emma does when she edits: not 'is this word correct?' but 'is this the right member of the set?'","table":{"rows":[["смотре́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"смотреть\">🔊</button>","to look — neutral, safe anywhere"],["гляде́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"глядеть\">🔊</button>","to gaze — warm, folksy, poetic"],["взира́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"взирать\">🔊</button>","to behold — lofty, solemn (высо́кое)"],["пя́литься <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"пялиться\">🔊</button>","to gawk, stare — rude, colloquial"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Neutral: “She was looking out the window.”","en":"Нейтра́льно: «Она́ <b>смотре́ла</b> в окно́».","say":"Нейтрально: она смотрела в окно."},{"ru":"Lofty: “The statue gazes down upon the city.”","en":"Высо́ко: «Ста́туя <b>взира́ет</b> на го́род све́рху».","say":"Высоко: статуя взирает на город сверху."},{"ru":"Rude: “Stop gawking at me!”","en":"Гру́бо: «Хва́тит на меня́ <b>пя́литься</b>!»","say":"Грубо: хватит на меня пялиться!"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"В нау́чной статье́: «А́втор пя́лится на пробле́му».","right":"В нау́чной статье́: «А́втор рассма́тривает пробле́му».","why":"<b>пя́литься</b> is rude slang — impossible in a scientific article. The neutral-bookish choice is <b>рассма́тривать</b>. Same idea, wrong member of the family."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the situation, then tap the fitting word.","q":"In a solemn, lofty passage, “to look upon” is best rendered by…","opts":["пя́литься","взира́ть","смотре́ть"],"answer":1,"explain":"Solemn and lofty → <b>взира́ть</b>. смотре́ть is merely neutral; пя́литься is rude."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"The one neutral verb for “to look,” safe in any style (not folksy, not lofty, not rude). Type it:","accept":["смотреть"],"placeholder":"смотр…","explain":"<b>смотре́ть</b> — the neutral default of the whole family."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Synonyms by intensity","title":"<em>Большо́й — огро́мный — грома́дный</em>: turning up the dial","paras":["Some synonym sets differ not in register but in <b>intensity</b> — how strong the quality is. “Big” climbs a ladder: <b>большо́й</b> (big) → <b>огро́мный</b> (huge) → <b>грома́дный</b> (enormous) → <b>колосса́льный</b> (colossal). Each rung is stronger; picking a high rung for a small thing sounds off.","Feelings ride the same ladders. “Glad” goes <b>рад</b> → <b>сча́стлив</b> (happy) → <b>в восто́рге</b> (thrilled). “Tired” goes <b>уста́л</b> → <b>изму́чен</b> (worn out) → <b>без сил</b> (out of strength). Reach as high as the truth, and no higher.","Intensity and register can combine: <b>грома́дный</b> is strong <i>and</i> a bit expressive, so a dry scientific text prefers the calm <b>большо́й</b> — or a precise figure instead."],"audio":"Some synonym sets differ not in register but in intensity — how strong the quality is. 'Big' climbs a ladder: bolshoy, big; ogromny, huge; gromadny, enormous; kolossalny, colossal. Each rung is stronger; picking a high rung for a small thing sounds off. Feelings ride the same ladders. 'Glad' goes rad, schastliv, v vostorge — thrilled. 'Tired' goes ustal, izmuchen, bez sil. Reach as high as the truth, and no higher. Intensity and register can combine: gromadny is strong and a bit expressive, so a dry scientific text prefers the calm bolshoy, or a precise figure instead.","table":{"rows":[["большо́й — огро́мный — грома́дный — колосса́льный <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"большой, огромный, громадный, колоссальный\">🔊</button>","big → huge → enormous → colossal (rising)"],["рад — сча́стлив — в восто́рге <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"рад, счастлив, в восторге\">🔊</button>","glad → happy → thrilled (rising)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Calm and neutral: “a big project.”","en":"Споко́йно: «<b>большо́й</b> прое́кт».","say":"Спокойно: большой проект."},{"ru":"Turned all the way up: “an enormous, colossal project!”","en":"На макси́муме: «<b>грома́дный</b>, колосса́льный прое́кт!»","say":"На максимуме: громадный, колоссальный проект!"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Я колосса́льно рад, что ты купи́л хлеб».","right":"«Я рад, что ты купи́л хлеб».","why":"колосса́льно sits at the top of the intensity ladder — absurd over a loaf of bread. Match the strength of the word to the size of the thing; overshooting intensity is a real C1 slip."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"A dry scientific report needs the calmest word for “big.” Choose:","opts":["грома́дный","большо́й","колосса́льный"],"answer":1,"explain":"Neutral and calm → <b>большо́й</b>. грома́дный and колосса́льный are expressive — too loud for science."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order — a rising ladder, glad → thrilled.","q":"Build: <b>“I'm not just glad, I'm thrilled.”</b>","want":"Я не про́сто рад, я в восто́рге","answer":"Я не про́сто рад, я в восто́рге"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Connotation","title":"The <em>attitude</em> a word smuggles in","paras":["Beyond style and strength, a word can carry a built-in <b>attitude</b> — its <b>connotation</b> (коннота́ция). Two words point at the same thing yet feel approving, neutral, or contemptuous. Of a firm-willed person: <b>принципиа́льный</b> (principled — warm approval), <b>упря́мый</b> (stubborn — neutral), <b>упёртый</b> (pig-headed — scorn, colloquial).","Same for a talker: <b>ора́тор</b> (orator — respectful), <b>говору́н</b> (chatterbox — light mockery), <b>болту́н</b> (blabbermouth — dismissive). The denotation barely moves; the attitude flips completely. A native reads that attitude instantly — and so must you.","Watch the <b>false friends</b> in this abstract layer, too: <b>симпати́чный</b> means <i>likeable</i>, not “sympathetic”; <b>па́фос</b> is <i>lofty enthusiasm / high emotion</i>, not English “pathos”; <b>претендова́ть</b> is <i>to lay claim to</i>, not “to pretend.”"],"audio":"Beyond style and strength, a word can carry a built-in attitude — its connotation. Two words point at the same thing yet feel approving, neutral, or contemptuous. Of a firm-willed person: printsipialny, principled, warm approval; upryamy, stubborn, neutral; upyorty, pig-headed, scorn, colloquial. Same for a talker: orator, respectful; govorun, chatterbox, light mockery; boltun, blabbermouth, dismissive. The denotation barely moves; the attitude flips completely. Watch the false friends here too: simpatichny means likeable, not sympathetic; pafos is lofty enthusiasm, not English pathos; pretendovat is to lay claim to, not to pretend.","table":{"rows":[["принципиа́льный — упря́мый — упёртый <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"принципиальный, упрямый, упёртый\">🔊</button>","firm-willed: approving — neutral — scornful"],["ора́тор — говору́н — болту́н <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"оратор, говорун, болтун\">🔊</button>","one who talks: respectful — teasing — dismissive"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Approving: “A principled, firm person.”","en":"С одобре́нием: «<b>Принципиа́льный</b>, твёрдый челове́к».","say":"С одобрением: принципиальный, твёрдый человек."},{"ru":"Scornful: “He's just pig-headed.”","en":"С презре́нием: «Он про́сто <b>упёртый</b>».","say":"С презрением: он просто упёртый."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"На собесе́довании о себе́: «Я упёртый».","right":"На собесе́довании о себе́: «Я принципиа́льный и насто́йчивый».","why":"упёртый carries contempt — a strange word to choose about yourself in a job interview. The same trait, but принципиа́льный and насто́йчивый carry approval. Connotation, not denotation, decides."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"You want to praise a friend's determination warmly. Choose:","opts":["упёртый","принципиа́льный","болту́н"],"answer":1,"explain":"Warm praise → <b>принципиа́льный</b>. упёртый is scornful; болту́н isn't even about determination."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Он о́чень симпати́чный» actually means…","opts":["He's very sympathetic","He's very likeable","He's very sad"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>симпати́чный</b> is a false friend: it means <b>likeable</b>, not “sympathetic.”"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Antonyms in register","title":"Even the <em>opposite</em> has to match","paras":["When you reach for a contrast, its <b>antonym</b> (анто́ним — the opposite word) must sit in the same register as its partner. Neutral <b>у́мный ↔ глу́пый</b> (clever ↔ stupid); bookish <b>му́дрый ↔ неразу́мный</b> (wise ↔ unwise). Don't pair a lofty word with a slangy opposite — the seam shows.","Register colours evaluation, too. Neutral “to praise / to scold”: <b>хвали́ть / руга́ть</b>. In elevated prose: <b>восхваля́ть / порица́ть</b>. Choose the matched pair, not just the single word.","By now the habit is forming: for every word, run three quick checks — <b>register</b> (which style?), <b>intensity</b> (how strong?), <b>connotation</b> (what attitude?). That triple check is the ear you're building all month."],"audio":"When you reach for a contrast, its antonym — the opposite word — must sit in the same register as its partner. Neutral: umny versus glupy, clever versus stupid. Bookish: mudry versus nerazumny, wise versus unwise. Don't pair a lofty word with a slangy opposite — the seam shows. Register colours evaluation too. Neutral 'to praise, to scold': hvalit, rugat. In elevated prose: voskhvalyat, poritsat. Choose the matched pair. By now the habit is forming: for every word, run three checks — register, which style; intensity, how strong; connotation, what attitude. That triple check is the ear you're building all month.","table":{"rows":[["у́мный ↔ глу́пый <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"умный, глупый\">🔊</button>","clever ↔ stupid — neutral pair"],["восхваля́ть ↔ порица́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"восхвалять, порицать\">🔊</button>","to extol ↔ to censure — lofty pair"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Neutral pair: “to praise and to scold.”","en":"Нейтра́льно: «<b>хвали́ть</b> и <b>руга́ть</b>».","say":"Нейтрально: хвалить и ругать."},{"ru":"Lofty pair: “to extol and to censure.”","en":"Высо́ко: «<b>восхваля́ть</b> и <b>порица́ть</b>».","say":"Высоко: восхвалять и порицать."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Оди́н геро́й — му́дрый, друго́й — тупо́й».","right":"«Оди́н геро́й — му́дрый, друго́й — неразу́мный».","why":"му́дрый is bookish; pairing it with the blunt colloquial тупо́й snaps the register mid-sentence. The matched bookish antonym is <b>неразу́мный</b>."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"In elevated prose you've written восхваля́ть (to extol). Its register-matched antonym is…","opts":["руга́ть","порица́ть","чихво́стить"],"answer":1,"explain":"Lofty ↔ lofty: <b>порица́ть</b> (to censure). руга́ть is neutral; чихво́стить is rough colloquial."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and read it — feel the register rise on the last word.","target":"Он не про́сто у́мный — он му́дрый.","sub":"neutral «у́мный» lifting into bookish «му́дрый»","want":"он не просто умный он мудрый"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Эмма is stuck on one line of a translation. Ка́тя, Ива́н's friend, peers over her shoulder. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Что застря́ла? Одно́ предложе́ние — це́лый час.","ru":"Why are you stuck? One sentence for a whole hour."},{"who":"A","en":"«He looked at her». Как перевести́ «looked»? У меня́ четы́ре ва́рианта.","ru":"“He looked at her.” How do I translate “looked”? I've got four options."},{"who":"B","en":"Четы́ре? Смотре́л — и всё.","ru":"Four? “Smotrel” — and that's it."},{"who":"A","en":"Смотре́л — е́сли ро́вно. Но геро́й — стари́к в дере́вне. Мо́жет, гляде́л?","ru":"“Smotrel” if it's plain. But the hero is an old man in a village. Maybe “glyadel”?"},{"who":"B","en":"А, тепло́, по-стари́нному. Да, гляде́л ему́ идёт.","ru":"Ah — warm, old-fashioned. Yes, “glyadel” suits him."},{"who":"A","en":"А е́сли бы он был гру́бым — пя́лился.","ru":"And if he were crude — “pyalilsya.”"},{"who":"B","en":"Ну нет, пя́лился — э́то по́шло. Не про э́того стари́ка.","ru":"No way — “pyalilsya” is vulgar. Not for this old man."},{"who":"A","en":"Вот и рабо́та реда́ктора: не сло́во, а отте́нок.","ru":"That's the editor's job for you: not the word, the shade."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the shades between synonyms. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"сино́ним","ru":"synonym","ex":"У э́того сло́ва мно́го сино́нимов."},{"en":"анто́ним","ru":"antonym, opposite","ex":"Анто́ним к «у́мный» — «глу́пый»."},{"en":"отте́нок","ru":"shade, nuance","ex":"Ва́жен то́нкий отте́нок сло́ва."},{"en":"коннота́ция","ru":"connotation (built-in attitude)","ex":"У «упёртый» отрица́тельная коннота́ция."},{"en":"гляде́ть","ru":"to gaze (warm, folksy)","ex":"Стари́к до́лго гляде́л вдаль."},{"en":"взира́ть","ru":"to behold (lofty)","ex":"Она́ мо́лча взира́ла на не́бо."},{"en":"пя́литься","ru":"to gawk, stare (rude)","ex":"Хва́тит пя́литься на прохо́жих."},{"en":"огро́мный","ru":"huge","ex":"Э́то был огро́мный успе́х."},{"en":"грома́дный","ru":"enormous (expressive)","ex":"Пе́ред на́ми стоя́л грома́дный дом."},{"en":"восхваля́ть","ru":"to extol (lofty)","ex":"Поэ́т восхваля́л родну́ю приро́ду."},{"en":"упёртый","ru":"pig-headed (colloq., scornful)","ex":"Он упёртый — его́ не переубеди́ть."},{"en":"интенси́вность","ru":"intensity, strength","ex":"У сино́нимов ра́зная интенси́вность."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Four ways to say “look” — match each to its register.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each verb on the left onto its register — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["смотре́ть","neutral — safe anywhere"],["гляде́ть","warm, folksy"],["взира́ть","lofty, elevated"],["пя́литься","rude, colloquial"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions on shades and synonyms. If one bites, glance back at the rule.","items":[{"q":"In a dry scientific text, “big” is best as…","opts":["грома́дный","большо́й","колосса́льный"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Хва́тит на меня́ пя́литься!» belongs to which register?","opts":["lofty (высо́кое)","neutral","rude, colloquial"],"answer":2},{"q":"Which verb for “look” is lofty (высо́кое)?","opts":["смотре́ть","взира́ть","пя́литься"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Он симпати́чный» means…","opts":["he's likeable","he's sympathetic","he's pathetic"],"answer":0},{"q":"The register-matched antonym of bookish му́дрый is…","opts":["тупо́й","неразу́мный","глу́пый"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"One concept, a family of words — read each line aloud and hear the register shift. (Same idea: a person is looking.)","title":"«Смотре́ть» и его́ семья́","sentences":["Одно́ англи́йское «look» — а по-ру́сски це́лая семья́ слов.","В отчёте геро́й смо́трит; в ска́зке — гляди́т; на па́мятнике — взира́ет.","А гру́бый сосе́д про́сто пя́лится из окна́.","Смысл оди́н, но отте́нок ка́ждый раз друго́й.","Вы́брать ну́жный отте́нок — и есть иску́сство перево́да."],"translation":"A single English “look” — but in Russian, a whole family of words. In a report the hero looks; in a fairy tale he gazes; on a monument he beholds. And the rude neighbour just gawks out of the window. The meaning is one, but the shade is different every time. Choosing the right shade is the very art of translation."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — feel the dial turn.","prompt":"Pick one everyday adjective — say “good,” “big,” or “glad” — and write it in Russian THREE times about the same thing, climbing <b>up in intensity or register</b>: neutral → stronger → strongest (or neutral → bookish). Label each rung. Three short lines.","hint":"For “big”: большо́й → огро́мный → грома́дный. For “glad”: рад → сча́стлив → в восто́рге. Keep it about one single thing so you can hear the dial turn 🙂","example":"Нейтра́льно: Э́то большо́й дом. Си́льнее: Э́то огро́мный дом. На макси́муме: Э́то про́сто грома́дный дом!"}},"3":{"day":3,"week":"01","themeRu":"Words with an attitude","themeEn":"Су́ффиксы оце́нки: до́мик и доми́ще","intro":"«До́мик», «доми́ще», «доми́шко» — three words for one and the same house. What changes isn't the building; it's how the speaker <b>feels</b> about it. Russian can bolt an attitude straight onto a noun with a suffix: warmth (до́мик — a dear little house), awe (доми́ще — a great hulking house), contempt (доми́шко — a sorry little shack). English needs whole phrases for this; Russian does it in a syllable. Today Эмма learns to hear the feeling hidden in the ending.","introAudio":"'Domik', 'domishche', 'domishko' — three words for one and the same house. What changes isn't the building; it's how the speaker feels about it. Russian can bolt an attitude straight onto a noun with a suffix: warmth, domik, a dear little house; awe, domishche, a great hulking house; contempt, domishko, a sorry little shack. English needs whole phrases for this; Russian does it in a syllable. Today Emma learns to hear the feeling hidden in the ending.","goals":["Read <b>diminutive-hypocoristic</b> suffixes (до́мик, ко́шечка, дружо́к) as <b>warmth</b>, not just smallness","Read <b>augmentative</b> suffixes (доми́ще, ручи́ща) as <b>size</b> with awe or roughness","Read <b>pejorative</b> suffixes (доми́шко, работёнка, лю́дишки) as <b>contempt</b>","Remember these suffixes are <b>colloquial</b> — out of place in a scientific or official text"],"learned":["Read diminutive-hypocoristic suffixes as warmth (до́мик, ко́шечка, дружо́к)","Read augmentative suffixes as awe or roughness (доми́ще, ручи́ща)","Read pejorative suffixes as contempt (городи́шко, работёнка)","Kept evaluation suffixes out of the scientific and official styles"],"review":{"intro":"Yesterday's shades first — a synonym chosen by register, an attitude read from connotation — then we bolt the attitude straight onto the word with a suffix.","introAudio":"Yesterday's shades first — a synonym chosen by register, an attitude read from connotation — then we bolt the attitude straight onto the word with a suffix.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. Yesterday's synonyms.","q":"For a solemn, lofty line, “to look upon” is best rendered by…","opts":["пя́литься","смотре́ть","взира́ть"],"answer":2,"explain":"Solemn and lofty → <b>взира́ть</b>. смотре́ть is neutral; пя́литься is rude."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word carries <b>scorn</b>?","opts":["принципиа́льный","упёртый","насто́йчивый"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>упёртый</b> (pig-headed) is scornful. принципиа́льный and насто́йчивый carry approval."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order — a rising ladder.","q":"Build: <b>“not just big — enormous.”</b>","want":"не про́сто большо́й, а огро́мный","answer":"не про́сто большо́й, а огро́мный"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The affectionate suffix","title":"<em>До́мик, ко́шечка, дружо́к</em>: the warmth suffix","paras":["Russian carries feeling in its <b>suffixes of subjective evaluation</b> (су́ффиксы субъекти́вной оце́нки). The first family is <b>diminutive-hypocoristic</b> (уменьши́тельно-ласка́тельные): -ик, -ок/-ёк, -очк-/-ечк-, -ушк-. They add <b>tenderness</b> — a house you love is a <b>до́мик</b>, a cat you adore is a <b>ко́шечка</b>, a dear friend is a <b>дружо́к</b>.","The key at C1: it isn't really about size. A <b>до́мик</b> can be a perfectly normal house you simply feel warmly toward; <b>со́лнышко</b> (“little sun”) is a term of endearment for a person. The suffix speaks the heart, not the tape measure.","This is the language of home, children and affection — the <b>разгово́рный</b> (conversational) style. Warm and lovely there; wrong the moment you step into a report or a contract."],"audio":"Russian carries feeling in its suffixes of subjective evaluation. The first family is diminutive-hypocoristic: they add tenderness — a house you love is a domik, a cat you adore is a koshechka, a dear friend is a druzhok. The key at C1: it isn't really about size. A domik can be a perfectly normal house you simply feel warmly toward; solnyshko, little sun, is a term of endearment for a person. The suffix speaks the heart, not the tape measure. This is the language of home, children and affection — the conversational style. Warm and lovely there; wrong the moment you step into a report or a contract.","table":{"rows":[["дом → до́мик <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"дом, домик\">🔊</button>","house → dear little house (affection)"],["ко́шка → ко́шечка <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"кошка, кошечка\">🔊</button>","cat → sweet little cat"],["друг → дружо́к <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"друг, дружок\">🔊</button>","friend → dear pal, buddy"],["со́лнце → со́лнышко <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"солнце, солнышко\">🔊</button>","sun → “sweetheart” (endearment)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Warm: “What a cosy little house!”","en":"С не́жностью: «Како́й ую́тный <b>до́мик</b>!»","say":"С нежностью: какой уютный домик!"},{"ru":"Endearment: “Sleep, my little sun.”","en":"Ла́сково: «Спи, моё <b>со́лнышко</b>».","say":"Ласково: спи, моё солнышко."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"В нау́чной статье́: «Мы изучи́ли ко́шечек в лаборато́рии».","right":"В нау́чной статье́: «Мы изучи́ли ко́шек в лаборато́рии».","why":"The affectionate <b>ко́шечка</b> is conversational — it drips warmth into a place that must stay neutral. Science needs the plain <b>ко́шка</b>. The suffix isn't wrong Russian; it's the wrong register."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the situation, then tap the word.","q":"You're cooing over a friend's new kitten. The warm, affectionate word is…","opts":["ко́шечка","ко́шка","ко́тище"],"answer":0,"explain":"Affection → <b>ко́шечка</b>. ко́шка is neutral; ко́тище is a big rough “tomcat.”"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"The affectionate form of «дом» — a beloved little house. Type it:","accept":["домик"],"placeholder":"до́м…","explain":"<b>до́мик</b> — the warmth suffix -ик on «дом»."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The augmentative suffix","title":"<em>Доми́ще, ручи́ща</em>: the “huge!” suffix","paras":["The opposite family is <b>augmentative</b> (увеличи́тельные): -ищ-. It says <b>big</b> — often with a gasp of awe or a note of rough admiration. A <b>доми́ще</b> is a great hulking house; a <b>ручи́ща</b> is a huge paw of a hand; a booming voice is a <b>голоси́ще</b>.","Notice the gender some take: masculine <b>доми́ще</b> (from дом), but feminine <b>ручи́ща</b> (from рука́), <b>бороди́ща</b> (from борода́). The suffix stays -ищ-; the ending follows the base word's world.","Like the affectionate ones, augmentatives live in <b>lively, colloquial</b> speech. They carry emotion — great! enormous! — so they belong in conversation and expressive prose, never in a neutral report."],"audio":"The opposite family is augmentative, with the suffix -ishch-. It says big — often with a gasp of awe or a note of rough admiration. A domishche is a great hulking house; a ruchishcha is a huge paw of a hand; a booming voice is a golosishche. Notice the gender some take: masculine domishche from dom, but feminine ruchishcha from ruka, borodishcha from boroda. The suffix stays -ishch-; the ending follows the base word's world. Like the affectionate ones, augmentatives live in lively, colloquial speech. They carry emotion, so they belong in conversation and expressive prose, never in a neutral report.","table":{"rows":[["дом → доми́ще <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"дом, домище\">🔊</button>","house → a great hulking house"],["рука́ → ручи́ща <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"рука, ручища\">🔊</button>","hand → a huge paw of a hand"],["го́лос → голоси́ще <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"голос, голосище\">🔊</button>","voice → a booming great voice"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Awe: “Look what an enormous house they threw up!”","en":"С восхище́нием: «Вон како́й <b>доми́ще</b> отгроха́ли!»","say":"С восхищением: вон какой домище отгрохали!"},{"ru":"Rough admiration: “He's got hands like shovels.”","en":"Гру́бовато: «У него́ <b>ручи́щи</b> как лопа́ты».","say":"Грубовато: у него ручищи как лопаты."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"В объявле́нии о прода́же: «Продаётся хоро́ший доми́ще».","right":"В объявле́нии о прода́же: «Продаётся большо́й дом».","why":"<b>доми́ще</b> is the expressive, colloquial “great hulking house” — it clashes with the flat, businesslike tone of an advert. Neutral <b>большо́й дом</b> is what belongs there."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Marvelling at a giant house with a friend, you'd say…","opts":["до́мик","доми́шко","доми́ще"],"answer":2,"explain":"Awe at size → the augmentative <b>доми́ще</b>. до́мик is affectionate; доми́шко is scornful."},{"type":"choice","q":"«У него́ ручи́ща!» tells us his hand is…","opts":["tiny and cute","huge","ordinary"],"answer":1,"explain":"The augmentative -ищ- makes <b>ручи́ща</b> a huge hand."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The scornful suffix","title":"<em>Городи́шко, работёнка</em>: attitude, not size","paras":["The trickiest family is <b>pejorative</b> (пренебрежи́тельные): -ишк-, -ёнк-/-онк-. Here is the C1 trap. <b>Городи́шко</b> is not “a small town” — it's a town the speaker <b>looks down on</b>: a dump, a nowhere place. <b>Работёнка</b> is a job spoken of with a shrug; <b>кни́жонка</b> is a trashy little book; <b>лю́дишки</b> are “wretched little people.”","So the suffix changes the <b>attitude</b>, not the size. Miss it and you read «городи́шко» as a neutral “small town” — and you lose the sneer the writer built in. In literature this contempt is a deliberate tool; catch it, don't smooth it over.","And once more: these are <b>colloquial-to-low</b> in register. A pejorative suffix in a neutral or official text is a glaring style error — you've smuggled scorn into a place that must stay flat."],"audio":"The trickiest family is pejorative, with -ishk-, -yonk-, -onk-. Here is the C1 trap. Gorodishko is not 'a small town' — it's a town the speaker looks down on: a dump, a nowhere place. Rabotyonka is a job spoken of with a shrug; knizhonka is a trashy little book; lyudishki are wretched little people. So the suffix changes the attitude, not the size. Miss it and you read gorodishko as a neutral small town, and you lose the sneer the writer built in. In literature this contempt is a deliberate tool; catch it, don't smooth it over. And once more: these are colloquial-to-low in register. A pejorative suffix in a neutral or official text is a glaring style error.","table":{"rows":[["го́род → городи́шко <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"город, городишко\">🔊</button>","town → a dump of a town (contempt)"],["рабо́та → работёнка <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"работа, работёнка\">🔊</button>","job → a measly little job"],["кни́га → кни́жонка <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"книга, книжонка\">🔊</button>","book → a trashy little book"],["лю́ди → лю́дишки <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"люди, людишки\">🔊</button>","people → wretched little people"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Contempt: “He grew up in some dump of a town.”","en":"С презре́нием: «Он вы́рос в како́м-то <b>городи́шке</b>».","say":"С презрением: он вырос в каком-то городишке."},{"ru":"Dismissive: “Found myself some little job.”","en":"Пренебрежи́тельно: «Нашёл себе́ каку́ю-то <b>работёнку</b>».","say":"Пренебрежительно: нашёл себе какую-то работёнку."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Он роди́лся в ма́леньком городи́шке» (as a neutral fact).","right":"«Он роди́лся в ма́леньком го́роде».","why":"To state a plain fact, <b>городи́шко</b> is wrong — it sneers at the town. If you don't mean contempt, use the neutral <b>го́род</b>. The suffix is an attitude marker, not a size marker."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Городи́шко» tells you the speaker feels the town is…","opts":["beneath contempt — a dump","simply small","very beautiful"],"answer":0,"explain":"The pejorative -ишк- adds <b>contempt</b>: a dump of a town, not a neutrally “small” one."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and read it — hear the contempt the suffix adds.","target":"Э́то не го́род, а городи́шко.","sub":"the pejorative суффикс turning “town” into “dump”","want":"это не город а городишко"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · One base, three attitudes","title":"The whole ladder — and where it's <em>banned</em>","paras":["Put the family together and one base word fans into a spectrum of feeling: <b>до́мик</b> (dear) — <b>дом</b> (neutral) — <b>доми́ще</b> (huge) — <b>доми́шко</b> (wretched). Same referent, four attitudes. Reading Russian well means catching which one the writer chose, and why.","You can even stack the tenderness: <b>ма́ма → ма́мочка</b>, <b>сын → сыно́чек</b>, <b>Ва́ня → Ва́нечка</b>. First names take these endings constantly — that's why Russians have so many warm forms of one name.","The C1 boundary: every one of these suffixes is <b>expressive and colloquial</b>. In a scientific article, a law or a business letter they're a register error — those styles want the bare base word (дом, го́род, рабо́та). Save the suffixes for speech, letters to friends, and literature."],"audio":"Put the family together and one base word fans into a spectrum of feeling: domik, dear; dom, neutral; domishche, huge; domishko, wretched. Same referent, four attitudes. Reading Russian well means catching which one the writer chose, and why. You can even stack the tenderness: mama to mamochka, syn to synochek, Vanya to Vanechka. First names take these endings constantly — that's why Russians have so many warm forms of one name. The C1 boundary: every one of these suffixes is expressive and colloquial. In a scientific article, a law or a business letter they're a register error — those styles want the bare base word. Save the suffixes for speech, letters to friends, and literature.","table":{"rows":[["до́мик — дом — доми́ще — доми́шко <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"домик, дом, домище, домишко\">🔊</button>","dear — neutral — huge — wretched"],["сын → сыно́чек <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"сын, сыночек\">🔊</button>","son → dear little son (tender)"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"In a chat: “We rented a cute little house by the sea.”","en":"В ча́те: «Сня́ли ми́лый <b>до́мик</b> у мо́ря».","say":"В чате: сняли милый домик у моря."},{"ru":"In a contract: “The house is transferred to the buyer.”","en":"В догово́ре: «<b>Дом</b> передаётся покупа́телю».","say":"В договоре: дом передаётся покупателю."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"В заявле́нии: «Прошу́ вы́делить мне кварти́рку».","right":"В заявле́нии: «Прошу́ вы́делить мне кварти́ру».","why":"<b>кварти́рка</b> is a cosy, colloquial “little flat” — its warmth is out of place in an official application. The document needs the neutral <b>кварти́ра</b>. The highest-register styles reject evaluation suffixes."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"In which style are evaluation suffixes (до́мик, доми́ще) a mistake?","opts":["in a friendly chat","in a scientific article","in a fairy tale"],"answer":1,"explain":"The <b>scientific</b> style bans expressive suffixes. Chat and fiction welcome them."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Ва́нечка», compared with «Ва́ня», sounds…","opts":["warmer and more affectionate","colder and official","ruder"],"answer":0,"explain":"The -ечк- ending is hypocoristic: it makes the name <b>warmer and fonder</b>."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Эмма reads a page of the novel she's translating aloud to Ле́на, and stops at one word. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"A","en":"Слу́шай: «Он вы́рос в се́ром городи́шке». Не «в го́роде» — в городи́шке.","ru":"Listen: “He grew up in a grey dump of a town.” Not “in a town” — in a “gorodishko.”"},{"who":"B","en":"И что? Ма́ленький го́род.","ru":"So what? A small town."},{"who":"A","en":"Не про разме́р. А́втор его́ презира́ет — «дыра́», «глушь».","ru":"It's not about size. The author despises it — “a hole,” “the sticks.”"},{"who":"B","en":"А, то́чно. Оди́н су́ффикс — и це́лое отноше́ние.","ru":"Ah, right. One suffix — and a whole attitude."},{"who":"A","en":"И́менно. А ря́дом — «до́мик ба́бушки». Тут уже́ тепло́, с любо́вью.","ru":"Exactly. And next to it — “grandma's dear little house.” Here it's warm, loving."},{"who":"B","en":"Оди́н дом — до́мик, друго́й — доми́шко?","ru":"One house is a “domik,” another a “domishko”?"},{"who":"A","en":"Да. Разме́р тот же, а чу́вство ра́зное.","ru":"Yes. The size is the same, but the feeling is different."},{"who":"B","en":"Ру́сский язы́к пря́мо ды́шит э́тими су́ффиксами.","ru":"Russian practically breathes with these suffixes."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the attitude hidden in a suffix. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"су́ффикс","ru":"suffix","ex":"Су́ффикс мо́жет нести́ оце́нку."},{"en":"оце́нка","ru":"evaluation, appraisal","ex":"В сло́ве слы́шно оце́нку."},{"en":"до́мик","ru":"dear little house (affection)","ex":"Мы сня́ли ую́тный до́мик."},{"en":"доми́ще","ru":"a huge house (augmentative)","ex":"Вот э́то доми́ще!"},{"en":"городи́шко","ru":"dump of a town (contempt)","ex":"Ску́чный городи́шко."},{"en":"работёнка","ru":"a measly job (dismissive)","ex":"Так, кака́я-то работёнка."},{"en":"ко́шечка","ru":"sweet little cat (tender)","ex":"Кака́я ми́лая ко́шечка!"},{"en":"ручи́ща","ru":"a huge hand (augmentative)","ex":"У него́ ручи́ща как лопа́та."},{"en":"ла́сковый","ru":"tender, affectionate","ex":"Ла́сковое сло́во прия́тно."},{"en":"пренебрежи́тельный","ru":"dismissive, scornful","ex":"У сло́ва пренебрежи́тельный отте́нок."},{"en":"уменьши́тельный","ru":"diminutive","ex":"Э́то уменьши́тельная фо́рма."},{"en":"презре́ние","ru":"contempt, scorn","ex":"В его́ го́лосе бы́ло презре́ние."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each word to the attitude its suffix carries.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each word on the left onto its attitude — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["до́мик","affection (a dear little house)"],["доми́ще","awe (a huge house)"],["городи́шко","contempt (a wretched town)"],["ко́шечка","tenderness (a sweet cat)"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions on the attitude-suffixes. If one bites, glance back at the rule.","items":[{"q":"«Городи́шко» mainly signals that the town is…","opts":["looked down on — a dump","tiny but pretty","far away"],"answer":0},{"q":"Which suffix makes a word affectionate and warm?","opts":["доми́ще","городи́шко","до́мик"],"answer":2},{"q":"In a scientific article, a suffix like ко́шечка is…","opts":["more precise","a register mistake","required"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Ручи́ща» describes a hand that is…","opts":["huge","dainty","injured"],"answer":0},{"q":"Compared with «Ва́ня», the form «Ва́нечка» sounds…","opts":["more official","warmer and fonder","plural"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"One root, three feelings — read each line aloud and hear the attitude change. (Same idea: buildings and a town.)","title":"Оди́н дом, три чу́вства","sentences":["У реки́ стоя́л ста́рый до́мик — тёплый, родно́й.","За холмо́м вы́рос но́вый доми́ще — бога́тый и хвастли́вый.","А да́льше начина́лся се́рый городи́шко, кото́рый а́втор я́вно не люби́л.","Оди́н ко́рень — «дом», «го́род» — но в ка́ждом сло́ве своё отноше́ние.","Чита́ть по-ру́сски — зна́чит слы́шать э́ти отте́нки."],"translation":"By the river stood an old little house — warm, homey. Beyond the hill a huge new house had gone up — rich and boastful. And further on began a grey dump of a town, which the author clearly disliked. One root — “house,” “town” — yet each word carries its own attitude. To read in Russian is to hear these shades."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — bolt on the feeling.","prompt":"Describe the same thing — a house, a car, a town, or a job — in Russian TWICE with evaluation suffixes: once with <b>warmth</b> (a diminutive) and once with <b>contempt</b> (a pejorative). Label each. Two short sentences. Then, in English, name the one style where both would be wrong.","hint":"Warmth: до́мик, маши́нка, кварти́рка. Contempt: доми́шко, городи́шко, работёнка. Remember — both are colloquial, never scientific or official 🙂","example":"С любо́вью: Мы купи́ли ма́ленький ую́тный до́мик. С презре́нием: Да э́то не дом, а како́й-то доми́шко. (О́ба сло́ва — разгово́рные.)"}},"4":{"day":4,"week":"01","themeRu":"The language of science","themeEn":"Нау́чный стиль","intro":"Макси́м writes «I think this works really well» in his report — and Эмма's job is to make it sound as though nobody thinks anything at all. Welcome to the <b>научный стиль</b> (scientific style), where the author vanishes and the facts speak for themselves: impersonal, nominal, cool. No “I,” no feelings — just «представля́ется, что…» and «сле́довательно». It's the reverse of the English reflex, and today you build it.","introAudio":"Maksim writes 'I think this works really well' in his report — and Emma's job is to make it sound as though nobody thinks anything at all. Welcome to the nauchny stil, the scientific style, where the author vanishes and the facts speak for themselves: impersonal, nominal, cool. No 'I,' no feelings — just 'predstavlyaetsya chto' and 'sledovatelno.' It's the reverse of the English reflex, and today you build it.","goals":["Write in the <b>impersonal</b> voice of science: «представля́ется, что…», not «я ду́маю»","Turn verbs into <b>verbal nouns</b> (отглаго́льные): реши́ть → реше́ние, изуча́ть → изуче́ние","Bind an argument with <b>logical connectors</b>: сле́довательно, таки́м о́бразом","Keep the tone <b>precise and unemotional</b> — no feelings, no expressive suffixes"],"learned":["Wrote in the impersonal scientific voice (представля́ется, отмеча́ется)","Built verbal nouns and nominal phrases (реше́ние зада́чи)","Linked reasoning with сле́довательно / таки́м о́бразом","Kept emotion and personality out of the scientific style"],"review":{"intro":"One look back at yesterday's attitude-suffixes, plus a B2 reflex — the -ся passive — because science leans on it heavily. Then: the cool, nominal voice of Russian science.","introAudio":"One look back at yesterday's attitude-suffixes, plus a B2 reflex — the reflexive passive — because science leans on it heavily. Then: the cool, nominal voice of Russian science.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. Yesterday's suffixes.","q":"«Городи́шко» carries which attitude?","opts":["affection","contempt","awe"],"answer":1,"explain":"The pejorative -ишк- adds <b>contempt</b> — a dump of a town, not a neutrally small one."},{"type":"choice","q":"“The problem is being examined” (impersonal -ся passive) —","opts":["Пробле́му рассма́тривают","Пробле́ма рассма́тривается","Пробле́ма рассма́тривать"],"answer":1,"explain":"The -ся passive: <b>рассма́тривается</b>, with the subject in the nominative. The first is active (“they examine”); the third isn't finite."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order — the scientific voice.","q":"Build: <b>“The study shows that the question is open.”</b>","want":"Иссле́дование пока́зывает, что вопро́с откры́т","answer":"Иссле́дование пока́зывает, что вопро́с откры́т"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Science speaks in nouns","title":"Russian science is <em>nominal</em> and <em>impersonal</em>","paras":["The <b>научный</b> (scientific) style has a distinctive shape. First, it is <b>impersonal</b>: it hides the “I / we” behind constructions with no subject. Not «я ду́маю, что…» but <b>«представля́ется, что…»</b> (it appears that), <b>«сле́дует отме́тить»</b> (it should be noted), <b>«счита́ется»</b> (it is considered), <b>«отмеча́ется»</b> (it is noted).","Second, it is <b>nominal</b> (именно́й) — it prefers nouns, especially <b>verbal nouns</b> (отглаго́льные существи́тельные) built from verbs: реши́ть → <b>реше́ние</b>, изуча́ть → <b>изуче́ние</b>, приме́нять → <b>примене́ние</b>. Where speech says “we studied and concluded,” science says “the study led to the conclusion.”","Third, it is <b>precise and unemotional</b>: terms, exact figures, no evaluation, no expressive suffixes, no particles. The tone is cool, careful and depersonalised on purpose."],"audio":"The scientific style has a distinctive shape. First, it is impersonal: it hides the I or we behind constructions with no subject. Not 'ya dumayu chto' but 'predstavlyaetsya chto', it appears that; 'sleduet otmetit', it should be noted; 'schitaetsya', it is considered. Second, it is nominal — it prefers nouns, especially verbal nouns built from verbs: reshit to reshenie, izuchat to izuchenie, primenyat to primenenie. Where speech says 'we studied and concluded,' science says 'the study led to the conclusion.' Third, it is precise and unemotional: terms, exact figures, no evaluation, no expressive suffixes, no particles. The tone is cool and depersonalised on purpose.","table":{"rows":[["представля́ется, что… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"представляется, что\">🔊</button>","“it appears that…” (impersonal)"],["сле́дует отме́тить <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"следует отметить\">🔊</button>","“it should be noted”"],["реши́ть → реше́ние <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"решить, решение\">🔊</button>","verb → verbal noun (nominal style)"],["изуча́ть → изуче́ние <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"изучать, изучение\">🔊</button>","to study → the study of"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Impersonal: “It appears that the question remains open.”","en":"Безли́чно: «<b>Представля́ется</b>, что вопро́с остаётся откры́тым».","say":"Безлично: представляется, что вопрос остаётся открытым."},{"ru":"Nominal: “The study of the problem requires a new approach.”","en":"Именно́: «<b>Изуче́ние</b> пробле́мы тре́бует но́вого подхо́да».","say":"Именно: изучение проблемы требует нового подхода."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"В статье́: «Я ду́маю, что э́то о́чень кла́ссная тео́рия».","right":"В статье́: «Представля́ется, что да́нная тео́рия заслу́живает внима́ния».","why":"«Я ду́маю» + the slangy «кла́ссная» break the scientific style twice — it must be impersonal and unemotional. Science replaces “I think it's great” with “it appears the theory merits attention.”"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the situation, then tap the answer.","q":"In a scientific article, “I think that…” is best replaced by…","opts":["«представля́ется, что…»","«я счита́ю, что…»","«мне ка́жется, что…»"],"answer":0,"explain":"Science is impersonal → <b>«представля́ется, что…»</b>. The other two keep the personal “I.”"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks are just for you.","q":"The verbal noun (отглаго́льное) from «реши́ть». Type it:","accept":["решение"],"placeholder":"реш…","explain":"<b>реше́ние</b> — the -ение noun packing the verb «реши́ть» into a noun."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Verbal nouns","title":"<em>Реше́ние, обеспе́чение</em>: turning verbs into nouns","paras":["The engine of scientific density is <b>nominalization</b> — packing a verb's meaning into a noun. The -ние/-ение and -ция endings do the work: обеспе́чить → <b>обеспе́чение</b> (provision), развива́ться → <b>разви́тие</b> (development), взаимоде́йствовать → <b>взаимоде́йствие</b> (interaction).","These nouns let you build compact, term-heavy phrases: «<b>обеспе́чение безопа́сности</b>» (the provision of safety), «<b>реше́ние зада́чи</b>» (the solution of the task). Chains of nouns in the genitive are the visual signature of the style.","Watch the stress on these bookish words — it's where English speakers slip: <b>обеспе́чение</b> (not «обеспече́ние»), <b>сосредото́чение</b>, <b>обобще́ние</b>. Say them slowly and correctly; the prescriptive norm matters at C1."],"audio":"The engine of scientific density is nominalization — packing a verb's meaning into a noun. The -nie and -tsiya endings do the work: obespechit to obespechenie, provision; razvivatsya to razvitie, development; vzaimodeystvovat to vzaimodeystvie, interaction. These nouns let you build compact, term-heavy phrases: obespechenie bezopasnosti, the provision of safety; reshenie zadachi, the solution of the task. Chains of nouns in the genitive are the visual signature of the style. Watch the stress on these bookish words — it's where English speakers slip: obespechenie, not obespechenie with the wrong stress. The prescriptive norm matters at C1.","table":{"rows":[["обеспе́чить → обеспе́чение <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"обеспечить, обеспечение\">🔊</button>","to provide → provision (mind the stress!)"],["развива́ться → разви́тие <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"развиваться, развитие\">🔊</button>","to develop → development"],["взаимоде́йствовать → взаимоде́йствие <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"взаимодействовать, взаимодействие\">🔊</button>","to interact → interaction"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"“The solution of the task depends on many factors.”","en":"«<b>Реше́ние</b> зада́чи зави́сит от мно́гих фа́кторов».","say":"Решение задачи зависит от многих факторов."},{"ru":"“The provision of safety is the priority.”","en":"«<b>Обеспе́чение</b> безопа́сности — гла́вный приорите́т».","say":"Обеспечение безопасности главный приоритет."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«обеспече́ние» (wrong stress)","right":"обеспе́чение","why":"A classic C1 stress trap: the norm is <b>обеспе́чение</b>, stress on the second «е», not «обеспече́ние». Bookish words demand the prescriptive stress — get it wrong and the ear notices."}],"tasks":[{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order — a nominal phrase.","q":"Build the noun phrase: <b>“the solution of the task.”</b>","want":"реше́ние зада́чи","answer":"реше́ние зада́чи"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which is the correct prescriptive C1 stress?","opts":["обеспече́ние","обеспе́чение","обе́спечение"],"answer":1,"explain":"The norm is <b>обеспе́чение</b> — stress on the second «е»."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Logical connectors","title":"<em>Сле́довательно, таки́м о́бразом</em>: the girders of an argument","paras":["Scientific prose is a chain of reasoning, and it names every link out loud with <b>logical connectors</b>. Consequence: <b>сле́довательно</b> (consequently), <b>таки́м о́бразом</b> (thus), <b>поэ́тому</b> (therefore). Sequence: <b>во-пе́рвых… во-вторы́х…</b> (firstly… secondly…). Addition: <b>кро́ме того́</b> (moreover).","Contrast and concession: <b>одна́ко</b> (however), <b>тем не ме́нее</b> (nevertheless). Reference: <b>как отме́чено вы́ше</b> (as noted above), <b>в да́нной рабо́те</b> (in the present work). These aren't decoration — they're the girders that hold the argument up.","In conversation you'd say «зна́чит» or «ну и вот»; science upgrades to «сле́довательно» and «таки́м о́бразом». Same logic, higher register — reach for the bookish connector in a bookish text."],"audio":"Scientific prose is a chain of reasoning, and it names every link out loud with logical connectors. Consequence: sledovatelno, consequently; takim obrazom, thus; poetomu, therefore. Sequence: vo-pervykh, vo-vtorykh — firstly, secondly. Addition: krome togo, moreover. Contrast: odnako, however; tem ne meneye, nevertheless. Reference: kak otmecheno vyshe, as noted above. These aren't decoration — they're the girders that hold the argument up. In conversation you'd say 'znachit' or 'nu i vot'; science upgrades to 'sledovatelno' and 'takim obrazom.' Same logic, higher register.","table":{"rows":[["сле́довательно <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"следовательно\">🔊</button>","consequently (marks a conclusion)"],["таки́м о́бразом <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"таким образом\">🔊</button>","thus, in this way"],["одна́ко <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"однако\">🔊</button>","however (bookish “but”)"],["кро́ме того́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"кроме того\">🔊</button>","moreover, besides"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"“The data are incomplete; consequently, the conclusion is preliminary.”","en":"«Да́нные непо́лны; <b>сле́довательно</b>, вы́вод предвари́телен».","say":"Данные неполны, следовательно, вывод предварителен."},{"ru":"“Thus, the hypothesis is confirmed.”","en":"«<b>Таки́м о́бразом</b>, гипо́теза подтвержда́ется».","say":"Таким образом, гипотеза подтверждается."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"В нау́чной рабо́те: «Ну и вот, зна́чит, тео́рия ве́рная».","right":"В нау́чной рабо́те: «Сле́довательно, тео́рия подтвержда́ется».","why":"«Ну и вот, зна́чит» is the connective tissue of <b>speech</b>, not science. The scientific register upgrades it to «сле́довательно». The logic is the same; the register is not."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"To mark a conclusion in a scientific text, use…","opts":["сле́довательно","ну и вот","да ла́дно"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>сле́довательно</b> (consequently) is the bookish connector for a conclusion. The others are conversational."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and read it evenly, like a lecture.","target":"Таки́м о́бразом, вопро́с остаётся откры́тым.","sub":"a scientific conclusion — flat, even intonation","want":"таким образом вопрос остаётся открытым"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · No feelings, please","title":"The style that <em>bans</em> emotion","paras":["Here's the trap for English speakers. English science can be a little personal — “we were surprised to find…”, “a remarkable result.” Russian science strips all of that out. No <b>«я / мы ду́маю»</b>, no evaluation like «прекра́сный, ужа́сный», no expressive suffixes, no particles «же / ведь». Feeling is a style error here.","Instead, everything is depersonalised and measured: «<b>отмеча́ется</b>, что…», «<b>устано́влено</b>, что…», «<b>результа́ты свиде́тельствуют о…</b>». The author disappears; the facts speak. Precision replaces expression.","So when Эмма edits a scientific translation, half her job is <b>deleting personality</b>: turning “I really think this is huge” into “the significance of this appears considerable.” Cool, nominal, impersonal — that is the sound of Russian science."],"audio":"Here's the trap for English speakers. English science can be a little personal — 'we were surprised to find,' 'a remarkable result.' Russian science strips all of that out. No 'ya dumayu,' no evaluation like 'prekrasny, uzhasny,' no expressive suffixes, no particles. Feeling is a style error here. Instead, everything is depersonalised and measured: 'otmechaetsya chto,' it is noted that; 'ustanovleno chto,' it has been established that; 'rezultaty svidetelstvuyut o,' the results testify to. The author disappears; the facts speak. So when Emma edits a scientific translation, half her job is deleting personality. Cool, nominal, impersonal — that is the sound of Russian science.","table":{"rows":[["отмеча́ется, что… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"отмечается, что\">🔊</button>","“it is noted that…”"],["устано́влено, что… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"установлено, что\">🔊</button>","“it has been established that…”"],["результа́ты свиде́тельствуют о… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"результаты свидетельствуют о\">🔊</button>","“the results testify to…”"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Personal (wrong for science): “I think this is a great result.”","en":"Ли́чно (не для нау́ки): «Я ду́маю, э́то отли́чный результа́т».","say":"Лично, не для науки: я думаю, это отличный результат."},{"ru":"Impersonal (scientific): “The result is deemed significant.”","en":"Безли́чно (нау́чно): «Результа́т счита́ется значи́мым».","say":"Безлично, научно: результат считается значимым."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Э́тот потряса́ющий экспериме́нт нас всех удиви́л!»","right":"«Результа́ты экспериме́нта оказа́лись неожи́данными».","why":"The exclamation, «потряса́ющий», «нас всех», «удиви́л» — pure emotion and personality. Scientific style removes the people and the feelings: “the results proved unexpected.”"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which sentence fits the scientific style?","opts":["«Э́то про́сто невероя́тное откры́тие!»","«Устано́влено, что показа́тель вы́рос»","«Я ва́м так скажу́: тео́рия кла́ссная»"],"answer":1,"explain":"Impersonal and measured → <b>«Устано́влено, что показа́тель вы́рос»</b>. The others are emotional or chatty."},{"type":"choice","q":"In Russian scientific writing, an emotional, personal tone is…","opts":["a style error","expected","more convincing"],"answer":0,"explain":"It's a <b>style error</b>: Russian science must stay impersonal and unemotional."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Макси́м, Ива́н and Ле́на's brother, is an engineer. Эмма is editing the English translation of his technical report. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"A","en":"Макси́м, здесь ты написа́л: «Мы ду́маем, что э́то отли́чно рабо́тает».","ru":"Maksim, here you wrote: “We think this works great.”"},{"who":"B","en":"Ну да. А что не так?","ru":"Well, yes. What's wrong with it?"},{"who":"A","en":"Для нау́чного отчёта — сли́шком ли́чно. На́до безли́чно: «представля́ется эффекти́вным».","ru":"For a scientific report — too personal. It needs to be impersonal: “appears to be effective.”"},{"who":"B","en":"Так су́хо? Без «мы ду́маем»?","ru":"So dry? Without “we think”?"},{"who":"A","en":"И́менно. И не «рабо́тает отли́чно», а «функциони́рует надёжно». Без эмо́ций.","ru":"Exactly. And not “works great,” but “functions reliably.” No emotions."},{"who":"B","en":"А «сле́довательно» вме́сто «ну и вот»?","ru":"And “consequently” instead of “well, so”?"},{"who":"A","en":"Вот тепе́рь ты понима́ешь нау́чный стиль.","ru":"Now you're getting the scientific style."},{"who":"B","en":"Ску́чно, зато́ то́чно.","ru":"Boring, but precise."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the voice of science. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"нау́чный","ru":"scientific","ex":"Э́то нау́чный текст."},{"en":"те́рмин","ru":"term","ex":"В нау́ке ва́жен то́чный те́рмин."},{"en":"иссле́дование","ru":"study, research","ex":"Иссле́дование заняло́ год."},{"en":"гипо́теза","ru":"hypothesis","ex":"Гипо́теза подтверди́лась."},{"en":"обеспе́чение","ru":"provision, supply","ex":"Обеспе́чение безопа́сности — гла́вное."},{"en":"реше́ние","ru":"solution, decision","ex":"Реше́ние ещё не на́йдено."},{"en":"представля́ться","ru":"to appear, seem (impersonal)","ex":"Представля́ется, что э́то ве́рно."},{"en":"сле́довательно","ru":"consequently, therefore","ex":"Фа́кты про́тив; сле́довательно, тео́рия слаба́."},{"en":"таки́м о́бразом","ru":"thus, in this way","ex":"Таки́м о́бразом, вы́вод я́сен."},{"en":"безли́чный","ru":"impersonal","ex":"Нау́ка лю́бит безли́чные фо́рмы."},{"en":"отглаго́льный","ru":"verbal (built from a verb)","ex":"Отглаго́льное существи́тельное — «реше́ние»."},{"en":"то́чность","ru":"precision, exactness","ex":"Нау́чный стиль тре́бует то́чности."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each phrase to its role in the scientific style.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each phrase on the left onto its role — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["представля́ется, что…","impersonal opinion (science)"],["таки́м о́бразом","logical connector"],["изуче́ние вопро́са","verbal-noun phrase"],["я счита́ю, э́то кру́то","too personal for science"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions on the scientific style. If one bites, glance back at the rule.","items":[{"q":"Russian scientific style prefers…","opts":["a personal voice","impersonal constructions","slang"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Представля́ется, что…» marks which style?","opts":["conversational","scientific","official"],"answer":1},{"q":"The verbal noun from «изуча́ть» is…","opts":["изуче́ние","изуча́тель","изу́ченный"],"answer":0},{"q":"The prescriptive C1 stress is…","opts":["обеспече́ние","обеспе́чение","обе́спечение"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Сле́довательно» signals…","opts":["a conclusion","a greeting","a doubt"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"A short paragraph in the scientific style — read it evenly, like a lecture, and hear the impersonal, nominal voice.","title":"Как говори́т нау́ка","sentences":["В да́нной рабо́те рассма́тривается вопро́с о вли́янии све́та на рост расте́ний.","Иссле́дование показа́ло, что при недоста́тке све́та рост замедля́ется.","Отмеча́ется та́кже зави́симость от температу́ры.","Сле́довательно, о́ба фа́ктора необходи́мо учи́тывать.","Таки́м о́бразом, полу́ченные да́нные подтвержда́ют гипо́тезу."],"translation":"The present work examines the question of the influence of light on plant growth. The study showed that with a shortage of light, growth slows. A dependence on temperature is also noted. Consequently, both factors must be taken into account. Thus, the data obtained confirm the hypothesis."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — sound like a scientist.","prompt":"Take one everyday claim (e.g. “coffee helps you concentrate,” “sleep improves memory”) and write it in Russian in the <b>scientific style</b>: impersonal (no «я ду́маю»), with a verbal noun and one logical connector (сле́довательно / таки́м о́бразом). 2–3 sentences.","hint":"Openers: «Иссле́дование пока́зывает, что…», «Представля́ется, что…», «Отмеча́ется, что…». Add a connector, delete all feelings 🙂","example":"Иссле́дование пока́зывает, что употребле́ние ко́фе повыша́ет концентра́цию внима́ния. Сле́довательно, у́мственная рабо́та стано́вится эффекти́внее. Таки́м о́бразом, влия́ние ко́фе на внима́ние представля́ется значи́мым."}},"5":{"day":5,"week":"01","themeRu":"The language of documents","themeEn":"Официа́льно-делово́й стиль","intro":"Ива́н needs to request a week of leave, and he starts typing «Приве́т, мо́жно мне о́тпуск?» — the way he'd text a friend. Эмма stops him: a <b>заявле́ние</b> doesn't work like that. The <b>официа́льно-делово́й</b> (official-business) style is the most rigid of the five — you don't compose it, you <b>assemble</b> it from frozen formulas: «прошу́ предоста́вить», «в связи́ с», «настоя́щим уведомля́ю». It's the highest, most marked register, and the one ТРКИ-3 tests hardest. Living, friendly language is exactly what's out of place.","introAudio":"Ivan needs to request a week of leave, and he starts typing 'Privet, mozhno mne otpusk?' — the way he'd text a friend. Emma stops him: a zayavlenie doesn't work like that. The ofitsialno-delovoy, official-business style, is the most rigid of the five — you don't compose it, you assemble it from frozen formulas: 'proshu predostavit,' 'v svyazi s,' 'nastoyashchim uvedomlyayu.' It's the highest, most marked register, and the one TORFL-three tests hardest. Living, friendly language is exactly what's out of place.","goals":["Assemble a document from <b>frozen formulas</b> (канцеляри́змы): в связи́ с, на основа́нии, прошу́ предоста́вить","Write a <b>заявле́ние</b> on its fixed skeleton (addressee, «Прошу́…», date, signature)","Open and close a <b>business letter</b>: «Уважа́емый…!» … «С уваже́нием,»","Hear that this register is <b>frozen both ways</b> — friendly words are as wrong here as officialese is in a chat"],"learned":["Assembled official text from frozen formulas (в связи́ с, прошу́ предоста́вить)","Wrote a заявле́ние on its fixed skeleton","Framed a business letter with «Уважа́емый…!» and «С уваже́нием,»","Judged the official register both ways — no friendliness in, no officialese out"],"review":{"intro":"Yesterday's scientific voice first — impersonal, nominal — then we step into its cousin: the frozen, formulaic world of official documents.","introAudio":"Yesterday's scientific voice first — impersonal, nominal — then we step into its cousin: the frozen, formulaic world of official documents.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. Yesterday's science.","q":"«Представля́ется, что…» is a marker of which style?","opts":["scientific","conversational","official"],"answer":0,"explain":"The impersonal <b>«представля́ется, что…»</b> is a hallmark of the <b>scientific</b> style."},{"type":"choice","q":"The verbal noun (отглаго́льное) from «реши́ть» is…","opts":["реша́тель","реше́ние","ре́шка"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>реше́ние</b> — the -ение noun packing the verb «реши́ть» into a noun."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order — a scientific conclusion.","q":"Build: <b>“Thus, the hypothesis is confirmed.”</b>","want":"Таки́м о́бразом, гипо́теза подтвержда́ется","answer":"Таки́м о́бразом, гипо́теза подтвержда́ется"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Frozen formulas","title":"The style built from <em>ready-made blocks</em>","paras":["The <b>официа́льно-делово́й</b> (official-business) style is the most rigid of all five. You don't compose it freshly; you assemble it from <b>frozen formulas</b> — <b>канцеляри́змы</b> (officialese). Cause: <b>в связи́ с</b> (in connection with), <b>на основа́нии</b> (on the basis of), <b>ввиду́</b> (in view of). Time: <b>в тече́ние</b> (during), <b>в срок до</b> (by the deadline of).","Announcing and requesting have their own set blocks: <b>настоя́щим уведомля́ю</b> (I hereby notify), <b>дово́жу до Ва́шего све́дения</b> (I bring to your attention), <b>прошу́ предоста́вить</b> (I request that you provide). You reach for the ready block, not for your own words.","The register is the <b>highest and most marked</b> of the five — and it's exactly what ТРКИ-3 tests. Emotion, imagery and warmth are all out; standard, impersonal precision is in."],"audio":"The official-business style is the most rigid of all five. You don't compose it freshly; you assemble it from frozen formulas — kantselyarizmy, officialese. Cause: v svyazi s, in connection with; na osnovanii, on the basis of; vvidu, in view of. Time: v techenie, during; v srok do, by the deadline of. Announcing and requesting have their own set blocks: nastoyashchim uvedomlyayu, I hereby notify; dovozhu do vashego svedeniya, I bring to your attention; proshu predostavit, I request that you provide. You reach for the ready block, not for your own words. The register is the highest and most marked of the five — and it's exactly what TORFL-three tests.","table":{"rows":[["в связи́ с… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в связи с\">🔊</button>","“in connection with…” (cause)"],["на основа́нии… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"на основании\">🔊</button>","“on the basis of…”"],["настоя́щим уведомля́ю <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"настоящим уведомляю\">🔊</button>","“I hereby notify”"],["прошу́ предоста́вить <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"прошу предоставить\">🔊</button>","“I request that you provide”"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"“In connection with the reorganization, I hereby notify you…”","en":"«<b>В связи́ с</b> реорганиза́цией <b>настоя́щим уведомля́ю</b> Вас…»","say":"В связи с реорганизацией настоящим уведомляю вас."},{"ru":"“I request that you grant me leave.”","en":"«<b>Прошу́ предоста́вить</b> мне о́тпуск».","say":"Прошу предоставить мне отпуск."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"В заявле́нии: «Да́йте мне, пожа́луйста, о́тпуск, а то я уста́л».","right":"В заявле́нии: «Прошу́ предоста́вить мне о́тпуск».","why":"«Да́йте, пожа́луйста… а то я уста́л» is warm, living speech — impossible in an application. The document wants the frozen formula <b>«прошу́ предоста́вить»</b>. Friendliness here is the style error."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the situation, then tap the answer.","q":"In an official application, “please give me…” becomes…","opts":["«да́йте мне…»","«прошу́ предоста́вить…»","«мне бы…»"],"answer":1,"explain":"The frozen request formula is <b>«прошу́ предоста́вить…»</b>. The others are conversational."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the phrase. Stress marks are just for you.","q":"The officialese for “in connection with” (a cause). Type it:","accept":["в связи с"],"placeholder":"в свя…","explain":"<b>в связи́ с</b> — the standard cause formula of the official style."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Writing an application","title":"<em>Заявле́ние</em>: a form you fill with words","paras":["A <b>заявле́ние</b> (formal application) has a fixed skeleton, and native writers never improvise it. Top right, the addressee and author: «<b>Дире́ктору ООО «Свет» Ивано́ву И. И. от Петро́ва П. П.</b>» — recipient in the dative, author with <b>от</b> + genitive.","Then the word «заявле́ние», and the body opens with the request formula: «<b>Прошу́</b> + infinitive» — «Прошу́ предоста́вить о́тпуск с 1 ию́ля». It closes with a date and a signature. Every заявле́ние on earth follows this shape.","The tone is neutral-to-formal, the verbs of request are fixed (прошу́, дово́жу, уведомля́ю), and personal feeling is absent. You're not expressing yourself — you're completing a genre."],"audio":"A zayavlenie, a formal application, has a fixed skeleton, and native writers never improvise it. Top right, the addressee and author: to the director, in the dative; from the author, with 'ot' plus the genitive. Then the word zayavlenie, and the body opens with the request formula: proshu plus an infinitive — proshu predostavit otpusk s pervogo iyulya, I request leave from the first of July. It closes with a date and a signature. Every zayavlenie on earth follows this shape. The tone is neutral-to-formal, the verbs of request are fixed, and personal feeling is absent. You're not expressing yourself — you're completing a genre.","table":{"rows":[["Дире́ктору… от… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"директору, от\">🔊</button>","addressee (dative) — author (от + genitive)"],["заявле́ние <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"заявление\">🔊</button>","the word, centred under the header"],["Прошу́ предоста́вить о́тпуск <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"прошу предоставить отпуск\">🔊</button>","the request: Прошу́ + infinitive"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"The request line of an application: “I request leave from 1 July.”","en":"Стро́ка заявле́ния: «<b>Прошу́ предоста́вить о́тпуск</b> с 1 ию́ля».","say":"Строка заявления: прошу предоставить отпуск с первого июля."},{"ru":"Bringing something to notice: “I bring to your attention that…”","en":"«<b>Дово́жу до Ва́шего све́дения</b>, что…»","say":"Довожу до вашего сведения, что."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Приве́т! Хочу́ в о́тпуск с ию́ля, ла́дно?»","right":"«Прошу́ предоста́вить о́тпуск с 1 ию́ля».","why":"«Приве́т… ла́дно?» is a text message, not an application. The заявле́ние genre demands «Прошу́ предоста́вить» — and no greeting at all. Wrong genre, wrong every word."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"The body of a заявле́ние opens with…","opts":["«Приве́т, слу́шай…»","«Прошу́ предоста́вить…»","«Дорого́й дире́ктор!»"],"answer":1,"explain":"The fixed request formula <b>«Прошу́ предоста́вить…»</b> opens the body. The others belong to chat or a personal letter."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order — the request line.","q":"Build the request line: <b>“I request that you grant me leave.”</b>","want":"Прошу́ предоста́вить мне о́тпуск","answer":"Прошу́ предоста́вить мне о́тпуск"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The business letter","title":"<em>Делово́е письмо́</em>: formulas top to bottom","paras":["A <b>деловое письмо́</b> (business letter) is framed by set formulas. It opens with a respectful address: «<b>Уважа́емый Ива́н Ива́нович!</b>» (note the exclamation mark, and the capital <b>Вы</b> throughout). It closes with «<b>С уваже́нием</b>, …» (Yours respectfully).","In between, standard blocks carry the content: «<b>Настоя́щим сообща́ем, что…</b>» (we hereby inform you that), «<b>В отве́т на Ваш запро́с…</b>» (in reply to your inquiry), «<b>Про́сим Вас рассмотре́ть…</b>» (we ask you to consider). Warmth is conveyed only through the fixed courtesy formulas, never through chatty language.","Notice the capital <b>Вы / Вас / Ваш</b> — in a formal letter to one addressee, respect is spelled with a capital. It's a small marker that instantly signals the official register."],"audio":"A business letter is framed by set formulas. It opens with a respectful address: Uvazhaemy Ivan Ivanovich, with an exclamation mark, and the capital 'Vy' throughout. It closes with 'S uvazheniem', yours respectfully. In between, standard blocks carry the content: nastoyashchim soobshchaem chto, we hereby inform you that; v otvet na vash zapros, in reply to your inquiry; prosim vas rassmotret, we ask you to consider. Warmth is conveyed only through the fixed courtesy formulas, never through chatty language. Notice the capital Vy, Vas, Vash — in a formal letter to one addressee, respect is spelled with a capital.","table":{"rows":[["Уважа́емый Ива́н Ива́нович! <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"уважаемый Иван Иванович\">🔊</button>","opening address (with !)"],["Настоя́щим сообща́ем, что… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"настоящим сообщаем, что\">🔊</button>","“we hereby inform you that…”"],["С уваже́нием, … <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"с уважением\">🔊</button>","“Yours respectfully,” (closing)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Opening a business letter: “Dear Ivan Ivanovich!”","en":"Нача́ло письма́: «<b>Уважа́емый Ива́н Ива́нович!</b>»","say":"Начало письма: уважаемый Иван Иванович!"},{"ru":"Closing: “We ask you to consider our proposal. Yours respectfully, …”","en":"«<b>Про́сим Вас рассмотре́ть</b> на́ше предложе́ние. <b>С уваже́нием</b>,…»","say":"Просим вас рассмотреть наше предложение. С уважением."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Партнёру в делово́м письме́: «Приве́т! Ну что, по цене́ договори́лись?»","right":"Партнёру в делово́м письме́: «Уважа́емый Анто́н Петро́вич! Гото́вы обсуди́ть усло́вия цены́».","why":"«Приве́т! Ну что…» is chat-speak — jarring in a business letter. The genre opens with «Уважа́емый …!» and keeps the neutral-formal register throughout."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"A business letter to «Ива́н Ива́нович» should open with…","opts":["«Приве́т, Ва́ня!»","«Уважа́емый Ива́н Ива́нович!»","«Эй, ты там?»"],"answer":1,"explain":"The formal opener is <b>«Уважа́емый Ива́н Ива́нович!»</b> — name-and-patronymic, with an exclamation mark."},{"type":"choice","q":"In a formal letter to one person, respect is shown by writing…","opts":["«вы» with a small letter","«Вы» with a capital","no pronoun at all"],"answer":1,"explain":"Capitalised <b>«Вы / Вас / Ваш»</b> marks respect to a single addressee in the official register."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Frozen on purpose","title":"Why the clichés are a <em>feature</em>, not a bug","paras":["It feels strange that good style here means using <b>clichés</b> — everywhere else they're a fault. But in documents the frozen formula is a <b>virtue</b>: it's unambiguous, impersonal and instantly recognised. «в соотве́тствии с пу́нктом 3» leaves no room for interpretation. Predictability beats originality.","So the boundary runs both ways. A friendly «да ла́дно, договори́лись» in a contract is wrong — but «настоя́щим уведомля́ю» to a friend is just as wrong, absurdly stiff. Each style guards its border; the skill is crossing back and forth on purpose.","This closes the pair that anchors the week: <b>научный</b> (impersonal, nominal, no feeling) and <b>официа́льно-делово́й</b> (frozen formulas, standard genres). Both are cool, bookish and highly marked — and both are where English speakers most often leak the wrong register."],"audio":"It feels strange that good style here means using clichés — everywhere else they're a fault. But in documents the frozen formula is a virtue: it's unambiguous, impersonal and instantly recognised. 'V sootvetstvii s punktom tri' leaves no room for interpretation. Predictability beats originality. So the boundary runs both ways. A friendly 'da ladno, dogovorilis' in a contract is wrong — but 'nastoyashchim uvedomlyayu' to a friend is just as wrong, absurdly stiff. This closes the pair that anchors the week: nauchny, impersonal and nominal, and ofitsialno-delovoy, frozen formulas and standard genres. Both are cool, bookish and highly marked — and both are where English speakers most often leak the wrong register.","table":{"rows":[["в соотве́тствии с… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в соответствии с\">🔊</button>","“in accordance with…”"],["в тече́ние… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в течение\">🔊</button>","“during / within…” (time)"],["за исключе́нием… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"за исключением\">🔊</button>","“with the exception of…”"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Official (precise): “in accordance with clause 3 of the contract.”","en":"Официа́льно: «<b>в соотве́тствии с</b> пу́нктом 3 догово́ра».","say":"Официально: в соответствии с пунктом три договора."},{"ru":"To a friend (right register): “Right, it's a deal!”","en":"Дру́гу: «Ну всё, договори́лись!»","say":"Другу: ну всё, договорились!"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Дру́гу в ча́те: «Настоя́щим уведомля́ю, что опозда́ю».","right":"Дру́гу в ча́те: «Слу́шай, я опозда́ю».","why":"«Настоя́щим уведомля́ю» to a friend is comically stiff — the official formula in a chat is as wrong as slang in a contract. Register runs in both directions."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Настоя́щим уведомля́ю, что опозда́ю» said to a friend in a chat is…","opts":["absurdly stiff — wrong register","perfectly normal","too rude"],"answer":0,"explain":"The official formula in a friendly chat is <b>absurdly stiff</b> — register is wrong both ways."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the phrase. Stress marks are just for you.","q":"The official cliché for “in accordance with.” Type it:","accept":["в соответствии с"],"placeholder":"в соотв…","explain":"<b>в соотве́тствии с</b> — the standard official formula for “in accordance with.”"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Ива́н has to write a formal заявле́ние to request leave, and asks Эмма — the professional — for help. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Э́мма, помоги́. Как написа́ть заявле́ние на о́тпуск?","ru":"Emma, help me. How do I write an application for leave?"},{"who":"A","en":"Пе́рвое пра́вило: ника́кого «приве́т». Све́рху — «Дире́ктору… от Ивано́ва».","ru":"First rule: no “hi.” At the top — “To the director… from Ivanov.”"},{"who":"B","en":"А да́льше про́сто: «Хочу́ в о́тпуск»?","ru":"And then simply: “I want leave”?"},{"who":"A","en":"Нет. «Прошу́ предоста́вить о́тпуск с пе́рвого а́вгуста».","ru":"No. “I request leave from the first of August.”"},{"who":"B","en":"Зву́чит как из друго́го ми́ра.","ru":"Sounds like it's from another world."},{"who":"A","en":"Э́то и есть официа́льно-делово́й стиль. Свои́ми слова́ми здесь не пи́шут — то́лько формулиро́вками.","ru":"That's exactly the official-business style. You don't write it in your own words — only in set formulas."},{"who":"B","en":"А в конце́?","ru":"And at the end?"},{"who":"A","en":"Да́та, по́дпись. Ко́ротко и стро́го — как настоя́щий докуме́нт.","ru":"Date, signature. Short and strict — like a real document."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the world of documents. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"заявле́ние","ru":"application, formal request","ex":"Я написа́л заявле́ние на о́тпуск."},{"en":"докуме́нт","ru":"document","ex":"Ну́жен официа́льный докуме́нт."},{"en":"канцеляри́зм","ru":"officialese cliché","ex":"Канцеляри́зм уме́стен то́лько в докуме́нте."},{"en":"уведомля́ть","ru":"to notify (formally)","ex":"Настоя́щим уведомля́ю вас."},{"en":"предоста́вить","ru":"to provide, to grant","ex":"Прошу́ предоста́вить спра́вку."},{"en":"основа́ние","ru":"basis, grounds","ex":"На основа́нии зако́на."},{"en":"уваже́ние","ru":"respect","ex":"С уваже́нием, Ива́н."},{"en":"по́дпись","ru":"signature","ex":"Поста́вьте по́дпись внизу́."},{"en":"о́тпуск","ru":"leave, vacation","ex":"Он ушёл в о́тпуск."},{"en":"официа́льный","ru":"official, formal","ex":"Э́то официа́льный запро́с."},{"en":"запро́с","ru":"request, inquiry","ex":"В отве́т на ваш запро́с."},{"en":"формулиро́вка","ru":"wording, phrasing","ex":"В докуме́нте ва́жна то́чная формулиро́вка."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each phrase to where it belongs.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each phrase on the left onto its place — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Прошу́ предоста́вить…","official application"],["Ну всё, договори́лись!","friendly chat"],["С уваже́нием,","closing a business letter"],["В связи́ с…","official cause formula"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions on the official-business style. If one bites, glance back at the rule.","items":[{"q":"The official-business style is built mainly from…","opts":["frozen clichés and formulas","fresh, original phrasing","vivid imagery"],"answer":0},{"q":"«Прошу́ предоста́вить о́тпуск» belongs in a…","opts":["text to a friend","formal application","novel"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Настоя́щим уведомля́ю» said to a friend sounds…","opts":["absurdly stiff","perfectly normal","affectionate"],"answer":0},{"q":"A business letter closes with…","opts":["«Ну, пока́!»","«С уваже́нием,»","«Целу́ю!»"],"answer":1},{"q":"«В соотве́тствии с пу́нктом 3» is an example of…","opts":["conversational warmth","scientific emotion","official precision"],"answer":2}]},"reading":{"intro":"A miniature заявле́ние — see how fixed the skeleton is, and how no living language gets in.","title":"Заявле́ние: как э́то устро́ено","sentences":["Дире́ктору шко́лы № 5 Петро́ву П. П. от учи́теля Ивано́ва И. И.","Заявле́ние.","Прошу́ предоста́вить мне о́тпуск с 1 по 14 а́вгуста в связи́ с семе́йными обстоя́тельствами.","О причи́нах гото́в сообщи́ть допо́лнительно.","28 ию́ля 2026 г. По́дпись."],"translation":"To the director of School No. 5, P. P. Petrov, from the teacher I. I. Ivanov. Application. I request that you grant me leave from 1 to 14 August in connection with family circumstances. I am prepared to provide the reasons separately. 28 July 2026. Signature."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — write it like a document.","prompt":"Write one short <b>official-business</b> line for a real situation: request leave, notify someone of a change, or ask an office to provide a document. Use at least one frozen formula (прошу́ предоста́вить / настоя́щим уведомля́ю / в связи́ с). No greetings, no feelings. Then, in one English line, say why the same words would be wrong in a text to a friend.","hint":"Formulas to lean on: «Прошу́ предоста́вить…», «В связи́ с… уведомля́ю…», «Дово́жу до Ва́шего све́дения, что…». Cold and standard is correct here 🙂","example":"В связи́ с боле́знью прошу́ предоста́вить мне спра́вку о состоя́нии здоро́вья. (To a friend this would sound absurdly stiff — you'd just say «Слу́шай, мне нужна́ спра́вка».)"}},"6":{"day":6,"week":"01","themeRu":"Say it in fewer words","themeEn":"Кни́жное сжа́тие: оборо́ты и отглаго́льные","intro":"Эмма is trimming a translated paragraph that runs too long. The English says «After he returned to Moscow, he wrote a report». Word for word that's «По́сле того́ как он верну́лся в Москву́, он написа́л отчёт» — correct, but a Russian editor tightens it to «По возвраще́нии в Москву́ он написа́л отчёт». Same meaning, half the words. Today's move is <b>bookish condensation</b> (кни́жное сжа́тие): swapping a whole clause for a single phrase or a verbal noun. It's what makes scientific and official Russian dense — and it's the structure English speakers most often miss.","introAudio":"Emma is trimming a translated paragraph that runs too long. The English says 'After he returned to Moscow, he wrote a report'. Word for word that's 'Posle togo kak on vernulsya v Moskvu, on napisal otchyot' — correct, but a Russian editor tightens it to 'Po vozvrashchenii v Moskvu on napisal otchyot'. Same meaning, half the words. Today's move is bookish condensation, knizhnoye szhatiye: swapping a whole clause for a single phrase or a verbal noun. It's what makes scientific and official Russian dense — and it's the structure English speakers most often miss.","goals":["Turn a finite clause into a <b>participial or gerundial phrase</b> (оборо́т): «Когда́ он верну́лся…» → «Верну́вшись…»","Turn a clause into a <b>preposition + verbal noun</b> (отглаго́льное существи́тельное): «по́сле того́ как он верну́лся» → «по возвраще́нии»","Reach for the right bookish preposition: <b>по, ввиду́, в связи́ с, из-за, при</b> + a verbal noun","Know when <b>not</b> to condense — a nominalization in a chat is bureaucratese (канцеляри́т)"],"learned":["Turned finite clauses into participial and gerundial phrases (оборо́ты)","Condensed clauses into preposition + verbal noun (по возвраще́нии, из-за дождя́)","Chose the right bookish preposition for cause, time and condition","Kept condensation for the bookish styles and left live speech alone"],"review":{"intro":"Before we compress, three quick pulls — a participle you already build, an official cliché from yesterday, and a register call. Then we squeeze.","introAudio":"Before we compress, three quick pulls — a participle you already build, an official cliché from yesterday, and a register call. Then we squeeze.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. You built participles at B2.","q":"“a law adopted last year” (passive, past) —","opts":["зако́н, приня́вший в про́шлом году́","зако́н, при́нятый в про́шлом году́","зако́н, приня́вшийся в про́шлом году́"],"answer":1,"explain":"Passive past participle → <b>при́нятый</b>. приня́вший is active; приня́вшийся isn't standard here."},{"type":"choice","q":"In an <b>application</b>, “I ask you to grant me leave” is best as —","opts":["Дай мне о́тпуск, пожа́луйста.","Прошу́ предоста́вить мне о́тпуск.","Мне бы о́тпуск, а?"],"answer":1,"explain":"Official register → the fixed formula <b>Прошу́ предоста́вить</b>. The others are conversational."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build the gerund version: <b>“Having finished the work, I went home.”</b>","want":"Зако́нчив рабо́ту, я пошёл домо́й","answer":"Зако́нчив рабо́ту, я пошёл домо́й"},{"type":"choice","q":"Scientific style prefers the noun. «Учёные при́няли реше́ние» as a nominal phrase —","opts":["приня́тие реше́ния учёными","учёные реша́ют","реши́ли учёные"],"answer":0,"explain":"The action becomes a verbal noun with the agent in the instrumental: <b>приня́тие реше́ния учёными</b>."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · One phrase for a whole clause","title":"Bookish Russian <em>compresses</em> — a clause becomes a phrase","paras":["English happily strings clauses together: “after he returned, because it was raining, he stayed home.” Bookish Russian finds that loose. Its instinct is to <b>compress</b> (сжима́ть) — to fold a whole subordinate clause into a single <b>phrase</b> (оборо́т) or a single <b>verbal noun</b> (отглаго́льное существи́тельное). The result is dense, precise and unmistakably written.","There are two routes. Route one keeps a verb form but drops the conjunction: the clause becomes a <b>participial or gerundial phrase</b> — «Когда́ он верну́лся» → «Верну́вшись». Route two loses the verb entirely and turns the action into a noun: «по́сле того́ как он верну́лся» → «по возвраще́нии».","This is exactly what makes the scientific and official styles feel so tight. It isn't new grammar — you built participles and gerunds at B2. It's the C1 habit of reaching for them when the register calls for density."],"audio":"English happily strings clauses together: after he returned, because it was raining, he stayed home. Bookish Russian finds that loose. Its instinct is to compress — szhimat — to fold a whole subordinate clause into a single phrase, an oborot, or a single verbal noun, an otglagolnoye sushchestvitelnoye. There are two routes. Route one keeps a verb form but drops the conjunction: the clause becomes a participial or gerundial phrase — Kogda on vernulsya becomes Vernuvshis. Route two loses the verb entirely and turns the action into a noun: posle togo kak on vernulsya becomes po vozvrashchenii. This is what makes scientific and official Russian feel so tight. It isn't new grammar — you built participles and gerunds at B2. It's the C1 habit of reaching for them when the register calls for density.","table":{"rows":[["Когда́ он верну́лся → верну́вшись <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"вернувшись\">🔊</button>","when he returned → having returned (gerund)"],["кни́га, кото́рую написа́л а́втор → напи́санная а́втором <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"книга написанная автором\">🔊</button>","the book the author wrote → written by the author (participle)"],["по́сле того́ как он верну́лся → по возвраще́нии <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"по возвращении\">🔊</button>","after he returned → upon returning (verbal noun)"],["так как шёл дождь → из-за дождя́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"из-за дождя\">🔊</button>","because it was raining → because of the rain (verbal noun)"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"Upon finishing university, she began to work.","en":"<b>По оконча́нии</b> университе́та она́ начала́ рабо́тать.","say":"По окончании университета она начала работать."},{"ru":"Having read the article, the editor made corrections.","en":"<b>Прочита́в</b> статью́, реда́ктор внёс пра́вки.","say":"Прочитав статью, редактор внёс правки."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"В нау́чной статье́: «По́сле того́ как мы получи́ли результа́ты, мы их проанализи́ровали».","right":"В нау́чной статье́: «По́сле получе́ния результа́тов был проведён их ана́лиз».","why":"Scientific Russian condenses the clause into a noun phrase (получе́ние результа́тов) and turns the personal «мы» impersonal. The loose, verbal version is grammatically fine but reads as the wrong register for an article."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"Condense the который-clause. «Отчёт, кото́рый подгото́вил Макси́м» →","opts":["отчёт, подгото́вивший Макси́ма","отчёт, подгото́вленный Макси́мом","отчёт, подгото́вивши Макси́м"],"answer":1,"explain":"Passive past participle + instrumental agent: <b>подгото́вленный Макси́мом</b>."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Condense to a gerund: <b>“When she returned, she called.”</b>","want":"Верну́вшись, она́ позвони́ла","answer":"Верну́вшись, она́ позвони́ла"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Route one — оборо́т (keep a verb form)","title":"<em>Который</em>-clause → participle; <em>когда́ / так как</em>-clause → gerund","paras":["The gentler route keeps a verb but sheds the conjunction. A <b>который</b>-clause folds into a <b>participial phrase</b> (причастный оборо́т): «письмо́, кото́рое написа́л Ива́н» → «письмо́, напи́санное Ива́ном». Active or passive, present or past — you already form these; now you deploy them to save a clause.","A time- or reason-clause with <b>когда́, по́сле того́ как, так как</b> folds into a <b>gerundial phrase</b> (деепричастный оборо́т) — but only if both actions share one subject: «Когда́ я закры́л дверь, я ушёл» → «Закры́в дверь, я ушёл». Watch that one-subject rule: «Когда́ я вошёл, зазвони́л телефо́н» can't become a gerund, because the phone isn't the one who entered.","This route is a notch less formal than nominalization: it's at home in careful writing of any bookish style, and even in polished speech."],"audio":"The gentler route keeps a verb but sheds the conjunction. A kotory-clause folds into a participial phrase, a prichastny oborot: pismo, kotoroye napisal Ivan becomes pismo, napisannoye Ivanom. Active or passive, present or past — you already form these; now you deploy them to save a clause. A time or reason clause with kogda, posle togo kak, tak kak folds into a gerundial phrase, a deyeprichastny oborot — but only if both actions share one subject: Kogda ya zakryl dver, ya ushyol becomes Zakryv dver, ya ushyol. Watch that one-subject rule: Kogda ya voshyol, zazvonil telefon can't become a gerund, because the phone isn't the one who entered. This route is a notch less formal than nominalization: at home in careful writing of any bookish style, and even in polished speech.","table":{"rows":[["письмо́, кото́рое написа́л Ива́н → напи́санное Ива́ном <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"письмо написанное Иваном\">🔊</button>","the letter Ivan wrote → written by Ivan (participle)"],["Когда́ я закры́л дверь → закры́в дверь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"закрыв дверь\">🔊</button>","when I closed the door → having closed the door (gerund)"],["Так как он не знал → не зна́я <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"не зная\">🔊</button>","since he didn't know → not knowing (gerund)"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"The delegation arriving in Moscow was met at the airport.","en":"Делега́цию, <b>прибы́вшую</b> в Москву́, встре́тили в аэропорту́.","say":"Делегацию, прибывшую в Москву, встретили в аэропорту."},{"ru":"Choosing his words carefully, he answered.","en":"<b>Тща́тельно выбира́я</b> слова́, он отве́тил.","say":"Тщательно выбирая слова, он ответил."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Верну́вшись домо́й, мне позвони́л Макси́м.","right":"Когда́ я верну́лся домо́й, мне позвони́л Макси́м.","why":"A gerund needs the <b>same subject</b> as the main verb. Here the returner is «я», but the caller is Макси́м, so «верну́вшись» dangles. Keep the full clause when the subjects differ."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which correctly condenses «Так как он был за́нят, он не пришёл»?","opts":["Бу́дучи за́нят, он не пришёл.","Быв за́нятым, он не пришёл.","За́нят, он не пришёл."],"answer":0,"explain":"«бу́дучи» is the gerund of быть: “being busy, he didn't come.” The others aren't standard."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the phrase. Stress marks are optional.","q":"Turn «кни́га, кото́рую перевела́ Эмма» into a participial phrase (the book translated by Emma):","accept":["книга, переведённая эммой","книга переведённая эммой","переведённая эммой","книга, переведенная эммой"],"placeholder":"кни́га, перев…","explain":"Passive past participle from перевести́: <b>переведённая Э́ммой</b>."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Route two — preposition + verbal noun","title":"<em>По возвраще́нии, из-за дождя́, в связи́ с приня́тием</em>","paras":["The fully bookish route drops the verb altogether and turns the action into a <b>verbal noun</b>, governed by a preposition. This is the densest move and the signature of the scientific and official styles. «По́сле того́ как зако́н при́няли» becomes «по́сле приня́тия зако́на» or, more officially, «в связи́ с приня́тием зако́на».","The trick is pairing the right preposition with the noun. Time / “upon”: <b>по</b> + prepositional — «по оконча́нии, по прибы́тии, по возвраще́нии». Cause: <b>из-за</b> + genitive (everyday), <b>ввиду́</b> / <b>в связи́ с</b> (bookish) — «из-за дождя́», «ввиду́ боле́зни», «в связи́ с ремо́нтом». Condition / “during”: <b>при</b> + prepositional — «при рассмотре́нии вопро́са».","Notice «по» here means “upon / after,” and it takes the <b>prepositional</b>, not the dative: по возвраще́нии, not по возвраще́нию. That -ии ending is the tell of this formal construction."],"audio":"The fully bookish route drops the verb altogether and turns the action into a verbal noun, governed by a preposition. This is the densest move and the signature of the scientific and official styles. Posle togo kak zakon prinyali becomes posle prinyatiya zakona or, more officially, v svyazi s prinyatiyem zakona. The trick is pairing the right preposition with the noun. Time or upon: po plus prepositional — po okonchanii, po pribytii, po vozvrashchenii. Cause: iz-za plus genitive for everyday, vvidu or v svyazi s for bookish — iz-za dozhdya, vvidu bolezni, v svyazi s remontom. Condition or during: pri plus prepositional — pri rassmotrenii voprosa. Notice po here means upon or after, and it takes the prepositional, not the dative: po vozvrashchenii, not po vozvrashcheniyu. That double-i ending is the tell of this formal construction.","table":{"rows":[["по оконча́нии, по прибы́тии, по возвраще́нии <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"по окончании, по прибытии, по возвращении\">🔊</button>","upon finishing / arriving / returning (по + prep.)"],["из-за дождя́, ввиду́ боле́зни <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"из-за дождя, ввиду болезни\">🔊</button>","because of rain / owing to illness (cause)"],["в связи́ с приня́тием зако́на <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в связи с принятием закона\">🔊</button>","in connection with the law's adoption (official)"],["при рассмотре́нии вопро́са <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"при рассмотрении вопроса\">🔊</button>","upon / during examining the matter (при + prep.)"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"Upon returning to Moscow, Emma took on a new translation.","en":"<b>По возвраще́нии</b> в Москву́ Э́мма взяла́сь за но́вый перево́д.","say":"По возвращении в Москву Эмма взялась за новый перевод."},{"ru":"The meeting was postponed owing to the director's illness.","en":"Собра́ние перенесли́ <b>ввиду́ боле́зни</b> дире́ктора.","say":"Собрание перенесли ввиду болезни директора."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"по возвраще́нию домо́й","right":"по возвраще́нии домо́й","why":"In this “upon / after” meaning, «по» governs the <b>prepositional</b> (-ии), not the dative (-ию): <b>по возвраще́нии</b>. «по возвраще́нию» would read like “along the return” — nonsense here."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"In a report: “Because of the frost, the works were halted.” Most bookish —","opts":["Из-за моро́за рабо́ты останови́ли.","Ввиду́ моро́за рабо́ты бы́ли приостано́влены.","Моро́з уда́рил, и рабо́ты вста́ли."],"answer":1,"explain":"All are correct Russian, but a report wants the bookish cause + passive: <b>ввиду́ моро́за … бы́ли приостано́влены</b>. «из-за» is neutral; the third is conversational."},{"type":"type","q":"Condense «по́сле того́ как он прие́хал» into «по + noun» (upon arrival):","accept":["по приезде","по приезде."],"placeholder":"по при…","explain":"«прие́зд» → <b>по прие́зде</b> (prepositional, “upon arrival”)."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"По оконча́нии рабо́ты он сра́зу уе́хал.","sub":"a bookish “upon finishing” sentence — read it evenly","want":"по окончании работы он сразу уехал"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Compress the page, not the chat","title":"The other error: <em>канцеляри́т</em> where life should be","paras":["Condensation is a register tool, not an upgrade. Pile nominalizations into everyday speech and you get <b>канцеляри́т</b> (bureaucratese) — the deadening “official-ese” that Russians mock. To a friend you say «Прие́ду — позвоню́», never «По прибы́тии осуществлю́ звоно́к». The second is grammatically flawless and socially absurd.","So the C1 skill runs both ways. In a scientific or official text, <b>reach</b> for the phrase and the verbal noun — English speakers habitually leave clauses loose there. In speech or a friendly message, <b>leave the clause alone</b> — the live verb is the natural, warm choice.","Rule of thumb: the denser the register, the more you compress. A law is almost all nouns; a chat is almost all verbs. Match the pressure to the room."],"audio":"Condensation is a register tool, not an upgrade. Pile nominalizations into everyday speech and you get kantselyarit, bureaucratese — the deadening official-ese that Russians mock. To a friend you say Priyedu — pozvonyu, never Po pribytii osushchestvlyu zvonok. The second is grammatically flawless and socially absurd. So the C1 skill runs both ways. In a scientific or official text, reach for the phrase and the verbal noun — English speakers habitually leave clauses loose there. In speech or a friendly message, leave the clause alone — the live verb is the natural, warm choice. Rule of thumb: the denser the register, the more you compress. A law is almost all nouns; a chat is almost all verbs. Match the pressure to the room.","examples":[{"ru":"To a friend (natural): “When I get there, I'll call.”","en":"Дру́гу: «Как прие́ду, сра́зу позвоню́».","say":"Другу: как приеду, сразу позвоню."},{"ru":"In a notice (natural): “Upon arrival, please register.”","en":"В объявле́нии: «По прибы́тии про́сьба зарегистри́роваться».","say":"В объявлении: по прибытии просьба зарегистрироваться."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Дру́гу: «Ввиду́ уста́лости я вы́нужден отказа́ться от встре́чи».","right":"Дру́гу: «Что́-то я уста́л, дава́й в друго́й раз».","why":"To a friend, «ввиду́ уста́лости» and «вы́нужден отказа́ться» are pure канцеляри́т — bureaucratic and cold. The live, verbal version fits the room. Over-condensing casual speech is as much a register error as under-condensing a report."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Where does «по́сле рассмотре́ния зая́вки» (upon review of the application) belong?","opts":["в официа́льном письме́","в сообще́нии дру́гу","в разгово́ре за обе́дом"],"answer":0,"explain":"The verbal-noun phrase is bookish → an <b>official letter</b>. To a friend you'd say «когда́ посмо́трят зая́вку»."},{"type":"choice","q":"A friend texts about a delay. Natural reply —","opts":["По причи́не за́держки прибу́ду по́зже.","Слу́шай, я немно́го опозда́ю.","Ввиду́ обстоя́тельств вы́нужден задержа́ться."],"answer":1,"explain":"To a friend the live verb wins: «я немно́го опозда́ю». The other two are канцеляри́т in a chat."},{"type":"type","q":"One word for the deadening bureaucratic style you should NOT use with friends (canc…):","accept":["канцелярит"],"placeholder":"канцеляри́т","explain":"<b>канцеляри́т</b> — bureaucratese: nominalizations and clichés dumped where plain speech belongs."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Эмма is compressing a bloated paragraph in a report she's editing. Ива́н watches the clauses vanish. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Ты то́лько что вы́черкнула полстроки́. Ничего́ не пропа́ло?","ru":"You just crossed out half a line. Nothing lost?"},{"who":"A","en":"Ничего́. «По́сле того́ как коми́ссия рассмотре́ла прое́кт» ста́ло «по́сле рассмотре́ния прое́кта».","ru":"Nothing. «After the commission examined the project» became «after examination of the project»."},{"who":"B","en":"И так мо́жно?","ru":"And that's allowed?"},{"who":"A","en":"В докуме́нте — ну́жно. Кни́жный стиль лю́бит существи́тельные вме́сто це́лых прида́точных.","ru":"In a document — it's required. The bookish style loves nouns instead of whole subordinate clauses."},{"who":"B","en":"А е́сли б я так написа́л в ча́те?","ru":"And if I wrote that in a chat?"},{"who":"A","en":"Тогда́ бы э́то был канцеляри́т. Дру́гу пи́шут «как рассмо́трят — скажу́», а не «по рассмотре́нии уве́домлю».","ru":"Then it'd be bureaucratese. To a friend you write «when they look it over — I'll tell you», not «upon examination I shall notify»."},{"who":"B","en":"Поня́тно: в отчёте сжима́ю, в ча́те — живу́.","ru":"Got it: in a report I compress, in a chat I live."},{"who":"A","en":"Вот и́менно. Пло́тность — по ме́сту.","ru":"Exactly. Density — to fit the place."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve tools for compression — phrases, verbal nouns and the bookish prepositions that govern them. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"сжа́тие","ru":"condensation, compression","ex":"Кни́жное сжа́тие де́лает текст пло́тным."},{"en":"оборо́т","ru":"phrase (participial / gerundial)","ex":"Прида́точное ста́ло оборо́том."},{"en":"отглаго́льное существи́тельное","ru":"verbal noun (nominalization)","ex":"«Реше́ние» — отглаго́льное существи́тельное."},{"en":"по возвраще́нии","ru":"upon returning","ex":"По возвраще́нии он написа́л отчёт."},{"en":"по оконча́нии","ru":"upon finishing","ex":"По оконча́нии ку́рса вы́дадут дипло́м."},{"en":"ввиду́","ru":"owing to, in view of (+ gen.)","ex":"Ввиду́ боле́зни встре́ча отменена́."},{"en":"в связи́ с","ru":"in connection with (+ instr.)","ex":"В связи́ с ремо́нтом вход закры́т."},{"en":"из-за","ru":"because of (+ gen.)","ex":"Из-за дождя́ мы оста́лись до́ма."},{"en":"при","ru":"upon, during (+ prep.)","ex":"При рассмотре́нии вопро́са возни́кли спо́ры."},{"en":"пло́тность","ru":"density (of text)","ex":"У нау́чного те́кста высо́кая пло́тность."},{"en":"канцеляри́т","ru":"bureaucratese","ex":"Канцеляри́т убива́ет живу́ю речь."},{"en":"сжима́ть","ru":"to compress, condense","ex":"Реда́ктор сжима́ет дли́нные фра́зы."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each condensed phrase to what it means.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["по возвраще́нии","upon returning"],["ввиду́ боле́зни","owing to illness"],["прочита́в статью́","having read the article"],["в связи́ с приня́тием","in connection with the adoption"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions on today's compression. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"«По́сле того́ как он верну́лся» condensed to a noun phrase is —","opts":["по возвраще́нию","по возвраще́нии","возвраща́ясь"],"answer":1},{"q":"Which condenses «кни́га, кото́рую написа́л Ива́н»?","opts":["кни́га, напи́санная Ива́ном","кни́га, писа́вшая Ива́на","кни́га, пи́шущая Ива́ном"],"answer":0},{"q":"“Owing to illness” in a formal notice —","opts":["из-за боле́зни","ввиду́ боле́зни","боле́л, поэ́тому"],"answer":1},{"q":"To a friend in a chat, the natural choice is —","opts":["По прибы́тии осуществлю́ звоно́к.","Как прие́ду — позвоню́.","По причи́не прие́зда позвоню́."],"answer":1},{"q":"The deadening bureaucratic overuse of nominalization is called —","opts":["канцеляри́т","публици́стика","просторе́чие"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Эмма keeps two versions of one paragraph side by side — the loose draft and her condensed edit. Read both aloud and feel the register climb.","title":"Оди́н абза́ц, две пло́тности","sentences":["Чернови́к: По́сле того́ как а́втор зако́нчил кни́гу, и потому́ что сро́ки поджима́ли, реда́ктор торопи́лся.","Пра́вка: По оконча́нии кни́ги, ввиду́ сжа́тых сро́ков, реда́ктор торопи́лся.","Два прида́точных преврати́лись в два коро́тких оборо́та.","Смысл тот же, но текст стал пло́тным и кни́жным.","А дру́гу Э́мма напи́шет про́ще: «Дописа́л — и скоре́е пра́вим, вре́мени в обре́з»."],"translation":"Draft: After the author finished the book, and because the deadline was pressing, the editor was in a hurry. Edit: Upon finishing the book, owing to the tight deadline, the editor was in a hurry. Two subordinate clauses turned into two short phrases. The meaning is the same, but the text became dense and bookish. And to a friend Emma will write more simply: “Finished it — now we edit fast, time's tight.”"},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — squeeze one, loosen the other.","prompt":"Take a two-clause idea (e.g. “After I finished work, because it was late, I went home”). Write it in Russian TWICE: once <b>condensed</b> for a report (use a phrase or a preposition + verbal noun), once <b>loose and live</b> for a message to a friend. Label each. 2–4 sentences.","hint":"Condensed reaches for <b>по оконча́нии, ввиду́, в связи́ с</b> + a verbal noun; the friendly version keeps live verbs — «зако́нчил, бы́ло по́здно, пошёл домо́й» 🙂","example":"В отчёте: По оконча́нии рабо́ты, ввиду́ по́зднего вре́мени, сотру́дник поки́нул о́фис. Дру́гу: Дорабо́тал, уже́ по́здно бы́ло — ну я и пошёл домо́й."}},"7":{"day":7,"week":"01","themeRu":"Review 1","themeEn":"Повторе́ние 1","intro":"A week ago “formal” and “informal” were the only two dials you had. Now you hear five styles and a five-step register scale, you pick a synonym by its shade, you catch the attitude a suffix smuggles in, and you can fold a whole clause into «по возвраще́нии». Nothing new today — just one lap around everything, to feel how it fits together. Эмма says the same thing happened to her: one week, and suddenly she couldn't <i>un</i>-hear register.","introAudio":"A week ago 'formal' and 'informal' were the only two dials you had. Now you hear five styles and a five-step register scale, you pick a synonym by its shade, you catch the attitude a suffix smuggles in, and you can fold a whole clause into po vozvrashchenii. Nothing new today — just one lap around everything, to feel how it fits together. Emma says the same thing happened to her: one week, and suddenly she couldn't un-hear register.","goals":["Name the <b>five styles</b> and place a word on the <b>register scale</b> (высо́кое — кни́жное — нейтра́льное — разгово́рное — просторе́чное)","Pick a synonym by <b>shade</b> and read the <b>attitude</b> a suffix adds (до́мик vs доми́ще vs доми́шко)","Recognise the <b>scientific</b> and <b>official</b> styles by their markers and clichés","Condense a clause into a phrase or verbal noun — and know when <b>not</b> to"],"learned":["Named the five styles and placed words on the register scale","Chose synonyms by shade and read the attitude in evaluation suffixes","Recognised the scientific and official styles by their markers","Condensed clauses for bookish styles and left live speech alone"],"review":{"intro":"Straight in — one pull from each corner of the week. You built all of this; no hints needed.","introAudio":"Straight in — one pull from each corner of the week. You built all of this; no hints needed.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. It's all week-1 material.","q":"«Настоя́щим уведомля́ю, что…» — which style?","opts":["разгово́рный","официа́льно-делово́й","худо́жественный"],"answer":1,"explain":"The fixed formula «настоя́щим уведомля́ю» is a classic <b>официа́льно-делово́й</b> marker."},{"type":"choice","q":"At a conference, “the professor's clever face” —","opts":["у́мная физионо́мия профе́ссора","у́мное лицо́ профе́ссора","у́мный лик профе́ссора"],"answer":1,"explain":"Neutral <b>лицо́</b> fits a conference. физионо́мия is colloquial-jokey; лик is lofty/poetic."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks are optional.","q":"Add a warm diminutive to «дом» — a dear little house:","accept":["домик"],"placeholder":"дом…","explain":"<b>до́мик</b> — the affectionate diminutive (-ик)."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Condense to a verbal noun: <b>“after finishing the course”</b>","want":"по оконча́нии ку́рса","answer":"по оконча́нии ку́рса"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The map you built","title":"<em>Five styles, five registers</em> — and how to read them","paras":["Start with the frame. Russian sorts speech into <b>five functional styles</b>: <b>нау́чный</b> (scientific), <b>официа́льно-делово́й</b> (official), <b>публицисти́ческий</b> (journalistic), <b>разгово́рный</b> (conversational), <b>худо́жественный</b> (literary). Cutting across them is the <b>register scale</b> — how elevated a word is: <b>высо́кое, кни́жное, нейтра́льное, разгово́рное, просторе́чное</b>.","You don't need a label to know where a text sits — the markers give it away. Terms and impersonal verbs → scientific. Fixed clichés («на основа́нии, в связи́ с») → official. Vivid words beside standard phrases → journalistic. Particles and short lines → conversational. Imagery → literary.","The neutral word is your safe default in any style; the marked words are deliberate tools. That single idea — correct <b>and</b> appropriate — is the whole level in one line."],"audio":"Start with the frame. Russian sorts speech into five functional styles: nauchny, scientific; ofitsialno-delovoy, official; publitsistichesky, journalistic; razgovorny, conversational; khudozhestvenny, literary. Cutting across them is the register scale — how elevated a word is: vysokoye, knizhnoye, neytralnoye, razgovornoye, prostorechnoye. You don't need a label to know where a text sits — the markers give it away. Terms and impersonal verbs mean scientific. Fixed clichés, na osnovanii, v svyazi s, mean official. Vivid words beside standard phrases mean journalistic. Particles and short lines mean conversational. Imagery means literary. The neutral word is your safe default; the marked words are deliberate tools. Correct and appropriate — the whole level in one line.","table":{"rows":[["нау́чный — «иссле́дование пока́зывает» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"научный, исследование показывает\">🔊</button>","scientific — terms, impersonal"],["официа́льно-делово́й — «на основа́нии» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"официально-деловой, на основании\">🔊</button>","official — clichés, formulas"],["публицисти́ческий — «как изве́стно» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"публицистический, как известно\">🔊</button>","journalistic — expressive + standard"],["разгово́рный — «ну вот, что́-то уста́л» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"разговорный, ну вот, что-то устал\">🔊</button>","conversational — particles, short"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Neutral anywhere: “The result depends on the method.”","en":"Нейтра́льно: «Результа́т зави́сит от ме́тода».","say":"Нейтрально: результат зависит от метода."},{"ru":"Colloquial, with friends: “Well, it worked out!”","en":"Разгово́рно: «Ну всё, получи́лось!»","say":"Разговорно: ну всё, получилось!"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Настоя́щим уведомля́ю, что я о́чень уста́л».","right":"«Настоя́щим уведомля́ю о невозмо́жности прису́тствовать».","why":"The official opener needs an official continuation. Mixing the cliché «настоя́щим уведомля́ю» with a chatty «я о́чень уста́л» is the classic non-native giveaway — hold one register."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Как изве́стно, э́то о́стрый вопро́с» — which style?","opts":["нау́чный","публицисти́ческий","разгово́рный"],"answer":1,"explain":"Evaluation («о́стрый вопро́с») next to a standard phrase («как изве́стно») → the <b>публицисти́ческий</b> style."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which word is <b>neutral</b> — safe in any style?","opts":["о́чи","глаза́","гляде́лки"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>глаза́</b> is neutral. о́чи is lofty/poetic; гляде́лки is colloquial-jokey."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The word and its attitude","title":"<em>Смотре́ть / взира́ть / пя́литься</em> — and <em>до́мик / доми́ще / доми́шко</em>","paras":["One English word hides a Russian family. “To look” is <b>смотре́ть</b> (neutral), <b>взира́ть</b> (lofty), <b>пя́литься</b> (rude, to stare). “Big” is <b>большо́й</b> (neutral), <b>огро́мный / грома́дный</b> (bigger, warmer). At C1 you don't reach for “a word” — you choose the member of the family whose <b>shade</b> (отте́нок) and register fit.","Suffixes add a second layer — not size, but <b>attitude</b>. <b>-ик / -очк-</b> warm it up: <b>до́мик</b> (dear little house), <b>ко́шечка</b>. <b>-ищ-</b> enlarges: <b>доми́ще</b> (a whopping house). <b>-ишк-</b> belittles or scorns: <b>доми́шко, городи́шко</b> (a sorry little town). «Городи́шко» isn't a small city — it's a city you look down on.","And these suffixes are <b>colloquial</b> by default. A «работёнка» is fine over coffee and wrong in a report. Same rule as always: the attitude has to fit the room."],"audio":"One English word hides a Russian family. To look is smotret, neutral; vzirat, lofty; pyalitsya, rude, to stare. Big is bolshoy, neutral; ogromny or gromadny, bigger and warmer. At C1 you don't reach for a word — you choose the member of the family whose shade, ottenok, and register fit. Suffixes add a second layer — not size, but attitude. -ik and -ochk warm it up: domik, dear little house; koshechka. -ishch enlarges: domishche, a whopping house. -ishk belittles or scorns: domishko, gorodishko, a sorry little town. Gorodishko isn't a small city — it's a city you look down on. And these suffixes are colloquial by default. A rabotyonka is fine over coffee and wrong in a report. The attitude has to fit the room.","table":{"rows":[["смотре́ть — взира́ть — пя́литься <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"смотреть, взирать, пялиться\">🔊</button>","to look: neutral — lofty — rude"],["большо́й — огро́мный — грома́дный <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"большой, огромный, громадный\">🔊</button>","big — huge — enormous (intensity)"],["до́мик — доми́ще — доми́шко <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"домик, домище, домишко\">🔊</button>","house: dear — huge — sorry (attitude)"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"Warm: “What a cosy little house!”","en":"Ла́сково: «Како́й ую́тный до́мик!»","say":"Ласково: какой уютный домик!"},{"ru":"Scornful: “They live in some sorry little town.”","en":"Пренебрежи́тельно: «Живу́т в како́м-то городи́шке».","say":"Пренебрежительно: живут в каком-то городишке."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"В нау́чной статье́: «Э́ти учёные написа́ли неплоху́ю работёнку».","right":"В нау́чной статье́: «Э́ти учёные написа́ли соли́дную рабо́ту».","why":"«работёнка» carries a jokey, dismissive shade and is colloquial — wrong in a scientific text. The neutral «рабо́та» (here «соли́дная рабо́та») fits. A single suffix can break your register."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"You look down on a small, shabby town. The word with that scorn —","opts":["городо́к","городи́ще","городи́шко"],"answer":2,"explain":"-ишк- = contempt → <b>городи́шко</b>. городо́к is a plain little town; городи́ще is a huge city."},{"type":"type","q":"Turn «дом» into a warm, affectionate little house:","accept":["домик"],"placeholder":"дом…","explain":"<b>до́мик</b> — the affectionate diminutive (-ик)."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Writing that's dense on purpose","title":"<em>Представля́ется целесообра́зным… по возвраще́нии…</em> — the bookish machine","paras":["The two most marked styles run on the same fuel: <b>nouns and impersonality</b>. <b>Scientific</b> Russian avoids “I think” — it says «представля́ется, что…», «отмеча́ется тенде́нция», leans on terms and verbal nouns (реше́ние, обеспе́чение), and links with «сле́довательно, таки́м о́бразом».","<b>Official</b> Russian runs on fixed clichés: «на основа́нии, в связи́ с, в тече́ние, настоя́щим уведомля́ю», «прошу́ предоста́вить». You don't invent phrasing — you assemble standard formulas. Its heavy markedness is exactly what ТРКИ-3 tests.","And both love <b>condensation</b>: fold clauses into phrases and verbal nouns — «по́сле того́ как рассмотре́ли» → «по́сле рассмотре́ния», «так как шёл дождь» → «ввиду́ дождя́». Just remember the flip side: that same density in a chat is канцеляри́т."],"audio":"The two most marked styles run on the same fuel: nouns and impersonality. Scientific Russian avoids I think — it says predstavlyayetsya chto, otmechayetsya tendentsiya, leans on terms and verbal nouns like resheniye and obespecheniye, and links with sledovatelno, takim obrazom. Official Russian runs on fixed clichés: na osnovanii, v svyazi s, v techeniye, nastoyashchim uvedomlyayu, proshu predostavit. You don't invent phrasing — you assemble standard formulas. Its heavy markedness is exactly what TORFL-III tests. And both love condensation: fold clauses into phrases and verbal nouns — posle togo kak rassmotreli becomes posle rassmotreniya, tak kak shyol dozhd becomes vvidu dozhdya. Just remember the flip side: that same density in a chat is kantselyarit.","table":{"rows":[["представля́ется, что… / отмеча́ется <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"представляется что, отмечается\">🔊</button>","scientific: impersonal, nominal"],["на основа́нии, в связи́ с, прошу́ предоста́вить <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"на основании, в связи с, прошу предоставить\">🔊</button>","official: fixed clichés"],["по́сле рассмотре́ния, ввиду́ дождя́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"после рассмотрения, ввиду дождя\">🔊</button>","condensation: clause → verbal noun"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"Scientific: “It appears that the question remains open.”","en":"Нау́чно: «Представля́ется, что вопро́с остаётся откры́тым».","say":"Научно: представляется, что вопрос остаётся открытым."},{"ru":"Official: “On the basis of the order, leave is granted.”","en":"Официа́льно: «На основа́нии прика́за предоставля́ется о́тпуск».","say":"Официально: на основании приказа предоставляется отпуск."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"В статье́: «Я ду́маю, что э́то о́чень кла́ссная тео́рия».","right":"В статье́: «Представля́ется, что да́нная тео́рия заслу́живает внима́ния».","why":"Scientific style is impersonal and neutral: not «я ду́маю» + the colloquial «кла́ссная», but «представля́ется, что…» + neutral evaluation. A personal, emotional tone is a style error in an article."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Condense for a report: «по́сле того́ как ко́мплекс сда́ли» →","opts":["по́сле сда́чи ко́мплекса","когда́ ко́мплекс сда́ли-то","сда́ли ко́мплекс, во!"],"answer":0,"explain":"Verbal noun → <b>по́сле сда́чи ко́мплекса</b>. The others are conversational."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which opens an <b>official</b> application?","opts":["Слу́шайте, мне тут на́до…","На основа́нии ста́тьи 5 прошу́…","Я про́сто ду́маю, что…"],"answer":1,"explain":"The fixed cliché «На основа́нии… прошу́» opens an official application."},{"type":"type","q":"The impersonal scientific verb for “it appears / it is deemed” (predst…):","accept":["представляется"],"placeholder":"представля́ется…","explain":"«представля́ется, что…» — the hallmark impersonal of scientific style."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"At Ле́на's café, Эмма plays a game with Ле́на — say one thing in three styles. A week of C1 in one coffee break. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Ты за неде́лю ста́ла говори́ть как-то по-ра́зному. Э́то заме́тно.","ru":"This week you've started speaking somehow differently. It shows."},{"who":"A","en":"Пра́вда? Дава́й прове́рим. Возьмём мысль «нам ну́жно бо́льше де́нег».","ru":"Really? Let's test it. Take the thought «we need more money»."},{"who":"B","en":"Ну, де́нег не хвата́ет — вот и вся мысль.","ru":"Well, there's not enough money — that's the whole thought."},{"who":"A","en":"Э́то разгово́рный. Официа́льно: «Тре́буется дополни́тельное финанси́рование».","ru":"That's conversational. Officially: «Additional funding is required»."},{"who":"B","en":"Ой, зву́чит как из отчёта!","ru":"Oh, that sounds like it's from a report!"},{"who":"A","en":"И́менно. А в статье́ напи́шут: «Вопро́с фина́нсов стои́т как никогда́ о́стро».","ru":"Exactly. And in an article they'd write: «The question of finances is more acute than ever»."},{"who":"B","en":"Оди́н смысл — и высо́кое, и просторе́чное, и всё ме́жду.","ru":"One meaning — lofty, low, and everything in between."},{"who":"A","en":"Вот э́то и есть неде́ля пе́рвая. Тепе́рь ты слы́шишь реги́стр.","ru":"That's week one, right there. Now you hear register."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for talking about style — the week's meta-toolkit in one deck. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"реги́стр","ru":"register (level of speech)","ex":"Держи́ оди́н реги́стр в те́ксте."},{"en":"нейтра́льный","ru":"neutral (register)","ex":"Нейтра́льное сло́во уме́стно везде́."},{"en":"кни́жный","ru":"bookish (register)","ex":"«Ны́не» — кни́жное сло́во."},{"en":"просторе́чный","ru":"low, non-standard (register)","ex":"«Жра́ть» — просторе́чное сло́во."},{"en":"отте́нок","ru":"shade, nuance","ex":"У сино́нимов ра́зные отте́нки."},{"en":"коннота́ция","ru":"connotation","ex":"У ка́ждого сло́ва есть коннота́ция."},{"en":"уме́стный","ru":"appropriate, fitting","ex":"Ва́жно вы́брать уме́стное сло́во."},{"en":"су́ффикс оце́нки","ru":"evaluative suffix","ex":"Су́ффикс оце́нки несёт отноше́ние."},{"en":"безли́чность","ru":"impersonality (of a text)","ex":"Нау́чный стиль лю́бит безли́чность."},{"en":"клише́","ru":"cliché, fixed formula","ex":"Официа́льный стиль по́лон клише́."},{"en":"номинализа́ция","ru":"nominalization","ex":"Номинализа́ция сжима́ет фра́зу."},{"en":"сжима́ть","ru":"to compress, condense","ex":"Кни́жный стиль сжима́ет прида́точные."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each phrase to what it signals — a style or a register move.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its label — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Представля́ется целесообра́зным…","scientific"],["На основа́нии зако́на…","official-business"],["Живу́т в городи́шке.","scornful / colloquial"],["По возвраще́нии он написа́л отчёт.","bookish condensation"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Eight questions — one or two from every day this week. Pass this and week-1 register is yours.","items":[{"q":"The style of laws and applications —","opts":["разгово́рный","официа́льно-делово́й","худо́жественный"],"answer":1},{"q":"Which word is <b>neutral</b>?","opts":["лик","лицо́","физионо́мия"],"answer":1},{"q":"“To stare” rudely at someone —","opts":["взира́ть","смотре́ть","пя́литься"],"answer":2},{"q":"Which is <b>lofty</b> for “to look”?","opts":["взира́ть","гляде́ть","пя́литься"],"answer":0},{"q":"«Городи́шко» means —","opts":["a small city (just its size)","a city you look down on","a huge city"],"answer":1},{"q":"The impersonal scientific opener —","opts":["Я ду́маю, что…","Представля́ется, что…","Слу́шай, по-мо́ему…"],"answer":1},{"q":"Which opens an official notice?","opts":["Настоя́щим уведомля́ю…","Ну что, наро́д…","Как ды́шится?"],"answer":0},{"q":"«по́сле того́ как он верну́лся» condensed —","opts":["по возвраще́нию","по возвраще́нии","верну́лся-то"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Эмма ends week one with a short note to herself — every idea of the week hides inside it. Read it aloud.","title":"Неде́ля, за кото́рую я ста́ла слы́шать стиль","sentences":["Ра́ньше я зна́ла то́лько «ве́жливо» и «про́сто» — тепе́рь я слы́шу пять стиле́й.","Оди́н смысл живёт в ра́зных слова́х: «лицо́», «лик», «физионо́мия».","Су́ффикс уме́ет и приласка́ть, и уколо́ть: «до́мик» — с любо́вью, «городи́шко» — с презре́нием.","В нау́чном те́ксте я убира́ю «я ду́маю» и пишу́ «представля́ется».","А дли́нные прида́точные сжима́ю: «по́сле того́ как» превраща́ется в «по возвраще́нии».","И гла́вное: тепе́рь я спра́шиваю не то́лько «пра́вильно ли», но и «уме́стно ли»."],"translation":"Before, I knew only «politely» and «plainly» — now I hear five styles. One meaning lives in different words: «лицо́», «лик», «физионо́мия». A suffix can caress or sting: «до́мик» with love, «городи́шко» with contempt. In a scientific text I drop «I think» and write «it appears». And I compress long clauses: «after he returned» turns into «upon returning». And the main thing: now I ask not only «is it correct», but also «is it appropriate»."},"essay":{"intro":"Your week-1 test — one idea, the full register range.","prompt":"Take one simple message (e.g. “the meeting is moved to Friday”). Write it in Russian in <b>three</b> registers: <b>official</b> (clichés + a verbal noun), <b>neutral</b>, and <b>conversational</b> (particles, a warm suffix if it fits). Label each. 3–4 sentences.","hint":"Official leans on «в связи́ с, перено́сится»; neutral is plain and safe; conversational lets in «слу́шай, дава́й» 🙂 Same meaning, three worlds.","example":"Официа́льно: В связи́ с обстоя́тельствами встре́ча перено́сится на пя́тницу. Нейтра́льно: Встре́ча переноси́тся на пя́тницу. По-дру́жески: Слу́шай, дава́й перенесём встре́чу на пя́тницу, ла́дно?"}},"8":{"day":8,"week":"02","themeRu":"The language of the press","themeEn":"Публицисти́ческий стиль","intro":"«Нельзя́ молча́ть!» — a headline Эмма just edited screams off the page, and two lines down sits the dry «как изве́стно». That collision is the whole trick of the <b>journalistic style</b> (публицисти́ческий стиль): it fuses the <b>standard</b> — clichés, set formulas — with raw <b>expression</b> — vivid images, open evaluation, a call to the reader. Not the cold officialese of a contract, not the chatty warmth of a text to a friend. Its own sound: persuasion in print. Today you learn to hear it and to write a line of it.","introAudio":"Nelzya molchat! — a headline Emma just edited screams off the page, and two lines down sits the dry kak izvestno. That collision is the whole trick of the journalistic style, publitsistichesky stil: it fuses the standard — clichés, set formulas — with raw expression — vivid images, open evaluation, a call to the reader. Not the cold officialese of a contract, not the chatty warmth of a text to a friend. Its own sound: persuasion in print. Today you learn to hear it and to write a line of it.","goals":["Hear the press blend: <b>standard cliché + live expression</b> in one breath (как изве́стно + a vivid image)","Spot <b>evaluation and appeal</b>: о́стрый вопро́с, вопию́щий, rhetorical questions, «Заду́маемся»","Open with a <b>hook</b> and link with journalistic connectors (тем не ме́нее, ме́жду тем)","Read a <b>headline</b> — short, evaluative, often a question or a play on words"],"learned":["Heard the press blend of standard cliché and live expression","Spotted evaluation and appeal — evaluative words, rhetorical questions, calls to the reader","Opened with a hook and linked with journalistic connectors","Read and shaped a punchy, evaluative headline"],"review":{"intro":"One pull from the styles you now own, and a connector or two — then into the press, where they all get mixed on purpose.","introAudio":"One pull from the styles you now own, and a connector or two — then into the press, where they all get mixed on purpose.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. Register from week 1.","q":"Which is the <b>official</b>, not journalistic, phrasing?","opts":["Настоя́щим уведомля́ю о повыше́нии цен.","Це́ны сно́ва по́лзут вверх — кто винова́т?","Це́ны расту́т, и э́то трево́жит всех."],"answer":0,"explain":"The cliché «настоя́щим уведомля́ю» is official. The others evaluate and question — that's journalistic."},{"type":"choice","q":"The <b>neutral</b> verb for “to increase” (in a plain report) —","opts":["взлете́ть","вы́расти","подскочи́ть"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>вы́расти</b> is neutral. взлете́ть and подскочи́ть are vivid — press, not report."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the phrase. Stress marks are optional.","q":"A bookish connector for “nevertheless” (tem…):","accept":["тем не менее"],"placeholder":"тем не…","explain":"«тем не ме́нее» — a staple linking connector."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Condense to a verbal-noun phrase: <b>“owing to the crisis”</b>","want":"ввиду́ кри́зиса","answer":"ввиду́ кри́зиса"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Standard meets expression","title":"The press <em>fuses</em> cliché and fire","paras":["The journalistic style has a split personality, and that's the point. One half is <b>standard</b> (станда́рт): ready-made phrases the reader recognises instantly — «как изве́стно», «по да́нным…», «нельзя́ не согласи́ться», «в после́днее вре́мя». The other half is <b>expression</b> (экспре́ссия): vivid images, strong evaluation, a direct appeal. A good article welds the two in a single sentence.","That weld is what makes it neither the <b>official</b> style (all standard, no fire) nor the <b>conversational</b> one (all warmth, no polish). «Как изве́стно, це́ны расту́т» is dry alone; «це́ны бьют реко́рды» is loud alone; together — «Как изве́стно, це́ны вновь бьют реко́рды» — that's the press.","So when you read a newspaper, expect the collision. When you write one line of it, reach for a standard opener and then let one bright, evaluative word do the pushing."],"audio":"The journalistic style has a split personality, and that's the point. One half is standard, standart: ready-made phrases the reader recognises instantly — kak izvestno, po dannym, nelzya ne soglasitsya, v posledneye vremya. The other half is expression, ekspressiya: vivid images, strong evaluation, a direct appeal. A good article welds the two in a single sentence. That weld is what makes it neither the official style, all standard and no fire, nor the conversational one, all warmth and no polish. Kak izvestno, tseny rastut is dry alone; tseny byut rekordy is loud alone; together — Kak izvestno, tseny vnov byut rekordy — that's the press. So when you read a newspaper, expect the collision. When you write one line of it, reach for a standard opener and then let one bright, evaluative word do the pushing.","table":{"rows":[["Станда́рт: как изве́стно, по да́нным… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"как известно, по данным\">🔊</button>","standard: recognisable formulas"],["Экспре́ссия: бьют реко́рды, о́стрый вопро́с <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"бьют рекорды, острый вопрос\">🔊</button>","expression: vivid, evaluative"],["Сплав: «Как изве́стно, це́ны вновь бьют реко́рды». <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"как известно, цены вновь бьют рекорды\">🔊</button>","the fusion — press in one line"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"Press: “It is no secret that the city is changing before our eyes.”","en":"В газе́те: «Ни для кого́ не секре́т, что го́род меня́ется на глаза́х».","say":"В газете: ни для кого не секрет, что город меняется на глазах."},{"ru":"Press: “The figures, alas, speak for themselves.”","en":"В газе́те: «Ци́фры, увы́, говоря́т са́ми за себя́».","say":"В газете: цифры, увы, говорят сами за себя."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"В статье́: «Настоя́щим сообща́ется, что име́ет ме́сто рост цен».","right":"В статье́: «Це́ны сно́ва расту́т — и э́то не мо́жет не трево́жить».","why":"Pure officialese («настоя́щим сообща́ется, име́ет ме́сто») is too cold for the press. Journalism keeps a recognisable phrase but adds evaluation and a human pulse: «не мо́жет не трево́жить». Dry officialese in an article is a style error."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which line sounds like the <b>press</b> (standard + expression)?","opts":["Дово́жу до ва́шего све́дения об изме́нении цен.","Как изве́стно, це́ны вновь бьют реко́рды.","Слу́шай, це́ны опя́ть взлете́ли, у́жас."],"answer":1,"explain":"Standard «как изве́стно» + vivid «бьют реко́рды» = journalistic. The first is official, the third conversational."},{"type":"type","q":"The classic press opener meaning “as is well known” (kak…):","accept":["как известно"],"placeholder":"как из…","explain":"«как изве́стно» — a standard journalistic opener you weld to a vivid claim."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Taking a side, moving the reader","title":"<em>Оце́нка</em> and <em>призы́в</em> — the press has an opinion","paras":["Unlike a scientific text, journalism <b>evaluates</b> openly (оце́ночность). It reaches for charged words: a problem is «о́стрый», «наболе́вший», «вопию́щий»; a success is «беспреце́дентный», «долгожда́нный». The writer isn't hiding behind impersonality — they're steering you.","It also <b>appeals</b> (призы́вность): it speaks to the reader and asks for a response. Rhetorical questions — «Кто винова́т?», «Доко́ле?»; direct calls — «Заду́маемся», «Нельзя́ молча́ть», «Пора́ де́йствовать». These aren't real questions or orders; they're pressure, applied in print.","Dose it, though. One strong evaluative word per thought lands; five in a row tips into a rant. The press persuades — it doesn't shout."],"audio":"Unlike a scientific text, journalism evaluates openly, otsenochnost. It reaches for charged words: a problem is ostry, nabolevshy, vopiyushchy; a success is bespretsedentny, dolgozhdanny. The writer isn't hiding behind impersonality — they're steering you. It also appeals, prizyvnost: it speaks to the reader and asks for a response. Rhetorical questions — Kto vinovat? Dokole?; direct calls — Zadumayemsya, Nelzya molchat, Pora deystvovat. These aren't real questions or orders; they're pressure, applied in print. Dose it, though. One strong evaluative word per thought lands; five in a row tips into a rant. The press persuades — it doesn't shout.","table":{"rows":[["Оце́нка: о́стрый, наболе́вший, вопию́щий <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"острый, наболевший, вопиющий\">🔊</button>","evaluation: charged adjectives"],["Вопро́с к чита́телю: Кто винова́т? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"кто виноват\">🔊</button>","rhetorical question"],["Призы́в: Нельзя́ молча́ть. Пора́ де́йствовать. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"нельзя молчать, пора действовать\">🔊</button>","a call to the reader"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"“This is a burning question that can no longer be ignored.”","en":"«Э́то наболе́вший вопро́с, кото́рый нельзя́ бо́льше не замеча́ть».","say":"Это наболевший вопрос, который нельзя больше не замечать."},{"ru":"“Let us stop and think: who benefits from this?”","en":"«Заду́маемся: кому́ э́то вы́годно?»","say":"Задумаемся: кому это выгодно?"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"В нау́чной статье́: «Э́то про́сто вопию́щее безобра́зие!»","right":"В газе́тной коло́нке: «Э́то вопию́щее безобра́зие!»","why":"Open evaluation and exclamation belong to the <b>journalistic</b> column, not a scientific paper — science stays impersonal and neutral. Right word, wrong style. Match the charge to the genre."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which is a journalistic <b>appeal</b> to the reader?","opts":["Иссле́дование пока́зывает, что…","Заду́маемся, куда́ мы идём.","Прошу́ предоста́вить отчёт."],"answer":1,"explain":"«Заду́маемся…» is a call to the reader — journalistic. The others are scientific and official."},{"type":"choice","q":"For a hard-hitting column, the strongest evaluation of “a scandal” —","opts":["ме́лкое недоразуме́ние","вопию́щее безобра́зие","не́которая пробле́ма"],"answer":1,"explain":"«вопию́щее безобра́зие» carries the charge the press wants; the others soften it."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The hook, and holding it together","title":"Grab them in line one — <em>Ни для кого́ не секре́т…</em>","paras":["A newspaper piece has seconds to hook you, so it opens with a <b>gambit</b>: a shared truth — «Как изве́стно…», «Ни для кого́ не секре́т…»; a scene — «Предста́вьте…»; a startling figure; or a straight question. The opening does two jobs at once: signal the standard, and light the spark.","Then it has to <b>hold together</b>. Journalism leans on the same connectors you use for essays, tuned for contrast and drama: «тем не ме́нее» (nevertheless), «ме́жду тем» (meanwhile), «как ни стра́нно» (strangely enough), «бо́лее того́» (moreover), «одна́ко» (however). They pace the argument and keep the reader moving.","This is your B2 connector kit, now doing journalistic work: set the hook, then walk the reader from claim to claim without a stumble."],"audio":"A newspaper piece has seconds to hook you, so it opens with a gambit: a shared truth — Kak izvestno, Ni dlya kogo ne sekret; a scene — Predstavte; a startling figure; or a straight question. The opening does two jobs at once: signal the standard, and light the spark. Then it has to hold together. Journalism leans on the same connectors you use for essays, tuned for contrast and drama: tem ne meneye, nevertheless; mezhdu tem, meanwhile; kak ni stranno, strangely enough; boleye togo, moreover; odnako, however. They pace the argument and keep the reader moving. This is your B2 connector kit, now doing journalistic work: set the hook, then walk the reader from claim to claim without a stumble.","table":{"rows":[["Крючо́к: Ни для кого́ не секре́т… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ни для кого не секрет\">🔊</button>","hook: a shared-truth opener"],["Контра́ст: тем не ме́нее, одна́ко <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"тем не менее, однако\">🔊</button>","contrast connectors"],["Драмати́зм: ме́жду тем, как ни стра́нно <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"между тем, как ни странно\">🔊</button>","meanwhile / strangely enough"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"“Imagine a city without a single park. Meanwhile, that's exactly where things are heading.”","en":"«Предста́вьте го́род без еди́ного па́рка. Ме́жду тем всё идёт и́менно к э́тому».","say":"Представьте город без единого парка. Между тем всё идёт именно к этому."},{"ru":"“The plan looks fine. Nevertheless, the questions remain.”","en":"«План вы́глядит непло́хо. Тем не ме́нее вопро́сы остаю́тся».","say":"План выглядит неплохо. Тем не менее вопросы остаются."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Коло́нка открыва́ется так: «В соотве́тствии с пу́нктом 3.2…»","right":"Коло́нка открыва́ется так: «Ни для кого́ не секре́т, что го́род задыха́ется от про́бок».","why":"An official cross-reference makes a dead opening for a column. The press hooks with a shared truth and a vivid image («задыха́ется от про́бок»). Open with the spark, not the paragraph number."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which is a journalistic <b>hook</b>?","opts":["Ни для кого́ не секре́т, что вре́мя ухо́дит.","Настоя́щим уведомля́ю о собра́нии.","Гипо́теза прове́рена в трёх экспериме́нтах."],"answer":0,"explain":"A shared-truth opener → a journalistic hook. The others are official and scientific."},{"type":"type","q":"The connector for “meanwhile” (mezh…):","accept":["между тем"],"placeholder":"ме́жду…","explain":"«ме́жду тем» — “meanwhile,” a dramatic pivot in journalism."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · The headline","title":"<em>Заголо́вок</em> — a few words that must land","paras":["The <b>headline</b> (заголо́вок) is the press in miniature: short, evaluative, built to stop you. Russian headlines love a <b>question</b> — «Кто отве́тит за доро́ги?»; a punchy <b>fragment</b> — «Го́род без во́здуха»; or a <b>play on a familiar phrase</b> — twisting a proverb or a famous line. That last trick is so central it gets its own day tomorrow.","English headlines often stack nouns and drop verbs («City Traffic Crisis Talks»). Russian headlines stay more <b>verbal and vivid</b>, and they carry open evaluation a straight news label wouldn't. Translate the effect, not the grammar.","And keep the register honest: a headline can be bold and playful, but it isn't slang and it isn't a legal notice. It's the sharpest point of the journalistic blend."],"audio":"The headline, zagolovok, is the press in miniature: short, evaluative, built to stop you. Russian headlines love a question — Kto otvetit za dorogi?; a punchy fragment — Gorod bez vozdukha; or a play on a familiar phrase — twisting a proverb or a famous line. That last trick is so central it gets its own day tomorrow. English headlines often stack nouns and drop verbs, City Traffic Crisis Talks. Russian headlines stay more verbal and vivid, and they carry open evaluation a straight news label wouldn't. Translate the effect, not the grammar. And keep the register honest: a headline can be bold and playful, but it isn't slang and it isn't a legal notice. It's the sharpest point of the journalistic blend.","table":{"rows":[["Вопро́с: Кто отве́тит за доро́ги? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"кто ответит за дороги\">🔊</button>","headline as a question"],["Фрагме́нт: Го́род без во́здуха <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"город без воздуха\">🔊</button>","punchy verbless fragment"],["Игра́ слов: обы́гранная цита́та <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"обыгранная цитата\">🔊</button>","play on a familiar phrase (→ day 9)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Headline: “Who will answer for the roads?”","en":"Заголо́вок: «Кто отве́тит за доро́ги?»","say":"Заголовок: кто ответит за дороги?"},{"ru":"Headline: “A city without air.”","en":"Заголо́вок: «Го́род без во́здуха».","say":"Заголовок: город без воздуха."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Заголо́вок: «О состоя́нии доро́жного покры́тия в городско́м окру́ге».","right":"Заголо́вок: «Доро́ги, по кото́рым бо́льно е́хать».","why":"The first is a bureaucratic file name, not a headline — no spark, no reader. A headline is short and evaluative: «бо́льно е́хать» makes you feel the potholes. Grab, don't catalogue."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which works as a Russian <b>headline</b>?","opts":["Уведомле́ние о повыше́нии тари́фов на прое́зд.","Прое́зд подорожа́л. Опя́ть.","Я счита́ю, что прое́зд стал доро́же, и э́то пло́хо."],"answer":1,"explain":"Short, punchy, evaluative (the parcelled «Опя́ть.») → a headline. The first is official, the third a full opinion sentence."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Кто отве́тит за э́тот о́стрый вопро́с?","sub":"a journalistic rhetorical question — read it with a rising, challenging tone","want":"кто ответит за этот острый вопрос"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Эмма is polishing a magazine column and its headline. Ка́тя, who's studying journalism, leans in. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Заголо́вок ску́чный. «Информа́ция о ро́сте цен» — кто э́то ста́нет чита́ть?","ru":"The headline's boring. «Information on the rise in prices» — who's going to read that?"},{"who":"A","en":"Согла́сна. В публици́стике заголо́вок до́лжен цепля́ть. Дава́й: «Це́ны сно́ва бьют реко́рды».","ru":"Agreed. In journalism the headline has to grab. Try: «Prices break records again»."},{"who":"B","en":"О, сра́зу друга́я эне́ргия! А в те́ксте мо́жно так же?","ru":"Oh, totally different energy! And can the text be like that too?"},{"who":"A","en":"Мо́жно и ну́жно. Но с ме́рой: одно́ клише́ — «как изве́стно» — и оди́н живо́й о́браз.","ru":"It can and should. But with measure: one cliché — «as is well known» — and one live image."},{"who":"B","en":"А е́сли одни́ то́лько во́згласы?","ru":"And if it's nothing but exclamations?"},{"who":"A","en":"Тогда́ э́то не статья́, а крик. Публици́стика убежда́ет, а не кричи́т.","ru":"Then it's not an article, it's a scream. Journalism persuades, it doesn't shout."},{"who":"B","en":"Поняла́: клише́ де́ржит, о́браз убежда́ет, ме́ра спаса́ет.","ru":"Got it: the cliché holds, the image persuades, measure saves it."},{"who":"A","en":"Вот и весь публицисти́ческий стиль в одно́й стро́чке.","ru":"And that's the whole journalistic style in one line."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words of the press — the levers that persuade in print. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"публици́стика","ru":"journalism, the press style","ex":"Публици́стика убежда́ет чита́теля."},{"en":"экспре́ссия","ru":"expressiveness","ex":"В публици́стике мно́го экспре́ссии."},{"en":"станда́рт","ru":"standard, set formula","ex":"«Как изве́стно» — э́то станда́рт."},{"en":"оце́ночность","ru":"evaluativeness, taking a side","ex":"Оце́ночность — черта́ публици́стики."},{"en":"призы́в","ru":"call, appeal","ex":"«Нельзя́ молча́ть» — э́то призы́в."},{"en":"о́стрый вопро́с","ru":"a pressing, burning issue","ex":"Э́то о́стрый вопро́с для го́рода."},{"en":"наболе́вший","ru":"long-festering, sore (issue)","ex":"Наболе́вшая пробле́ма ждёт реше́ния."},{"en":"вопию́щий","ru":"flagrant, crying (injustice)","ex":"Э́то вопию́щая несправедли́вость."},{"en":"заголо́вок","ru":"headline","ex":"Заголо́вок до́лжен цепля́ть."},{"en":"как изве́стно","ru":"as is well known","ex":"Как изве́стно, вре́мя не ждёт."},{"en":"тем не ме́нее","ru":"nevertheless","ex":"Тем не ме́нее вопро́с оста́лся."},{"en":"ме́жду тем","ru":"meanwhile","ex":"Ме́жду тем це́ны росли́."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each press device to what it is.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its label — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Как изве́стно, …","standard opener"],["вопию́щее безобра́зие","open evaluation"],["Кто винова́т?","rhetorical question"],["Це́ны бьют реко́рды","vivid expression"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions on the press style. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"The journalistic style fuses the standard with —","opts":["expression","legal formulas only","slang only"],"answer":0},{"q":"Which is a press <b>hook</b> opening?","opts":["Ни для кого́ не секре́т, что…","В соотве́тствии с зако́ном…","Гипо́теза состои́т в том, что…"],"answer":0},{"q":"A rhetorical question aimed at the reader —","opts":["Ско́лько сто́ит биле́т?","Доко́ле э́то бу́дет продолжа́ться?","Вы уже́ поза́втракали?"],"answer":1},{"q":"Which word carries the strongest journalistic <b>evaluation</b>?","opts":["не́который","вопию́щий","обы́чный"],"answer":1},{"q":"A good Russian headline is —","opts":["long and impersonal","short, vivid, evaluative","a legal formula"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"A few lines from a column Эмма is editing — watch the press blend: a standard opener, an image, a question, a call. Read it aloud.","title":"Го́род и его́ доро́ги","sentences":["Ни для кого́ не секре́т: доро́ги в на́шем го́роде ста́ли больны́м вопро́сом.","Как изве́стно, ремо́нт обеща́ют ка́ждый год — тем не ме́нее я́мы всё глу́бже.","Ме́жду тем во́дители теря́ют и вре́мя, и не́рвы, и колёса.","Кто отве́тит за э́то вопию́щее безобра́зие?","Заду́маемся — и потре́буем отве́та, пока́ не по́здно."],"translation":"It's no secret: the roads in our city have become a sore subject. As is well known, repairs are promised every year — nevertheless the potholes only get deeper. Meanwhile drivers lose their time, their nerves, and their wheels. Who will answer for this flagrant disgrace? Let us stop and think — and demand an answer, before it's too late."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — write one line of the press.","prompt":"Pick any topic you care about (transport, prices, a park, screen time). Write 2–3 sentences of <b>journalistic</b> Russian: open with a standard hook («Как изве́стно…», «Ни для кого́ не секре́т…»), add one vivid or evaluative word, and end with a rhetorical question or a call to the reader. Then give it a short <b>headline</b>.","hint":"The blend is the goal: a recognisable opener + one bright image + «Заду́маемся» or «Кто винова́т?» — persuade, don't shout 🙂","example":"Заголо́вок: «Го́род без ти́шины». Ни для кого́ не секре́т, что шум стал наболе́вшим вопро́сом. Маши́ны реву́т днём и но́чью, тем не ме́нее нас уверя́ют, что всё в но́рме. Доко́ле мы бу́дем э́то терпе́ть?"}},"9":{"day":9,"week":"02","themeRu":"Quotes everyone knows","themeEn":"Прецеде́нтные те́ксты и крыла́тые слова́","intro":"Halfway through a magazine piece, Эмма hits a headline with no quotation marks and no explanation: «А во́з и ны́не там». Every Russian reader smiles — it's a line from a Krylov fable, and it means <i>nothing has budged</i>. Miss the allusion and the joke, and the whole article goes flat. These are <b>precedent texts</b> (прецеде́нтные те́ксты) and <b>winged words</b> (крыла́тые слова́): quotations a whole culture shares — from Pushkin, film, songs — dropped in unmarked, expecting you to know them. Today you learn to <b>recognise</b> them, and to use a few yourself.","introAudio":"Halfway through a magazine piece, Emma hits a headline with no quotation marks and no explanation: A voz i nyne tam. Every Russian reader smiles — it's a line from a Krylov fable, and it means nothing has budged. Miss the allusion and the joke, and the whole article goes flat. These are precedent texts, pretsedentnye teksty, and winged words, krylatye slova: quotations a whole culture shares — from Pushkin, film, songs — dropped in unmarked, expecting you to know them. Today you learn to recognise them, and to use a few yourself.","goals":["Know what <b>precedent texts</b> and <b>winged words</b> are — shared quotations dropped in unmarked (крыла́тые слова́)","Recognise high-frequency ones: <b>А во́з и ны́не там, ли́шний челове́к, ру́кописи не горя́т</b>","Hear a <b>twisted quotation</b> in a headline (Быть и́ли не быть → …)","Use two or three transparent ones yourself — and know they're bookish and playful, not official"],"learned":["Learned what precedent texts and winged words are","Recognised high-frequency winged words and their meanings","Caught twisted quotations in headlines","Used a few transparent allusions — in the right, playful register"],"review":{"intro":"A quick pull from yesterday's press style — then we open the layer that makes those headlines actually work: the quotes everyone shares.","introAudio":"A quick pull from yesterday's press style — then we open the layer that makes those headlines actually work: the quotes everyone shares.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. Press style from yesterday.","q":"Which is a journalistic <b>hook</b>?","opts":["Ни для кого́ не секре́т, что ле́то ушло́.","Настоя́щим извеща́ю о переры́ве.","Да́нные обрабо́таны по мето́дике."],"answer":0,"explain":"A shared-truth opener → a journalistic hook. The others are official and scientific."},{"type":"choice","q":"The most evaluative, press-style word for a mess —","opts":["небольшо́е неудо́бство","вопию́щий беспоря́док","обы́чное де́ло"],"answer":1,"explain":"«вопию́щий» carries the charge the press wants; the others flatten it."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build a rhetorical press question: <b>“Who is to blame?”</b>","want":"Кто винова́т?","answer":"Кто винова́т?"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks are optional.","q":"One word for a punchy news title (zag…):","accept":["заголовок"],"placeholder":"заго…","explain":"«заголо́вок» — a headline, the sharpest point of the press style."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The quotes a culture shares","title":"<em>Прецеде́нтные те́ксты</em> — winged words everyone knows","paras":["Every culture has lines that everyone knows and no one has to explain. In Russian they're called <b>прецеде́нтные те́ксты</b> (precedent texts) or, for the short quotable ones, <b>крыла́тые слова́</b> (winged words). They come from the classics — Pushkin, Griboyedov, the fabulist Krylov — and from film and song. A native can finish them and feels the whole source behind them.","A few you'll meet constantly: «<b>А во́з и ны́не там</b>» (from a Krylov fable — “and the cart's still right where it was,” i.e. nothing has moved); «<b>ли́шний челове́к</b>» (the “superfluous man” of 19th-century novels — a gifted person with no place); «<b>ру́кописи не горя́т</b>» (Bulgakov — “manuscripts don't burn,” the truth survives).","The point for you isn't to memorise hundreds. It's to <b>recognise</b> that a phrase is a quotation at all — because in a real text it arrives with no quotation marks, no footnote, expecting you to already know."],"audio":"Every culture has lines that everyone knows and no one has to explain. In Russian they're called pretsedentnye teksty, precedent texts, or, for the short quotable ones, krylatye slova, winged words. They come from the classics — Pushkin, Griboyedov, the fabulist Krylov — and from film and song. A native can finish them and feels the whole source behind them. A few you'll meet constantly: A voz i nyne tam, from a Krylov fable — and the cart's still right where it was, meaning nothing has moved; lishny chelovek, the superfluous man of nineteenth-century novels, a gifted person with no place; rukopisi ne goryat, Bulgakov — manuscripts don't burn, the truth survives. The point isn't to memorise hundreds. It's to recognise that a phrase is a quotation at all — because in a real text it arrives with no quotation marks, no footnote, expecting you to already know.","table":{"rows":[["А во́з и ны́не там <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"а воз и ныне там\">🔊</button>","nothing has budged (Krylov)"],["ли́шний челове́к <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"лишний человек\">🔊</button>","the superfluous man (a literary type)"],["ру́кописи не горя́т <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"рукописи не горят\">🔊</button>","manuscripts don't burn — truth survives (Bulgakov)"],["Быть и́ли не быть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"быть или не быть\">🔊</button>","to be or not to be — the big dilemma (Hamlet)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"“They've been promising the repair for a year, but the cart's still right where it was.”","en":"«Ремо́нт обеща́ют год, а во́з и ны́не там».","say":"Ремонт обещают год, а воз и ныне там."},{"ru":"“He's talented, but restless — a real superfluous man.”","en":"«Он тала́нтлив, но не нахо́дит себе́ ме́ста — настоя́щий ли́шний челове́к».","say":"Он талантлив, но не находит себе места — настоящий лишний человек."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«А во́з и ны́не там» → чита́тель и́щет настоя́щую теле́гу.","right":"«А во́з и ны́не там» → чита́тель слы́шит: де́ло не дви́нулось.","why":"Taken literally, the phrase is baffling — there's no actual cart. It's a Krylov quotation meaning “no progress.” Recognising the allusion, not translating the words, is the whole skill."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«А во́з и ны́не там» in an article means —","opts":["a cart has arrived","nothing has moved / no progress","the road is blocked"],"answer":1,"explain":"It's a Krylov quotation for stalled, no-progress situations."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Ли́шний челове́к» describes —","opts":["an extra ticket","a gifted person who can't find a place","one guest too many"],"answer":1,"explain":"The literary “superfluous man” — a talented soul with no role."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Spotting the allusion","title":"They arrive <em>unmarked</em> — learn to hear the quote","paras":["The trap is exactly that these lines come with no warning label. A headline reads «Го́ре от ума́»; a column drops «сча́стливые часо́в не наблюда́ют» into a sentence about a festival. If you don't recognise the Griboyedov behind them, the words parse but the meaning and the wink are lost — the text goes opaque even though you know every word.","So build a reflex: when a phrase sounds a touch too <b>neat, old, or oddly poetic</b> for its surroundings, suspect a quotation. «Ру́кописи не горя́т» in a piece about censorship isn't the writer's own image — it's Bulgakov, carrying his whole novel with it.","You won't catch them all at once, and that's fine. Each one you learn lights up a hundred texts. This recognition layer is a named C1 skill — journalism and literature are built on it."],"audio":"The trap is exactly that these lines come with no warning label. A headline reads Gore ot uma; a column drops schastlivye chasov ne nablyudayut into a sentence about a festival. If you don't recognise the Griboyedov behind them, the words parse but the meaning and the wink are lost — the text goes opaque even though you know every word. So build a reflex: when a phrase sounds a touch too neat, old, or oddly poetic for its surroundings, suspect a quotation. Rukopisi ne goryat in a piece about censorship isn't the writer's own image — it's Bulgakov, carrying his whole novel with it. You won't catch them all at once, and that's fine. Each one you learn lights up a hundred texts. This recognition layer is a named C1 skill — journalism and literature are built on it.","table":{"rows":[["Го́ре от ума́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"горе от ума\">🔊</button>","“Woe from Wit” — trouble from being too clever (Griboyedov)"],["Сча́стливые часо́в не наблюда́ют <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"счастливые часов не наблюдают\">🔊</button>","the happy take no note of time (Griboyedov)"],["Мёртвые ду́ши <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"мёртвые души\">🔊</button>","“Dead Souls” — padded / fictitious names (Gogol)"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"A headline over a stalled reform: “And the cart's still there.”","en":"Заголо́вок о засто́е: «А во́з и ны́не там».","say":"Заголовок о застое: а воз и ныне там."},{"ru":"“This scandal is pure Gogol — dead souls on the payroll.”","en":"«Э́тот сканда́л — чи́стый Го́голь: мёртвые ду́ши в ведомостя́х».","say":"Этот скандал — чистый Гоголь: мёртвые души в ведомостях."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Чита́тель ду́мает, что «мёртвые ду́ши» — про мисти́ческий рома́н у́жасов.","right":"Чита́тель понима́ет: «мёртвые ду́ши» — фикти́вные лю́ди в спи́сках (Го́голь).","why":"Without the Gogol reference, «мёртвые ду́ши» reads like a horror title. In the press it means fake names padding a list. Recognise the source and the real meaning arrives with it."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"You should suspect a hidden quotation when a phrase sounds —","opts":["too neat, old or oddly poetic for its surroundings","full of terms and numbers","short and slangy"],"answer":0,"explain":"That “too-neat, borrowed” ring is the tell of an allusion."},{"type":"type","q":"The winged phrase «__ не горя́т» (manuscripts don't burn), first word:","accept":["рукописи"],"placeholder":"ру́кописи…","explain":"«Ру́кописи не горя́т» — Bulgakov: truth and art survive."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The twisted quote","title":"Headlines <em>bend</em> a famous line — and you enjoy the original underneath","paras":["Yesterday's headline day promised this one. The press loves to take a line everyone knows and <b>twist</b> it: the fun is that you hear the original and the swap at once. «Быть и́ли не быть» (to be or not to be) becomes «<b>Пить</b> и́ли не пить» over an article on alcohol; «Учи́ться, учи́ться и учи́ться» becomes «<b>Лечи́ться</b>, лечи́ться и лечи́ться» over one on healthcare.","It works only if you hold the original in your head. That's why recognition (Rule 2) comes first: you can't catch the joke in the twist if you never knew the straight version. A twisted proverb — «Семь раз отме́рь» → «Семь раз отме́рь, оди́н раз купи́» — winks the same way.","This is the sharpest meeting point of the last two days: the journalistic headline (day 8) built on a precedent text (today). When a headline feels like a pun, look for the quotation underneath."],"audio":"Yesterday's headline day promised this one. The press loves to take a line everyone knows and twist it: the fun is that you hear the original and the swap at once. Byt ili ne byt, to be or not to be, becomes Pit ili ne pit over an article on alcohol; Uchitsya, uchitsya i uchitsya becomes Lechitsya, lechitsya i lechitsya over one on healthcare. It works only if you hold the original in your head. That's why recognition comes first: you can't catch the joke in the twist if you never knew the straight version. A twisted proverb — sem raz otmer becomes sem raz otmer, odin raz kupi — winks the same way. This is the sharpest meeting point of the last two days: the journalistic headline built on a precedent text. When a headline feels like a pun, look for the quotation underneath.","table":{"rows":[["Быть и́ли не быть → Пить и́ли не пить <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"быть или не быть, пить или не пить\">🔊</button>","Hamlet, twisted for an alcohol piece"],["Учи́ться, учи́ться и учи́ться <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"учиться, учиться и учиться\">🔊</button>","Lenin's slogan — often twisted"],["Семь раз отме́рь, оди́н — отре́жь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"семь раз отмерь, один отрежь\">🔊</button>","“measure seven times…” — a twistable proverb"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Headline on healthcare, twisting Lenin: “To be treated, treated and treated.”","en":"Заголо́вок о медици́не: «Лечи́ться, лечи́ться и лечи́ться».","say":"Заголовок о медицине: лечиться, лечиться и лечиться."},{"ru":"“To buy or not to buy — that is the question.”","en":"«Купи́ть и́ли не купи́ть — вот в чём вопро́с».","say":"Купить или не купить — вот в чём вопрос."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Заголо́вок «Пить и́ли не пить» → чита́тель ви́дит то́лько опеча́тку.","right":"Заголо́вок «Пить и́ли не пить» → чита́тель слы́шит Га́млета под шу́ткой.","why":"If you don't know «быть и́ли не быть», the twist looks like a typo. Once you do, the pun lands. The twisted headline rewards exactly the recognition you're building."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"A headline «Пить и́ли не пить» twists which famous line?","opts":["Быть и́ли не быть","Что де́лать?","Жи́ли-бы́ли"],"answer":0,"explain":"It bends Hamlet's «быть и́ли не быть»."},{"type":"build","q":"Build the original Hamlet line (to be or not to be):","want":"Быть и́ли не быть","answer":"Быть и́ли не быть"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Using a few — with care","title":"Drop an allusion on purpose — the transparent ones only","paras":["You'll mostly <b>recognise</b> these, but a handful are safe to <b>use</b>, and they make you sound startlingly native. To say a project is stalled: «А во́з и ны́не там». For a real dilemma: «Быть и́ли не быть». For something that won't stay buried: «Ру́кописи не горя́т». Slip one into a discussion and Russians grin.","Two cautions. First, use only the <b>common, transparent</b> ones — a rare or misremembered quotation sounds pretentious or just wrong. Second, they carry a <b>register</b>: winged words are bookish-to-journalistic and often playful. Great in an essay, a column or lively talk; out of place in a dry official document.","So the balance is the same as all week: recognise widely, deploy narrowly, and always check that the allusion fits the room."],"audio":"You'll mostly recognise these, but a handful are safe to use, and they make you sound startlingly native. To say a project is stalled: A voz i nyne tam. For a real dilemma: Byt ili ne byt. For something that won't stay buried: Rukopisi ne goryat. Slip one into a discussion and Russians grin. Two cautions. First, use only the common, transparent ones — a rare or misremembered quotation sounds pretentious or just wrong. Second, they carry a register: winged words are bookish-to-journalistic and often playful. Great in an essay, a column or lively talk; out of place in a dry official document. So the balance is the same as all week: recognise widely, deploy narrowly, and always check that the allusion fits the room.","examples":[{"ru":"In a discussion: “We've talked for a year, and the cart's still where it was.”","en":"В диску́ссии: «Говори́м год, а во́з и ны́не там».","say":"В дискуссии: говорим год, а воз и ныне там."},{"ru":"In an essay: “For the hero it's a question of to be or not to be.”","en":"В эссе́: «Для геро́я э́то вопро́с “быть и́ли не быть”».","say":"В эссе: для героя это вопрос быть или не быть."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"В официа́льном заявле́нии: «Прошу́ реши́ть вопро́с, а то во́з и ны́не там».","right":"В официа́льном заявле́нии: «Прошу́ уско́рить рассмотре́ние вопро́са».","why":"A winged word is playful and bookish-journalistic — it clashes with the dry official register of an application. Keep the allusion for the essay or the column; the document wants a neutral formula."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Where is «а во́з и ны́не там» a good fit?","opts":["в официа́льном догово́ре","в живо́й диску́ссии о засто́е","в юриди́ческом уведомле́нии"],"answer":1,"explain":"Winged words are bookish-playful → a lively discussion, not a legal document."},{"type":"type","q":"Complete the winged phrase for “no progress”: «А во́з и ны́не …»","accept":["там"],"placeholder":"…","explain":"«А во́з и ны́не там» — Krylov: still exactly where it started."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Мы спо́рим уже́ год, а во́з и ны́не там.","sub":"drop a winged word naturally — say it with a wry shrug","want":"мы спорим уже год а воз и ныне там"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Эмма shows Макси́м a headline she loved. He reads it flat; she hears the quotation. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"«А во́з и ны́не там». Кака́я маши́на? Статья́ про доро́ги?","ru":"«And the cart's still there». What car? Is the article about roads?"},{"who":"A","en":"Нет, про рефо́рму. Э́то Крыло́в — зна́чит, ничего́ не сдви́нулось.","ru":"No, about a reform. It's Krylov — meaning nothing has budged."},{"who":"B","en":"То есть теле́ги вообще́ нет?","ru":"So there's no cart at all?"},{"who":"A","en":"Нет, коне́чно. Э́то крыла́тое сло́во. Их вставля́ют в текст без кавы́чек.","ru":"Of course not. It's a winged word. They're slipped into a text without quotation marks."},{"who":"B","en":"А как я пойму́, что э́то цита́та?","ru":"And how do I tell it's a quotation?"},{"who":"A","en":"По чутью́: фра́за звучи́т сли́шком скла́дно и ста́ро для но́вости.","ru":"By feel: the phrase sounds too neat and too old for a news item."},{"who":"B","en":"Зна́чит, на́до знать исто́чник, ина́че смысл прохо́дит ми́мо.","ru":"So you have to know the source, or the meaning passes you by."},{"who":"A","en":"И́менно. Узна́л цита́ту — по́нял и текст, и шу́тку.","ru":"Exactly. Recognise the quote — and you get both the text and the joke."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve for the culture layer — the meta-words plus the winged words themselves. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"прецеде́нтный текст","ru":"precedent text (shared quotation)","ex":"Заголо́вок ча́сто — прецеде́нтный текст."},{"en":"крыла́тое сло́во","ru":"winged word, catchphrase","ex":"«Ру́кописи не горя́т» — крыла́тое сло́во."},{"en":"цита́та","ru":"quotation","ex":"Он вста́вил цита́ту без кавы́чек."},{"en":"аллю́зия","ru":"allusion","ex":"Чита́тель по́нял аллю́зию."},{"en":"исто́чник","ru":"source (of a quote)","ex":"У ка́ждой цита́ты есть исто́чник."},{"en":"обыгра́ть","ru":"to play on, twist (a phrase)","ex":"Заголо́вок обыгра́л изве́стную цита́ту."},{"en":"а во́з и ны́не там","ru":"nothing has budged (Krylov)","ex":"Обеща́ли год — а во́з и ны́не там."},{"en":"ли́шний челове́к","ru":"the superfluous man (a type)","ex":"Оне́гин — класси́ческий ли́шний челове́к."},{"en":"быть и́ли не быть","ru":"to be or not to be — a big dilemma","ex":"Для него́ э́то «быть и́ли не быть»."},{"en":"ру́кописи не горя́т","ru":"manuscripts don't burn (Bulgakov)","ex":"Пра́вду не спря́чешь: ру́кописи не горя́т."},{"en":"мёртвые ду́ши","ru":"“dead souls” — fictitious names (Gogol)","ex":"В ве́домости нашли́ мёртвые ду́ши."},{"en":"го́ре от ума́","ru":"“woe from wit” (Griboyedov)","ex":"У него́ настоя́щее го́ре от ума́."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each winged word to what it means.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["А во́з и ны́не там","nothing has budged"],["ли́шний челове́к","a gifted person with no place"],["ру́кописи не горя́т","the truth survives"],["мёртвые ду́ши","fictitious names on a list"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions on winged words. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"A “precedent text” is —","opts":["a legal precedent","a shared cultural quotation","the first draft"],"answer":1},{"q":"«А во́з и ны́не там» means —","opts":["the cart arrived","nothing has moved","the road is new"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Ли́шний челове́к» is —","opts":["a superfluous, displaced type","an extra passenger","a rude person"],"answer":0},{"q":"A headline «Пить и́ли не пить» twists —","opts":["a Krylov fable","«быть и́ли не быть»","a weather report"],"answer":1},{"q":"Winged words fit best in —","opts":["an essay or a column","a legal contract","a lab protocol"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Эмма writes a few lines about the day the hidden quotations finally lit up for her. Read it aloud.","title":"День, когда́ я начала́ узнава́ть цита́ты","sentences":["Ра́ньше заголо́вки вро́де «А во́з и ны́не там» меня́ ста́вили в тупи́к.","Я иска́ла в те́ксте маши́ну, а там пря́тался Крыло́в.","Тепе́рь я слы́шу цита́ту да́же без кавы́чек: Го́голь, Булга́ков, Пу́шкин.","Оди́н заголо́вок обыгра́л «быть и́ли не быть» — и я улыбну́лась, бу́дто узна́ла ста́рого дру́га.","Оказа́лось, поня́ть язы́к — э́то ещё и знать, отку́да его́ слова́."],"translation":"Before, headlines like «And the cart's still there» left me stumped. I'd look for a car in the text, while Krylov was hiding in it. Now I hear the quotation even without quotation marks: Gogol, Bulgakov, Pushkin. One headline played on «to be or not to be» — and I smiled, as if I'd recognised an old friend. It turned out that understanding a language also means knowing where its words come from."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — catch one, and try one.","prompt":"In 2–3 sentences of Russian, do two things: (1) name one <b>winged word</b> you now recognise and say what it means, and (2) use one transparent allusion yourself — «а во́з и ны́не там», «быть и́ли не быть» or «ру́кописи не горя́т» — in a sentence of your own. Keep it in a lively, essay-like register.","hint":"Recognition + a light touch: “«Ру́кописи не горя́т» — that's Bulgakov, the truth survives… and about my repair: they promise a month, а во́з и ны́не там” 🙂 Use only the transparent ones.","example":"«Ру́кописи не горя́т» — э́то Булга́ков, о том, что пра́вду не спря́тать. А про мой ремо́нт мо́жно сказа́ть про́сто: обеща́ют ме́сяц, а во́з и ны́не там."}},"10":{"day":10,"week":"02","themeRu":"Set phrases by register","themeEn":"Фразеологи́змы: кни́жные и разгово́рные","intro":"Yesterday, precedent phrases — the quotes a whole culture shares. Their close cousins are <b>идио́мы</b> (idioms): set phrases you can't take apart. «<b>Бить баклу́ши</b>» has nothing to do with any <i>баклу́ши</i> — it just means to loaf about. Эмма meets dozens a day in the texts she edits, and here's the C1 twist: every idiom carries its own <b>register</b>. Drop a chatty one into a scientific paper and you've made a style mistake, however “correct” the words. Today: idioms by theme <i>and</i> by register.","introAudio":"Yesterday, precedent phrases — the quotes a whole culture shares. Their close cousins are idiomy, idioms: set phrases you can't take apart. 'Bit baklushi' has nothing to do with any baklushi — it just means to loaf about. Emma meets dozens a day in the texts she edits, and here's the C1 twist: every idiom carries its own register. Drop a chatty one into a scientific paper and you've made a style mistake, however correct the words. Today: idioms by theme and by register.","goals":["Read an idiom's <b>register</b>: <b>кни́жное</b> (bookish) vs <b>разгово́рное</b> (colloquial) vs <b>просторе́чное</b> (low)","Meet a themed batch of common idioms and a couple of <b>посло́вицы</b> (proverbs)","Match an idiom to the style it belongs in — and catch the mismatch","Stop <b>calquing</b> English idioms word-for-word — reach for the Russian image"],"learned":["Placed idioms on the register scale (bookish / colloquial / low)","Learned a themed set of common idioms and proverbs","Matched each idiom to its proper style, and spotted the style error","Stopped translating English idioms literally"],"review":{"intro":"Before the idioms, a quick warm-up on the week so far: a famous quote, a press-style line, and a word's register.","introAudio":"Before the idioms, a quick warm-up on the week so far: a famous quote, a press-style line, and a word's register.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer.","q":"Which of these is a <b>precedent phrase</b> — a quote a whole culture shares?","opts":["«Да́й, пожа́луйста, соль»","«Быть и́ли не быть»","«В связи́ с зако́ном»"],"answer":1,"explain":"«Быть и́ли не быть» is Hamlet — a precedent phrase every educated Russian recognises. The others are just an ordinary request and an official cliché."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Как изве́стно, э́то о́стрый вопро́с» — which style is that?","opts":["нау́чный","официа́льно-делово́й","публицисти́ческий"],"answer":2,"explain":"«Как изве́стно» plus open evaluation («о́стрый вопро́с») is the <b>публицисти́ческий</b> (journalistic) marker from day 8."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks are just for you.","q":"The neutral, everyday word for money — not the official «сре́дства», not the slang «ба́бки». Type it:","accept":["деньги"],"placeholder":"де́ньги…","explain":"The neutral middle of the scale is <b>де́ньги</b> — safe in any style."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · An idiom has a register","title":"A set phrase carries its <em>own</em> register","paras":["An <b>идио́ма</b> / <b>фразеологи́зм</b> is a phrase whose meaning you can't build up from its words: «<b>бить баклу́ши</b>» = to idle, «<b>ка́нуть в Ле́ту</b>» = to vanish without a trace. You already met a few at B2; C1 is about knowing <i>how many</i> — and which world each one lives in.","Here's the catch that trips English speakers: an idiom sits on the same <b>register scale</b> (шкала́ регистра) as a single word. «<b>Ка́нуть в Ле́ту</b>» is <b>кни́жное</b> (bookish) — at home in an essay or an obituary. «<b>Бить баклу́ши</b>» is <b>разгово́рное</b> (colloquial) — at home in a kitchen chat. «<b>До ла́мпочки</b>» is <b>просторе́чное</b> (low, blunt).","So an idiom is never just “correct” or “wrong.” It's <i>bookish, neutral, colloquial or low</i> — and it has to match the text around it. That's the whole lesson."],"audio":"An idioma, a set phrase, means something you can't build from its words: 'bit baklushi' is to idle, 'kanut v Letu' is to vanish without a trace. You met a few at B2; C1 is knowing how many, and which world each lives in. The catch for English speakers: an idiom sits on the same register scale as a single word. 'Kanut v Letu' is knizhnoye, bookish — at home in an essay or an obituary. 'Bit baklushi' is razgovornoye, colloquial — at home in a kitchen chat. 'Do lampochki' is prostorechnoye, low and blunt. So an idiom is never just correct or wrong; it's bookish, neutral, colloquial or low, and it must match the text around it.","table":{"rows":[["ка́нуть в Ле́ту <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"кануть в лету\">🔊</button>","to vanish without a trace — bookish"],["бить баклу́ши <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"бить баклуши\">🔊</button>","to idle about — colloquial"],["до ла́мпочки <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"до лампочки\">🔊</button>","(I) couldn't care less — low / rough"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"In an essay: “That old dispute has sunk into oblivion.”","en":"В эссе́: «Тот ста́рый спор <b>ка́нул в Ле́ту</b>».","say":"В эссе: тот старый спор канул в лету."},{"ru":"To a friend: “Stop loafing about!”","en":"Дру́гу: «Хва́тит <b>бить баклу́ши</b>!»","say":"Другу: хватит бить баклуши!"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"В нау́чной статье́: «Ученики́ на уро́ках бьют баклу́ши».","right":"В нау́чной статье́: «Ученики́ на уро́ках безде́льничают».","why":"«Бить баклу́ши» is colloquial — fine in speech, jarring in a scientific paper. The neutral verb <b>безде́льничать</b> fits. The idiom was “correct” — just wrong for the register."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"Where does «ка́нуть в Ле́ту» belong?","opts":["a bookish text — an essay or an obituary","a casual text to a friend","a shopping list"],"answer":0,"explain":"«Ка́нуть в Ле́ту» is <b>bookish</b> — it lives in elevated writing, not in chat."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The bookish layer","title":"<em>Кни́жные</em> idioms: the elevated shelf","paras":["Bookish idioms give writing weight; they fill essays, journalism and literary prose. Learn them as whole units: «<b>кра́сной ни́тью</b>» (running through as a central theme), «<b>ка́мень преткнове́ния</b>» (a stumbling block), «<b>я́блоко раздо́ра</b>» (a bone of contention), «<b>при́тча во язы́цех</b>» (the talk of the town).","Notice they often come from books, the Bible or antiquity — «<b>ка́нуть в Ле́ту</b>» sends you to the river Lethe, «<b>дамо́клов меч</b>» to a Greek tyrant's hall. That literary origin is exactly what makes them feel elevated.","Used in place, they read as educated and precise. Used in a chat, they sound pompous — register cuts both ways."],"audio":"Bookish idioms give writing weight; they fill essays, journalism and literary prose. Learn them whole: 'krasnoy nityu', running through as a central theme; 'kamen pretknoveniya', a stumbling block; 'yabloko razdora', a bone of contention; 'pritcha vo yazytsekh', the talk of the town. They often come from books, the Bible or antiquity — 'kanut v Letu' sends you to the river Lethe, 'damoklov mech' to a Greek tyrant's hall. That origin is what makes them feel elevated. In place they read as educated and precise; in a chat they sound pompous. Register cuts both ways.","table":{"rows":[["кра́сной ни́тью <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"красной нитью\">🔊</button>","running through as a central theme"],["ка́мень преткнове́ния <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"камень преткновения\">🔊</button>","a stumbling block"],["я́блоко раздо́ра <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"яблоко раздора\">🔊</button>","a bone of contention"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"The theme of memory runs through the whole novel.","en":"Те́ма па́мяти прохо́дит <b>кра́сной ни́тью</b> че́рез весь рома́н.","say":"Тема памяти проходит красной нитью через весь роман."},{"ru":"Money became the bone of contention at the talks.","en":"Де́ньги ста́ли <b>я́блоком раздо́ра</b> на перегово́рах.","say":"Деньги стали яблоком раздора на переговорах."}],"mistakes":[],"tasks":[{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the two words. Stress marks are optional.","q":"Bookish idiom: a theme that runs through the whole book goes «…» — through it as a central thread. Fill «кра́сной …»:","accept":["красной нитью","нитью"],"placeholder":"кра́сной …","explain":"«<b>Кра́сной ни́тью</b>» — literally “as a red thread”: a theme that runs right through a text. A classic bookish idiom."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The colloquial layer","title":"<em>Разгово́рные</em> idioms: the language's warm kitchen","paras":["The colloquial idioms are where everyday Russian sparkles: «<b>води́ть за́ нос</b>» (to string someone along), «<b>спусти́ть на тормоза́х</b>» (to quietly let a matter drop), «<b>заруби́ть на носу́</b>» (to get it firmly into your head), «<b>ви́лами по воде́ пи́сано</b>» (it's anyone's guess).","A rougher, <b>просторе́чный</b> shelf sits just below: «<b>до ла́мпочки</b>» (couldn't care less), «<b>ни бельме́са</b>» (not a single thing). Blunt, expressive — and out of place anywhere official.","These are Эмма's spoken register: perfect over coffee, a red flag in a contract. When she edits a formal text, half the job is catching a colloquial idiom that slipped in."],"audio":"The colloquial idioms are where everyday Russian sparkles: 'vodit za nos', to string someone along; 'spustit na tormozakh', to quietly let a matter drop; 'zarubit na nosu', to get it firmly into your head; 'vilami po vode pisano', it's anyone's guess. A rougher, prostorechny shelf sits just below: 'do lampochki', couldn't care less; 'ni belmesa', not a single thing. Blunt, expressive — and out of place anywhere official. These are Emma's spoken register: perfect over coffee, a red flag in a contract. When she edits a formal text, half the job is catching a colloquial idiom that slipped in.","table":{"rows":[["води́ть за́ нос <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"водить за нос\">🔊</button>","to string someone along"],["спусти́ть на тормоза́х <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"спустить на тормозах\">🔊</button>","to quietly let a matter drop"],["заруби́ть на носу́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"зарубить на носу\">🔊</button>","to remember firmly"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Don't string me along — tell me straight.","en":"Не <b>води́ меня́ за́ нос</b> — скажи́ пря́мо.","say":"Не води меня за нос — скажи прямо."},{"ru":"Mark my words (remember it firmly): he'll be late.","en":"<b>Заруби́ на носу́</b>: он опозда́ет.","say":"Заруби на носу: он опоздает."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"В догово́ре: «Сто́роны реши́ли спусти́ть де́ло на тормоза́х».","right":"В догово́ре: «Сто́роны реши́ли прекрати́ть разбира́тельство».","why":"«Спусти́ть на тормоза́х» is colloquial; a contract needs the neutral-official «прекрати́ть разбира́тельство». Same idea, right register."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Over coffee, you gossip about a lazy colleague. Which line fits the chat?","opts":["Он на рабо́те предаётся пра́здности.","Он на рабо́те бьёт баклу́ши.","Он уклоня́ется от исполне́ния обя́занностей."],"answer":1,"explain":"A friendly chat wants the colloquial idiom «<b>бьёт баклу́ши</b>». The first is bookish, the third is official — both too stiff for coffee talk."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Don't calque; and proverbs","title":"The Russian image is <em>different</em> — never translate an idiom literally","paras":["The deepest trap is translating an English idiom word-for-word. «It's raining cats and dogs» is <b>not</b> «ко́шки и соба́ки» — Russians say «<b>льёт как из ведра́</b>» (it's pouring as if from a bucket). «To kill two birds with one stone» → «<b>уби́ть двух за́йцев</b>» (two hares). Reach for the Russian image, not the English words.","Proverbs (<b>посло́вицы</b>) work the same way — fixed, culturally loaded: «<b>Без труда́ не вы́тащишь и ры́бку из пруда́</b>» (no pain, no gain), «<b>Ти́ше е́дешь — да́льше бу́дешь</b>» (slow and steady wins the race).","So two rules for life at C1: match an idiom to its register, and find the native equivalent instead of calquing your own. Do both, and idioms become one of the fastest ways to sound truly Russian."],"audio":"The deepest trap is translating an English idiom word for word. 'It's raining cats and dogs' is not 'koshki i sobaki' — Russians say 'lyot kak iz vedra', it's pouring as if from a bucket. 'To kill two birds with one stone' becomes 'ubit dvukh zaytsev', two hares. Reach for the Russian image, not the English words. Proverbs work the same way — fixed and culturally loaded: 'Bez truda ne vytashchish i rybku iz pruda', no pain no gain; 'Tishe yedesh, dalshe budesh', slow and steady wins. So two rules at C1: match an idiom to its register, and find the native equivalent instead of calquing your own.","examples":[{"ru":"“It's raining cats and dogs” → “it's pouring as if from a bucket.”","en":"«It's raining cats and dogs» → «<b>Льёт как из ведра́</b>».","say":"Льёт как из ведра."},{"ru":"“Kill two birds with one stone” → “kill two hares.”","en":"«Kill two birds with one stone» → «<b>Уби́ть двух за́йцев</b>».","say":"Убить двух зайцев."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«На у́лице льёт ко́шками и соба́ками».","right":"«На у́лице льёт как из ведра́».","why":"A word-for-word calque of the English idiom is nonsense in Russian. The native image is <b>как из ведра́</b> — “as if from a bucket.”"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“To kill two birds with one stone” in natural Russian is…","opts":["бро́сить ка́мень в двух птиц","уби́ть двух птиц одни́м ка́мнем","уби́ть двух за́йцев одни́м вы́стрелом"],"answer":2,"explain":"The Russian image is two <b>hares</b> and one shot: «уби́ть двух за́йцев». The bird-and-stone versions are calques of the English."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the last word.","q":"“No pain, no gain” matches the proverb «Без труда́ не вы́тащишь и ры́бку из …». Type the missing word:","accept":["пруда"],"placeholder":"из пру…","explain":"«Без труда́ не вы́тащишь и ры́бку из <b>пруда́</b>» — you won't even pull a fish out of a pond without effort."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Эмма is editing at Лена's café when Ива́н drops by and reads over her shoulder. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Над чем колду́ешь? Лицо́ у тебя́ озада́ченное.","ru":"What are you slaving over? You look puzzled."},{"who":"A","en":"Реда́ктирую перево́д догово́ра. И тут натыка́юсь на «спусти́ть на тормоза́х».","ru":"Editing a contract translation. And here I hit “to let it quietly drop.”"},{"who":"B","en":"А что не так? Норма́льно же зву́чит.","ru":"What's wrong with it? Sounds fine to me."},{"who":"A","en":"Зву́чит-то жи́во, но э́то разгово́рный фразеологи́зм. В догово́ре он как ковбо́йская шля́па на профе́ссоре.","ru":"It sounds lively, sure, but it's a colloquial idiom. In a contract it's like a cowboy hat on a professor."},{"who":"B","en":"И на что ты его́ заме́нишь?","ru":"So what will you replace it with?"},{"who":"A","en":"Напишу́ нейтра́льно: «прекрати́ть разбира́тельство». Смысл тот же, реги́стр — друго́й.","ru":"I'll go neutral: “to terminate the proceedings.” Same meaning, different register."},{"who":"B","en":"А быва́ет, что идио́ма, наоборо́т, к ме́сту?","ru":"And does an idiom sometimes fit just right, the other way round?"},{"who":"A","en":"И́менно. В статье́ я бы да́же оста́вила «кра́сной ни́тью» — кни́жно и то́чно. Всё де́ло в том, куда́ его́ ста́вишь.","ru":"Exactly. In an article I'd even keep “as a central thread” — bookish and precise. It's all about where you put it."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve set phrases — sorted by register. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"фразеологи́зм","ru":"idiom, set phrase","ex":"У ка́ждого фразеологи́зма есть свой реги́стр."},{"en":"посло́вица","ru":"proverb (a full saying)","ex":"«Ти́ше е́дешь — да́льше бу́дешь» — посло́вица."},{"en":"погово́рка","ru":"saying, byword","ex":"Погово́рка коро́че посло́вицы."},{"en":"крыла́тое сло́во","ru":"winged word (a famous quote-phrase)","ex":"«А во́з и ны́не там» — крыла́тое сло́во."},{"en":"ка́нуть в Ле́ту","ru":"to sink into oblivion (bookish)","ex":"Ста́рый спор ка́нул в Ле́ту."},{"en":"кра́сной ни́тью","ru":"as a central recurring theme (bookish)","ex":"Э́та мысль прохо́дит кра́сной ни́тью."},{"en":"ка́мень преткнове́ния","ru":"stumbling block (bookish)","ex":"Цена́ ста́ла ка́мнем преткнове́ния."},{"en":"бить баклу́ши","ru":"to twiddle one's thumbs, idle (colloquial)","ex":"Хва́тит бить баклу́ши!"},{"en":"спусти́ть на тормоза́х","ru":"to quietly let a matter drop (colloquial)","ex":"Де́ло реши́ли спусти́ть на тормоза́х."},{"en":"води́ть за́ нос","ru":"to string someone along (colloquial)","ex":"Не води́ меня́ за́ нос."},{"en":"заруби́ть на носу́","ru":"to remember firmly (colloquial)","ex":"Заруби́ на носу́: рабо́та пре́жде всего́."},{"en":"до ла́мпочки","ru":"(I) couldn't care less (low, rough)","ex":"Ему́ всё до ла́мпочки."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Place each set phrase on the register scale.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its register — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["ка́нуть в Ле́ту","bookish"],["спусти́ть на тормоза́х","colloquial"],["до ла́мпочки","low / rough"],["Без труда́ не вы́тащишь ры́бку из пруда́.","a proverb"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"Which idiom is <b>bookish</b> — at home in an essay?","opts":["бить баклу́ши","ка́нуть в Ле́ту","до ла́мпочки"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Спусти́ть на тормоза́х» belongs to which register?","opts":["bookish — for a scientific paper","colloquial — for everyday speech","official — for a contract"],"answer":1},{"q":"“It's raining cats and dogs” in natural Russian is…","opts":["льёт ко́шками и соба́ками","льёт как из ведра́","идёт си́льный кот"],"answer":1},{"q":"A colloquial idiom dropped into a scientific article is…","opts":["a nice touch of style","a register mistake","required at C1"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Без труда́ не вы́тащишь и ры́бку из пруда́» is a…","opts":["посло́вица (proverb)","official cliché","rhetorical question"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"A short note in Эмма's editor voice — every idiom has an address. Read it aloud and hear the register shift.","title":"У ка́ждой идио́мы — свой а́дрес","sentences":["Фразеологи́зм — э́то не про́сто краси́вая фра́за, а сло́во со свои́м реги́стром.","«Ка́нуть в Ле́ту» уме́стно в эссе́, но неле́по в дру́жеской перепи́ске.","«Бить баклу́ши» — наоборо́т: своё в разгово́ре, чужо́е в нау́чной статье́.","И никогда́ не переводи́те англи́йскую идио́му досло́вно: у ру́сского языка́ свой о́браз.","Зна́ешь реги́стр идио́мы — зна́ешь полови́ну её жи́зни в языке́."],"translation":"An idiom isn't just a pretty phrase — it's a word with its own register. “To sink into Lethe” fits an essay but is absurd in a friendly chat. “To twiddle your thumbs” is the opposite: at home in speech, a stranger in a scientific paper. And never translate an English idiom literally: Russian has its own image. Know an idiom's register, and you know half of its life in the language."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn to play editor.","prompt":"Pick one meaning — say, “to work hard” or “to do nothing” — and write it in Russian TWICE: once with a <b>bookish</b> idiom and once with a <b>colloquial</b> one. Label each with its register. 2–3 sentences.","hint":"Colloquial: «бить баклу́ши», «рабо́тать не поклада́я рук». Bookish: «преда́ться пра́здности», «труди́ться в по́те лица́». Same idea, two shelves 🙂","example":"Разгово́рно: «Хва́тит бить баклу́ши, дава́й рабо́тать!» Кни́жно: «Не сто́ит преда́ваться пра́здности, когда́ есть де́ло»."}},"11":{"day":11,"week":"02","themeRu":"Word order as emphasis","themeEn":"Экспресси́вный си́нтаксис: инве́рсия, парцелля́ция","intro":"Read these two aloud: «Я вчера́ ви́дел Ива́на» and «Ива́на я ви́дел вчера́». Same words, same grammar — but a Russian hears two different emphases, just from the <b>order</b>. English leans on the voice and on “it was…” frames; Russian moves the words instead. Today Эмма trades her editor's scalpel for a composer's baton: <b>инве́рсия</b> (inversion), <b>парцелля́ция</b> (chopping a sentence into punchy fragments), ellipsis, the rhetorical question — the toolkit of expressive syntax. Powerful, and easy to overdo.","introAudio":"Read these two aloud: 'Ya vchera videl Ivana' and 'Ivana ya videl vchera'. Same words, same grammar — but a Russian hears two different emphases, just from the order. English leans on the voice and on 'it was...' frames; Russian moves the words instead. Today Emma trades her editor's scalpel for a composer's baton: inversiya, inversion; partsellyatsiya, chopping a sentence into punchy fragments; ellipsis; the rhetorical question — the toolkit of expressive syntax. Powerful, and easy to overdo.","goals":["Put the key word where it lands hardest — Russian marks emphasis by <b>order</b>, not articles","Use <b>инве́рсия</b> (inversion) and <b>парцелля́ция</b> (fragmenting) for punch","Read a rhetorical question and an ellipsis (a deliberately dropped word) for effect","Dose these devices — overuse turns force into <b>графома́ния</b> (purple prose)"],"learned":["Placed the key word by moving it, not by stressing it","Built emphasis with inversion and parcellation","Recognised rhetorical questions and ellipsis as devices","Learned to use expressive syntax sparingly"],"review":{"intro":"A half-minute look back — an idiom's register, a device, and a clean B2 structure — then we make word order do the emphasising.","introAudio":"A half-minute look back — an idiom's register, a device, and a clean B2 structure — then we make word order do the emphasising.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer.","q":"«Бить баклу́ши» in a scientific article is…","opts":["perfectly fine","required at C1","a register mistake — it's colloquial"],"answer":2,"explain":"«Бить баклу́ши» is colloquial; in a scientific paper the neutral «безде́льничать» fits. Wrong register, not wrong grammar."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Кра́сной ни́тью» is a … idiom.","opts":["colloquial","bookish","low / rough"],"answer":1,"explain":"«Кра́сной ни́тью» — a theme running right through a text — is <b>bookish</b>, at home in essays and articles."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build (B2 concession): <b>“Although it was late, he stayed.”</b>","want":"Хотя́ бы́ло по́здно, он оста́лся","answer":"Хотя́ бы́ло по́здно, он оста́лся"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The end is the spotlight","title":"New information goes <em>last</em> — move a word to move the emphasis","paras":["Russian word order is famously “free,” but it isn't random — it's <b>meaningful</b>. In a neutral sentence the known part (the <b>те́ма</b>, theme) comes first and the new, important part (the <b>ре́ма</b>, rheme) comes <i>last</i>. The end of the sentence is the spotlight.","So «Вчера́ мне позвони́л <b>Ива́н</b>» answers “who called?” — Ива́н is new, so it lands last. «Ива́н позвони́л мне <b>вчера́</b>» answers “when?” — now <i>вчера́</i> is the point. Same words; the spotlight moved.","English usually keeps subject–verb–object fixed and marks emphasis with the voice or with “it was … who.” Russian just re-orders. Learn to hear where the spotlight falls, and you control the meaning."],"audio":"Russian word order is famously free, but it isn't random — it's meaningful. In a neutral sentence the known part, the tema or theme, comes first, and the new important part, the rema or rheme, comes last. The end of the sentence is the spotlight. So 'Vchera mne pozvonil Ivan' answers who called — Ivan is new, so it lands last. 'Ivan pozvonil mne vchera' answers when — now vchera is the point. Same words; the spotlight moved. English keeps subject-verb-object fixed and marks emphasis with the voice or with 'it was ... who'. Russian just re-orders. Learn to hear where the spotlight falls, and you control the meaning.","table":{"rows":[["Вчера́ мне позвони́л Ива́н. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"вчера мне позвонил иван\">🔊</button>","‘Who called?’ — Ива́н is the point (last)"],["Ива́н позвони́л мне вчера́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"иван позвонил мне вчера\">🔊</button>","‘When did he call?’ — вчера́ is the point (last)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"‘What did you buy?’ — a book (the new thing, last).","en":"— Что ты купи́л? — Я купи́л <b>кни́гу</b>.","say":"Что ты купил? Я купил книгу."},{"ru":"‘Who bought the book?’ — I did (the new thing, last).","en":"— Кто купи́л кни́гу? — Кни́гу купи́л <b>я</b>.","say":"Кто купил книгу? Книгу купил я."}],"mistakes":[],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"You want to stress WHEN Ivan called (yesterday). Which order?","opts":["Вчера́ позвони́л Ива́н.","Ива́н мне вчера́ позвони́л.","Ива́н позвони́л мне вчера́."],"answer":2,"explain":"The stressed word goes last, so “yesterday” lands at the end: «Ива́н позвони́л мне <b>вчера́</b>»."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Inversion","title":"<em>Инве́рсия</em>: fronting a word to hit it hard","paras":["<b>Инве́рсия</b> (inversion) is deliberately breaking the neutral order for effect. Front a word that would normally sit later, and it rings out: «<b>Тру́дным</b> был э́тот год» (What a hard year it was) hits harder than the neutral «Э́тот год был тру́дным».","Putting the adjective <i>after</i> its noun lifts the tone to something poetic or folkloric: «ночь <b>тёмная</b>», «путь <b>да́льний</b>» — you'll feel the fairy tale or the song. And opening with the predicate — «<b>Хорошо́</b> в лесу́ весно́й!» — colours the whole line with feeling.","Inversion is the written cousin of a raised voice. Used once, it lands; used in every sentence, it exhausts the reader."],"audio":"Inversiya, inversion, is deliberately breaking the neutral order for effect. Front a word that would normally sit later and it rings out: 'Trudnym byl etot god', what a hard year it was, hits harder than the neutral 'Etot god byl trudnym'. Putting the adjective after its noun lifts the tone to something poetic or folkloric: 'noch temnaya', 'put dalny' — you feel the fairy tale or the song. And opening with the predicate — 'Khorosho v lesu vesnoy' — colours the whole line with feeling. Inversion is the written cousin of a raised voice: used once it lands, used in every sentence it exhausts the reader.","examples":[{"ru":"Elevated: “A hard year that was.” (predicate fronted)","en":"<b>Тру́дным</b> был э́тот год.","say":"Трудным был этот год."},{"ru":"Folk-poetic tone: “the dark night has fallen” (adjective after noun)","en":"Опусти́лась ночь <b>тёмная</b>.","say":"Опустилась ночь тёмная."}],"mistakes":[],"tasks":[{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the inverted sentence. Stress marks are optional.","q":"Make it emphatic by fronting the predicate. Neutral: «Э́тот год был тру́дным». Type the inverted version, starting with «Тру́дным…»:","accept":["трудным был этот год"],"placeholder":"Тру́дным…","explain":"Fronting «тру́дным» throws all the weight onto the hardship — a textbook <b>инве́рсия</b>."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Parcellation & ellipsis","title":"<em>Парцелля́ция</em>: chop it for punch — and <em>ellipsis</em>: drop the obvious","paras":["<b>Парцелля́ция</b> (parcellation) snaps one sentence into separate pieces with full stops, so each fragment lands like a blow: «Он пришёл. По́здно. Оди́н.» Grammatically it's one thought; on the page it's three hammer-strikes.","<b>Э́ллипсис</b> (ellipsis) is the opposite move — leaving out a word everyone can supply, for speed and energy: «Я — домо́й, а он — на рабо́ту» (no verb needed). Both compress emotion into fewer words.","These are strong flavours. A parcelled line at the end of a paragraph is unforgettable; ten in a row read like a teenager's diary. Use them at the moment that matters."],"audio":"Partsellyatsiya, parcellation, snaps one sentence into separate pieces with full stops, so each fragment lands like a blow: 'On prishol. Pozdno. Odin.' Grammatically it's one thought; on the page it's three hammer-strikes. Ellipsis is the opposite move — leaving out a word everyone can supply, for speed and energy: 'Ya — domoy, a on — na rabotu', no verb needed. Both compress emotion into fewer words. These are strong flavours: a parcelled line at the end of a paragraph is unforgettable; ten in a row read like a teenager's diary. Use them at the moment that matters.","examples":[{"ru":"Parcellation: “He came. Late. Alone.”","en":"Он пришёл. <b>По́здно. Оди́н.</b>","say":"Он пришёл. Поздно. Один."},{"ru":"Ellipsis: “I'm off home, and he's off to work.”","en":"Я — <b>домо́й</b>, а он — <b>на рабо́ту</b>.","say":"Я домой, а он на работу."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я встал. У́тром. Ра́но. Вы́пил ко́фе. Чёрный. Горя́чий. Пошёл. На рабо́ту.","right":"У́тром я встал ра́но, вы́пил чёрного горя́чего ко́фе и пошёл на рабо́ту.","why":"Parcellation is a spice, not a diet. Chopping every phrase into a fragment is <b>графома́ния</b> (purple prose) — the plain version reads far better. Save the break for one line that must land."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Он пришёл. По́здно. Оди́н.» is an example of…","opts":["ellipsis","парцелля́ция (parcellation)","a rhetorical question"],"answer":1,"explain":"One thought chopped into stand-alone fragments is <b>парцелля́ция</b> — each piece hits separately."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Rhetorical questions & dosing","title":"The <em>rhetorical question</em> — and the golden rule: less is more","paras":["A <b>ритори́ческий вопро́с</b> (rhetorical question) isn't asking for an answer — it states something with force: «Кто из нас не мечта́л о свобо́де?» means <i>everyone did</i>. It pulls the reader in and is a favourite opener in the journalistic style.","All of today's devices — inversion, parcellation, ellipsis, the rhetorical question — are <b>экспресси́вный си́нтаксис</b> (expressive syntax). They exist to add force. Which is exactly why they must be rationed: force everywhere is force nowhere.","The native writer plants one device where it counts and leaves the rest of the paragraph calm. That contrast — calm around a single sharp move — is what makes the move hit. Correct <i>and</i> measured."],"audio":"A ritorichesky vopros, rhetorical question, isn't asking for an answer — it states something with force: 'Kto iz nas ne mechtal o svobode?' means everyone did. It pulls the reader in and is a favourite opener in the journalistic style. All of today's devices — inversion, parcellation, ellipsis, the rhetorical question — are ekspressivny sintaksis, expressive syntax. They exist to add force, which is exactly why they must be rationed: force everywhere is force nowhere. The native writer plants one device where it counts and leaves the rest of the paragraph calm. That contrast is what makes the move hit. Correct and measured.","examples":[{"ru":"Rhetorical opener: “Who among us hasn't dreamed of freedom?”","en":"<b>Кто из нас не мечта́л о свобо́де?</b>","say":"Кто из нас не мечтал о свободе?"},{"ru":"One sharp break in calm prose lands hardest.","en":"Го́род жил свое́й жи́знью. И вдруг — <b>тишина́</b>.","say":"Город жил своей жизнью. И вдруг — тишина."}],"mistakes":[],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Using inversion and parcellation in every single sentence produces…","opts":["strong, native prose","графома́нию (purple prose)","a scientific report"],"answer":1,"explain":"Overuse is <b>графома́ния</b>: the devices work by contrast with calm text, so a page of them cancels itself out."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Кто из нас не мечта́л о лу́чшем ми́ре?","sub":"a rhetorical question — lift the pitch, don't drop it like a real question","want":"кто из нас не мечтал о лучшем мире"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Эмма shows Ле́на a paragraph she has just rewritten. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Ты опя́ть переписа́ла оди́н абза́ц пять раз. Что с ним бы́ло не так?","ru":"You've rewritten one paragraph five times again. What was wrong with it?"},{"who":"A","en":"Слова́ те же, а зву́чит вя́ло. Смотри́: «Он ушёл ти́хо и незаме́тно».","ru":"Same words, but it sounds limp. Look: “He left quietly and unnoticed.”"},{"who":"B","en":"Норма́льное предложе́ние.","ru":"A perfectly normal sentence."},{"who":"A","en":"А тепе́рь: «Он ушёл. Ти́хо. Незаме́тно». Чу́вствуешь уда́р?","ru":"And now: “He left. Quietly. Unnoticed.” Feel the punch?"},{"who":"B","en":"Ого́. Как бу́дто ка́ждое сло́во бьёт отде́льно.","ru":"Whoa. As if each word hits on its own."},{"who":"A","en":"Э́то парцелля́ция. И ещё оди́н приём — сдвиг поря́дка: «Тру́дным был тот день».","ru":"That's parcellation. And one more device — shifting the order: “A hard day, that was.”"},{"who":"B","en":"Зву́чит как из рома́на.","ru":"Sounds like something out of a novel."},{"who":"A","en":"И́менно. Но е́сли так писа́ть ка́ждую фра́зу — вы́йдет не рома́н, а графома́ния. Приём рабо́тает то́лько в тишине́ вокру́г.","ru":"Exactly. But write every phrase like that and you get not a novel but purple prose. A device works only with quiet around it."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the machinery of emphasis. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"поря́док слов","ru":"word order","ex":"Поря́док слов меня́ет смысл."},{"en":"инве́рсия","ru":"inversion (marked word order)","ex":"Инве́рсия усили́вает сло́во."},{"en":"парцелля́ция","ru":"parcellation (chopping into fragments)","ex":"Парцелля́ция ре́жет фра́зу на куски́."},{"en":"э́ллипсис","ru":"ellipsis (a deliberately dropped word)","ex":"Э́ллипсис — про́пуск поня́тного сло́ва."},{"en":"ритори́ческий вопро́с","ru":"rhetorical question","ex":"Ритори́ческий вопро́с не ждёт отве́та."},{"en":"те́ма","ru":"theme (the known part)","ex":"Те́ма — то, что уже́ изве́стно."},{"en":"ре́ма","ru":"rheme (the new part)","ex":"Ре́ма — но́вое, и она́ в конце́."},{"en":"уда́рение","ru":"stress, emphasis (logical stress)","ex":"Логи́ческое уда́рение па́дает в коне́ц."},{"en":"подчеркну́ть","ru":"to emphasise, underline","ex":"Поря́док помога́ет подчеркну́ть гла́вное."},{"en":"приём","ru":"device, technique","ex":"Инве́рсия — стилисти́ческий приём."},{"en":"вырази́тельность","ru":"expressiveness","ex":"Э́ти приёмы даю́т вырази́тельность."},{"en":"графома́ния","ru":"purple prose, overwriting","ex":"Перебо́р приёмов — э́то графома́ния."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each line to the device it uses.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its device — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Он пришёл. По́здно. Оди́н.","parcellation"],["Тру́дным был э́тот год.","inversion"],["Я — домо́й, ты — на рабо́ту.","ellipsis"],["Кто не мечта́л об э́том?","a rhetorical question"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"In a neutral Russian sentence, the new / most important word goes…","opts":["first","in the middle","last"],"answer":2},{"q":"«Тру́дным был э́тот год» uses which device?","opts":["инве́рсия (inversion)","ellipsis","a proverb"],"answer":0},{"q":"«Он пришёл. По́здно. Оди́н.» is…","opts":["a rhetorical question","ellipsis","парцелля́ция (parcellation)"],"answer":2},{"q":"«Кто из нас не мечта́л о свобо́де?» is a…","opts":["ритори́ческий вопро́с (rhetorical question)","official cliché","proverb"],"answer":0},{"q":"Using inversion and parcellation in every sentence is…","opts":["ideal at C1","overuse — graphomania","impossible"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"A short note on emphasis in Эмма's editor voice — read it aloud and feel the order do the work.","title":"Поря́док, кото́рый бьёт","sentences":["В ру́сском предложе́нии ва́жное сло́во стреми́тся в коне́ц.","Хо́чешь уси́лить его́ — вы́неси вперёд: «Тру́дным был э́тот путь».","Хо́чешь уда́рить — разбе́й фра́зу: «Он верну́лся. По́здно. Наконе́ц».","Но си́ла приёма — в его́ ре́дкости, а не в частоте́.","Кто из нас не встреча́л те́кста, где ка́ждая стро́чка кричи́т — и оттого́ не слы́шно ничего́?"],"translation":"In a Russian sentence the important word gravitates to the end. Want to strengthen it — bring it forward: “A hard road that was.” Want to strike — break the phrase: “He came back. Late. At last.” But a device's power lies in its rarity, not its frequency. Which of us hasn't met a text where every line shouts — and so nothing can be heard?"},"essay":{"intro":"Give a flat line some muscle.","prompt":"Take a plain sentence (e.g. “He left quietly and unnoticed” / “It was a hard year”) and rewrite it in Russian using ONE expressive device — inversion <i>or</i> parcellation. Write the plain version and the punchy version, and name the device. 2–3 sentences.","hint":"Inversion: front the key word — «Тру́дным был год». Parcellation: chop it — «Он ушёл. Ти́хо. Совсе́м». One device is plenty 🙂","example":"Обы́чно: «Он ушёл ти́хо и незаме́тно». С парцелля́цией: «Он ушёл. Ти́хо. Незаме́тно». Оди́н приём — и фра́за бьёт."}},"12":{"day":12,"week":"02","themeRu":"Little words that colour speech","themeEn":"Части́цы и вво́дные: же, ведь, мол","intro":"«Я <b>же</b> тебе́ говори́л!» Cross out that little «<b>же</b>» and the sentence still parses — “I told you” — but something dies. Russian is full of these tiny, untranslatable words — <b>же, ведь, -то, ну, вот, мол</b> — that carry no dictionary meaning yet colour everything: impatience, shared knowledge, doubt, reproach. English has no «же». Skip them and your Russian is correct but <i>flat</i> and foreign; sprinkle them right and you finally sound native. This is the single biggest “sounds Russian” upgrade — so today, Эмма's spoken register.","introAudio":"'Ya zhe tebe govoril!' Cross out that little 'zhe' and the sentence still parses — I told you — but something dies. Russian is full of these tiny, untranslatable words — zhe, ved, -to, nu, vot, mol — that carry no dictionary meaning yet colour everything: impatience, shared knowledge, doubt, reproach. English has no 'zhe'. Skip them and your Russian is correct but flat and foreign; sprinkle them right and you finally sound native. This is the single biggest 'sounds Russian' upgrade — so today, Emma's spoken register.","goals":["Colour a sentence with modal particles: <b>же, ведь, -то, ну, вот</b>","Ask with attitude: <b>ра́зве, неуже́ли</b> (surprise) and soften with <b>-ка</b>","Report someone's words at a distance: <b>мол, де́скать</b> (supposedly he said)","Hedge and signpost with вво́дные words (<b>ка́жется, разуме́ется, по-ви́димому</b>)"],"learned":["Added modal particles for impatience, shared knowledge and emphasis","Used ра́зве / неуже́ли for surprise and -ка to soften a request","Marked reported speech with мол / де́скать","Hedged and signposted with parenthetical words"],"review":{"intro":"Thirty seconds on yesterday's expressive syntax and an idiom, then today's tiny, mighty words.","introAudio":"Thirty seconds on yesterday's expressive syntax and an idiom, then today's tiny, mighty words.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer.","q":"«Он пришёл. По́здно. Оди́н.» — which device is that?","opts":["ellipsis","inversion","парцелля́ция (parcellation)"],"answer":2,"explain":"One thought chopped into fragments is <b>парцелля́ция</b> — each piece lands like a separate blow."},{"type":"choice","q":"In neutral Russian, the new / stressed word goes…","opts":["last","first","anywhere at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"The rheme — the new, important word — lands at the end. Move it, and you move the emphasis."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the missing word.","q":"Colloquial idiom for “to idle / do nothing”: «бить …». Type the missing word:","accept":["баклуши"],"placeholder":"бить …","explain":"«Бить баклу́ши» — to twiddle your thumbs, loaf about. Colloquial."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Modal particles","title":"<em>Же, ведь, -то, ну, вот</em> — words with no meaning and all the feeling","paras":["Russian sprinkles tiny <b>части́цы</b> (particles) that you can't really translate — they don't add facts, they add <i>attitude</i>. <b>Же</b> presses: «Я <b>же</b> говори́л!» (I DID tell you — where were you?). «Что <b>же</b> де́лать?» (well what on earth is one to do?).","<b>Ведь</b> appeals to shared knowledge: «Ты <b>ведь</b> его́ зна́ешь» (you know him, after all). <b>-То</b> quietly singles a word out: «Я-<b>то</b> согла́сен» (I, for one, agree). <b>Ну</b> and <b>вот</b> open and point: «<b>Ну</b> что, пойдём?», «<b>Вот</b> э́то да!».","None of these has an English word — they're pure colour. Drop them and the grammar is perfect but the voice is dead. This is the number-one fix for “correct but foreign” Russian."],"audio":"Russian sprinkles tiny chastitsy, particles, that you can't really translate — they don't add facts, they add attitude. 'Zhe' presses: 'Ya zhe govoril', I DID tell you, where were you? 'Chto zhe delat', well what on earth is one to do? 'Ved' appeals to shared knowledge: 'Ty ved yego znayesh', you know him, after all. '-To' quietly singles a word out: 'Ya-to soglasen', I, for one, agree. 'Nu' and 'vot' open and point: 'Nu chto, poydyom?', 'Vot eto da!'. None of these has an English word — they're pure colour. Drop them and the grammar is perfect but the voice is dead. This is the number-one fix for correct but foreign Russian.","table":{"rows":[["Я же говори́л! <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"я же говорил\">🔊</button>","‘I DID tell you!’ — reproach / impatience"],["Ты ведь его́ зна́ешь. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ты ведь его знаешь\">🔊</button>","‘you know him, after all’ — shared knowledge"],["Я-то согла́сен. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"я то согласен\">🔊</button>","‘I, for one, agree’ — singling out"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"‘Come on, shall we go, then?’ (opening + softening)","en":"<b>Ну</b> что, пойдём, а?","say":"Ну что, пойдём, а?"},{"ru":"‘But you promised!’ (reproach with же)","en":"Ты <b>же</b> обеща́л!","say":"Ты же обещал!"}],"mistakes":[],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"«Я же говори́л!» — what does «же» add?","opts":["a real question","reproach: ‘I DID tell you’","nothing at all"],"answer":1,"explain":"«Же» presses the point — “I told you and you didn't listen.” Pure attitude, no new fact."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Asking with attitude","title":"<em>Ра́зве, неуже́ли, -ка</em>: surprise, disbelief, a gentle nudge","paras":["Some particles live in questions. <b>Ра́зве</b> and <b>неуже́ли</b> both signal surprise or disbelief — “really?”, “can it be?”: «<b>Ра́зве</b> ты не знал?» (you mean you didn't know?), «<b>Неуже́ли</b> уже́ по́лночь?» (surely it's not midnight already?). Неуже́ли is the more emotional of the two.","<b>-Ка</b> tacks onto a command and softens it into a friendly nudge: «Да́й-<b>ка</b> посмотрю́» (let me just take a look), «Погоди́-<b>ка</b>» (hold on a sec). It turns an order into a request between equals.","There's also bookish <b>ли</b> for yes-no and indirect questions: «Не зна́ю, придёт <b>ли</b> он» (I don't know whether he'll come). One shade up in register from the rest."],"audio":"Some particles live in questions. 'Razve' and 'neuzheli' both signal surprise or disbelief — really?, can it be? 'Razve ty ne znal?', you mean you didn't know?; 'Neuzheli uzhe polnoch?', surely it's not midnight already? Neuzheli is the more emotional of the two. '-Ka' tacks onto a command and softens it into a friendly nudge: 'Day-ka posmotryu', let me just take a look; 'Pogodi-ka', hold on a sec. It turns an order into a request between equals. There's also bookish 'li' for yes-no and indirect questions: 'Ne znayu, pridyot li on', I don't know whether he'll come. One shade up in register from the rest.","examples":[{"ru":"‘You mean you really didn't know?’ (disbelief)","en":"<b>Неуже́ли</b> ты не знал?","say":"Неужели ты не знал?"},{"ru":"‘Let me just have a look.’ (softened request)","en":"<b>Да́й-ка</b> посмотрю́.","say":"Дай-ка посмотрю."}],"mistakes":[],"tasks":[{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the particle only.","q":"Soften the command «Дай посмотре́ть» into a friendly nudge: «Да́й-… посмотре́ть». Type the particle that does it:","accept":["ка","-ка"],"placeholder":"-…","explain":"«-Ка» turns an order into a nudge between equals: «Да́й-ка посмотре́ть»."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Someone else's words","title":"<em>Мол, де́скать, я́кобы</em>: “supposedly he said…”","paras":["Here's a shade English has no single word for. <b>Мол</b> and <b>де́скать</b> flag that you're relaying <i>someone else's</i> words, often with a hint of doubt or irony: «Он отказа́лся: <b>мол</b>, вре́мени нет» (he refused — no time, supposedly). «Она́ говори́т, <b>де́скать</b>, всё в поря́дке» (she says, so she claims, everything's fine).","<b>Я́кобы</b> pushes the doubt further — “allegedly,” you don't believe it: «Он <b>я́кобы</b> боле́л» (he was “ill,” or so he says). These let you quote without endorsing — a reporter's, and an editor's, favourite tool.","And <b>аво́сь</b> — a word that <i>is</i> Russia — means acting on blind hope it'll work out: «Пое́ду без ка́рты, <b>аво́сь</b> не заблужу́сь» (I'll set off with no map and just hope for the best). All of these are conversational — never in a formal document."],"audio":"Here's a shade English has no single word for. 'Mol' and 'deskat' flag that you're relaying someone else's words, often with a hint of doubt or irony: 'On otkazalsya: mol, vremeni net', he refused — no time, supposedly. 'Ona govorit, deskat, vsyo v poryadke', she says, so she claims, everything's fine. 'Yakoby' pushes the doubt further — allegedly, you don't believe it: 'On yakoby bolel', he was ill, or so he says. These let you quote without endorsing — a reporter's, and an editor's, favourite tool. And 'avos', a word that is Russia, means acting on blind hope it'll work out: 'Poyedu bez karty, avos ne zabluzhus', I'll set off with no map and just hope for the best. All of these are conversational — never in a formal document.","examples":[{"ru":"‘He refused — no time, supposedly.’","en":"Он отказа́лся: <b>мол</b>, вре́мени нет.","say":"Он отказался: мол, времени нет."},{"ru":"‘He was “ill” — allegedly.’","en":"Он <b>я́кобы</b> боле́л.","say":"Он якобы болел."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"В официа́льном письме́: «Сообща́ю, что кли́ент, мол, не запла́тит».","right":"В официа́льном письме́: «Сообща́ю, что кли́ент отка́зывается плати́ть».","why":"«Мол» is conversational — it drags gossip into a formal letter. The official style states facts plainly: «отка́зывается плати́ть». Particles like мол / де́скать live in speech, not documents."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Он сказа́л, мол, за́нят» — what does «мол» signal?","opts":["you're quoting him — ‘so he says’","he is very busy","a polite request"],"answer":0,"explain":"«Мол» marks reported words — you're relaying his claim, often with a shade of doubt."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Parenthetical words","title":"<em>Вво́дные слова́</em>: hedge, signpost, judge","paras":["<b>Вво́дные слова́</b> (parentheticals) are words set off by commas that comment on the sentence rather than belong to it. They hedge — «<b>ка́жется</b>, он опа́здывает» (he's late, it seems), «<b>наве́рное</b>, да» (probably) — or assert — «<b>разуме́ется</b>» (of course), «<b>безусло́вно</b>» (undoubtedly), «<b>по-ви́димому</b>» (apparently).","They also signpost an argument: «<b>во-пе́рвых</b>… во-вторы́х…», «<b>сле́довательно</b>», «<b>наприме́р</b>», «<b>кста́ти</b>». Unlike the particles, many of these are neutral-to-bookish — «разуме́ется» and «по-ви́димому» are at home in an essay too.","Together, particles and parentheticals are the fine-tuning knobs of Russian: the difference between a robot's correct sentence and a person's living one. Use them, and Эмма's secret becomes yours — the language stops sounding translated."],"audio":"Vvodnye slova, parentheticals, are words set off by commas that comment on the sentence rather than belong to it. They hedge — 'kazhetsya, on opazdyvayet', he's late, it seems; 'navernoye, da', probably — or assert — 'razumeyetsya', of course; 'bezuslovno', undoubtedly; 'po-vidimomu', apparently. They also signpost an argument: 'vo-pervykh... vo-vtorykh', 'sledovatelno', 'naprimer', 'kstati'. Unlike the particles, many of these are neutral to bookish — 'razumeyetsya' and 'po-vidimomu' are at home in an essay too. Together, particles and parentheticals are the fine-tuning knobs of Russian: the difference between a robot's correct sentence and a person's living one. Use them, and Emma's secret becomes yours — the language stops sounding translated.","examples":[{"ru":"‘Of course, that's important.’ (asserting parenthetical)","en":"Э́то, <b>разуме́ется</b>, ва́жно.","say":"Это, разумеется, важно."},{"ru":"‘He'll be late, apparently.’ (hedging parenthetical)","en":"<b>По-ви́димому</b>, он опозда́ет.","say":"По-видимому, он опоздает."}],"mistakes":[],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which word is a <b>hedge</b> — “it seems, not sure”?","opts":["безусло́вно","ка́жется","разуме́ется"],"answer":1,"explain":"«Ка́жется» softens (it seems). «Безусло́вно» and «разуме́ется» do the opposite — they assert certainty."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Ты же обеща́л, ведь пра́вда?","sub":"spoken register — let же and ведь carry the gentle reproach","want":"ты же обещал, ведь правда"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Макси́м (Ива́н and Ле́на's brother, an engineer) drags Эмма into a chat about visiting his building site — spoken Russian, particles and all. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Ну что, Э́мма, ты же обеща́ла зайти́ ко мне на объе́кт.","ru":"So, Emma, you did promise to drop by my site."},{"who":"A","en":"Обеща́ла, обеща́ла. Про́сто рабо́ты-то сейча́с невпроворо́т.","ru":"I did, I did. It's just that there's a mountain of work right now."},{"who":"B","en":"Да ла́дно, всего́ час. Тебе́ ведь само́й интере́сно.","ru":"Oh come on, just an hour. You're curious yourself, after all."},{"who":"A","en":"Интере́сно, коне́чно. Неуже́ли вы уже́ мост доде́лали?","ru":"Curious, of course. You mean you've actually finished the bridge?"},{"who":"B","en":"Почти́. Нача́льник говори́т, оста́лось, мол, па́ру дней. А там кто его́ зна́ет.","ru":"Almost. The boss says a couple of days left, supposedly. But who knows, really."},{"who":"A","en":"Поня́тно. Ну дава́й так: за́втра зайду́, аво́сь вы́рвусь.","ru":"Got it. Well, let's do this: I'll come tomorrow, if I can manage to get away."},{"who":"B","en":"Вот и договори́лись. Заруби́ на носу́: в де́сять у́тра.","ru":"That's settled, then. Mark my words: ten in the morning."},{"who":"A","en":"Да по́мню я, по́мню. Ты-то сам не опа́здывай.","ru":"I remember, I remember. Just don't you be late yourself."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve tiny words that carry all the feeling. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"же","ru":"(emphatic particle: impatience, reproach)","ex":"Ты же зна́ешь его́!"},{"en":"ведь","ru":"after all, you know (shared knowledge)","ex":"Ведь я тебя́ предупрежда́л."},{"en":"-то","ru":"(particle that singles a word out)","ex":"Я-то не про́тив."},{"en":"ну","ru":"well, come on (opener)","ex":"Ну что, начина́ем?"},{"en":"вот","ru":"here, there (pointing)","ex":"Вот э́то настоя́щий друг!"},{"en":"ра́зве","ru":"really? (surprise in a question)","ex":"Ра́зве уже́ по́здно?"},{"en":"неуже́ли","ru":"surely not? can it be?","ex":"Неуже́ли ты забы́л?"},{"en":"-ка","ru":"(softens a command into a nudge)","ex":"Погоди́-ка мину́тку."},{"en":"мол","ru":"supposedly (he said)","ex":"Он, мол, за́нят."},{"en":"де́скать","ru":"supposedly, as if to say","ex":"Молчи́т, де́скать, не зна́ю."},{"en":"аво́сь","ru":"on blind hope it works out","ex":"Пойдём без зонта́, аво́сь не промо́кнем."},{"en":"разуме́ется","ru":"of course, needless to say","ex":"Разуме́ется, я согла́сна."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each little word to the feeling it adds.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its job — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Я же говори́л!","reproach — ‘I DID tell you’"],["Ты ведь его́ зна́ешь.","shared knowledge — ‘after all’"],["Он, мол, за́нят.","reported words — ‘supposedly’"],["Да́й-ка посмотрю́.","a softened request"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"«Я же говори́л!» — «же» adds…","opts":["a factual detail","reproach / emphasis","politeness"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Мол» and «де́скать» mark…","opts":["someone else's reported words","a strong command","the future tense"],"answer":0},{"q":"Dropping particles like же and ведь makes correct Russian sound…","opts":["flat and foreign","overly emotional","rude"],"answer":0},{"q":"Which word does NOT belong in a formal document?","opts":["сле́довательно","мол","в соотве́тствии с"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Да́й-ка посмотре́ть» — the «-ка»…","opts":["makes it a real question","softens the command","adds doubt"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"The same line, with and without its little words — read each aloud and hear a person appear.","title":"Ма́ленькие слова́ — больша́я ра́зница","sentences":["Смотри́: «Я тебе́ говори́л» — су́хо и ро́вно.","А тепе́рь: «Я же тебе́ говори́л!» — и сра́зу слы́шно живо́го челове́ка.","«Ты ведь придёшь?» — здесь «ведь» напомина́ет о договорённости.","«Он, мол, за́нят» — а са́ми-то мы не о́чень ве́рим.","Э́ти слова́ невозмо́жно перевести́ на англи́йский одни́м сло́вом.","Но без них ру́сская речь пра́вильная, да мёртвая."],"translation":"Look: “I told you” — dry and level. Now: “But I DID tell you!” — and at once you hear a living person. “You're coming, though, right?” — here ведь reminds of an agreement. “He's busy, supposedly” — and we ourselves don't quite believe it. These words can't be rendered into English with a single word. But without them, Russian speech is correct — yet dead."},"essay":{"intro":"Bring a flat line to life.","prompt":"Write a short spoken exchange in Russian (friends or family). First write it <i>without</i> any particles, then rewrite it <i>with</i> «же», «ведь», «-то» or «мол» so it sounds native. Show both. 2–4 short lines.","hint":"Flat: «Ты обеща́л прийти́». Alive: «Ты же обеща́л прийти́!» Add ведь for shared knowledge, мол to quote someone 🙂","example":"Пло́ско: «Ты не позвони́л». Живо́: «Ты же обеща́л позвони́ть! Я ведь ждала́». — «Да за́нят был, мол, пото́м, а вре́мя-то и ушло́»."}},"13":{"day":13,"week":"02","themeRu":"Holding an argument together","themeEn":"Свя́зность те́кста и абза́ца","intro":"Yesterday's little particles gave Эмма's <i>speech</i> its colour. Today we build the backbone of her <i>writing</i>. Beginners list — “It's cheap. It's fast. It's good.” — three bricks in a heap. A C1 writer <b>binds</b> them: “It's not only cheap but fast; moreover, it's reliable — and therefore worth it.” That web of <b>connectors</b> (тем не ме́нее, с одно́й стороны́, сле́довательно) and the paragraph shape <i>thesis → development → conclusion</i> are what carry a real argument. This is the spine of every C1 essay and spoken рассужде́ние.","introAudio":"Yesterday's little particles gave Emma's speech its colour. Today we build the backbone of her writing. Beginners list — it's cheap, it's fast, it's good — three bricks in a heap. A C1 writer binds them: it's not only cheap but fast; moreover, it's reliable — and therefore worth it. That web of connectors — tem ne meneye, s odnoy storony, sledovatelno — and the paragraph shape thesis, development, conclusion are what carry a real argument. This is the spine of every C1 essay and spoken rassuzhdeniye.","goals":["Reach for the right <b>connector</b>: contrast, addition, cause, topic-shift","Frame two sides: <b>с одно́й стороны́… с друго́й стороны́</b>","Shape a paragraph: <b>те́зис → разви́тие → вы́вод</b> (thesis → development → conclusion)","Bind sentences into an argument instead of <b>listing</b> them"],"learned":["Sorted connectors by function (contrast / addition / cause / topic)","Built a balanced two-sided paragraph","Structured a paragraph thesis → development → conclusion","Replaced flat listing with argumentative cohesion"],"review":{"intro":"A quick backward glance — a particle, a device, a parenthetical — before we build the paragraph's backbone.","introAudio":"A quick backward glance — a particle, a device, a parenthetical — before we build the paragraph's backbone.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer.","q":"«Мол» in «Он, мол, за́нят» signals…","opts":["a command","reported words — ‘supposedly’","the future tense"],"answer":1,"explain":"«Мол» marks that you're relaying someone else's words, often with a shade of doubt."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Тру́дным был э́тот год» is an example of…","opts":["parcellation","inversion","ellipsis"],"answer":1,"explain":"Fronting the predicate «тру́дным» is <b>инве́рсия</b> — marked order for emphasis."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word.","q":"The parenthetical that means “of course / needless to say.” Type it:","accept":["разумеется"],"placeholder":"разуме́ется…","explain":"«Разуме́ется» — a parenthetical asserting certainty (of course)."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The connector toolbox","title":"Four jobs, one <em>connector</em> for each","paras":["<b>Свя́зность</b> (cohesion) is what turns a pile of sentences into a text. The tools are <b>сре́дства свя́зи</b> — connectors — and they sort by job. <b>Contrast:</b> «<b>одна́ко</b>», «<b>тем не ме́нее</b>» (nevertheless), «<b>напро́тив</b>» (on the contrary), «<b>всё же</b>» (still).","<b>Addition:</b> «<b>кро́ме того́</b>» (moreover), «<b>бо́лее того́</b>» (what's more), «<b>к тому́ же</b>» (besides). <b>Cause &amp; result:</b> «<b>поэ́тому</b>» (so), «<b>сле́довательно</b>» (consequently), «<b>таки́м о́бразом</b>» (thus). <b>Topic-shift:</b> «<b>что каса́ется…</b>» (as for…), «<b>что до…</b>».","These are mostly neutral-to-bookish — the natural mortar of an essay. Pick the connector whose <i>job</i> matches the join you need, and the reader is never lost."],"audio":"Svyaznost, cohesion, is what turns a pile of sentences into a text. The tools are sredstva svyazi, connectors, and they sort by job. Contrast: 'odnako', 'tem ne meneye', nevertheless; 'naprotiv', on the contrary; 'vsyo zhe', still. Addition: 'krome togo', moreover; 'boleye togo', what's more; 'k tomu zhe', besides. Cause and result: 'poetomu', so; 'sledovatelno', consequently; 'takim obrazom', thus. Topic-shift: 'chto kasayetsya', as for; 'chto do'. These are mostly neutral to bookish — the natural mortar of an essay. Pick the connector whose job matches the join you need, and the reader is never lost.","table":{"rows":[["тем не ме́нее <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"тем не менее\">🔊</button>","contrast — nevertheless"],["кро́ме того́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"кроме того\">🔊</button>","addition — moreover"],["сле́довательно <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"следовательно\">🔊</button>","result — consequently"],["что каса́ется… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"что касается\">🔊</button>","topic — as for…"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"‘The task is hard. Nevertheless, a solution exists.’","en":"Зада́ча сло́жная. <b>Тем не ме́нее</b>, реше́ние есть.","say":"Задача сложная. Тем не менее, решение есть."},{"ru":"‘The data held up; consequently, the hypothesis is confirmed.’","en":"Да́нные подтверди́лись; <b>сле́довательно</b>, гипо́теза верна́.","say":"Данные подтвердились; следовательно, гипотеза верна."}],"mistakes":[],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"You need to signal a RESULT (‘and therefore…’). Which connector?","opts":["что каса́ется","напро́тив","сле́довательно"],"answer":2,"explain":"«Сле́довательно» marks a conclusion / result. «Что каса́ется» shifts topic; «напро́тив» contrasts."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Weighing two sides","title":"<em>С одно́й стороны́… с друго́й стороны́…</em>","paras":["A grown-up argument weighs both sides, and Russian has a fixed frame for it: «<b>С одно́й стороны́</b>, … <b>с друго́й стороны́</b>, …» (on one hand… on the other…). The second half is <i>expected</i> — open the frame and the reader waits for the balance.","Its partners: «<b>не то́лько</b>… <b>но и</b>…» (not only… but also…), «<b>несмотря́ на</b>…» (despite…), «<b>хотя́</b>…, всё же…» (although…, still…). These bind two ideas into one weighed thought instead of two flat statements.","This is exactly where English speakers tend to <i>list</i> — “It's good. But it's expensive.” — where Russian argument wants them fused: «<b>Хотя́</b> э́то до́рого, оно́ того́ сто́ит»."],"audio":"A grown-up argument weighs both sides, and Russian has a fixed frame for it: 'S odnoy storony, ... s drugoy storony, ...', on one hand... on the other... The second half is expected — open the frame and the reader waits for the balance. Its partners: 'ne tolko... no i...', not only... but also; 'nesmotrya na...', despite; 'khotya..., vsyo zhe...', although..., still. These bind two ideas into one weighed thought instead of two flat statements. This is exactly where English speakers tend to list — it's good, but it's expensive — where Russian argument wants them fused: 'Khotya eto dorogo, ono togo stoit', although it's dear, it's worth it.","examples":[{"ru":"‘On one hand it's convenient; on the other, expensive.’","en":"<b>С одно́й стороны́</b>, э́то удо́бно; <b>с друго́й</b> — до́рого.","say":"С одной стороны, это удобно; с другой — дорого."},{"ru":"‘It's not only cheap but also reliable.’","en":"Э́то <b>не то́лько</b> дёшево, <b>но и</b> надёжно.","say":"Это не только дёшево, но и надёжно."}],"mistakes":[],"tasks":[{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the missing noun. Stress marks are optional.","q":"Open the two-sided frame: «С одно́й …, а с друго́й …». Type the noun that fills both blanks:","accept":["стороны"],"placeholder":"с одно́й …","explain":"«С одно́й <b>стороны́</b>… с друго́й <b>стороны́</b>…» — the balance frame that weighs two sides."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The shape of a paragraph","title":"<em>Те́зис → разви́тие → вы́вод</em>","paras":["A C1 paragraph (<b>абза́ц</b>) has a shape. It opens with a <b>те́зис</b> (a claim): «<b>Сле́дует отме́тить, что</b>…», «<b>Изве́стно, что</b>…». Then comes <b>разви́тие</b> (development) — reasons and examples, chained with the connectors from Rule 1.","It closes with a <b>вы́вод</b> (conclusion): «<b>Таки́м о́бразом</b>,…», «<b>Сле́довательно</b>,…», «<b>В заключе́ние</b>,…». Inside, you signpost the steps: «<b>во-пе́рвых</b>… во-вторы́х… наконе́ц…».","That arc — claim, support, conclusion — is what a marker (and a listener) looks for in the C1 essay and the spoken рассужде́ние. A paragraph without it reads as notes, not argument."],"audio":"A C1 paragraph, abzats, has a shape. It opens with a tezis, a claim: 'Sleduyet otmetit, chto...', 'Izvestno, chto...'. Then comes razvitiye, development — reasons and examples, chained with the connectors from Rule 1. It closes with a vyvod, a conclusion: 'Takim obrazom', 'Sledovatelno', 'V zaklyucheniye'. Inside, you signpost the steps: 'vo-pervykh... vo-vtorykh... nakonets'. That arc — claim, support, conclusion — is what a marker and a listener look for in the C1 essay and the spoken rassuzhdeniye. A paragraph without it reads as notes, not argument.","examples":[{"ru":"Thesis opener: ‘It should be noted that the question is complex.’","en":"<b>Сле́дует отме́тить, что</b> вопро́с непро́ст.","say":"Следует отметить, что вопрос непрост."},{"ru":"Conclusion: ‘Thus, the answer is clear.’","en":"<b>Таки́м о́бразом</b>, отве́т очеви́ден.","say":"Таким образом, ответ очевиден."}],"mistakes":[],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which phrase opens a paragraph's CONCLUSION?","opts":["Сле́дует отме́тить, что…","Таки́м о́бразом,…","С одно́й стороны́,…"],"answer":1,"explain":"«Таки́м о́бразом» sums up and closes. «Сле́дует отме́тить» opens a claim; «с одно́й стороны́» opens a two-sided weighing."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Bind, don't list","title":"The C1 spine: <em>sustain</em> the argument, don't stack it","paras":["Here's the signature English-speaker habit at C1: <b>listing</b> where the language wants <b>cohesion</b>. Three short sentences in a row — «Э́то удо́бно. Э́то бы́стро. Э́то надёжно.» — are grammatically fine but read as a shopping list, not a thought.","Bind them: «Э́то <b>не то́лько</b> удо́бно, <b>но и</b> бы́стро; <b>кро́ме того́</b>, надёжно, <b>а зна́чит</b>, того́ сто́ит.» One sustained sentence, four ideas, a clear line of reasoning. That's the C1 <b>пери́од</b> — the long, controlled sentence.","So the whole week comes together here: hold one register, place a device or idiom with intent, colour speech with particles — and, in writing, <i>bind</i> your sentences into an argument. Cohesion is the backbone the essay and the monologue stand on."],"audio":"Here's the signature English-speaker habit at C1: listing where the language wants cohesion. Three short sentences in a row — 'Eto udobno. Eto bystro. Eto nadyozhno.' — are grammatically fine but read as a shopping list, not a thought. Bind them: 'Eto ne tolko udobno, no i bystro; krome togo, nadyozhno, a znachit, togo stoit.' One sustained sentence, four ideas, a clear line of reasoning. That's the C1 period — the long, controlled sentence. So the whole week comes together here: hold one register, place a device or idiom with intent, colour speech with particles — and, in writing, bind your sentences into an argument. Cohesion is the backbone the essay and the monologue stand on.","examples":[{"ru":"Listing (flat): ‘It's convenient. It's fast. It's reliable.’","en":"Пло́ско: «Э́то удо́бно. Э́то бы́стро. Э́то надёжно».","say":"Плоско: это удобно. Это быстро. Это надёжно."},{"ru":"Bound (an argument): ‘Not only convenient but fast, and moreover reliable.’","en":"Свя́зно: «Э́то не то́лько удо́бно, но и бы́стро, а кро́ме того́ надёжно».","say":"Связно: это не только удобно, но и быстро, а кроме того надёжно."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"В эссе́: «Кни́га интере́сная. Она́ поле́зная. Её сто́ит прочита́ть».","right":"В эссе́: «Кни́га не то́лько интере́сная, но и поле́зная, а сле́довательно, её сто́ит прочита́ть».","why":"Three loose sentences read as a list, not an argument. Binding them with «не то́лько… но и…» and «сле́довательно» builds the cohesion a C1 essay needs."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"The C1 trap in argument is to…","opts":["bind sentences with connectors","list short sentences instead of binding them","use too many idioms"],"answer":1,"explain":"English speakers list; Russian argument wants the sentences bound into one sustained, cohesive period."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"С одно́й стороны́, э́то ри́скованно; с друго́й — необходи́мо.","sub":"read it as one weighed thought, a slight pause at the semicolon","want":"с одной стороны, это рискованно; с другой — необходимо"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Ка́тя (Ива́н's friend) asks Эмма to look over an essay. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Э́мма, посмо́тришь моё эссе́? Ка́жется, чего́-то не хвата́ет.","ru":"Emma, will you look at my essay? Something seems to be missing."},{"who":"A","en":"Мы́сли хоро́шие, но они́ про́сто сва́лены в ку́чу. Смотри́: три коро́тких предложе́ния подря́д.","ru":"The ideas are good, but they're just dumped in a heap. Look: three short sentences in a row."},{"who":"B","en":"И что де́лать? По смы́слу же всё ве́рно.","ru":"And what should I do? In terms of meaning it's all correct."},{"who":"A","en":"Свяжи́ их. Не «удо́бно, бы́стро, надёжно», а «не то́лько удо́бно, но и бы́стро, а сле́довательно — надёжно».","ru":"Bind them. Not “convenient, fast, reliable,” but “not only convenient but fast, and therefore reliable.”"},{"who":"B","en":"А, то есть ну́жен ка́ркас: те́зис, до́воды, вы́вод?","ru":"Ah, so I need a frame: thesis, arguments, conclusion?"},{"who":"A","en":"И́менно. И свя́зки: «с одно́й стороны́», «тем не ме́нее», «таки́м о́бразом».","ru":"Exactly. And connectors: “on one hand,” “nevertheless,” “thus.”"},{"who":"B","en":"Тепе́рь абза́ц зазвуча́л как рассужде́ние, а не как спи́сок.","ru":"Now the paragraph sounds like reasoning, not a list."},{"who":"A","en":"Вот э́то и есть письмо́ у́ровня C1: не перечисля́й — веди́ мысль.","ru":"That's exactly what C1-level writing is: don't list — lead the thought."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for holding a paragraph together. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"свя́зность","ru":"cohesion, connectedness","ex":"Свя́зность де́лает те́кст те́кстом."},{"en":"абза́ц","ru":"paragraph","ex":"Ка́ждый абза́ц — одна́ мысль."},{"en":"те́зис","ru":"thesis, main claim","ex":"Начни́ с чёткого те́зиса."},{"en":"до́вод","ru":"argument, reason","ex":"Ка́ждый до́вод подкрепи́ приме́ром."},{"en":"вы́вод","ru":"conclusion","ex":"В конце́ — я́сный вы́вод."},{"en":"тем не ме́нее","ru":"nevertheless (contrast)","ex":"Тру́дно; тем не ме́нее, возмо́жно."},{"en":"сле́довательно","ru":"consequently, therefore (result)","ex":"Фа́кты ве́рны, сле́довательно, вы́вод то́же."},{"en":"что каса́ется","ru":"as for, as regards (topic-shift)","ex":"Что каса́ется це́ны, она́ вы́росла."},{"en":"с одно́й стороны́","ru":"on one hand","ex":"С одно́й стороны́, э́то удо́бно."},{"en":"кро́ме того́","ru":"moreover, besides (addition)","ex":"Он у́мный; кро́ме того́, до́брый."},{"en":"таки́м о́бразом","ru":"thus, in this way (conclusion)","ex":"Таки́м о́бразом, те́зис дока́зан."},{"en":"напро́тив","ru":"on the contrary","ex":"Он не уста́л; напро́тив, по́лон сил."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each connector to the job it does.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its function — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["тем не ме́нее","contrast — nevertheless"],["сле́довательно","result — therefore"],["кро́ме того́","addition — moreover"],["что каса́ется…","topic-shift — as for…"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"To signal a RESULT or conclusion, you use…","opts":["сле́довательно / таки́м о́бразом","с одно́й стороны́","напро́тив"],"answer":0},{"q":"«С одно́й стороны́…» should be followed by…","opts":["«…и на э́том всё»","«наприме́р…»","«с друго́й стороны́…»"],"answer":2},{"q":"A C1 paragraph runs…","opts":["thesis → development → conclusion","conclusion → thesis → nothing","a random list"],"answer":0},{"q":"«Тем не ме́нее» signals…","opts":["addition","a result","contrast (nevertheless)"],"answer":2},{"q":"The English-speaker's cohesion trap is to…","opts":["bind everything too tightly","list short sentences instead of binding them","overuse particles"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"One paragraph that holds together — thesis, development, conclusion. Read it aloud and follow the connectors.","title":"Абза́ц, кото́рый де́ржится","sentences":["Чте́ние на ру́сском ка́жется тру́дным, и в э́том есть до́ля пра́вды.","С одно́й стороны́, слова́ незнако́мы, а грамма́тика сложна́.","Одна́ко, с друго́й стороны́, ка́ждая страни́ца де́лает сле́дующую ле́гче.","Кро́ме того́, чте́ние даёт то, чего́ не даст ни оди́н уче́бник, — живо́й стиль.","Сле́довательно, броса́ть не сто́ит: тру́дность здесь — не стена́, а ступе́нька.","Таки́м о́бразом, упо́рство ва́жнее тала́нта."],"translation":"Reading in Russian seems hard, and there's a grain of truth in that. On one hand, the words are unfamiliar and the grammar is complex. On the other hand, however, each page makes the next one easier. What's more, reading gives what no textbook can — a living style. Consequently, it's not worth giving up: the difficulty here is not a wall but a step. Thus, persistence matters more than talent."},"essay":{"intro":"Build one paragraph that holds together.","prompt":"Write ONE cohesive paragraph in Russian (4–5 sentences) arguing for or against something simple (learning a language, living in a big city, working from home). Open with a <b>те́зис</b>, develop it with at least two connectors (<b>с одно́й стороны́ / тем не ме́нее / кро́ме того́</b>), and close with a <b>вы́вод</b> (<b>сле́довательно / таки́м о́бразом</b>). Don't list — bind.","hint":"Skeleton: «Сле́дует отме́тить, что… С одно́й стороны́,… Тем не ме́нее,… Кро́ме того́,… Таки́м о́бразом,…». Hold one register throughout 🙂","example":"Жить в большо́м го́роде непро́сто. С одно́й стороны́, здесь шум и спе́шка. Тем не ме́нее, и́менно го́род даёт рабо́ту и во́зможности. Кро́ме того́, здесь всегда́ есть чему́ учи́ться. Сле́довательно, тру́дности того́ сто́ят."}},"14":{"day":14,"week":"02","themeRu":"Review 2","themeEn":"Повторе́ние 2","intro":"Seven days, and Эмма now writes the way a Moscow columnist does — a worn cliché struck against a bright image, a «же» dropped exactly where a native would, a paragraph that pulls its own weight. Nothing new today. We gather week two into one place: the press voice, the quotations everyone recognises, idioms that carry their own register, the punch of a broken-up line, and the little words that make Russian sound Russian.","introAudio":"Seven days, and Emma now writes the way a Moscow columnist does — a worn cliché struck against a bright image, a 'zhe' dropped exactly where a native would, a paragraph that pulls its own weight. Nothing new today. We gather week two into one place: the press voice, the quotations everyone recognises, idioms that carry their own register, the punch of a broken-up line, and the little words that make Russian sound Russian.","goals":["Blend the <b>journalistic</b> voice: standard cliché + a vivid, evaluative image","Catch a <b>precedent text</b> — a quotation reshaped in a headline (<b>Быть и́ли не быть</b>, <b>А во́з и ны́не там</b>)","Pick an idiom by <b>register</b>: bookish <b>ка́нуть в Ле́ту</b> vs colloquial <b>бить баклу́ши</b>","Colour a line with <b>particles</b> (же, ведь, -то, мол) and hold a paragraph together with connectors"],"learned":["Blended the journalistic voice: standard cliché + a vivid, evaluative image","Caught precedent texts reshaped in headlines (Быть и́ли не быть, А во́з и ны́не там)","Picked idioms by register: bookish ка́нуть в Ле́ту vs colloquial бить баклу́ши","Coloured lines with particles (же, ведь, -то, мол) and stitched paragraphs with connectors"],"review":{"intro":"Straight into it — a quick pull from across the week. The press voice, a quotation everyone knows, an idiom with a register, a particle, a connector. You built all of this.","introAudio":"Straight into it — a quick pull from across the week. The press voice, a quotation everyone knows, an idiom with a register, a particle, a connector. You built all of this.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. It's all week-2 material.","q":"A column opens «Как изве́стно, …» and then paints a vivid image. Which style is that?","opts":["нау́чный","публицисти́ческий","официа́льно-делово́й"],"answer":1,"explain":"Standard cliché (как изве́стно) welded to a vivid, evaluative image → the <b>публицисти́ческий</b> (journalistic) style."},{"type":"choice","q":"A headline «Быть и́ли не быть» is a nod to…","opts":["a government decree","Shakespeare's Hamlet","a scientific abstract"],"answer":1,"explain":"It's a <b>precedent text</b> — everyone recognises Hamlet. Miss the quotation and the headline's wit is invisible."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“On one hand there's risk, on the other there's no choice.”</b>","want":"С одно́й стороны́ риск, с друго́й вы́бора нет","answer":"С одно́й стороны́ риск, с друго́й вы́бора нет"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the particle. Stress marks are optional.","q":"Add the particle for “after all / you know”: «Ты … по́мнишь наш разгово́р?»","accept":["ведь"],"placeholder":"Ты … по́мнишь…","explain":"<b>ведь</b> = “after all / you know” — it leans on shared knowledge. Drop it and the line goes flat."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which idiom is <b>colloquial</b> (fine with friends, wrong in a paper)?","opts":["ка́нуть в Ле́ту","бить баклу́ши","приня́ть реше́ние"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>бить баклу́ши</b> (“to idle”) is colloquial. ка́нуть в Ле́ту is bookish; приня́ть реше́ние is neutral."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The press voice & the quotation behind it","title":"<em>Публицисти́ческий</em> style: standard struck against spark","paras":["The journalistic style (публицисти́ческий) is a deliberate alloy: worn, reassuring <b>clichés</b> (как изве́стно, нельзя́ не согласи́ться, о́стрый вопро́с) welded to a <b>vivid, evaluative</b> image. It informs and persuades at once — never the dry impersonality of science, never the fixed formulas of a document.","And it leans on <b>precedent texts</b> (прецеде́нтные те́ксты) — lines every Russian carries: from Hamlet «Быть и́ли не быть», from Krylov «А во́з и ны́не там» (“and the cart is still there” = nothing has moved). A headline often bends one of these, and if you miss the quotation, the joke stays invisible even when every word is clear.","So the press asks two things at once: hear the <b>register</b> — expressive but public, not chummy — and catch the <b>allusion</b> hiding in a title."],"audio":"The journalistic style is a deliberate alloy: worn, reassuring clichés — kak izvestno, nelzya ne soglasitsya, ostry vopros — welded to a vivid, evaluative image. It informs and persuades at once, never the dry impersonality of science, never the fixed formulas of a document. And it leans on precedent texts, lines every Russian carries: from Hamlet, byt ili ne byt; from Krylov, a voz i nyne tam — 'and the cart is still there', meaning nothing has moved. A headline often bends one of these, and if you miss the quotation the joke stays invisible even when every word is clear. So the press asks two things at once: hear the register, expressive but public, not chummy; and catch the allusion hiding in a title.","table":{"rows":[["Как изве́стно… + я́ркий о́браз <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"как известно, яркий образ\">🔊</button>","journalistic blend: cliché + vivid image"],["Быть и́ли не быть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"быть или не быть\">🔊</button>","precedent text (Hamlet) — endlessly reshaped"],["А во́з и ны́не там <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"а воз и ныне там\">🔊</button>","precedent text (Krylov) — “nothing has moved”"],["о́стрый вопро́с, нельзя́ не согласи́ться <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"острый вопрос, нельзя не согласиться\">🔊</button>","evaluative press clichés"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"A hooking headline: “To build or not to build — that is the question for the city centre.”","en":"Заголо́вок-крючо́к: «<b>Стро́ить и́ли не стро́ить</b> — вот в чём вопро́с для це́нтра го́рода».","say":"Заголовок-крючок: строить или не строить — вот в чём вопрос для центра города."},{"ru":"The press blend: “As is known, the problem hasn't budged — the cart is still there.”","en":"Публицисти́ческий сплав: «Как изве́стно, пробле́ма не сдви́нулась — а во́з и ны́не там».","say":"Публицистический сплав: как известно, проблема не сдвинулась — а воз и ныне там."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"В коло́нке: «Учёные установи́ли зави́симость. Кла́ссно, да?»","right":"В коло́нке: «Учёные установи́ли ва́жную зави́симость — и э́того нельзя́ переоцени́ть».","why":"«Кла́ссно, да?» is chatty (разг.). The journalistic register is expressive but <b>public</b> — reach for the evaluation the press uses (нельзя́ переоцени́ть), not chat."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"Which line is the <b>journalistic</b> blend (standard + spark)?","opts":["Настоя́щим уведомля́ю о нача́ле рабо́т.","Как изве́стно, доро́гу обеща́ют уже́ год — а во́з и ны́не там.","Иссле́дование выявля́ет усто́йчивую корреля́цию."],"answer":1,"explain":"Cliché (как изве́стно) + evaluation + a precedent text (а во́з и ны́не там) = the press voice. The others are official and scientific."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the missing English word.","q":"A quotation everyone recognises, reshaped in a headline, is a … text. Type the word:","accept":["precedent","precedent text"],"placeholder":"a … text","explain":"A <b>precedent</b> text (прецеде́нтный) — the shared cultural quotation the press plays with."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Idioms and particles both carry register","title":"<em>Ка́нуть в Ле́ту</em> vs <em>бить баклу́ши</em> — and же, ведь, мол","paras":["An idiom is never register-neutral. <b>Ка́нуть в Ле́ту</b> (“to sink into the Lethe” = vanish without trace) is bookish; <b>бить баклу́ши</b> (“to idle”) is colloquial; and there's plainer просторе́чие below that. A bookish idiom in a chat, or a colloquial one in a paper, is a style error even when the phrase itself is correct — and never translate an English idiom word for word.","Then the <b>particles</b> English simply doesn't have: <b>же</b> (contrast, insistence — «Я же говори́л!»), <b>ведь</b> (“after all, you know” — «Ты ведь по́мнишь?»), <b>-то</b> (soft focus — «Я-то зна́ю»), <b>мол</b> / <b>де́скать</b> (reported, sceptical speech — «он, мол, за́нят»). Drop them and the grammar is flawless but the line is flat and foreign.","Register lives in your idioms; life lives in your particles. C1 speech needs both — each chosen for the world you're in."],"audio":"An idiom is never register-neutral. Kanut v Letu — to sink into the Lethe, to vanish without trace — is bookish; bit baklushi — to idle — is colloquial; and there's plainer prostorechie below that. A bookish idiom in a chat, or a colloquial one in a paper, is a style error even when the phrase itself is correct — and never translate an English idiom word for word. Then the particles English simply doesn't have: zhe, contrast and insistence, ya zhe govoril; ved, after all, you know, ty ved pomnish; -to, soft focus, ya-to znayu; mol and deskat, reported and sceptical speech, on, mol, zanyat. Drop them and the grammar is flawless but the line is flat and foreign. Register lives in your idioms; life lives in your particles. C1 speech needs both, each chosen for the world you're in.","table":{"rows":[["ка́нуть в Ле́ту <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"кануть в Лету\">🔊</button>","bookish: to vanish without trace"],["бить баклу́ши <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"бить баклуши\">🔊</button>","colloquial: to idle, waste time"],["же / ведь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"же, ведь\">🔊</button>","insistence / “after all, you know”"],["мол, де́скать <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"мол, дескать\">🔊</button>","“he says / supposedly” — reported speech"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Bookish, in an essay: “Those debates have long sunk into oblivion.”","en":"Кни́жно, в эссе́: «Те спо́ры давно́ <b>ка́нули в Ле́ту</b>».","say":"Книжно, в эссе: те споры давно канули в Лету."},{"ru":"Colloquial, to a friend: “Stop idling — you promised, you know!”","en":"Разгово́рно, дру́гу: «Хва́тит <b>бить баклу́ши</b> — ты <b>ведь</b> обеща́л!»","say":"Разговорно, другу: хватит бить баклуши — ты ведь обещал!"},{"ru":"Reported, sceptical: “He called — busy, supposedly.”","en":"Чужа́я речь: «Он звони́л — за́нят, <b>мол</b>».","say":"Чужая речь: он звонил — занят, мол."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"В нау́чной статье́: «А́втор всё вре́мя бьёт баклу́ши».","right":"В нау́чной статье́: «А́втор укло́няется от гла́вного вопро́са».","why":"<b>бить баклу́ши</b> is colloquial — fine with friends, wrong in a paper. Same meaning, wrong register: pick a neutral/bookish phrasing for science."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"In a formal essay, which idiom fits the register?","opts":["бить баклу́ши","ка́нуть в Ле́ту","валя́ть дурака́"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>ка́нуть в Ле́ту</b> is bookish — right for an essay. The other two are colloquial."},{"type":"type","q":"Add the particle for reported, sceptical speech (“supposedly”): «Он сказа́л, что за́нят» → «за́нят, …». Type it:","accept":["мол","дескать","мол."],"placeholder":"за́нят, …","explain":"<b>мол</b> (or де́скать) frames someone else's words with a touch of doubt: «за́нят, мол» = “busy, or so he claims”."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Я же говори́л, что э́то ва́жно!","sub":"the particle «же» adds insistence — “I did tell you!”","want":"я же говорил что это важно"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Emphasis by order & break — and the thread that holds","title":"<em>По́здно. Оди́н.</em> — parcellation, inversion, and connectors","paras":["Russian carries emphasis where English uses stress or an article: by <b>word order</b> (inversion — the weighty word moves to the front or the end) and by <b>parcellation</b> — snapping one sentence into fragments for punch: «Он пришёл. По́здно. Оди́н.» Used sparingly it hits; overused it's just noise.","And across a paragraph you hold the thread with <b>connectors</b>: с одно́й стороны́… с друго́й (on one hand… on the other), тем не ме́нее (nevertheless), что каса́ется (as for), сле́довательно (therefore). English tends to list; Russian argues — thesis, development, conclusion, stitched with these links.","Break for effect, connect for logic. That control over the long sentence and the paragraph is the backbone of every C1 essay and monologue."],"audio":"Russian carries emphasis where English uses stress or an article: by word order — inversion, the weighty word moving to the front or the end — and by parcellation, snapping one sentence into fragments for punch: on prishyol, pozdno, odin. Used sparingly it hits; overused it's just noise. And across a paragraph you hold the thread with connectors: s odnoy storony, s drugoy; tem ne meneye, nevertheless; chto kasaetsya, as for; sledovatelno, therefore. English tends to list; Russian argues — thesis, development, conclusion, stitched with these links. Break for effect, connect for logic. That control over the long sentence and the paragraph is the backbone of every C1 essay and monologue.","table":{"rows":[["Он пришёл. По́здно. Оди́н. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"он пришёл. поздно. один.\">🔊</button>","parcellation — fragments for punch"],["Осо́бенно тру́дно бы́ло молча́ть. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"особенно трудно было молчать\">🔊</button>","inversion — weight to the front"],["с одно́й стороны́… с друго́й <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"с одной стороны, с другой\">🔊</button>","balancing connector"],["тем не ме́нее / сле́довательно <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"тем не менее, следовательно\">🔊</button>","nevertheless / therefore"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"Parcellation for punch: “She agreed. At once. Without a single question.”","en":"Парцелля́ция: «Она́ согласи́лась. <b>Сра́зу. Без еди́ного вопро́са.</b>»","say":"Парцелляция: она согласилась. сразу. без единого вопроса."},{"ru":"Connectors argue: “On one hand — risk; on the other — no choice; therefore we act.”","en":"Свя́зки спо́рят: «<b>С одно́й стороны́</b> — риск, <b>с друго́й</b> — вы́бора нет, <b>сле́довательно</b>, де́йствуем».","say":"Связки спорят: с одной стороны — риск, с другой — выбора нет, следовательно, действуем."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"И пото́м она́ пришла́, и пото́м всё измени́лось, и пото́м...","right":"Она́ пришла́. По́здно. И всё измени́лось.","why":"Russian argues, it doesn't merely chain «и пото́м». Break for effect and connect for logic instead of endlessly listing."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which line uses <b>parcellation</b> (fragments for punch)?","opts":["Он пришёл по́здно и оди́н, потому́ что опозда́л на по́езд.","Он пришёл. По́здно. Оди́н.","Он, как изве́стно, пришёл по́здно."],"answer":1,"explain":"Parcellation snaps one thought into hard fragments: <b>Он пришёл. По́здно. Оди́н.</b>"},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“As for the price, nevertheless it's worth it.”</b>","want":"Что каса́ется цены́, тем не ме́нее оно́ того́ сто́ит","answer":"Что каса́ется цены́, тем не ме́нее оно́ того́ сто́ит"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Эмма shows Ива́н a newspaper column she's been reading for work — every trick of the week sits on one page. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Что чита́ешь с таки́м интере́сом?","ru":"What are you reading so intently?"},{"who":"A","en":"Коло́нку. Заголо́вок — «А во́з и ны́не там». Э́то же Крыло́в!","ru":"A column. The headline is “And the cart is still there.” That's Krylov, you know!"},{"who":"B","en":"То́чно. Про рефо́рму, кото́рая ника́к не дви́жется?","ru":"Right. About a reform that just won't move?"},{"who":"A","en":"И́менно. А да́льше — «как изве́стно» и сра́зу я́ркий о́браз. Класси́ческий публицисти́ческий сплав.","ru":"Precisely. And then “as is known” and straight into a vivid image. The classic journalistic blend."},{"who":"B","en":"А ты бы как написа́ла?","ru":"And how would you write it?"},{"who":"A","en":"Ко́ротко и с уда́ром. «Обеща́ли. Ещё весно́й. А во́з и ны́не там.»","ru":"Short, with a punch. “They promised. Back in spring. And the cart is still there.”"},{"who":"B","en":"Парцелля́ция! Ты уже́ ду́маешь как реда́ктор.","ru":"Parcellation! You're already thinking like an editor."},{"who":"A","en":"Ведь в э́том и смысл — не про́сто поня́ть, а услы́шать стиль.","ru":"That's the whole point, after all — not just to understand, but to hear the style."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the week's toolkit — the press, the quotation, the idiom, the particle, the break, the link. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"публицисти́ческий","ru":"journalistic (style)","ex":"Э́то публицисти́ческий сплав кли́ше и о́браза."},{"en":"как изве́стно","ru":"as is known (press cliché)","ex":"Как изве́стно, вопро́с непро́стой."},{"en":"о́стрый вопро́с","ru":"a burning issue","ex":"Э́то о́стрый вопро́с для го́рода."},{"en":"прецеде́нтный","ru":"precedent, culturally familiar","ex":"Заголо́вок — прецеде́нтный текст."},{"en":"крыла́тое сло́во","ru":"a winged word, famous saying","ex":"«А во́з и ны́не там» — крыла́тое сло́во."},{"en":"фразеологи́зм","ru":"set phrase, idiom","ex":"У ка́ждого фразеологи́зма свой реги́стр."},{"en":"ка́нуть в Ле́ту","ru":"to sink into oblivion (bookish)","ex":"Те спо́ры давно́ ка́нули в Ле́ту."},{"en":"бить баклу́ши","ru":"to idle, waste time (colloquial)","ex":"Хва́тит бить баклу́ши!"},{"en":"части́ца","ru":"particle (же, ведь, -то, мол)","ex":"Части́ца «же» добавля́ет наста́ивание."},{"en":"инве́рсия","ru":"inversion (marked word order)","ex":"Инве́рсия ста́вит ва́жное сло́во вперёд."},{"en":"парцелля́ция","ru":"parcellation (a line snapped apart)","ex":"Парцелля́ция бьёт ко́ротко и то́чно."},{"en":"тем не ме́нее","ru":"nevertheless (connector)","ex":"Тем не ме́нее наде́жда остаётся."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each phrase to what it is.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its label — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["А во́з и ны́не там","precedent text (Krylov)"],["ка́нуть в Ле́ту","bookish idiom"],["бить баклу́ши","colloquial idiom"],["Он пришёл. По́здно. Оди́н.","parcellation"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Eight questions — one or two from every day this week. Pass this and week two is yours.","items":[{"q":"The style that welds cliché to a vivid, persuasive image is…","opts":["нау́чный","официа́льно-делово́й","публицисти́ческий"],"answer":2},{"q":"«А во́з и ны́не там» in a headline means…","opts":["the matter still hasn't moved","the road is finished","the cart was sold"],"answer":0},{"q":"Which idiom is <b>bookish</b> (right for an essay)?","opts":["бить баклу́ши","ка́нуть в Ле́ту","валя́ть дурака́"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Он, мол, за́нят» — the particle «мол» signals…","opts":["an order","reported speech with a hint of doubt","a polite request"],"answer":1},{"q":"Which line uses <b>parcellation</b>?","opts":["Она́ согласи́лась. Сра́зу. Без вопро́сов.","Она́ согласи́лась сра́зу и без вопро́сов.","Она́, разуме́ется, согласи́лась."],"answer":0},{"q":"Which particle adds insistence — “I <b>did</b> tell you”?","opts":["Я ведь говори́л.","Я же говори́л.","Я мол говори́л."],"answer":1},{"q":"The connector that means “nevertheless” is…","opts":["тем не ме́нее","что каса́ется","как изве́стно"],"answer":0},{"q":"In a serious column, which continuation keeps the register?","opts":["…и э́того нельзя́ переоцени́ть.","…кла́ссно, да?","…ну про́сто жесть."],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Эмма drafts the opening of her own column — the whole week is hiding in five lines. Read it aloud and hear the register.","title":"Абза́ц, кото́рый де́ржит уда́р","sentences":["Как изве́стно, о ремо́нте на́шей у́лицы говоря́т уже́ це́лый год.","С одно́й стороны́, пла́ны краси́вые; с друго́й — а во́з и ны́не там.","Обеща́ли. Ещё весно́й. Пря́мо при всех.","Но ведь де́ло так и не сдви́нулось, что бы там ни говори́ли.","Тем не ме́нее наде́жда есть — и э́то, пожа́луй, гла́вное."],"translation":"As is known, they've been talking about repairing our street for a whole year. On one hand, the plans are pretty; on the other — the cart is still there. They promised. Back in spring. Right in front of everyone. But the matter hasn't budged, after all, whatever they say. Nevertheless there's hope — and that, perhaps, is the main thing."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — write like a columnist for one short paragraph.","prompt":"Write a short <b>journalistic</b> paragraph (3–4 sentences) on any everyday issue — your street, your café, your city. Use at least one <b>idiom</b> chosen for register, two <b>connectors</b> (с одно́й стороны́… с друго́й, тем не ме́нее, сле́довательно), and one <b>particle</b> (же / ведь). Try one parcellated line for punch.","hint":"Open with a cliché the press loves (как изве́стно), argue with connectors, then snap a line for effect: «Обеща́ли. Год наза́д.» 🙂","example":"Как изве́стно, о но́вом па́рке говоря́т давно́. С одно́й стороны́, прое́кт краси́вый; с друго́й — де́ло не дви́жется. Обеща́ли. Ещё весно́й. Но ведь наде́жда есть, и, сле́довательно, ждём."}},"15":{"day":15,"week":"03","themeRu":"The language of literature","themeEn":"Худо́жественный стиль: тро́пы","intro":"Эмма has a novel open on her desk — her first real literary translation. One line stops her: «Го́род не спал». The city wasn't sleeping? Cities don't sleep — and that's exactly the point. Literature never says a thing straight; it paints it. Today: the four tropes that turn plain words into images, and the freedom fiction takes to break every style rule you've drilled — on purpose.","introAudio":"Emma has a novel open on her desk — her first real literary translation. One line stops her: gorod ne spal, the city wasn't sleeping. Cities don't sleep — and that's exactly the point. Literature never says a thing straight; it paints it. Today: the four tropes that turn plain words into images, and the freedom fiction takes to break every style rule you've drilled, on purpose.","goals":["Name the four tropes: <b>мета́фора, эпи́тет, сравне́ние, олицетворе́ние</b>","Read the <b>image</b>, not the literal word (<b>го́род не спал</b> = the city was alive, awake)","See <b>register-mixing</b> as a device (приём): a plain word in a lofty passage can be deliberate art","Hear the <b>narrative voice</b> — whose eyes we see through, and the author's stance"],"learned":["Named the four tropes: мета́фора, эпи́тет, сравне́ние, олицетворе́ние","Read the image, not the literal word (го́род не спал = the city alive, awake)","Saw register-mixing as a device, not an error","Heard the narrative voice and read the author's stance"],"review":{"intro":"One pull back through last week — the press voice, a particle, a connector — then we step into fiction, where the rules bend.","introAudio":"One pull back through last week — the press voice, a particle, a connector — then we step into fiction, where the rules bend.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. Week-2 material.","q":"«А во́з и ны́не там» in a headline is…","opts":["a scientific term","a precedent text (a known quotation)","an official cliché"],"answer":1,"explain":"It's a <b>precedent text</b> from Krylov — “nothing has moved.” The press bends such quotations into headlines."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the particle. Stress marks are optional.","q":"Add insistence — “I <b>did</b> tell you”: «Я … говори́л!»","accept":["же"],"placeholder":"Я … говори́л!","explain":"<b>же</b> presses the point: «Я же говори́л!» = “I did tell you!”"},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“On one hand it's expensive, nevertheless it's worth it.”</b>","want":"С одно́й стороны́ до́рого, тем не ме́нее сто́ит","answer":"С одно́й стороны́ до́рого, тем не ме́нее сто́ит"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The four tropes","title":"<em>Мета́фора, эпи́тет, сравне́ние, олицетворе́ние</em>","paras":["The literary style (худо́жественный стиль) works by <b>tropes</b> (тро́пы) — words turned from their plain sense to make an image. Two are comparisons: <b>мета́фора</b> (metaphor — a hidden one, no «как»: «вре́мя лети́т», «золото́е се́рдце») and <b>сравне́ние</b> (simile — an open one, with как/сло́вно/бу́дто: «глаза́, как звёзды»).","Two more colour and animate: <b>эпи́тет</b> (epithet — a vivid, evaluative modifier, not mere information: «золота́я о́сень», «немо́е го́ре») and <b>олицетворе́ние</b> (personification — life lent to the lifeless: «ве́тер во́ет», «приро́да засну́ла»).","A metaphor merges two things without «как»; a simile keeps the «как». An epithet colours; a personification breathes. Naming them is the first step of any analysis."],"audio":"The literary style works by tropes — words turned from their plain sense to make an image. Two are comparisons: metafora, metaphor, a hidden one with no 'kak' — vremya letit, time flies; zolotoye serdtse, a heart of gold — and sravneniye, simile, an open one with kak, slovno, budto: glaza kak zvyozdy, eyes like stars. Two more colour and animate: epitet, epithet, a vivid evaluative modifier — zolotaya osen, golden autumn — and olitsetvoreniye, personification, life lent to the lifeless: veter voyet, the wind howls; priroda zasnula, nature fell asleep. A metaphor merges two things without 'kak'; a simile keeps the 'kak'. An epithet colours; a personification breathes. Naming them is the first step of any analysis.","table":{"rows":[["мета́фора: вре́мя лети́т, золото́е се́рдце <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"метафора: время летит, золотое сердце\">🔊</button>","metaphor — hidden comparison (no «как»)"],["сравне́ние: глаза́, как звёзды <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"сравнение: глаза, как звёзды\">🔊</button>","simile — open comparison (как/сло́вно/бу́дто)"],["эпи́тет: золота́я о́сень, немо́е го́ре <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"эпитет: золотая осень, немое горе\">🔊</button>","epithet — vivid, evaluative modifier"],["олицетворе́ние: ве́тер во́ет <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"олицетворение: ветер воет\">🔊</button>","personification — life to the lifeless"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Metaphor: “A sea of lights lay below the plane.”","en":"Мета́фора: «Под самолётом лежа́ло <b>мо́ре огне́й</b>».","say":"Метафора: под самолётом лежало море огней."},{"ru":"Simile: “Her voice was soft, like snow.”","en":"Сравне́ние: «Её го́лос был мя́гким, <b>как снег</b>».","say":"Сравнение: её голос был мягким, как снег."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Глаза́, как звёзды» — э́то мета́фора.","right":"«Глаза́, как звёзды» — э́то сравне́ние: есть сло́во «как».","why":"With «как/сло́вно/бу́дто» it's a <b>simile</b> (сравне́ние). A metaphor merges the two images without «как» (глаза́-звёзды)."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the trope.","q":"«Ве́тер во́ет за окно́м» — which trope?","opts":["сравне́ние","олицетворе́ние","эпи́тет"],"answer":1,"explain":"A lifeless thing (the wind) is given a living act (howling) → <b>олицетворе́ние</b> (personification)."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the trope's name.","q":"A comparison built openly with «как» or «сло́вно» is called a … (Russian term). Type it:","accept":["сравнение","сравнение."],"placeholder":"с…","explain":"<b>сравне́ние</b> (simile) — the open comparison that keeps «как/сло́вно/бу́дто»."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Read the image, not the dictionary","title":"«<em>Го́род не спал</em>» — cities don't sleep, and that's the point","paras":["Here's the trap that catches every careful reader of Russian fiction: you render the words correctly and still miss the meaning. «Го́род не спал» is no weather report — the city is alive, restless, watching. The literal sense is only the doorway; the image is the room.","So when a text says «его́ ду́ша окамене́ла» (his soul turned to stone), don't reach for geology — reach for the feeling: he went numb, unmoved. Ask always what the picture <i>does</i>: what mood, what judgement does it carry?","Denotation (the dictionary meaning) is where you start; connotation and image are where literature lives. Reading closely means reading past the literal."],"audio":"Here's the trap that catches every careful reader of Russian fiction: you render the words correctly and still miss the meaning. Gorod ne spal, the city wasn't sleeping — no weather report; the city is alive, restless, watching. The literal sense is only the doorway; the image is the room. So when a text says yego dusha okamenela, his soul turned to stone, don't reach for geology — reach for the feeling: he went numb, unmoved. Ask always what the picture does: what mood, what judgement does it carry? Denotation, the dictionary meaning, is where you start; connotation and image are where literature lives. Reading closely means reading past the literal.","table":{"rows":[["Го́род не спал <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"город не спал\">🔊</button>","the city was alive, awake, watching"],["Его́ душа́ окамене́ла <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"его душа окаменела\">🔊</button>","he went numb, unfeeling"],["Желе́зная во́ля <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"железная воля\">🔊</button>","an unbending will (not literal metal)"],["Со́лнце улыбну́лось <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"солнце улыбнулось\">🔊</button>","a bright, warm, kind moment"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Read the night, not the verb: “The wind was tearing at the trees.”","en":"Чита́й ночь, а не глаго́л: «<b>Ве́тер рвал дере́вья</b>».","say":"Читай ночь, а не глагол: ветер рвал деревья."},{"ru":"Read the mood: “The house was watching them with dark windows.”","en":"Чита́й настрое́ние: «Дом <b>смотре́л</b> на них тёмными о́кнами».","say":"Читай настроение: дом смотрел на них тёмными окнами."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Желе́зная во́ля» — зна́чит, во́ля из мета́лла.","right":"«Желе́зная во́ля» — о́браз: непрекло́нная, твёрдая реши́мость.","why":"The epithet <b>желе́зная</b> isn't about metal — read the image: an unbending, hard resolve. Take the picture, not the dictionary entry."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Его́ душа́ окамене́ла» most likely means…","opts":["he turned literally to stone","he went cold and numb inside","he studied geology"],"answer":1,"explain":"It's an image of numbness — he became unfeeling. Read the picture, not the literal stone."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and read it as an image.","target":"Го́род не спал: он ждал, слу́шал и смотре́л.","sub":"personification — the city is alive, not literally awake","want":"город не спал он ждал слушал и смотрел"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · When breaking the rules is the art","title":"Colloquial in a lofty passage — a <em>device</em>, not a slip","paras":["All month you've guarded register: keep one style, don't let slang crash a formal line. Literature is the one place that breaks this on purpose. A sudden colloquial word in a solemn passage — or a lofty word in a plain one — is a <b>device</b> (приём): it jolts, it characterises, it colours.","When a narrator drops «а он возьми́ да и приди́» (“and he just up and shows up”) into an otherwise bookish paragraph, that shift is doing work — it lets a live voice in, a shrug, a wink. The mismatch you'd fix in an essay is here a deliberate brushstroke.","So in fiction a register clash isn't automatically wrong: ask whether it's an accident or an <b>effect</b>. Reading well means telling the two apart."],"audio":"All month you've guarded register: keep one style, don't let slang crash a formal line. Literature is the one place that breaks this on purpose. A sudden colloquial word in a solemn passage — or a lofty word in a plain one — is a device, a priyom: it jolts, it characterises, it colours. When a narrator drops 'a on vozmi da i pridi', and he just up and shows up, into an otherwise bookish paragraph, that shift is doing work — it lets a live voice in, a shrug, a wink. The mismatch you'd fix in an essay is here a deliberate brushstroke. So in fiction a register clash isn't automatically wrong: ask whether it's an accident or an effect. Reading well means telling the two apart.","table":{"rows":[["смеше́ние регистро́в <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"смешение регистров\">🔊</button>","register-mixing — for effect"],["приём <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"приём\">🔊</button>","a deliberate literary device"],["И вели́кий челове́к про́сто стру́сил. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"и великий человек просто струсил\">🔊</button>","colloquial jolt inside a lofty line"],["Он соизво́лил вкуси́ть су́па. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"он соизволил вкусить супа\">🔊</button>","lofty words for irony over a plain thing"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"A colloquial jolt in a solemn line: “And the great man, as they say, simply chickened out.”","en":"Разгово́рный слом в высо́кой фра́зе: «И вели́кий челове́к, что называ́ется, про́сто <b>стру́сил</b>».","say":"Разговорный слом в высокой фразе: и великий человек, что называется, просто струсил."},{"ru":"Lofty words for irony: “He deigned to partake of some plain soup.”","en":"Высо́кие слова́ для иро́нии: «Он <b>соизво́лил вкуси́ть</b> просто́го су́па».","say":"Высокие слова для иронии: он соизволил вкусить простого супа."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"В рома́не разг. сло́во а́втора «испра́вили» на нейтра́льное.","right":"В худо́жественном те́ксте разг. сло́во ча́сто — приём; не тро́гай его́.","why":"Outside fiction a register clash is an error to fix; inside it, it's usually the author's <b>device</b>. Flattening it erases the voice."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"A blunt colloquial word suddenly appears in a solemn literary passage. Most likely it is…","opts":["the author's mistake to correct","a deliberate device (приём) — a voice, irony, a jolt","proof the text is not literary"],"answer":1,"explain":"In fiction the clash is usually a <b>приём</b> — it brings in a voice or an irony. Don't flatten it."},{"type":"type","q":"The Russian word for a deliberate literary “device / technique” is …. Type it:","accept":["приём","прием","приём."],"placeholder":"при…","explain":"<b>приём</b> — a device the author uses on purpose, register-mixing among them."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Whose eyes, whose voice","title":"Narrative voice and the author's stance (<em>а́вторская пози́ция</em>)","paras":["Every literary text is told by someone. The <b>narrative voice</b> (повествова́тельный го́лос) may be a cool outsider, a character living inside the story, or an «я» remembering. Notice whose eyes you see through — it shapes everything the words mean.","And behind the voice sits the <b>author's stance</b> (а́вторская пози́ция) — sympathy, irony, judgement — rarely stated, usually shown through word choice and the detail lingered on. When a narrator calls a man «ва́жный» while showing him do something petty, that gap is the irony, and the irony is the stance.","Naming the voice and reading the stance are the heart of филологи́ческий ана́лиз — tomorrow's work. Today, just start hearing that a story always has a teller, and the teller has a view."],"audio":"Every literary text is told by someone. The narrative voice, povestvovatelny golos, may be a cool outsider, a character living inside the story, or an 'ya' remembering. Notice whose eyes you see through — it shapes everything the words mean. And behind the voice sits the author's stance, avtorskaya pozitsiya — sympathy, irony, judgement — rarely stated, usually shown through word choice and the detail lingered on. When a narrator calls a man 'vazhny', important, while showing him do something petty, that gap is the irony, and the irony is the stance. Naming the voice and reading the stance are the heart of filologichesky analiz — tomorrow's work. Today, just start hearing that a story always has a teller, and the teller has a view.","table":{"rows":[["повествова́тельный го́лос <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"повествовательный голос\">🔊</button>","the narrative voice — who tells it"],["а́вторская пози́ция <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"авторская позиция\">🔊</button>","the author's stance — sympathy, irony, judgement"],["расска́з от пе́рвого лица́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"рассказ от первого лица\">🔊</button>","first-person «я» narration"],["иро́ния <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ирония\">🔊</button>","irony — saying one thing, meaning another"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"Whose eyes: “I remember that evening as if it were now.”","en":"Чей взгляд: «Я по́мню тот ве́чер, <b>сло́вно</b> сейча́с».","say":"Чей взгляд: я помню тот вечер, словно сейчас."},{"ru":"The stance is irony: “Our hero, of course, was above such trifles.”","en":"Пози́ция — иро́ния: «Наш геро́й, <b>разуме́ется</b>, был вы́ше таки́х мелоче́й».","say":"Позиция — ирония: наш герой, разумеется, был выше таких мелочей."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Раз а́втор назва́л геро́я «ва́жным», зна́чит, он им восхища́ется.","right":"«Ва́жный» ря́дом с ме́лкими посту́пками — иро́ния: пози́ция а́втора обра́тна слова́м.","why":"The author's stance often contradicts the surface word. «ва́жный» beside petty acts signals irony — read the stance, not just the adjective."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"A narrator calls a cowardly man «наш герой» and «вы́ше мелоче́й». The author's stance is…","opts":["sincere admiration","irony — the opposite of the words","no stance at all"],"answer":1,"explain":"Grand words over petty deeds = <b>irony</b>. The stance sits in the gap between word and act."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I remember that evening as if it were now.”</b>","want":"Я по́мню тот ве́чер, сло́вно сейча́с","answer":"Я по́мню тот ве́чер, сло́вно сейча́с"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Эмма reads a few lines of her translation aloud to Ка́тя, who studied literature. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Что перево́дишь сего́дня?","ru":"What are you translating today?"},{"who":"A","en":"Рома́н. Вот стро́чка: «Го́род не спал». Краси́во, пра́вда?","ru":"A novel. Here's a line: “The city wasn't sleeping.” Beautiful, isn't it?"},{"who":"B","en":"Но города́ ведь не спят...","ru":"But cities don't actually sleep..."},{"who":"A","en":"В том и де́ло. Э́то олицетворе́ние — го́род живо́й, беспоко́йный. Чита́ешь не сло́во, а о́браз.","ru":"That's just it. It's personification — the city is alive, restless. You read not the word but the image."},{"who":"B","en":"А да́льше?","ru":"And after that?"},{"who":"A","en":"«Дома́ смотре́ли тёмными о́кнами, как уста́лые лю́ди». Сравне́ние — ви́дишь «как»?","ru":"“The houses watched with dark windows, like tired people.” A simile — see the “like”?"},{"who":"B","en":"И сра́зу настрое́ние друго́е. Гру́стное.","ru":"And instantly the mood is different. Sad."},{"who":"A","en":"И́менно. Моя́ зада́ча — переда́ть не то́лько смысл, но и э́ту гру́сть.","ru":"Exactly. My job is to carry across not just the meaning, but that sadness."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for reading fiction — the tropes, the image, the device, the voice. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"худо́жественный","ru":"literary, artistic (style)","ex":"Худо́жественный стиль по́лон о́бразов."},{"en":"троп","ru":"a trope (a word turned to image)","ex":"Мета́фора — са́мый ча́стый троп."},{"en":"мета́фора","ru":"metaphor (hidden comparison)","ex":"«Вре́мя лети́т» — мета́фора."},{"en":"эпи́тет","ru":"epithet (vivid modifier)","ex":"«Золота́я о́сень» — краси́вый эпи́тет."},{"en":"сравне́ние","ru":"simile, comparison (with «как»)","ex":"«Бе́лый, как снег» — сравне́ние."},{"en":"олицетворе́ние","ru":"personification","ex":"«Ве́тер во́ет» — олицетворе́ние."},{"en":"о́браз","ru":"image, figure","ex":"За сло́вом стои́т о́браз."},{"en":"приём","ru":"device, technique","ex":"Смеше́ние стиле́й — э́то приём."},{"en":"повествова́ние","ru":"narration, storytelling","ex":"Повествова́ние идёт от пе́рвого лица́."},{"en":"а́вторская пози́ция","ru":"the author's stance","ex":"А́вторская пози́ция скры́та в иро́нии."},{"en":"иро́ния","ru":"irony","ex":"В его́ слова́х чу́вствуется иро́ния."},{"en":"настрое́ние","ru":"mood, atmosphere","ex":"О́браз создаёт настрое́ние."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each line to its trope.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its trope — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["золото́е се́рдце","metaphor"],["глаза́, как звёзды","simile"],["золота́я о́сень","epithet"],["ве́тер во́ет","personification"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five questions on today's tropes and how fiction bends the rules. If one bites, glance back at the rule.","items":[{"q":"«Вре́мя лети́т» is a…","opts":["сравне́ние","мета́фора","эпи́тет"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Глаза́, как звёзды» is a…","opts":["сравне́ние","олицетворе́ние","мета́фора"],"answer":0},{"q":"«Го́род не спал» tells you the city was…","opts":["literally awake with insomnia","alive, restless, watching","empty and quiet"],"answer":1},{"q":"A colloquial word inside a solemn literary passage is usually…","opts":["a mistake to correct","a deliberate device (приём)","a sign of bad writing"],"answer":1},{"q":"A narrator calls a petty man «наш вели́кий геро́й». The stance is…","opts":["irony","sincere praise","a scientific fact"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"A few lines from Эмма's translation draft — five tropes hiding in five sentences. Read it aloud and feel the mood before you name the devices.","title":"Ве́чер над го́родом","sentences":["Го́род не спал.","Дома́ смотре́ли на у́лицу тёмными, уста́лыми о́кнами.","Ве́тер, как ста́рый друг, тихо́нько стуча́л в стекло́.","И вре́мя, каза́лось, останови́лось над э́той тишино́й.","То́лько где-то далеко́ горе́ло одино́кое золото́е окно́."],"translation":"The city was not sleeping. The houses looked out at the street with dark, tired windows. The wind, like an old friend, tapped softly at the glass. And time, it seemed, had stopped over this silence. Only somewhere far off a single golden window was burning."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — paint a moment, don't just report it.","prompt":"In 3–4 sentences, describe a place or a moment (an evening, a street, a room) in the <b>literary</b> style, using at least one <b>metaphor</b>, one <b>simile</b> (with как/сло́вно), one <b>epithet</b>, and one <b>personification</b>. You may label each device in brackets.","hint":"Let the lifeless act (ве́тер шепта́л), compare openly (как ста́рый друг), and colour with an epithet (уста́лое не́бо). Read for the image, not the fact 🙂","example":"Наступа́л ти́хий, уста́лый ве́чер (эпи́тет). Не́бо горе́ло, как ме́дленный костёр (сравне́ние). Ве́тер шепта́л что́-то дере́вьям (олицетворе́ние), и вре́мя текло́ ме́дленной реко́й (мета́фора)."}},"16":{"day":16,"week":"03","themeRu":"Aspect's finest shades","themeEn":"То́нкий вид и глаго́льный нюа́нс","intro":"«Вы чита́ли Толсто́го?» and «Вы прочита́ли Толсто́го?» look like one question to English — but a Russian hears two. The first asks if you've ever read him at all; the second, if you finished a particular book. This is the one place C1 goes back into grammar: aspect, at its finest resolution. Эмма lives it now, rereading a novel one careful phrase at a time.","introAudio":"Vy chitali Tolstogo? and Vy prochitali Tolstogo? look like one question to English — but a Russian hears two. The first asks if you've ever read him at all; the second, if you finished a particular book. This is the one place C1 goes back into grammar: aspect, at its finest resolution. Emma lives it now, rereading a novel one careful phrase at a time.","goals":["Use the imperfective's <b>general-factual</b> sense: «Вы чита́ли…?» = have you <i>ever</i>, vs «Вы прочита́ли…?» = did you <i>finish</i>","Choose aspect under <b>negation</b> and in the <b>imperative</b> (a ban vs a warning, an invite vs a command)","Read <b>motion verbs at scale</b> as fixed images: <b>речь идёт о, де́ло не дви́жется, вре́мя лети́т</b>","Feel the <b>semelfactive -ну-</b> (one instant) and the <b>iterative -ыва-</b> (habitual, nostalgic)"],"learned":["Used the general-factual imperfective (чита́ли = ever?) against the resultative perfective (прочита́ли = finished?)","Chose aspect under negation and in the imperative — ban vs warning, invite vs command","Read motion verbs at scale as fixed images (речь идёт о, де́ло не дви́жется)","Felt the semelfactive -ну- (one instant) and the iterative -ыва- (habitual, nostalgic)"],"review":{"intro":"A quick step back into fiction — a trope, an image, a device — then the one grammar deepening of C1: aspect's finest shades.","introAudio":"A quick step back into fiction — a trope, an image, a device — then the one grammar deepening of C1: aspect's finest shades.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. Yesterday's material.","q":"«Ве́тер во́ет за окно́м» — which trope?","opts":["мета́фора","олицетворе́ние","сравне́ние"],"answer":1,"explain":"A lifeless thing given a living act (the wind howling) → <b>олицетворе́ние</b> (personification)."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Го́род не спал» — read as an image, this means the city was…","opts":["empty and silent","alive, restless, watching","under repair"],"answer":1,"explain":"Read the image, not the literal word: the city is <b>alive and restless</b>."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the trope's name.","q":"«Её го́лос был мя́гким, как снег» — a comparison with «как» is a … (Russian term):","accept":["сравнение","сравнение."],"placeholder":"с…","explain":"<b>сравне́ние</b> (simile) — an open comparison marked by «как/сло́вно/бу́дто»."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The general-factual imperfective","title":"«<em>Чита́ли</em>?» (ever?) vs «<em>Прочита́ли</em>?» (finished?)","paras":["You've built aspect for years: perfective = one whole, completed action (<b>прочита́л</b>); imperfective = process, repetition, or the bare fact. The C1 refinement is the <b>general-factual</b> (общефакти́ческое) imperfective — it names only that an action <i>happened at all</i>, with no eye on the result.","«Вы <b>чита́ли</b> „Войну́ и мир“?» asks “have you ever read it, do you know it?” — the experience. «Вы <b>прочита́ли</b> „Войну́ и мир“?» asks “did you finish that particular copy?” — the result. English uses one “have you read”; Russian must choose.","So the imperfective past holds three jobs at once: process («чита́л весь ве́чер»), repetition («ка́ждый день чита́л»), and this bare general fact («чита́л ли ты?»). The perfective past means just one thing — done, with a result."],"audio":"You've built aspect for years: perfective is one whole completed action, prochital; imperfective is process, repetition, or the bare fact. The C1 refinement is the general-factual imperfective, obshchefakticheskoye — it names only that an action happened at all, with no eye on the result. Vy chitali voynu i mir? asks 'have you ever read it, do you know it?' — the experience. Vy prochitali voynu i mir? asks 'did you finish that particular copy?' — the result. English uses one 'have you read'; Russian must choose. So the imperfective past holds three jobs at once: process, chital ves vecher; repetition, kazhdy den chital; and this bare general fact, chital li ty? The perfective past means just one thing — done, with a result.","table":{"rows":[["Вы чита́ли Толсто́го? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"вы читали Толстого?\">🔊</button>","general fact — have you ever? (experience)"],["Вы прочита́ли статью́? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"вы прочитали статью?\">🔊</button>","result — did you finish it?"],["Я чита́л весь ве́чер. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"я читал весь вечер\">🔊</button>","process — was reading (duration)"],["Я ка́ждый день чита́л. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"я каждый день читал\">🔊</button>","repetition — used to read"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"General fact (ever?): “Have you seen this film?” — “Yes, I have.”","en":"Общефакти́ческое: «Ты <b>смотре́л</b> э́тот фильм?» — «Да, смотре́л».","say":"Общефактическое: ты смотрел этот фильм? да, смотрел."},{"ru":"Result (to the end?): “Did you watch it right to the end?”","en":"Результа́т: «Ты <b>посмотре́л</b> его́ до конца́?»","say":"Результат: ты посмотрел его до конца?"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Ты прочита́л Достое́вского?» (meaning: are you familiar with him?)","right":"«Ты чита́л Достое́вского?» (have you ever read him?)","why":"To ask whether someone <i>knows</i> an author, use the general-factual imperfective <b>чита́л</b>. The perfective <b>прочита́л</b> asks about finishing one specific text."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"You want to ask “Have you ever read Chekhov?” (do you know his work). Choose:","opts":["Ты прочита́л Че́хова?","Ты чита́л Че́хова?","Ты бу́дешь чита́ть Че́хова?"],"answer":1,"explain":"Experience / “ever” → the general-factual imperfective <b>чита́л</b>. прочита́л would ask about finishing one book."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the verb (any stress).","q":"Ask about the <b>result</b> — did you finish the report? «Ты … отчёт?» (perfective of чита́ть):","accept":["прочитал","прочитала","дочитал","дочитала"],"placeholder":"Ты … отчёт?","explain":"Result / completion → the perfective <b>прочита́л</b> (or дочита́л, “read to the end”)."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Aspect under negation & in the imperative","title":"A ban vs a warning, an invite vs a command","paras":["Aspect also turns on <b>negation</b> and the <b>imperative</b>. Negated, the imperfective is a plain denial — «Я <b>не чита́л</b> э́ту кни́гу» (I haven't read it). The negated perfective narrows to a <b>failure to complete</b> — «Я <b>не прочита́л</b>» (I didn't finish it), «Он <b>не сдал</b> экза́мен» (he failed the exam).","In requests, the imperfective imperative <b>invites</b> and welcomes a process — «<b>Проходи́те! Сади́тесь!</b>» feels warm; the perfective imperative asks for one <b>specific</b> done act — «<b>Прочита́йте</b> э́тот абза́ц».","And prohibitions split by aspect. «<b>Не входи́!</b>» (imperfective) is a flat ban — “don't (ever/now).” «<b>Не упади́!</b>» (perfective) is a warning — “mind you don't fall.” Same «не», opposite feel: rule vs caution."],"audio":"Aspect also turns on negation and the imperative. Negated, the imperfective is a plain denial: ya ne chital etu knigu, I haven't read it. The negated perfective narrows to a failure to complete: ya ne prochital, I didn't finish it; on ne sdal ekzamen, he failed the exam. In requests, the imperfective imperative invites and welcomes a process — prokhodite, sadites — it feels warm; the perfective imperative asks for one specific done act, prochitayte etot abzats. And prohibitions split by aspect. Ne vkhodi, imperfective, is a flat ban — don't, ever or now. Ne upadi, perfective, is a warning — mind you don't fall. Same 'ne', opposite feel: rule versus caution.","table":{"rows":[["Я не чита́л письмо́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"я не читал письмо\">🔊</button>","plain denial — haven't read it"],["Я не прочита́л письмо́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"я не прочитал письмо\">🔊</button>","failure to finish it"],["Проходи́те, сади́тесь! <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"проходите, садитесь\">🔊</button>","warm invitation (imperfective imperative)"],["Не упади́! <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"не упади\">🔊</button>","a warning — mind you don't (perfective)"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"Warm invite: “Come in, sit down, help yourself!”","en":"Тёплое приглаше́ние: «<b>Проходи́те, сади́тесь</b>, угоща́йтесь!»","say":"Тёплое приглашение: проходите, садитесь, угощайтесь!"},{"ru":"A ban vs a warning: “Don't go in there.” — “Careful, don't slip!”","en":"Запре́т и предостереже́ние: «<b>Не входи́</b> туда́». — «<b>Не поскользни́сь</b>!»","say":"Запрет и предостережение: не входи туда. не поскользнись!"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Го́стю у две́ри: «Пройди́те и ся́дьте».","right":"Го́стю у две́ри: «Проходи́те, сади́тесь, пожа́луйста».","why":"To a guest, the imperfective imperative (<b>проходи́те, сади́тесь</b>) invites warmly; the perfective (пройди́те, ся́дьте) sounds like a clipped command. Aspect carries the tone."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Warning a child on ice — “Careful, don't fall!” Choose the aspect:","opts":["Не па́дай!","Не упади́!","Не упа́л!"],"answer":1,"explain":"A one-off warning (“mind you don't”) takes the <b>perfective</b> imperative: <b>Не упади́!</b> «Не па́дай» would be a general ban on falling."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which sounds like a warm invitation, not a command?","opts":["Войди́те и ся́дьте.","Проходи́те, сади́тесь.","Вы должны́ войти́."],"answer":1,"explain":"The imperfective imperative <b>проходи́те, сади́тесь</b> welcomes and invites; the perfective is clipped and formal."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I didn't finish the letter.”</b> (failure to complete)","want":"Я не прочита́л письмо́ до конца́","answer":"Я не прочита́л письмо́ до конца́"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Motion verbs, gone figurative","title":"<em>Речь идёт о, де́ло не дви́жется, вре́мя лети́т</em>","paras":["Motion verbs long ago left the road. «<b>Идёт дождь</b>» (rain “goes” = it's raining), «<b>вре́мя идёт</b>» / «<b>вре́мя лети́т</b>» (time goes / flies), «<b>го́ды прохо́дят</b>» (the years pass). At C1 this is everywhere — motion is Russian's default metaphor for time, change and process.","Whole set phrases are built on it: <b>речь идёт о</b> (“it's about / the matter concerns”), <b>де́ло не дви́жется</b> (“the matter isn't progressing”), <b>дела́ иду́т</b> (“things are going”). Not one of them is about walking.","Read these as fixed images, not literal steps — and use them. They're the bloodstream of natural, idiomatic Russian, and mis-hearing them as literal motion is a classic tell."],"audio":"Motion verbs long ago left the road. Idyot dozhd, rain 'goes', meaning it's raining; vremya idyot, vremya letit, time goes, time flies; gody prokhodyat, the years pass. At C1 this is everywhere — motion is Russian's default metaphor for time, change and process. Whole set phrases are built on it: rech idyot o, it's about, the matter concerns; delo ne dvizhetsya, the matter isn't progressing; dela idut, things are going. Not one of them is about walking. Read these as fixed images, not literal steps — and use them. They're the bloodstream of natural, idiomatic Russian, and mis-hearing them as literal motion is a classic tell.","table":{"rows":[["Идёт дождь. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"идёт дождь\">🔊</button>","it's raining (rain “goes”)"],["Речь идёт о де́ньгах. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"речь идёт о деньгах\">🔊</button>","the matter concerns money / it's about money"],["Де́ло не дви́жется. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"дело не движется\">🔊</button>","the matter isn't progressing"],["Вре́мя лети́т. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"время летит\">🔊</button>","time flies"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"About the topic: “We're talking here about a serious problem.”","en":"О те́ме: «<b>Речь идёт</b> о серьёзной пробле́ме».","say":"О теме: речь идёт о серьёзной проблеме."},{"ru":"Stuck: “The work just isn't moving.”","en":"За́стопорилось: «Рабо́та ника́к <b>не дви́жется</b>».","say":"Застопорилось: работа никак не движется."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Разгово́р хо́дит о де́ньгах.","right":"Речь идёт о де́ньгах.","why":"The fixed phrase is <b>речь идёт о</b> — not «разгово́р хо́дит». These motion idioms are frozen: learn each one whole."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“It's about time” (the matter concerns time) — choose the idiom:","opts":["Речь идёт о вре́мени.","Разгово́р хо́дит о вре́мени.","Сло́во е́дет о вре́мени."],"answer":0,"explain":"The frozen phrase is <b>речь идёт о</b> + prepositional: <b>Речь идёт о вре́мени</b>."},{"type":"type","q":"Type the idiom for “the matter isn't progressing” (де́ло + не + motion verb):","accept":["дело не движется","дело не движется."],"placeholder":"де́ло не…","explain":"<b>Де́ло не дви́жется</b> — the matter is stuck, going nowhere."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · One instant, or a habit of old","title":"Semelfactive <em>-ну-</em> and iterative <em>-ыва-</em>, plus fine government","paras":["Two suffixes fine-tune a verb. The <b>semelfactive -ну-</b> carves one quick instance out of an ongoing action: маха́ть → <b>махну́ть</b> (to wave → give one wave), стуча́ть → <b>сту́кнуть</b> (to knock → one knock), крича́ть → <b>кри́кнуть</b> (to shout → one shout).","The <b>iterative -ыва-/-ива-</b> marks a repeated, habitual past, often bookish or folksy: сиде́ть → <b>си́живать</b> («быва́ло, си́живал здесь часа́ми» — he'd sit here for hours), ходи́ть → <b>ха́живать</b>, знать → <b>знава́л</b>. It colours the past with nostalgia.","And keep <b>government</b> steady across an aspect pair — the case a verb demands doesn't shift with aspect, but C1 verbs demand precise ones: <b>достига́ть / дости́гнуть це́ли</b> (+ gen), <b>каса́ться вопро́са</b> (+ gen), <b>доби́ться результа́та</b> (+ gen)."],"audio":"Two suffixes fine-tune a verb. The semelfactive -nu- carves one quick instance out of an ongoing action: makhat becomes makhnut, to wave becomes to give one wave; stuchat becomes stuknut, one knock; krichat becomes kriknut, one shout. The iterative -yva-, -iva- marks a repeated, habitual past, often bookish or folksy: sidet becomes sizhivat — byvalo, sizhival zdes chasami, he'd sit here for hours; khodit becomes khazhivat; znat becomes znaval. It colours the past with nostalgia. And keep government steady across an aspect pair — the case a verb demands doesn't shift with aspect, but C1 verbs demand precise ones: dostigat, dostignut tseli, plus genitive; kasatsya voprosa, plus genitive; dobitsya rezultata, plus genitive.","table":{"rows":[["маха́ть → махну́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"махать, махнуть\">🔊</button>","one wave (semelfactive -ну-)"],["кри́кнуть, сту́кнуть, пры́гнуть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"крикнуть, стукнуть, прыгнуть\">🔊</button>","single, instant acts"],["Быва́ло, си́живал часа́ми. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"бывало, сиживал часами\">🔊</button>","habitual past (-ыва-, nostalgic)"],["достига́ть це́ли (+ род.) <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"достигать цели\">🔊</button>","government stays with the verb"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"One instant: “He knocked once and went quiet.”","en":"Оди́н миг: «Он <b>сту́кнул</b> раз и зати́х».","say":"Один миг: он стукнул раз и затих."},{"ru":"Habitual, nostalgic: “In the old days he'd sit here for hours.”","en":"С носта́льгией: «Быва́ло, он <b>си́живал</b> здесь часа́ми».","say":"С ностальгией: бывало, он сиживал здесь часами."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Он махну́л руко́й весь ве́чер.","right":"Он маха́л руко́й весь ве́чер.","why":"<b>-ну-</b> names one quick instant, so it can't stretch across «весь ве́чер». For the ongoing action use the imperfective <b>маха́л</b>."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which verb names a <b>single, instant</b> act?","opts":["крича́л","кри́кнул","крича́л ча́сто"],"answer":1,"explain":"The semelfactive <b>-ну-</b> makes <b>кри́кнул</b> = gave one shout. крича́л is the ongoing/repeated act."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“He waved once and left.”</b>","want":"Он махну́л руко́й и ушёл","answer":"Он махну́л руко́й и ушёл"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Ива́н asks Эмма about the novel she's rereading — and the whole aspect system quietly shows up. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Ты чита́ла «А́нну Каре́нину»?","ru":"Have you (ever) read Anna Karenina?"},{"who":"A","en":"Чита́ла, давно́. А сейча́с перечи́тываю — по рабо́те.","ru":"I have, long ago. And now I'm rereading it — for work."},{"who":"B","en":"И уже́ прочита́ла?","ru":"And have you finished it already?"},{"who":"A","en":"Ещё нет, не дочита́ла. Де́ло дви́жется ме́дленно — ка́ждая фра́за ва́жная.","ru":"Not yet, haven't finished. It's going slowly — every phrase matters."},{"who":"B","en":"Поня́тно. Кста́ти, проходи́, сади́сь — ко́фе бу́дешь?","ru":"I see. By the way, come in, sit down — want some coffee?"},{"who":"A","en":"С удово́льствием. То́лько не налива́й мно́го.","ru":"Gladly. Just don't pour a lot."},{"who":"B","en":"И не обожги́сь — ча́шка горя́чая.","ru":"And don't burn yourself — the cup's hot."},{"who":"A","en":"Ви́дишь, да́же ты игра́ешь ви́дом: «не налива́й» — запре́т, «не обожги́сь» — предостереже́ние.","ru":"See, even you play with aspect: “don't pour” is a ban, “don't burn yourself” is a warning."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for aspect at its finest — the terms and the verbs that carry the nuance. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"вид","ru":"aspect (perfective / imperfective)","ex":"Вид меня́ет отте́нок глаго́ла."},{"en":"соверше́нный вид","ru":"perfective aspect (result)","ex":"«Прочита́л» — соверше́нный вид."},{"en":"несоверше́нный вид","ru":"imperfective aspect (process / fact)","ex":"«Чита́л» — несоверше́нный вид."},{"en":"общефакти́ческое","ru":"the general-factual use (“ever?”)","ex":"«Ты чита́л?» — э́то общефакти́ческое."},{"en":"результа́т","ru":"result, outcome","ex":"Соверше́нный вид ука́зывает на результа́т."},{"en":"запре́т","ru":"a ban, prohibition","ex":"«Не входи́» — э́то запре́т."},{"en":"предостереже́ние","ru":"a warning","ex":"«Не упади́» — э́то предостереже́ние."},{"en":"речь идёт о","ru":"it's about, concerns (+ prep.)","ex":"Речь идёт о ва́жном вопро́се."},{"en":"дви́гаться","ru":"to move, progress","ex":"Де́ло не дви́жется."},{"en":"махну́ть","ru":"to give one wave (semelfactive)","ex":"Он махну́л руко́й и ушёл."},{"en":"кри́кнуть","ru":"to give one shout (semelfactive)","ex":"Она́ кри́кнула то́лько раз."},{"en":"достига́ть","ru":"to achieve, reach (+ gen.)","ex":"Мы достига́ем це́ли ша́г за ша́гом."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each form to the shade it carries.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Вы чита́ли Толсто́го?","general fact — have you ever?"],["Вы прочита́ли статью́?","result — did you finish?"],["Не упади́!","a warning — mind you don't"],["Он кри́кнул раз.","one instant (semelfactive)"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five questions on aspect's finest shades. If one bites, glance back at the rule.","items":[{"q":"“Have you ever read Tolstoy?” (do you know his work) —","opts":["Ты прочита́л Толсто́го?","Ты чита́л Толсто́го?","Ты чита́ешь Толсто́го?"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Я не прочита́л статью́» most precisely means…","opts":["I never read it at all","I didn't finish reading it","I don't read articles"],"answer":1},{"q":"A warm invitation at the door uses…","opts":["Проходи́те, сади́тесь.","Пройди́те, ся́дьте.","Вы обя́заны войти́."],"answer":0},{"q":"«Речь идёт о но́вом зако́не» means…","opts":["the speech is walking somewhere","it's about / the matter concerns a new law","the law is moving house"],"answer":1},{"q":"Which verb names <b>one single</b> act?","opts":["маха́л","махну́л","маха́л до́лго"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Эмма's note on rereading a classic — every shade of aspect from today is woven through it. Read it aloud.","title":"Ме́дленное чте́ние","sentences":["Ра́ньше я чита́ла Толсто́го бы́стро, то́лько ра́ди сюже́та.","Тепе́рь перечи́тываю — и де́ло дви́жется ме́дленно.","Ка́ждую фра́зу хо́чется не про́сто прочита́ть, а поня́ть до конца́.","Вре́мя лети́т, а я всё сижу́ над одно́й страни́цей.","Но и́менно так, ме́дленно, текст открыва́ется по-настоя́щему."],"translation":"I used to read Tolstoy fast, just for the plot. Now I'm rereading him — and it's going slowly. Each phrase I want not just to read but to understand fully. Time flies, and I'm still sitting over a single page. But it's exactly this way, slowly, that the text truly opens up."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — make aspect do the work.","prompt":"Write 3–4 sentences that play the aspect nuance on purpose: ask a <b>general-factual</b> question («Ты чита́л…?» = have you ever), state a <b>result</b> («я прочита́л…»), and add one aspect-based <b>instruction</b> — a warm invitation (проходи́, сади́сь) or a warning (не упади́!). Slip in one <b>figurative-motion</b> idiom (вре́мя лети́т, речь идёт о, де́ло дви́жется).","hint":"Remember the pair: чита́л = ever, прочита́л = finished; не входи́ = a ban, не упади́ = a warning 🙂","example":"Ты чита́л Че́хова? Я вот то́лько что прочита́л его́ расска́зы — и вре́мя лете́ло незаме́тно. Заходи́, сади́сь, поговори́м. То́лько не спеши́: речь идёт о настоя́щей литерату́ре."}},"17":{"day":17,"week":"03","themeRu":"Reading a text closely","themeEn":"Филологи́ческий ана́лиз те́кста","intro":"An old man sits by a window; autumn tires beyond the glass. You could translate that in a breath — and miss everything. Close reading (филологи́ческий ана́лиз) asks the other question: not <i>what</i> the passage says, but <i>how</i> it's built and <i>why</i>. It's a named ТРКИ-3 skill, and today the reading passage below is the whole lesson — Эмма takes apart the very excerpt she's translating.","introAudio":"An old man sits by a window; autumn tires beyond the glass. You could translate that in a breath — and miss everything. Close reading, filologichesky analiz, asks the other question: not what the passage says, but how it's built and why. It's a named TRKI-3 skill, and today the reading passage below is the whole lesson — Emma takes apart the very excerpt she's translating.","goals":["Run the four questions of <b>close reading</b>: style, images/devices, voice/stance, mood","Move from a named trope to its <b>effect</b> (сре́дства вырази́тельности — the means of expression)","Read the narrator's voice and the <b>author's stance</b> (sympathy, irony, sorrow)","State a small analysis aloud — <b>подчёркивает, создаёт, передаёт</b> — not a plot retelling"],"learned":["Ran the four questions of close reading: style, images/devices, voice/stance, mood","Moved from a named trope to its effect (сре́дства вырази́тельности)","Read the narrator's voice and the author's stance","Stated a small analysis aloud with подчёркивает / создаёт / передаёт — not a retelling"],"review":{"intro":"A quick pull on this week's tropes — the raw material of today's analysis — and then straight into a real passage.","introAudio":"A quick pull on this week's tropes — the raw material of today's analysis — and then straight into a real passage.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. This week's tropes.","q":"«Золото́е се́рдце» — which trope?","opts":["сравне́ние","мета́фора","олицетворе́ние"],"answer":1,"explain":"A hidden comparison with no «как» → <b>мета́фора</b> (metaphor)."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Приро́да засну́ла» — which trope?","opts":["олицетворе́ние","эпи́тет","сравне́ние"],"answer":0,"explain":"Life lent to the lifeless (nature “fell asleep”) → <b>олицетворе́ние</b> (personification)."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian term.","q":"A vivid, evaluative modifier like «золота́я о́сень» is an … (Russian term):","accept":["эпитет","эпитет."],"placeholder":"э…","explain":"<b>эпи́тет</b> (epithet) — a colouring, evaluative modifier, not mere information."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Analysis, not translation","title":"Reading a text <em>closely</em>: four questions","paras":["Yesterday you named the tropes. Today you use them — for <b>philological analysis</b> (филологи́ческий ана́лиз): taking a passage apart to see <i>how</i> it's built and <i>why</i>. It's a named ТРКИ-3 skill, and the trap is instinct: you want to translate. Analysis asks a different thing — not “what does it say?” but “how does it work?”","Four questions carry you through any excerpt: <b>What style / register?</b> (which of the five; does it mix them?) <b>What images and devices?</b> (tropes, word order, sound). <b>Whose voice, what stance?</b> (narrator, sympathy or irony). <b>What mood does it build, and by what means?</b>","You don't need every answer — you need to ask. A reader who only translates sees the surface; a reader who analyses sees the machine underneath."],"audio":"Yesterday you named the tropes. Today you use them — for philological analysis, filologichesky analiz: taking a passage apart to see how it's built and why. It's a named TRKI-3 skill, and the trap is instinct: you want to translate. Analysis asks a different thing — not 'what does it say?' but 'how does it work?' Four questions carry you through any excerpt: what style and register — which of the five, does it mix them? What images and devices — tropes, word order, sound? Whose voice, what stance — narrator, sympathy or irony? What mood does it build, and by what means? You don't need every answer — you need to ask. A reader who only translates sees the surface; a reader who analyses sees the machine underneath.","table":{"rows":[["Како́й стиль и реги́стр? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"какой стиль и регистр?\">🔊</button>","which style; any mixing?"],["Каки́е о́бразы и приёмы? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"какие образы и приёмы?\">🔊</button>","images and devices (tropes, word order)"],["Чей го́лос, кака́я пози́ция? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"чей голос, какая позиция?\">🔊</button>","voice and stance (irony? sympathy?)"],["Како́е настрое́ние и чем со́здано? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"какое настроение и чем создано?\">🔊</button>","mood, and by what means"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"Not translation, analysis: “By what means does the author build the sadness?”","en":"Не перево́д, а ана́лиз: «Каки́ми <b>сре́дствами</b> а́втор создаёт грусть?»","say":"Не перевод, а анализ: какими средствами автор создаёт грусть?"},{"ru":"The core question: “How is this made, and why?”","en":"Гла́вный вопро́с: «Как э́то <b>сде́лано</b> и заче́м?»","say":"Главный вопрос: как это сделано и зачем?"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"На вопро́с о приёмах ученик про́сто пересказа́л сюже́т.","right":"Не переска́зывай сюже́т — назови́ приёмы и их эффе́кт.","why":"Analysis isn't retelling the plot. Point to the devices (an epithet, a personification) and say what each one <i>does</i> to the reader."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"The goal of филологи́ческий ана́лиз is to…","opts":["translate the passage word for word","explain how the text is built and why","retell the plot briefly"],"answer":1,"explain":"Analysis asks <b>how</b> and <b>why</b> — the devices and their effect — not a translation or a retelling."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the missing English word.","q":"Close reading asks not <i>what</i> the text says, but <i>how</i> it's made and … . Type the word:","accept":["why"],"placeholder":"how and …","explain":"How it's made and <b>why</b> — the two questions at the heart of analysis."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Spotting the devices at work","title":"From named trope to living effect (<em>сре́дства вырази́тельности</em>)","paras":["In a real passage the tropes don't announce themselves — you find them. «Устава́ла о́сень» (autumn was growing tired) is <b>personification</b>; «жёлтый лист» a plain <b>epithet</b>; «па́дал… сло́вно не хоте́л расстава́ться» a <b>simile</b>. Together these are the <b>сре́дства вырази́тельности</b> — the means of expression.","Naming them is step one; step two is the <b>effect</b>. Why a tired autumn, why a reluctant leaf? Together they slow the world and lend it a quiet sorrow — the devices <i>build the mood</i>. Always pair the name with the effect: “personification (устава́ла) → the world feels weary, alive.”","Watch sound and rhythm too: short sentences hush a scene, a long flowing one carries it. In a good text nothing is accidental — that is the premise of close reading."],"audio":"In a real passage the tropes don't announce themselves — you find them. Ustavala osen, autumn was growing tired, is personification; zhyolty list, a plain epithet; padal slovno ne khotel rasstavatsya, a simile. Together these are the sredstva vyrazitelnosti — the means of expression. Naming them is step one; step two is the effect. Why a tired autumn, why a reluctant leaf? Together they slow the world and lend it a quiet sorrow — the devices build the mood. Always pair the name with the effect: personification, ustavala, makes the world feel weary and alive. Watch sound and rhythm too: short sentences hush a scene, a long flowing one carries it. In a good text nothing is accidental — that is the premise of close reading.","table":{"rows":[["Устава́ла о́сень <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"уставала осень\">🔊</button>","personification → a weary, living world"],["Жёлтый лист <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"жёлтый лист\">🔊</button>","epithet → a precise, autumnal image"],["Сло́вно не хоте́л расстава́ться <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"словно не хотел расставаться\">🔊</button>","simile → tenderness, reluctance"],["Коро́ткие фра́зы <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"короткие фразы\">🔊</button>","rhythm → a hushed, slow scene"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Name + effect: “The personification (time stood still) makes the moment feel frozen.”","en":"И́мя и эффе́кт: «Олицетворе́ние „вре́мя стоя́ло“ де́лает миг <b>засты́вшим</b>».","say":"Имя и эффект: олицетворение время стояло делает миг застывшим."},{"ru":"Devices build the mood: “Tired autumn and a slow leaf create a quiet sorrow.”","en":"Приёмы стро́ят настрое́ние: «Уста́лая о́сень и ме́дленный лист создаю́т ти́хую <b>грусть</b>».","say":"Приёмы строят настроение: усталая осень и медленный лист создают тихую грусть."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Здесь есть мета́фора» — и на э́том ана́лиз зако́нчился.","right":"«Здесь мета́фора „вре́мя стоя́ло“ — она́ остана́вливает вре́мя для геро́я».","why":"Spotting a device isn't the analysis — naming its <b>effect</b> is. Always answer “and what does it do?”"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Устава́ла о́сень» is a personification. A full analysis <b>also</b> says…","opts":["that it is grammatically correct","what it does — the world feels weary, alive","how to translate it into English"],"answer":1,"explain":"Name plus <b>effect</b>: the personification makes the world feel tired and alive. The name alone isn't analysis."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“The epithet creates a mood of sadness.”</b>","want":"Эпи́тет создаёт настрое́ние гру́сти","answer":"Эпи́тет создаёт настрое́ние гру́сти"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The voice behind the words","title":"Reading the narrator and the <em>а́вторская пози́ция</em>","paras":["A passage is never neutral. Ask whose eyes you're seeing through — a distant narrator, or one close to the hero's heart — and what the author <b>feels</b> about it. In today's excerpt the narrator sits beside the old man, warm and unhurried; the <b>stance</b> is tenderness, not pity.","Stance hides in word choice. Calling the shadows «ти́хие» and the friends «ста́рые» is sympathy; a colder writer would pick colder words. When surface praise sits over a petty act, that's <b>irony</b> — the gap is the judgement (you met this yesterday).","So read two layers at once: what happens, and how the teller colours it. The colouring — sympathy, irony, sorrow — is the author's position, and stating it is the heart of the analysis."],"audio":"A passage is never neutral. Ask whose eyes you're seeing through — a distant narrator, or one close to the hero's heart — and what the author feels about it. In today's excerpt the narrator sits beside the old man, warm and unhurried; the stance is tenderness, not pity. Stance hides in word choice. Calling the shadows tikhiye, quiet, and the friends staryye, old, is sympathy; a colder writer would pick colder words. When surface praise sits over a petty act, that's irony — the gap is the judgement. So read two layers at once: what happens, and how the teller colours it. The colouring — sympathy, irony, sorrow — is the author's position, and stating it is the heart of the analysis.","table":{"rows":[["Чей взгляд? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"чей взгляд?\">🔊</button>","narrator: distant, or close to the hero?"],["А́вторская пози́ция <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"авторская позиция\">🔊</button>","the stance: sympathy, irony, sorrow"],["Тёплые, ти́хие слова́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"тёплые, тихие слова\">🔊</button>","sympathy shows in the word choice"],["Иро́ния <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ирония\">🔊</button>","praise over a petty act = judgement"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"State the stance: “The narrator's voice is warm — he pities no one, he loves.”","en":"Назови́ пози́цию: «Го́лос а́втора тёплый — он не жале́ет, а <b>лю́бит</b>».","say":"Назови позицию: голос автора тёплый — он не жалеет, а любит."},{"ru":"Stance in word choice: “The quiet, gentle words give away his sympathy.”","en":"Пози́ция в слова́х: «Ти́хие, мя́гкие слова́ <b>выдаю́т</b> его́ сочу́вствие».","say":"Позиция в словах: тихие, мягкие слова выдают его сочувствие."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Автор про́сто опи́сывает ко́мнату и стари́к сиди́т».","right":"«А́втор не про́сто опи́сывает — он лю́бит э́того стари́ка, и э́то ви́дно по слова́м».","why":"A good text always has a stance. “Just describing” misses the point — find the sympathy or irony in the very choice of words."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"The narrator lingers on «ти́хих те́нях» and «ста́рых друзья́х» with warmth. The stance is…","opts":["cold irony","tender sympathy","no stance at all"],"answer":1,"explain":"The gentle, warm word choice reveals <b>sympathy</b> — the author loves this old man."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Го́лос а́втора тёплый: он не жале́ет геро́я, а лю́бит его́.","sub":"stating the author's stance in one clear line","want":"голос автора тёплый он не жалеет героя а любит его"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Saying it out loud","title":"A small analysis, in the right words","paras":["Now assemble the four answers into a few sentences — the way you'd speak in the exam. Lead with the mood, support it with named devices, then the stance: “The excerpt breathes a quiet sorrow. The author builds it with personification (устава́ла о́сень) and simile (сло́вно не хоте́л расстава́ться); the narrator's stance is warm, almost loving.”","Reach for the vocabulary of analysis: <b>о́браз</b> (image), <b>приём</b> / <b>сре́дство</b> (device), <b>настрое́ние</b> (mood), <b>а́вторская пози́ция</b> (stance), and the joint-verbs <b>подчёркивает</b> / <b>создаёт</b> / <b>передаёт</b> (emphasises / creates / conveys).","That's the whole skill: read past the literal, name the means, hear the voice, and say what it all does. Tomorrow the texts turn denser — but this method carries you through any of them."],"audio":"Now assemble the four answers into a few sentences — the way you'd speak in the exam. Lead with the mood, support it with named devices, then the stance: the excerpt breathes a quiet sorrow; the author builds it with personification, ustavala osen, and simile, slovno ne khotel rasstavatsya; the narrator's stance is warm, almost loving. Reach for the vocabulary of analysis: obraz, image; priyom or sredstvo, device; nastroyeniye, mood; avtorskaya pozitsiya, stance; and the joint-verbs podchyorkivayet, sozdayot, peredayot — emphasises, creates, conveys. That's the whole skill: read past the literal, name the means, hear the voice, and say what it all does. Tomorrow the texts turn denser — but this method carries you through any of them.","table":{"rows":[["о́браз, приём, сре́дство <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"образ, приём, средство\">🔊</button>","image, device, means"],["подчёркивает / создаёт / передаёт <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"подчёркивает, создаёт, передаёт\">🔊</button>","emphasises / creates / conveys"],["настрое́ние <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"настроение\">🔊</button>","mood, atmosphere"],["а́вторская пози́ция <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"авторская позиция\">🔊</button>","the stance to name at the end"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"A model line: “The epithet emphasises the loneliness, and the simile softens it.”","en":"Образе́ц: «Эпи́тет <b>подчёркивает</b> одино́чество, а сравне́ние его́ смягча́ет».","say":"Образец: эпитет подчёркивает одиночество, а сравнение его смягчает."},{"ru":"Conveying mood: “The short sentences convey silence and calm.”","en":"Настрое́ние: «Коро́ткие фра́зы <b>передаю́т</b> тишину́ и поко́й».","say":"Настроение: короткие фразы передают тишину и покой."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Текст о ста́ром челове́ке у окна́. Коне́ц».","right":"«Текст создаёт настрое́ние ти́хой гру́сти сре́дствами олицетворе́ния и сравне́ния».","why":"A summary isn't an analysis. Name the <b>mood</b> and the <b>means</b> that make it — that's what the exam rewards."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which sentence is an <b>analysis</b>, not a plot summary?","opts":["Стари́к сиде́л у окна́ и ду́мал.","Олицетворе́ние «устава́ла о́сень» создаёт настрое́ние ти́хой гру́сти.","Пото́м наступи́л ве́чер."],"answer":1,"explain":"Only the middle line names a <b>device</b> and its <b>effect</b>. The others just retell events."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“The author's stance is warm and tender.”</b>","want":"Пози́ция а́втора тёплая и не́жная","answer":"Пози́ция а́втора тёплая и не́жная"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Макси́м finds Эмма still on the same paragraph after hours — and gets a lesson in why. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Ты це́лый день над одни́м абза́цем. Что там разбира́ть?","ru":"You've spent all day on one paragraph. What's there to analyse?"},{"who":"A","en":"Смотри́: «устава́ла о́сень». О́сень не мо́жет устава́ть — э́то олицетворе́ние.","ru":"Look: “autumn was growing tired.” Autumn can't tire — that's personification."},{"who":"B","en":"И что э́то меня́ет?","ru":"And what does that change?"},{"who":"A","en":"Всё настрое́ние. Мир стано́вится живы́м и уста́лым, как сам стари́к.","ru":"The whole mood. The world becomes alive and weary, like the old man himself."},{"who":"B","en":"Ты как инжене́р — то́лько разбира́ешь не мото́р, а текст.","ru":"You're like an engineer — only you take apart a text, not an engine."},{"who":"A","en":"И́менно! Ана́лиз — э́то «как сде́лано и заче́м». Ка́ждая дета́ль рабо́тает.","ru":"Exactly! Analysis is “how it's made and why.” Every detail is working."},{"who":"B","en":"Тепе́рь я и коло́нку в газе́те бу́ду чита́ть по-друго́му.","ru":"Now I'll even read a newspaper column differently."},{"who":"A","en":"Вот э́то и есть настоя́щее чте́ние — не про́сто поня́ть, а услы́шать.","ru":"That's what real reading is — not just to understand, but to hear."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for taking a text apart — the toolkit of close reading. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"ана́лиз","ru":"analysis","ex":"Ана́лиз — э́то «как и заче́м»."},{"en":"филологи́ческий","ru":"philological","ex":"Э́то филологи́ческий разбо́р те́кста."},{"en":"разбира́ть","ru":"to analyse, take apart","ex":"Мы разбира́ем ка́ждую фра́зу."},{"en":"отры́вок","ru":"an excerpt, passage","ex":"Прочита́й э́тот отры́вок ещё раз."},{"en":"сре́дства вырази́тельности","ru":"means of expression (devices)","ex":"Тро́пы — э́то сре́дства вырази́тельности."},{"en":"подчёркивать","ru":"to emphasise, underline","ex":"Эпи́тет подчёркивает одино́чество."},{"en":"передава́ть","ru":"to convey, carry across","ex":"Ритм передаёт тишину́."},{"en":"создава́ть","ru":"to create, build","ex":"О́браз создаёт настрое́ние."},{"en":"настрое́ние","ru":"mood, atmosphere","ex":"Настрое́ние здесь — ти́хая грусть."},{"en":"пози́ция а́втора","ru":"the author's stance","ex":"Пози́ция а́втора здесь тёплая."},{"en":"подте́кст","ru":"subtext","ex":"В отры́вке есть глубо́кий подте́кст."},{"en":"вырази́тельный","ru":"expressive, vivid","ex":"Э́то о́чень вырази́тельный о́браз."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each line from today's excerpt to what it is.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its label — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["устава́ла о́сень","personification"],["жёлтый лист","epithet"],["сло́вно не хоте́л расстава́ться","simile"],["ти́хая грусть","the mood it builds"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five questions on close reading. If one bites, glance back at the rule — and at the excerpt.","items":[{"q":"The goal of <b>филологи́ческий ана́лиз</b> (close reading) is to…","opts":["translate the text word for word","explain how it's made and why","correct the author's grammar"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Устава́ла о́сень» is which device?","opts":["олицетворе́ние","сравне́ние","эпи́тет"],"answer":0},{"q":"Naming a device is only half the analysis. The other half is…","opts":["its effect on the reader / mood","its dictionary definition","the number of syllables"],"answer":0},{"q":"The narrator lingers warmly on the old man. The author's stance is…","opts":["mockery","tender sympathy","indifference"],"answer":1},{"q":"Which line is an <b>analysis</b>, not a retelling?","opts":["Стари́к сиде́л и молча́л.","Коро́ткие фра́зы передаю́т тишину́ и поко́й.","Пото́м он лёг спать."],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Today's centrepiece — a short literary passage to read <b>closely</b>. Read it aloud first for the mood; then take it apart with the four questions. (Эмма's own translation draft.)","title":"У окна́","sentences":["Стари́к до́лго сиде́л у окна́, не зажига́я све́та.","За стекло́м устава́ла о́сень: жёлтый лист па́дал ме́дленно, сло́вно не хоте́л расстава́ться с ве́ткой.","Ко́мната напо́лнилась ти́хими те́нями, и ка́ждая тень что-то напомина́ла.","Он ду́мал о лю́дях, кото́рые давно́ ка́нули в Ле́ту, и улыба́лся им, как ста́рым друзья́м.","Вре́мя не шло — оно́ стоя́ло ря́дом и молча́ло.","И в э́той тишине́ бы́ло бо́льше жи́зни, чем во всём шу́мном го́роде за окно́м."],"translation":"The old man sat a long time by the window, without lighting a lamp. Beyond the glass autumn was growing tired: a yellow leaf fell slowly, as if it did not want to part with its branch. The room filled with quiet shadows, and each shadow recalled something. He thought of people who had long since sunk into oblivion, and smiled at them as if at old friends. Time did not move — it stood beside him and kept silent. And in this silence there was more life than in the whole noisy city outside the window."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — be the analyst, not the translator.","prompt":"Write a short <b>analysis</b> (3–4 sentences, in Russian) of today's excerpt «У окна́». Name at least two <b>devices</b> and their effect (олицетворе́ние, эпи́тет, сравне́ние), state the <b>mood</b> (настрое́ние), and end with the <b>author's stance</b> (а́вторская пози́ция). Use the analysis verbs: подчёркивает, создаёт, передаёт.","hint":"Not a retelling — say how it's made and why. Formula: «Приём X создаёт настрое́ние Y; пози́ция а́втора — Z» 🙂","example":"Отры́вок создаёт настрое́ние ти́хой гру́сти. Олицетворе́ние «устава́ла о́сень» и сравне́ние «сло́вно не хоте́л расстава́ться» замедля́ют мир и де́лают его́ живы́м. Коро́ткие фра́зы передаю́т тишину́. Пози́ция а́втора тёплая: он не жале́ет стари́ка, а лю́бит его́."}},"18":{"day":18,"week":"03","themeRu":"Reading the law","themeEn":"Язы́к зако́на и постановле́ния","intro":"Yesterday you read a page of literature for its images — every word chosen to make you feel something. Today, the opposite pole of the language. Макси́м — Ива́н and Ле́на's brother, the engineer — just emailed Эмма a contract to look over before he signs, and it's three dense pages without one warm word in them. This is <b>официа́льно-делово́й</b> at its most extreme: the language of law. The goal today isn't to write it — it's to <b>read</b> it without flinching.","introAudio":"Yesterday you read a page of literature for its images — every word chosen to make you feel something. Today, the opposite pole of the language. Maksim — Ivan and Lena's brother, the engineer — just emailed Emma a contract to look over before he signs, and it's three dense pages without one warm word in them. This is ofitsialno-delovoy at its most extreme: the language of law. The goal today isn't to write it — it's to read it without flinching.","goals":["Decode the <b>referential machine</b>: <b>в соотве́тствии с, согла́сно, на основа́нии, в си́лу</b> and the cases they demand","Unfold <b>nominalization chains</b> — how law folds a whole clause into a stack of nouns","Read a legal period without fear: skip the inserts, find the <b>subject and verb</b> first","Keep your own production to the <b>safe day-5 clichés</b> — you READ the densest officialese, you don't imitate it"],"learned":["Decoded the referential machine: <b>в соотве́тствии с, согла́сно, на основа́нии, в си́лу</b>","Unfolded nominalization chains into their hidden verbs","Read a legal period by finding the subject and verb first","Kept production to the safe day-5 clichés — comprehension, not imitation"],"review":{"intro":"One minute back across the week — a trope, an aspect pair, a bookish compression — and that last one hands you straight into today's legalese.","introAudio":"One minute back across the week — a trope, an aspect pair, a bookish compression — and that last one hands you straight into today's legalese.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. Week-3 material — you've met all of it.","q":"«Го́род ещё спал» — the city is given a human action. Which trope (device)?","opts":["сравне́ние (simile)","олицетворе́ние (personification)","эпи́тет (epithet)"],"answer":1,"explain":"Giving a lifeless thing a living action — «го́род спал» — is <b>олицетворе́ние</b> (personification), from the literary style."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Вы чита́ли «Войну́ и мир»?» — the imperfective here asks…","opts":["whether you've <b>ever</b> read it (general fact)","whether you <b>finished</b> it this week (result)","whether you're reading it <b>right now</b>"],"answer":0,"explain":"The general-fact imperfective: «чита́ли?» = have you ever had the experience. «Вы прочита́ли?» would ask about a finished result."},{"type":"choice","q":"Bookish compression: «по́сле того́ как зако́н при́няли» folds into…","opts":["«по́сле приня́тия зако́на»","«когда́ зако́н при́мут»","«зако́н был при́нят по́сле»"],"answer":0,"explain":"The clause collapses into a nominalization: <b>по́сле приня́тия зако́на</b>. That is exactly the machinery today's legal style runs on."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The referential machine","title":"Law <em>points</em>, it doesn't explain: <em>в соотве́тствии с, согла́сно, на основа́нии</em>","paras":["Legal Russian rarely states a rule outright — it <b>points</b> to where the rule lives. A small set of referential prepositions does the pointing, and each demands its own case. <b>В соотве́тствии с</b> (in accordance with) takes the <b>instrumental</b>: <b>в соотве́тствии с пу́нктом 3</b>. <b>Согла́сно</b> (according to) takes the <b>dative</b>: <b>согла́сно статье́ 5</b>. <b>На основа́нии</b> (on the basis of) and <b>в си́лу</b> (by virtue of) take the <b>genitive</b>: <b>на основа́нии реше́ния</b>, <b>в си́лу зако́на</b>.","That is why a contract feels like a maze — it constantly sends you elsewhere. The sentence in front of you is often just a signpost: the real content sits in the clause it refers to.","One prescriptive trap: <b>согла́сно</b> governs the <b>dative</b> (согла́сно прика́зу), never the genitive. «Согла́сно прика́за» is a widespread bureaucratic error — a native editor like Эмма crosses it out on sight."],"audio":"Legal Russian rarely states a rule outright — it points to where the rule lives. A small set of referential prepositions does the pointing, and each demands its own case. V sootvetstvii s, in accordance with, takes the instrumental: v sootvetstvii s punktom 3. Soglasno, according to, takes the dative: soglasno statye 5. Na osnovanii, on the basis of, and v silu, by virtue of, take the genitive: na osnovanii resheniya, v silu zakona. That is why a contract feels like a maze — it constantly sends you elsewhere. One prescriptive trap: soglasno governs the dative, soglasno prikazu, never the genitive. Soglasno prikaza is a widespread bureaucratic error that a native editor crosses out on sight.","table":{"rows":[["в соотве́тствии с + <b>твор.</b> — пу́нктом <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в соответствии с пунктом\">🔊</button>","in accordance with (a clause) — instrumental"],["согла́сно + <b>дат.</b> — статье́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"согласно статье\">🔊</button>","according to (an article) — dative"],["на основа́нии + <b>род.</b> — реше́ния <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"на основании решения\">🔊</button>","on the basis of (a decision) — genitive"],["в си́лу + <b>род.</b> — зако́на <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в силу закона\">🔊</button>","by virtue of (the law) — genitive"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"In a contract: “In accordance with clause 4, the parties undertake to…”","en":"В догово́ре: «<b>В соотве́тствии с пу́нктом 4</b> сто́роны обя́зуются…»","say":"В договоре: в соответствии с пунктом 4 стороны обязуются."},{"ru":"Official: “The decision is made on the basis of the submitted documents.”","en":"Официа́льно: «Реше́ние принима́ется <b>на основа́нии</b> предста́вленных докуме́нтов».","say":"Официально: решение принимается на основании представленных документов."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"согла́сно прика́за","right":"согла́сно прика́зу","why":"<b>согла́сно</b> demands the <b>dative</b>: прика́зу, зако́ну, статье́. The genitive «согла́сно прика́за» is a common bureaucratic slip — correct-sounding to many, but non-standard."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"In a decree, the standard form is…","opts":["согла́сно распоряже́ния","согла́сно распоряже́нию","согла́сно распоряже́нием"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>согла́сно</b> + dative: <b>распоряже́нию</b>. The genitive form is the classic bureaucratic error."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the phrase. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"«В соотве́тствии с …» takes the instrumental. Type “with clause 7”:","accept":["пунктом 7","с пунктом 7","пунктом семь"],"placeholder":"пу́нктом…","explain":"<b>в соотве́тствии с + инструментальный</b>: с пу́нктом 7."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Nominalization chains","title":"A whole sentence, <em>folded into nouns</em>","paras":["The single densest feature of legal Russian: it turns actions into nouns and stacks them. «Кто-то не испо́лнил обяза́тельства» becomes <b>неисполне́ние обяза́тельств</b> — “non-fulfilment of obligations.” Then it chains genitive onto genitive: <b>в слу́чае наруше́ния усло́вий догово́ра</b> — “in the event of a violation of the terms of the contract.” Four nouns, one buried verb.","You met this on day 6 as <b>bookish compression</b> (по возвраще́нии, ввиду́). Law dials that same move to the maximum. To read it, <b>unfold</b>: find the action-noun and restore its hidden verb. <b>расторже́ние</b> → “someone terminates”; <b>вступле́ние в си́лу</b> → “it comes into force.”","Don't translate the stack word by word — decode what <i>happens</i> and <i>to what</i>, and the marble slab turns back into a sentence."],"audio":"The single densest feature of legal Russian: it turns actions into nouns and stacks them. Kto-to ne ispolnil obyazatelstva becomes neispolneniye obyazatelstv, non-fulfilment of obligations. Then it chains genitive onto genitive: v sluchaye narusheniya usloviy dogovora, in the event of a violation of the terms of the contract. Four nouns, one buried verb. You met this on day 6 as bookish compression. Law dials that same move to the maximum. To read it, unfold: find the action-noun and restore its hidden verb. Rastorzheniye means someone terminates; vstupleniye v silu means it comes into force. Don't translate the stack word by word — decode what happens and to what.","table":{"rows":[["неисполне́ние обяза́тельств <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"неисполнение обязательств\">🔊</button>","= “someone did not fulfil the obligations”"],["наруше́ние усло́вий догово́ра <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"нарушение условий договора\">🔊</button>","= “someone violated the terms of the contract”"],["расторже́ние догово́ра <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"расторжение договора\">🔊</button>","= “the contract is terminated”"],["с моме́нта подписа́ния <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"с момента подписания\">🔊</button>","= “from the moment (it) is signed”"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Legal: “Failure to pay entails a penalty.” (unfold: if you don't pay, you pay a fine)","en":"Юр.: «<b>Неупла́та</b> влечёт за собо́й неусто́йку».","say":"Юридически: неуплата влечёт за собой неустойку."},{"ru":"Legal: “Upon expiry of the term the agreement is terminated.”","en":"Юр.: «По <b>истече́нии сро́ка</b> соглаше́ние расторга́ется».","say":"Юридически: по истечении срока соглашение расторгается."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Reading «неисполне́ние обяза́тельств» as three unrelated words","right":"Unfolding it: «(кто-то) не испо́лнил обяза́тельства»","why":"The nominal stack hides a subject and a verb. Read it as an event — <b>who</b> does <b>what</b> — not as a list of dictionary entries. That's the whole skill of legal reading."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«В слу́чае наруше́ния сро́ков» unfolds to…","opts":["“in a case of some deadlines”","“if the deadlines are violated”","“the deadlines are a case of violation”"],"answer":1,"explain":"The nominal chain = a condition: <b>if someone violates the deadlines</b>. Restore the hidden verb and the sentence reappears."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which is the folded, legal-style version of «по́сле того́ как догово́р подпи́шут»?","opts":["«по́сле подписа́ния догово́ра»","«когда́ догово́р подпи́шется»","«догово́р подпи́шут по́сле»"],"answer":0,"explain":"Clause → nominalization: <b>по́сле подписа́ния догово́ра</b>. Same meaning, folded into nouns — the legal norm."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Find the spine","title":"Don't panic in a long period: find the <em>subject and the verb</em>","paras":["A legal sentence can run forty words, but its grammatical spine is tiny. The trick: mentally bracket every referential insert (<b>в соотве́тствии с пу́нктом 3 настоя́щего догово́ра</b>) and every nominal chain, then ask the two-word question — <b>кто? + что де́лает?</b> Usually the answer is short: <b>Сто́роны обя́зуются…</b>","It helps that law reuses a tiny closed set of verbs. Learn these six and most clauses open up: <b>обя́зуется</b> (undertakes), <b>вступа́ет в си́лу</b> (comes into force), <b>влечёт за собо́й</b> (entails), <b>утра́чивает си́лу</b> (loses force), <b>несёт отве́тственность</b> (bears liability), <b>впра́ве</b> (has the right to).","Read for the spine first, the inserts second. The fear comes from trying to hold all forty words at once; the spine is where the meaning actually lives."],"audio":"A legal sentence can run forty words, but its grammatical spine is tiny. The trick: mentally bracket every referential insert — v sootvetstvii s punktom 3 nastoyashchego dogovora — and every nominal chain, then ask the two-word question: kto, who, plus chto delayet, does what. Usually the answer is short: storony obyazuyutsya, the parties undertake. It helps that law reuses a tiny closed set of verbs. Learn these six and most clauses open up: obyazuyetsya, undertakes; vstupayet v silu, comes into force; vlechyot za soboy, entails; utrachivayet silu, loses force; nesyot otvetstvennost, bears liability; vprave, has the right to. Read for the spine first, the inserts second.","table":{"rows":[["обя́зуется <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"обязуется\">🔊</button>","undertakes, is obliged to"],["вступа́ет в си́лу <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"вступает в силу\">🔊</button>","comes into force"],["влечёт за собо́й <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"влечёт за собой\">🔊</button>","entails, brings about"],["несёт отве́тственность <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"несёт ответственность\">🔊</button>","bears liability / is liable"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"Spine only: “The parties undertake…” (everything else is an insert)","en":"То́лько спина́: «<b>Сто́роны обя́зуются</b>…»","say":"Только спина: стороны обязуются."},{"ru":"Legal: “The agreement comes into force from the day of its signing.”","en":"Юр.: «Соглаше́ние <b>вступа́ет в си́лу</b> со дня его́ подписа́ния».","say":"Юридически: соглашение вступает в силу со дня его подписания."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Trying to translate a 40-word clause left to right, word by word","right":"Bracketing the inserts, then reading the spine: «Сто́роны обя́зуются…»","why":"Legal reading is not linear. Find <b>who does what</b> first; hang the referential inserts on afterwards. Otherwise the sentence drowns you."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"In «В соотве́тствии с пу́нктом 2 настоя́щего догово́ра сторона́ несёт отве́тственность», the spine is…","opts":["«в соотве́тствии с пу́нктом 2»","«сторона́ несёт отве́тственность»","«настоя́щего догово́ра»"],"answer":1,"explain":"Bracket the referential insert; the spine is <b>сторона́ несёт отве́тственность</b> — who + does what."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and read it out loud — evenly, like an official reading a clause.","target":"Настоя́щий догово́р вступа́ет в си́лу с моме́нта подписа́ния.","sub":"a legal-style sentence — read it flat and even, no drama","want":"настоящий договор вступает в силу с момента подписания"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Read everything, write little","title":"Comprehend the full legalese — but <em>produce</em> only the safe clichés","paras":["Here is the honest boundary of today. You should <b>understand</b> «влечёт за собо́й отве́тственность, предусмо́тренную де́йствующим законода́тельством» — but you are not asked to <b>write</b> it. Full legal drafting is a specialist trade; even educated natives get the formulas wrong, which is exactly why Макси́м asks Эмма to check his contract.","When you yourself need the official register, stay on the safe clichés you already own from day 5: <b>настоя́щим уведомля́ю</b>, <b>прошу́ предоста́вить</b>, <b>в связи́ с</b>, <b>на основа́нии</b>, <b>в тече́ние</b>. These carry you through a formal letter or application without pretending to be a lawyer.","So today's C1 skill is <b>comprehension</b>: read the densest officialese calmly, extract the meaning, and leave the drafting to the professionals. Recognise it — don't imitate it."],"audio":"Here is the honest boundary of today. You should understand vlechyot za soboy otvetstvennost, predusmotrennuyu deystvuyushchim zakonodatelstvom — but you are not asked to write it. Full legal drafting is a specialist trade; even educated natives get the formulas wrong, which is why Maksim asks Emma to check his contract. When you yourself need the official register, stay on the safe clichés you already own from day 5: nastoyashchim uvedomlyayu, proshu predostavit, v svyazi s, na osnovanii, v techeniye. So today's C1 skill is comprehension: read the densest officialese calmly, extract the meaning, and leave the drafting to the professionals.","examples":[{"ru":"You READ this (law): “…entails liability provided for by the legislation in force.”","en":"Чита́ешь: «…влечёт за собо́й отве́тственность, предусмо́тренную де́йствующим законода́тельством».","say":"Читаешь: влечёт за собой ответственность, предусмотренную действующим законодательством."},{"ru":"You WRITE this (safe official): “Herewith I notify you that…”","en":"Пи́шешь: «<b>Настоя́щим уведомля́ю</b>, что…»","say":"Пишешь: настоящим уведомляю, что."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Дру́гу: Ты впра́ве вступи́ть в си́лу и понести́ отве́тственность за пи́ццу».","right":"«Дру́гу: С тебя́ пи́цца — договори́лись?»","why":"Lawyer-speak (<b>впра́ве, вступи́ть в си́лу, понести́ отве́тственность</b>) dropped into a friendly chat is absurd. You read that register; you don't wear it. Match the situation."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"You need to write a normal formal request. Which is appropriate — not overblown legalese?","opts":["«Настоя́щим прошу́ предоста́вить спра́вку».","«Сторона́ впра́ве в си́лу зако́на истре́бовать спра́вку».","«Дай спра́вку, ла́дно?»"],"answer":0,"explain":"The safe day-5 cliché <b>настоя́щим прошу́ предоста́вить</b> fits a formal request. The second is pretend-legalese; the third is too casual."},{"type":"choice","q":"Today's real skill with the language of law is…","opts":["to draft your own contracts","to read and understand it calmly","to use в си́лу in everyday chat"],"answer":1,"explain":"Comprehension, not imitation: read the densest officialese without fear and extract the meaning."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Макси́м has forwarded Эмма a work contract from a foreign partner and asks her to read it before he signs. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Э́мма, посмотри́, пожа́луйста. Я чита́ю — и не понима́ю ни одного́ предложе́ния.","ru":"Emma, take a look, please. I read it — and I don't understand a single sentence."},{"who":"A","en":"Норма́льно. Э́то не для того́, что́бы понима́ть с пе́рвого ра́за. Смотри́: во́т спина́.","ru":"That's normal. It isn't written to be understood on the first pass. Look: here's the spine."},{"who":"B","en":"Кака́я спина́? Тут со́рок слов в одно́м предложе́нии.","ru":"What spine? There are forty words in one sentence."},{"who":"A","en":"Убира́ем «в соотве́тствии с пу́нктом три» — э́то ссы́лка. Остаётся: «сто́роны обя́зуются».","ru":"We drop “in accordance with clause three” — that's a reference. What's left is: “the parties undertake.”"},{"who":"B","en":"А «неисполне́ние обяза́тельств влечёт отве́тственность»?","ru":"And “non-fulfilment of obligations entails liability”?"},{"who":"A","en":"По-челове́чески: не сде́лаешь — отве́тишь. Про́сто зако́н говори́т существи́тельными.","ru":"In human terms: if you don't do it, you answer for it. The law just speaks in nouns."},{"who":"B","en":"И ты во всём э́том разбира́ешься?","ru":"And you make sense of all this?"},{"who":"A","en":"Чита́ть — да. Писа́ть тако́е я не беру́сь — э́то к юри́сту.","ru":"Reading it — yes. Writing it I won't take on — that's for a lawyer."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve keys to the language of law — enough to read a clause and unfold it. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"зако́н","ru":"law, statute","ex":"Э́то предусмо́трено зако́ном."},{"en":"постановле́ние","ru":"decree, resolution","ex":"Вы́шло но́вое постановле́ние."},{"en":"догово́р","ru":"contract, agreement","ex":"Сто́роны подписа́ли догово́р."},{"en":"сторона́","ru":"party (to an agreement)","ex":"Ка́ждая сторона́ несёт отве́тственность."},{"en":"пункт","ru":"clause, point, item","ex":"Смотри́ пункт четы́ре."},{"en":"в соотве́тствии с","ru":"in accordance with (+ instr.)","ex":"В соотве́тствии с пу́нктом три…"},{"en":"согла́сно","ru":"according to (+ dat.)","ex":"Согла́сно статье́ пять…"},{"en":"на основа́нии","ru":"on the basis of (+ gen.)","ex":"Реше́ние при́нято на основа́нии докуме́нтов."},{"en":"обя́зуется","ru":"undertakes, is obliged to","ex":"Сторона́ обя́зуется вы́полнить рабо́ту."},{"en":"вступа́ть в си́лу","ru":"to come into force","ex":"Зако́н вступа́ет в си́лу с января́."},{"en":"отве́тственность","ru":"liability, responsibility","ex":"За э́то предусмо́трена отве́тственность."},{"en":"расторже́ние","ru":"termination (of a contract)","ex":"Расторже́ние возмо́жно по соглаше́нию сторо́н."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each legal phrase to its plain-English meaning.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["вступа́ет в си́лу","comes into force"],["влечёт за собо́й","entails, brings about"],["на основа́нии реше́ния","on the basis of the decision"],["неисполне́ние обяза́тельств","non-fulfilment of obligations"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick checks on the language of law. If one bites, the rule above has it.","items":[{"q":"«согла́сно» governs the…","opts":["genitive (согла́сно зако́на)","dative (согла́сно зако́ну)","instrumental (согла́сно зако́ном)"],"answer":1},{"q":"«в соотве́тствии с …» is followed by the…","opts":["instrumental (пу́нктом)","genitive (пу́нкта)","dative (пу́нкту)"],"answer":0},{"q":"«неисполне́ние обяза́тельств» really means…","opts":["a list of obligations","someone did not fulfil the obligations","the obligations were fulfilled"],"answer":1},{"q":"In a long legal sentence, read first for…","opts":["every referential insert, in order","the subject and the verb (the spine)","the longest noun"],"answer":1},{"q":"Today's skill with legal Russian is to…","opts":["read and understand it calmly","write your own laws","use it with friends"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"A short stretch of a real contract — dense on purpose. Read it aloud, then unfold each sentence into “who does what.”","title":"Из те́кста догово́ра","sentences":["Настоя́щий догово́р вступа́ет в си́лу с моме́нта его́ подписа́ния сторона́ми.","В соотве́тствии с пу́нктом четы́ре сто́роны обя́зуются испо́лнить приня́тые обяза́тельства в устано́вленный срок.","Неисполне́ние усло́вий догово́ра влечёт за собо́й отве́тственность, предусмо́тренную де́йствующим законода́тельством.","Расторже́ние догово́ра допуска́ется по соглаше́нию сторо́н ли́бо на основа́нии реше́ния суда́.","Во всём остально́м, что не предусмо́трено настоя́щим догово́ром, сто́роны руково́дствуются норма́ми гражда́нского ко́декса."],"translation":"This contract comes into force from the moment it is signed by the parties. In accordance with clause four, the parties undertake to fulfil the obligations they have assumed within the established term. Non-fulfilment of the terms of the contract entails liability as provided for by the legislation in force. Termination of the contract is permitted by agreement of the parties or on the basis of a court decision. In all else not provided for by this contract, the parties are governed by the norms of the civil code."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — write in the official register you actually own, not full legalese.","prompt":"Write a short <b>official notice</b> (2–3 sentences) in Russian — for example, that a meeting is postponed or that a document is requested. Use the safe day-5 clichés (<b>настоя́щим уведомля́ю</b>, <b>в связи́ с</b>, <b>прошу́</b>, <b>на основа́нии</b>). Do NOT try to write contract-style legalese — that's the whole point.","hint":"Comprehension of law ≠ imitation of it. Lean on the formulas you own: настоя́щим уведомля́ю, что…; в связи́ с…; прошу́ предоста́вить… 🙂","example":"Настоя́щим уведомля́ю, что в связи́ с техни́ческими причи́нами собра́ние перено́сится на сле́дующую неде́лю. Прошу́ подтверди́ть получе́ние настоя́щего уведомле́ния."}},"19":{"day":19,"week":"03","themeRu":"Boiling a source down","themeEn":"Рефера́т, те́зисы, аннота́ция","intro":"Эмма's editor handed her a twelve-page academic article this morning and asked for half a page by lunch — not her opinion of it, not a chatty retelling, just its content, shrunk and objective. That's a <b>рефера́т</b>, and it's one of the hardest quiet skills in the language: to compress someone else's thinking without leaking your own voice into it. Today you learn the three sizes of “less” — <b>аннота́ция, рефера́т, те́зисы</b> — and the clichés that make them sound like Russian.","introAudio":"Emma's editor handed her a twelve-page academic article this morning and asked for half a page by lunch — not her opinion of it, not a chatty retelling, just its content, shrunk and objective. That's a referat, and it's one of the hardest quiet skills in the language: to compress someone else's thinking without leaking your own voice into it. Today you learn the three sizes of less — annotatsiya, referat, tezisy — and the clichés that make them sound like Russian.","goals":["Tell apart the three genres of compression: <b>аннота́ция</b> (abstract), <b>рефера́т</b> (précis), <b>те́зисы</b> (key points)","Wield the metatext clichés: <b>в статье́ рассма́тривается…, а́втор прихо́дит к вы́воду…</b>","Hold <b>objectivity</b>: report the author's content, add none of your own opinion","Compress by craft: cut the examples, keep the thesis, nominalize"],"learned":["Told apart аннота́ция, рефера́т and те́зисы","Used the metatext clichés to report a text (в статье́ рассма́тривается…, а́втор прихо́дит к вы́воду…)","Held objectivity — the author's content, none of my own opinion","Compressed by craft: cut the examples, kept the thesis, nominalized"],"review":{"intro":"A minute back on the language of law from yesterday, plus the impersonal science voice — because objectivity is exactly what today needs.","introAudio":"A minute back on the language of law from yesterday, plus the impersonal science voice — because objectivity is exactly what today needs.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer — yesterday's legalese.","q":"The standard legal form is…","opts":["согла́сно догово́ра","согла́сно догово́ру","согла́сно догово́ром"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>согла́сно</b> takes the dative: <b>догово́ру</b>. The genitive is the classic bureaucratic error."},{"type":"choice","q":"Unfold the nominal chain «наруше́ние усло́вий»:","opts":["“a case about some conditions”","“someone violated the conditions”","“the conditions are fine”"],"answer":1,"explain":"The action-noun hides a verb: <b>someone violated the conditions</b>. Same skill you'll use to compress a source today."},{"type":"choice","q":"The scientific style avoids «я ду́маю, что…». Instead it says…","opts":["«представля́ется, что…»","«мне ка́жется, чо…»","«я счита́ю круто́, что…»"],"answer":0,"explain":"Impersonal and objective: <b>представля́ется, что…</b> — precisely the voice a рефера́т keeps."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Three genres of compression","title":"<em>Аннота́ция, рефера́т, те́зисы</em> — three sizes of “less”","paras":["Russian has three named ways to shrink a text, and they are not interchangeable. An <b>аннота́ция</b> (abstract) is the shortest — three or four sentences saying <i>what</i> the text is about and <i>for whom</i>, like a back-cover blurb. It names the subject; it does not deliver the content.","A <b>рефера́т</b> (précis) goes further: it restates the actual content — the author's claims and conclusions — compressed. Their thoughts, your economy. A set of <b>те́зисы</b> (theses / key points) is the bare skeleton: the argument reduced to its numbered bones.","All three obey one law: they carry <b>someone else's content, objectively</b>. Choose the size by the task — a blurb, a précis, or a skeleton — but never smuggle in your own opinion."],"audio":"Russian has three named ways to shrink a text, and they are not interchangeable. An annotatsiya, abstract, is the shortest — three or four sentences saying what the text is about and for whom, like a back-cover blurb. It names the subject; it does not deliver the content. A referat, précis, goes further: it restates the actual content, the author's claims and conclusions, compressed. Their thoughts, your economy. A set of tezisy, theses, is the bare skeleton: the argument reduced to its numbered bones. All three obey one law: they carry someone else's content, objectively.","table":{"rows":[["аннота́ция <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"аннотация\">🔊</button>","abstract — 3–4 sentences: what it's about, for whom"],["рефера́т <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"реферат\">🔊</button>","précis — the compressed content itself"],["те́зисы <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"тезисы\">🔊</button>","theses — the key points as a skeleton"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"Abstract-style: “The article is devoted to the problem of the megacity.”","en":"Аннота́ция: «Статья́ <b>посвящена́</b> пробле́ме мегапо́лиса».","say":"Аннотация: статья посвящена проблеме мегаполиса."},{"ru":"Précis-style: “The author examines the causes and proposes a solution.”","en":"Рефера́т: «А́втор <b>рассма́тривает</b> причи́ны и предлага́ет реше́ние».","say":"Реферат: автор рассматривает причины и предлагает решение."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Аннота́ция на две страни́цы с переска́зом всех при́меров","right":"Аннота́ция в три-четы́ре предложе́ния: о чём текст и для кого́","why":"An <b>аннота́ция</b> names the subject in a few lines — it is not a full retelling. If you're delivering the content, that's already a <b>рефера́т</b>. Match the genre to the size."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"You need three sentences saying what a book is about, for a catalogue. That's an…","opts":["аннота́ция","рефера́т","о́тзыв"],"answer":0,"explain":"A short “what it's about, for whom” is an <b>аннота́ция</b>. A рефера́т delivers the content; an о́тзыв adds evaluation."},{"type":"choice","q":"The bare skeleton of an argument as numbered key points is…","opts":["аннота́ция","те́зисы","цита́та"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>Те́зисы</b> — the argument reduced to its bones. A цита́та is a direct quotation."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The metatext toolkit","title":"Talk <em>about</em> the text: «в статье́ рассма́тривается…»","paras":["A small closed set of impersonal, third-person clichés lets you report a text without stepping into the frame yourself. These are the <b>-ся</b> passives and impersonals you built at B2 and on day 4, now doing real work.","Openers: <b>Статья́ посвящена́…</b> (the article is devoted to…), <b>В статье́ рассма́тривается…</b> (the article examines…), <b>Речь идёт о…</b> (it concerns…). Body: <b>А́втор анализи́рует…</b>, <b>Отмеча́ется, что…</b> (it is noted that…), <b>Осо́бое внима́ние уделя́ется…</b> (particular attention is given to…). Closer: <b>А́втор прихо́дит к вы́воду, что…</b> (the author concludes that…).","Notice the grammar: the author is <b>third person</b> or gone entirely into an impersonal verb. That is what keeps a рефера́т objective — the machinery does the distancing for you."],"audio":"A small closed set of impersonal, third-person clichés lets you report a text without stepping into the frame yourself. These are the -sya passives and impersonals you built at B2 and on day 4, now doing real work. Openers: statya posvyashchena, the article is devoted to; v statye rassmatrivayetsya, the article examines; rech idyot o, it concerns. Body: avtor analiziruyet; otmechayetsya chto, it is noted that; osoboye vnimaniye udelyayetsya, particular attention is given to. Closer: avtor prikhodit k vyvodu chto, the author concludes that. The author is third person or gone entirely into an impersonal verb — that is what keeps a referat objective.","table":{"rows":[["Статья́ посвящена́… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"статья посвящена\">🔊</button>","opener: the article is devoted to…"],["В статье́ рассма́тривается… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в статье рассматривается\">🔊</button>","opener: the article examines…"],["Осо́бое внима́ние уделя́ется… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"особое внимание уделяется\">🔊</button>","body: particular attention is given to…"],["А́втор прихо́дит к вы́воду, что… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"автор приходит к выводу что\">🔊</button>","closer: the author concludes that…"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"Scientific report: “The paper analyses the reasons for the decline.”","en":"Нау́чно: «В рабо́те <b>анализи́руются</b> причи́ны спа́да».","say":"Научно: в работе анализируются причины спада."},{"ru":"Closer: “The author concludes that the question remains open.”","en":"«А́втор <b>прихо́дит к вы́воду</b>, что вопро́с остаётся откры́тым».","say":"Автор приходит к выводу, что вопрос остаётся открытым."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Ну, а́втор тут пи́шет всё про тра́нспорт и так да́лее».","right":"«В статье́ рассма́триваются пробле́мы городско́го тра́нспорта».","why":"The chatty «ну… и так да́лее» is the conversational style leaking in. The metatext cliché <b>в статье́ рассма́триваются</b> keeps the neutral-scientific register a рефера́т needs."}],"tasks":[{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the missing word. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"Complete the closer: «А́втор прихо́дит к …, что рефо́рма необходи́ма». (the author concludes)","accept":["выводу","к выводу"],"placeholder":"прихо́дит к …","explain":"The fixed closer: <b>а́втор прихо́дит к вы́воду, что…</b> — “the author concludes that…”."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which opener is proper metatext for a рефера́т?","opts":["«Мне о́чень понра́вилась э́та статья́».","«Статья́ посвящена́ вопро́су экологи́и».","«Дава́й я расскажу́, про что тут»."],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>Статья́ посвящена́…</b> is the objective, third-person metatext. The first is opinion; the third is chatty retelling."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The objectivity trap","title":"A рефера́т is <em>not</em> a retelling and <em>not</em> your opinion","paras":["This is the trap the exam is built to catch. English speakers slide one of two ways: into a chatty <b>retelling</b> («и вот а́втор говори́т, что…, а пото́м…») or into their own <b>verdict</b> («я согла́сна, что…, э́то ва́жно»). A рефера́т does neither.","It stays in the third person about the author, reports what the text <b>claims</b>, and adds <b>zero evaluation</b>. Your own voice is invisible; the author's content is the entire substance. If you catch yourself writing «я ду́маю» or «мне понра́вилось», you've switched genres.","Save the verdict for an <b>о́тзыв</b> (a response — day 25), which is <i>built</i> to carry evaluation. Today the discipline is the opposite: report faithfully, judge nothing."],"audio":"This is the trap the exam is built to catch. English speakers slide one of two ways: into a chatty retelling — i vot avtor govorit chto, a potom — or into their own verdict — ya soglasna chto, eto vazhno. A referat does neither. It stays in the third person about the author, reports what the text claims, and adds zero evaluation. Your own voice is invisible; the author's content is the entire substance. If you catch yourself writing ya dumayu or mne ponravilos, you've switched genres. Save the verdict for an otzyv, a response — that is built to carry evaluation. Today the discipline is the opposite: report faithfully, judge nothing.","table":{"rows":[["НЕ так: «Мне понра́вилось, что…» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"мне понравилось что\">🔊</button>","opinion — belongs in an о́тзыв, not a рефера́т"],["НЕ так: «И вот а́втор говори́т…» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"и вот автор говорит\">🔊</button>","chatty retelling — wrong register"],["ТАК: «А́втор отмеча́ет, что…» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"автор отмечает что\">🔊</button>","objective metatext — right"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"Objective (right): “The author emphasises the role of education.”","en":"Объекти́вно: «А́втор <b>подчёркивает</b> роль образова́ния».","say":"Объективно: автор подчёркивает роль образования."},{"ru":"Opinion (wrong genre): “I really liked this idea.”","en":"Мне́ние (не сюда́): «Мне о́чень понра́вилась э́та мысль».","say":"Мнение не сюда: мне очень понравилась эта мысль."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Я счита́ю, что а́втор прав, и э́то о́чень интере́сно».","right":"«А́втор счита́ет, что рефо́рма необходи́ма».","why":"«Я счита́ю… интере́сно» is <b>your</b> evaluation — that's an о́тзыв. A рефера́т reports the author's position in the third person: <b>а́втор счита́ет, что…</b>."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which sentence belongs in a рефера́т (objective, no opinion)?","opts":["«А́втор анализи́рует причи́ны кри́зиса».","«Э́то, по-мо́ему, гениа́льная статья́».","«Мне ка́жется, а́втор немно́го не прав»."],"answer":0,"explain":"Only the first is objective metatext. The other two carry <b>your</b> verdict — that's an о́тзыв, a different genre."},{"type":"choice","q":"Evaluation and “I think” belong in a(n) …, not a рефера́т.","opts":["о́тзыв (response)","аннота́ция","те́зисы"],"answer":0,"explain":"An <b>о́тзыв</b> is built to carry judgement. The other genres, like the рефера́т, stay objective."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · The craft of compressing","title":"Cut the examples, keep the <em>thesis</em>","paras":["Now the actual shrinking. First, <b>cut</b>: drop illustrations, digressions, repeated points and stray numbers. What survives is the central <b>thesis</b> and the <b>conclusion</b> — the two things a reader of your рефера́т truly needs.","Then <b>compress the survivors</b> with tools you already own: nominalization (day 6, day 18) folds a clause into a noun, and a single even scientific register holds it together. A wordy paragraph of examples becomes one sentence of claim.","The finished shape is small and repeatable: <b>what question</b> the text asks + <b>what the author claims</b> + <b>what they conclude</b>. Twelve pages, honestly rendered, in a paragraph."],"audio":"Now the actual shrinking. First, cut: drop illustrations, digressions, repeated points and stray numbers. What survives is the central thesis and the conclusion — the two things a reader of your referat truly needs. Then compress the survivors with tools you already own: nominalization folds a clause into a noun, and a single even scientific register holds it together. A wordy paragraph of examples becomes one sentence of claim. The finished shape is small and repeatable: what question the text asks, plus what the author claims, plus what they conclude. Twelve pages, honestly rendered, in a paragraph.","examples":[{"ru":"Wordy original (many examples): buses, trolleybuses, trams… everyone uses them…","en":"Бы́ло: «Мно́гие, наприме́р студе́нты и пожилы́е, ка́ждый день е́здят на авто́бусах и трамва́ях…»","say":"Было: многие, например студенты и пожилые, каждый день ездят на автобусах и трамваях."},{"ru":"Compressed (thesis kept): “Public transport plays an important role in city life.”","en":"Ста́ло: «Обще́ственный тра́нспорт <b>игра́ет ва́жную роль</b> в жи́зни го́рода».","say":"Стало: общественный транспорт играет важную роль в жизни города."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Оставля́ть все приме́ры и ци́фры, но вы́бросить гла́вный те́зис","right":"Вы́бросить приме́ры, сохрани́ть те́зис и вы́вод","why":"Compression keeps the <b>argument</b>, not the decoration. Examples and stray numbers are the first to go; the thesis and conclusion are the last."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"When compressing a source, the first thing to cut is…","opts":["the author's main thesis","the illustrations and stray examples","the conclusion"],"answer":1,"explain":"Cut the decoration first — <b>examples, digressions, stray numbers</b>. Keep the thesis and the conclusion."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and read this metatext sentence aloud — even, neutral, objective.","target":"В статье́ рассма́триваются причи́ны и после́дствия явле́ния.","sub":"a рефера́т opener — flat, objective, no colour","want":"в статье рассматриваются причины и последствия явления"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Эмма is compressing an article at Ле́на's café; Ка́тя, curious, peeks at the screen. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Ты уже́ полдня́ сиди́шь над одно́й статьёй. Про что она́?","ru":"You've been sitting over one article for half a day. What's it about?"},{"who":"A","en":"Про городско́й тра́нспорт. Мне на́до сжать двена́дцать страни́ц в оди́н абза́ц.","ru":"About urban transport. I have to compress twelve pages into one paragraph."},{"who":"B","en":"И что, ты пи́шешь, понра́вилось и́ли нет?","ru":"So what, do you write whether you liked it or not?"},{"who":"A","en":"Нет, в э́том всё и де́ло. Рефера́т — не мне́ние. То́лько то, что утвержда́ет а́втор.","ru":"No, that's the whole point. A précis isn't an opinion. Only what the author claims."},{"who":"B","en":"А е́сли я не согла́сна с а́втором?","ru":"And if I disagree with the author?"},{"who":"A","en":"Тогда́ ты де́ржишь язы́к за зуба́ми. Твоё мне́ние — э́то уже́ друго́й жанр, о́тзыв.","ru":"Then you hold your tongue. Your opinion is already a different genre — a response."},{"who":"B","en":"Сло́жно писа́ть и молча́ть одновре́менно.","ru":"It's hard to write and stay silent at the same time."},{"who":"A","en":"В э́том и мастерство́: а́втор говори́т, а меня́ как бы нет.","ru":"That's exactly the craft: the author speaks, and I'm as if not there."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for shrinking a source objectively. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"рефера́т","ru":"précis, abstract-paper","ex":"Я пишу́ рефера́т статьи́."},{"en":"аннота́ция","ru":"abstract, annotation","ex":"Аннота́ция коро́ткая — три предложе́ния."},{"en":"те́зисы","ru":"theses, key points","ex":"Он изложи́л те́зисы докла́да."},{"en":"исто́чник","ru":"source","ex":"На́до указа́ть исто́чник."},{"en":"содержа́ние","ru":"content","ex":"Рефера́т передаёт содержа́ние статьи́."},{"en":"сжать","ru":"to compress, condense","ex":"На́до сжать текст в абза́ц."},{"en":"а́втор","ru":"author","ex":"А́втор прихо́дит к вы́воду."},{"en":"рассма́тривать","ru":"to examine, consider","ex":"В статье́ рассма́тривается вопро́с."},{"en":"вы́вод","ru":"conclusion","ex":"А́втор де́лает ва́жный вы́вод."},{"en":"объекти́вный","ru":"objective","ex":"Рефера́т до́лжен быть объекти́вным."},{"en":"посвящён","ru":"devoted (to), dedicated","ex":"Текст посвящён исто́рии."},{"en":"то́чка зре́ния","ru":"point of view","ex":"Э́то то́чка зре́ния а́втора."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each metatext cliché to its job.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["статья́ посвящена́…","the article is devoted to…"],["в статье́ рассма́тривается…","the article examines…"],["осо́бое внима́ние уделя́ется…","particular attention is given to…"],["а́втор прихо́дит к вы́воду…","the author concludes…"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five checks on compressing a source. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"Three sentences on what a text is about, for whom — that's an…","opts":["аннота́ция","рефера́т","те́зисы"],"answer":0},{"q":"Which is objective metatext for a рефера́т?","opts":["«Мне понра́вилась статья́».","«В статье́ рассма́тривается вопро́с».","«Я не согла́сен с а́втором»."],"answer":1},{"q":"«А́втор прихо́дит к вы́воду, что…» is the …","opts":["opener","closer","title"],"answer":1},{"q":"Opinion and “I think” belong in a(n)…","opts":["рефера́т","аннота́ция","о́тзыв"],"answer":2},{"q":"When compressing, the first thing to cut is…","opts":["the thesis","the examples and digressions","the conclusion"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"A model аннота́ция of an imagined article — notice: pure metatext, not one word of opinion. Read it aloud.","title":"Аннота́ция к статье́","sentences":["Статья́ посвящена́ пробле́ме городско́го тра́нспорта в кру́пных города́х.","В рабо́те рассма́триваются причи́ны тра́нспортных зато́ров и возмо́жные реше́ния.","Осо́бое внима́ние уделя́ется ро́ли обще́ственного тра́нспорта.","А́втор прихо́дит к вы́воду, что без разви́тия метро́ пробле́му не реши́ть.","Статья́ адресо́вана специали́стам и всем, кто интересу́ется урбани́стикой."],"translation":"The article is devoted to the problem of urban transport in large cities. The paper examines the causes of traffic jams and possible solutions. Particular attention is given to the role of public transport. The author concludes that without developing the metro the problem cannot be solved. The article is addressed to specialists and to anyone interested in urban studies."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — compress something, objectively.","prompt":"Write a short <b>аннота́ция или мини-рефера́т</b> (3–4 sentences) of an article, book or film you know — in Russian, using the metatext clichés (<b>посвящён / рассма́тривается / а́втор прихо́дит к вы́воду</b>). The rule: report the content, add <b>no opinion</b> of your own.","hint":"Stay in the third person about the author. If «мне понра́вилось» sneaks in, you've switched to an о́тзыв — cut it 🙂","example":"Статья́ посвящена́ вли́янию социа́льных сете́й на подро́стков. В рабо́те рассма́триваются как положи́тельные, так и отрица́тельные сто́роны явле́ния. А́втор прихо́дит к вы́воду, что гла́вную роль игра́ет не техноло́гия, а привы́чки по́льзователя."}},"20":{"day":20,"week":"03","themeRu":"Reasoning out loud","themeEn":"Моноло́г-рассужде́ние","intro":"A рефера́т hides your voice; a <b>моноло́г-рассужде́ние</b> is all voice. Эмма has been invited to say a few words at a public discussion on honesty, and she has three minutes to reason out loud — to state a claim, defend it, and land a conclusion, all in one even, unbroken register. This is the signature speaking task of ТРКИ-3, and it rewards structure over cleverness. Today: how to think aloud in Russian and sound like you meant every word.","introAudio":"A referat hides your voice; a monolog-rassuzhdeniye is all voice. Emma has been invited to say a few words at a public discussion on honesty, and she has three minutes to reason out loud — to state a claim, defend it, and land a conclusion, all in one even, unbroken register. This is the signature speaking task of TORFL-3, and it rewards structure over cleverness. Today: how to think aloud in Russian and sound like you meant every word.","goals":["Build the reasoning spine: <b>те́зис → аргуме́нты → вы́вод</b>","Scaffold it aloud with connectors: <b>во-пе́рвых, с одно́й стороны́, сле́довательно, в заключе́ние</b>","Paraphrase with <b>synonyms</b> instead of repeating one word","Hold <b>one register</b> from the first sentence to the last"],"learned":["Built the reasoning spine: те́зис → аргуме́нты → вы́вод","Scaffolded it aloud with connectors (во-пе́рвых, с одно́й стороны́, сле́довательно, в заключе́ние)","Paraphrased with synonyms instead of repeating one word","Held one register from first sentence to last"],"review":{"intro":"A minute back on yesterday's objective précis, on holding a text together, and on picking a synonym — three threads that all feed today's spoken reasoning.","introAudio":"A minute back on yesterday's objective précis, on holding a text together, and on picking a synonym — three threads that all feed today's spoken reasoning.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer — yesterday's рефера́т.","q":"Which sentence is objective метате́кст (fit for a рефера́т)?","opts":["«Мне ка́жется, статья́ скучнова́та».","«А́втор рассма́тривает причи́ны явле́ния».","«Обожа́ю таки́е те́ксты!»"],"answer":1,"explain":"Only <b>«а́втор рассма́тривает…»</b> is objective. The other two carry your opinion — that's an о́тзыв."},{"type":"choice","q":"Weighing two sides, you open «С одно́й стороны́…» and later balance it with…","opts":["«…и так да́лее».","«…с друго́й стороны́…»","«…в о́бщем-то»."],"answer":1,"explain":"The paired connector: <b>с одно́й стороны́… с друго́й стороны́</b> — the cohesion tool from day 13, now spoken."},{"type":"choice","q":"A more bookish synonym for «ду́мать» (to hold a view) is…","opts":["«полага́ть»","«ду́мку име́ть»","«башка́ ва́рит»"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>Полага́ть</b> is the elevated synonym of «ду́мать / счита́ть» — perfect for a reasoning monologue."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The reasoning spine","title":"<em>Те́зис → аргуме́нты → вы́вод</em>: the shape of reasoning","paras":["A монолог-рассуждение is neither a story nor a list — it's an argument that <b>moves</b>. It opens with a clear <b>те́зис</b> (your central claim), stated in one sentence at the top so the listener knows where you stand.","Then come two or three <b>аргуме́нты</b> — each a reason, developed a little, not just named. And it closes with a <b>вы́вод</b> that returns to the thesis, now earned by what came between. Thesis, support, conclusion: the same arc as an essay, but held in the air by voice alone.","That arc is the ТРКИ-3 speaking signature. Examiners aren't listening for fireworks — they're listening for whether the shape holds from first sentence to last."],"audio":"A monolog-rassuzhdeniye is neither a story nor a list — it's an argument that moves. It opens with a clear tezis, your central claim, stated in one sentence at the top so the listener knows where you stand. Then come two or three argumenty — each a reason, developed a little, not just named. And it closes with a vyvod that returns to the thesis, now earned by what came between. Thesis, support, conclusion: the same arc as an essay, but held in the air by voice alone. That arc is the TORFL-3 speaking signature. Examiners aren't listening for fireworks — they're listening for whether the shape holds.","table":{"rows":[["Те́зис: «Я убеждён, что…» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"я убеждён что\">🔊</button>","the central claim, up front"],["Аргуме́нт: «Во-пе́рвых, …» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"во-первых\">🔊</button>","a reason, developed"],["Вы́вод: «Таки́м о́бразом, …» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"таким образом\">🔊</button>","the conclusion, returning to the thesis"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Thesis, stated up front: “I am convinced that honesty matters more than comfort.”","en":"Те́зис: «Я <b>убеждён</b>, что че́стность важне́е комфо́рта».","say":"Тезис: я убеждён, что честность важнее комфорта."},{"ru":"Conclusion, returning to it: “Thus, the truth is worth the difficulty.”","en":"Вы́вод: «<b>Таки́м о́бразом</b>, пра́вда сто́ит э́тих тру́дностей».","say":"Вывод: таким образом, правда стоит этих трудностей."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Начина́ть с исто́рии и так и не сказа́ть, в чём твой те́зис","right":"Пе́рвой фра́зой назва́ть те́зис, пото́м его́ защища́ть","why":"A reasoning monologue must announce its <b>те́зис</b> early. If the listener reaches the end still unsure what you were arguing, the shape has failed — structure is the whole grade here."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"In a рассужде́ние, the те́зис should come…","opts":["at the very end, as a surprise","clearly at the start, then be defended","never — let the listener guess"],"answer":1,"explain":"State the <b>те́зис</b> up front, then support it. The вы́вод at the end returns to it."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which phrase signals the closing вы́вод?","opts":["«Во-пе́рвых, …»","«Таки́м о́бразом, …»","«Кста́ти, …»"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>Таки́м о́бразом</b> (thus) marks the conclusion. «Во-пе́рвых» opens an argument; «кста́ти» is a casual aside."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Scaffolding aloud","title":"Connectors carry the argument: <em>во-пе́рвых… сле́довательно… в заключе́ние</em>","paras":["Out loud, the listener can't see your paragraph breaks, so <b>connectors</b> do all the signposting. Enumerate your reasons: <b>во-пе́рвых, во-вторы́х, в-тре́тьих</b>. Weigh two sides: <b>с одно́й стороны́… с друго́й стороны́</b>. Concede, then push back: <b>тем не ме́нее, одна́ко</b>.","Draw the logical line: <b>сле́довательно, таки́м о́бразом, поэ́тому</b>. And land the whole thing: <b>в заключе́ние, подводя́ ито́г</b>. You met this inventory on day 13 as text cohesion; spoken, the same words become the beams that keep your monologue standing.","Drop the connectors and a perfectly correct monologue turns into a flat pile of sentences — the classic “listing where you should be arguing.” The beams are what make it sound like reasoning."],"audio":"Out loud, the listener can't see your paragraph breaks, so connectors do all the signposting. Enumerate your reasons: vo-pervykh, vo-vtorykh, v-tretyikh. Weigh two sides: s odnoy storony, s drugoy storony. Concede, then push back: tem ne meneye, odnako. Draw the logical line: sledovatelno, takim obrazom, poetomu. And land the whole thing: v zaklyucheniye, podvodya itog. You met this inventory on day 13 as text cohesion; spoken, the same words become the beams that keep your monologue standing. Drop the connectors and a correct monologue turns into a flat pile of sentences.","table":{"rows":[["во-пе́рвых, во-вторы́х <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"во-первых, во-вторых\">🔊</button>","enumerate the reasons"],["с одно́й стороны́… с друго́й <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"с одной стороны, с другой стороны\">🔊</button>","weigh two sides"],["тем не ме́нее, одна́ко <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"тем не менее, однако\">🔊</button>","concede, then push back"],["сле́довательно, в заключе́ние <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"следовательно, в заключение\">🔊</button>","conclude / land it"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"Enumerating: “Firstly, it concerns trust; secondly, self-respect.”","en":"«<b>Во-пе́рвых</b>, речь идёт о дове́рии; <b>во-вторы́х</b>, о самоуваже́нии».","say":"Во-первых, речь идёт о доверии; во-вторых, о самоуважении."},{"ru":"Landing it: “To sum up, sincerity costs effort but returns trust.”","en":"«<b>Подводя́ ито́г</b>, и́скренность сто́ит уси́лий, но возвраща́ет дове́рие».","say":"Подводя итог, искренность стоит усилий, но возвращает доверие."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Че́стность важна́. Ложь пло́хо. Я так ду́маю».","right":"«Во-пе́рвых, че́стность важна́. Тем не ме́нее ложь разруша́ет дове́рие. Сле́довательно, …»","why":"The first is three loose sentences — a list, not an argument. The connectors <b>во-пе́рвых / тем не ме́нее / сле́довательно</b> turn the same content into reasoning that moves."}],"tasks":[{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the connector. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"You start listing reasons aloud. The first one opens with «Во-…». Type it:","accept":["первых","во-первых","во первых"],"placeholder":"во-…","explain":"<b>Во-пе́рвых</b> opens the first reason; <b>во-вторы́х</b> the second, and so on."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which connector concedes a point before pushing back?","opts":["«тем не ме́нее»","«во-пе́рвых»","«наприме́р»"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>Тем не ме́нее</b> (nevertheless) grants the other side, then turns. «Во-пе́рвых» enumerates; «наприме́р» illustrates."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Synonymic paraphrase","title":"Don't repeat a word — <em>paraphrase</em> it","paras":["Here is a named C1 skill. English happily repeats “important… important… important”; a Russian ear hears that as poverty of language. The fix is <b>synonymic paraphrase</b>: say the idea a fresh way each time.","Build little synonym chains before you speak. <b>ва́жный</b> → значи́мый → суще́ственный → немаловажный. <b>пробле́ма</b> → вопро́с → тру́дность → зада́ча. <b>ду́мать</b> → счита́ть → полага́ть → быть убеждённым. Same meaning, rotating shape.","This is where days 2 and 10 pay off — your synonym stock and idiom stock become the raw material of a monologue that never sounds like it's circling one worn word."],"audio":"Here is a named C1 skill. English happily repeats important, important, important; a Russian ear hears that as poverty of language. The fix is synonymic paraphrase: say the idea a fresh way each time. Build little synonym chains before you speak. Vazhny becomes znachimy, sushchestvenny, nemalovazhny. Problema becomes vopros, trudnost, zadacha. Dumat becomes schitat, polagat, byt ubezhdyonnym. Same meaning, rotating shape. This is where days 2 and 10 pay off — your synonym stock becomes the raw material of a monologue that never sounds like it's circling one worn word.","table":{"rows":[["ва́жный → значи́мый → суще́ственный <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"важный, значимый, существенный\">🔊</button>","“important”, three shades"],["пробле́ма → вопро́с → тру́дность <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"проблема, вопрос, трудность\">🔊</button>","“problem / issue”"],["ду́мать → счита́ть → полага́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"думать, считать, полагать\">🔊</button>","“to think / hold a view”"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"Varied, not repeated: “The issue is significant; moreover, it's a serious matter.”","en":"«Э́то значи́мый <b>вопро́с</b>; бо́лее того́, э́то серьёзная <b>пробле́ма</b>».","say":"Это значимый вопрос; более того, это серьёзная проблема."},{"ru":"Varied verbs of opinion: “I believe… I am convinced… I hold that…”","en":"«Я <b>счита́ю</b>… я <b>убеждён</b>… я <b>полага́ю</b>, что…»","say":"Я считаю, я убеждён, я полагаю, что."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Э́то ва́жно, потому́ что э́то ва́жно, и вообще́ э́то о́чень ва́жно».","right":"«Э́то ва́жно, поско́льку затра́гивает ка́ждого; бо́лее того́, э́то принципиа́льно».","why":"Three «ва́жно» in a row signals a thin vocabulary. Rotate the synonyms — <b>значи́мо, суще́ственно, принципиа́льно</b> — and the same point sounds commanded, not stuck."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"To avoid repeating «ва́жный» a third time, reach for…","opts":["«ва́жный» again — it's fine","«суще́ственный»","«ва́жненький»"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>Суще́ственный</b> is a clean synonym in the same register. Repeating «ва́жный» sounds thin; «ва́жненький» is a jokey diminutive, wrong here."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which set are register-appropriate synonyms of «ду́мать» for a reasoning monologue?","opts":["счита́ть, полага́ть, быть убеждённым","ба́зарить, гна́ть, ду́мку кача́ть","молча́ть, забы́ть, спать"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>Счита́ть, полага́ть, быть убеждённым</b> — neutral-to-bookish, exactly the register. The second set is slang; the third isn't synonymous."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · One register, held","title":"Choose a register and <em>hold</em> it to the last word","paras":["A spoken monologue on ethics lives in the <b>neutral-to-bookish</b> band — elevated, but still living speech, not a dry lecture and not a chat. The real danger is <b>drift</b>: you open «Сле́довательно, свобо́да неотдели́ма от отве́тственности» and three sentences later slide into «ну, э́то во́обще жесть».","One slip of slang, or one lurch into stiff officialese, breaks the spell — the listener suddenly hears the seams. Keep the temperature even: the vocabulary, the connectors and even the intonation should belong to one world from start to finish.","Holding a single register across three unbroken minutes is, in the end, what C1 speaking <i>is</i>. Structure plus steadiness — that's the whole craft."],"audio":"A spoken monologue on ethics lives in the neutral-to-bookish band — elevated, but still living speech, not a dry lecture and not a chat. The real danger is drift: you open sledovatelno, svoboda neotdelima ot otvetstvennosti, and three sentences later slide into nu, eto voobshche zhest. One slip of slang, or one lurch into stiff officialese, breaks the spell — the listener suddenly hears the seams. Keep the temperature even: the vocabulary, the connectors and even the intonation should belong to one world from start to finish. Holding a single register across three unbroken minutes is, in the end, what C1 speaking is. Structure plus steadiness — that's the whole craft.","examples":[{"ru":"Held register (right): “Freedom is inseparable from responsibility.”","en":"Оди́н реги́стр: «Свобо́да <b>неотдели́ма</b> от отве́тственности».","say":"Один регистр: свобода неотделима от ответственности."},{"ru":"Register break (wrong): “…and that's just, like, totally nuts.”","en":"Слом реги́стра: «…и э́то, ну, во́обще <b>жесть</b>».","say":"Слом регистра: и это, ну, вообще жесть."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Сле́довательно, до́лг превы́ше всего́. Хотя́, блин, кому́ он ну́жен».","right":"«Сле́довательно, до́лг превы́ше всего́ — хотя́ соблюда́ть его́ нелегко́».","why":"The bookish opener collides with the slangy «блин, кому́ он ну́жен». Finish the thought in the <b>same</b> elevated register; a mid-sentence drop into slang is the tell of a broken monologue."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Mid-argument, dropping «ну э́то во́обще жесть» into a bookish monologue is…","opts":["fine — it livens things up","a register break that breaks the monologue","required at C1"],"answer":1,"explain":"It's a <b>register break</b>. Hold the neutral-to-bookish band from first word to last."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and deliver this thesis line — even, measured, one register.","target":"Я убеждён, что и́скренность важне́е сиюмину́тного удо́бства.","sub":"a thesis for a reasoning monologue — say it with quiet conviction","want":"я убеждён что искренность важнее сиюминутного удобства"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Эмма rehearses her three-minute talk on honesty; Ива́н plays the sceptic to test her argument. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"A","en":"Мой те́зис про́ст: пра́вда важне́е удо́бной лжи. Дай я порассужда́ю вслух.","ru":"My thesis is simple: the truth matters more than a convenient lie. Let me reason it out loud."},{"who":"B","en":"Дава́й. Но я бу́ду спо́рить — так интере́снее.","ru":"Go on. But I'll argue back — it's more interesting that way."},{"who":"A","en":"Во-пе́рвых, ложь разруша́ет дове́рие. Тем не ме́нее иногда́ пра́вда ра́нит.","ru":"Firstly, a lie destroys trust. Nevertheless, sometimes the truth wounds."},{"who":"B","en":"Вот! Зна́чит, иногда́ солга́ть — добре́е?","ru":"There! So sometimes to lie is kinder?"},{"who":"A","en":"С одно́й стороны́, да. С друго́й — ложь всегда́ возвраща́ется. Сле́довательно, че́стность надёжнее.","ru":"On the one hand, yes. On the other — a lie always comes back. Consequently, honesty is more reliable."},{"who":"B","en":"А ты не повторя́ешься? Три ра́за сказа́ла «ва́жно».","ru":"Aren't you repeating yourself? You said “important” three times."},{"who":"A","en":"Пойма́л. Ска́жем ина́че: значи́мо, суще́ственно, принципиа́льно.","ru":"Caught me. Let's put it differently: significant, substantial, fundamental."},{"who":"B","en":"Тепе́рь звучи́т как настоя́щее выступле́ние.","ru":"Now it sounds like a real speech."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for reasoning aloud — the spine, the beams, the shades. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"рассужде́ние","ru":"reasoning, line of argument","ex":"Он постро́ил чёткое рассужде́ние."},{"en":"те́зис","ru":"thesis, central claim","ex":"Сформули́руй свой те́зис я́сно."},{"en":"аргуме́нт","ru":"argument (reasoned point — not a quarrel)","ex":"У него́ был си́льный аргуме́нт."},{"en":"вы́вод","ru":"conclusion","ex":"К како́му вы́воду ты пришёл?"},{"en":"во-пе́рвых","ru":"firstly, in the first place","ex":"Во-пе́рвых, э́то нече́стно."},{"en":"сле́довательно","ru":"consequently, therefore","ex":"Сле́довательно, он был прав."},{"en":"тем не ме́нее","ru":"nevertheless","ex":"Тем не ме́нее я не согла́сен."},{"en":"полага́ть","ru":"to suppose, hold a view (bookish)","ex":"Я полага́ю, что э́то ва́жно."},{"en":"убеждён","ru":"convinced, persuaded","ex":"Я твёрдо убеждён в э́том."},{"en":"безусло́вно","ru":"undoubtedly, absolutely","ex":"Безусло́вно, вопро́с непросто́й."},{"en":"в заключе́ние","ru":"in conclusion, to close","ex":"В заключе́ние подведём ито́г."},{"en":"нра́вственный","ru":"moral, ethical","ex":"Э́то нра́вственный вы́бор."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each connector to the job it does in a spoken argument.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its job — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["во-пе́рвых","enumerate a reason"],["с одно́й стороны́","weigh one of two sides"],["тем не ме́нее","concede, then push back"],["таки́м о́бразом","draw the conclusion"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five checks on reasoning out loud. If one bites, the rule above has it.","items":[{"q":"The shape of a рассужде́ние is…","opts":["вы́вод → те́зис → приме́ры","те́зис → аргуме́нты → вы́вод","исто́рия → шу́тка → коне́ц"],"answer":1},{"q":"Which connector lands the conclusion?","opts":["«таки́м о́бразом»","«наприме́р»","«кста́ти»"],"answer":0},{"q":"To avoid repeating «пробле́ма», you can say…","opts":["«пробле́ма» again","«вопро́с» / «тру́дность»","«пробле́мка»"],"answer":1},{"q":"Dropping slang into a bookish monologue is…","opts":["a register break","good style","required"],"answer":0},{"q":"A bookish synonym of «ду́мать» is…","opts":["«башка́ ва́рит»","«полага́ть»","«молча́ть»"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"A model reasoning monologue on honesty — one register, connectors doing the work, no word repeated twice. Read it aloud.","title":"Всегда́ ли ну́жно говори́ть пра́вду?","sentences":["Всегда́ ли ну́жно говори́ть пра́вду? Вопро́с непросто́й, и однозна́чного отве́та нет.","С одно́й стороны́, че́стность — осно́ва дове́рия ме́жду людьми́.","Одна́ко, с друго́й стороны́, есть слу́чаи, когда́ пра́вда мо́жет ра́нить.","Тем не ме́нее я полага́ю, что ложь разруша́ет отноше́ния бы́стрее, чем го́рькая пра́вда.","Сле́довательно, и́скренность важне́е сиюмину́тного комфо́рта.","Подводя́ ито́г: говори́ть пра́вду нелегко́, но и́менно на ней де́ржится дове́рие."],"translation":"Should one always tell the truth? The question is not simple, and there is no single answer. On the one hand, honesty is the foundation of trust between people. However, on the other hand, there are cases when the truth can wound. Nevertheless, I hold that a lie destroys a relationship faster than a bitter truth. Consequently, sincerity matters more than momentary comfort. To sum up: telling the truth is not easy, but it is precisely on it that trust rests."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — reason out loud on paper first.","prompt":"Write a short <b>монолог-рассужде́ние</b> (4–5 sentences) on a moral question — e.g. “Is a white lie acceptable?”, “Does freedom require responsibility?”. Open with a <b>те́зис</b>, give two <b>аргуме́нты</b> with connectors (во-пе́рвых, тем не ме́нее…), and close with a <b>вы́вод</b>. Hold one register and don't repeat a key word — paraphrase it.","hint":"Spine first: те́зис → аргуме́нты → вы́вод. Then rotate synonyms so no word appears twice, and keep the temperature even 🙂","example":"Я убеждён, что свобо́да невозмо́жна без отве́тственности. Во-пе́рвых, ка́ждый вы́бор име́ет после́дствия для други́х. Тем не ме́нее свобо́да без грани́ц превраща́ется в произво́л. Сле́довательно, по́длинная свобо́да — э́то не отсу́тствие пра́вил, а осозна́нный вы́бор. Таки́м о́бразом, отвеча́ть за свои́ посту́пки — и есть при́знак зре́лой ли́чности."}},"21":{"day":21,"week":"03","themeRu":"Review 3","themeEn":"Повторе́ние 3","intro":"Six days took you from one edge of the language to the other — from a Turgenev metaphor to a clause of contract law, and back out through a précis and a spoken argument. Nothing new today: just a victory lap around the whole third week. You'll feel how the literary and the legal, the fine aspect and the reasoned voice, are all one instrument now — and you're learning to play it.","introAudio":"Six days took you from one edge of the language to the other — from a Turgenev metaphor to a clause of contract law, and back out through a précis and a spoken argument. Nothing new today: just a victory lap around the whole third week. You'll feel how the literary and the legal, the fine aspect and the reasoned voice, are all one instrument now — and you're learning to play it.","goals":["Read the <b>literary pole</b>: name the tropes — <b>мета́фора, эпи́тет, сравне́ние, олицетворе́ние</b>","Hold <b>aspect's fine shades</b>: <b>чита́ли</b> (ever) vs <b>прочита́ли</b> (result)","Read the <b>legal pole</b> by its spine, and shrink a source into an objective <b>рефера́т</b>","Reason aloud in shape: <b>те́зис → аргуме́нты → вы́вод</b>, in one register"],"learned":["Named the literary tropes (мета́фора, эпи́тет, сравне́ние, олицетворе́ние)","Held aspect's fine shades (чита́ли vs прочита́ли)","Read the legal pole by its spine and shrank a source into an objective рефера́т","Reasoned aloud in shape: те́зис → аргуме́нты → вы́вод, in one register"],"review":{"intro":"Straight into it — a quick pull from every day this week. No hints needed; you built all of this.","introAudio":"Straight into it — a quick pull from every day this week. No hints needed; you built all of this.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. It's all week-3 material.","q":"«Ве́тер шепта́л в ветвя́х» — the wind is given human speech. Which trope?","opts":["эпи́тет","олицетворе́ние","сравне́ние"],"answer":1,"explain":"A lifeless thing given a living action → <b>олицетворе́ние</b> (personification)."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Вы чита́ли „Анну Каре́нину“?» — the imperfective asks whether you…","opts":["have <b>ever</b> read it (general fact)","<b>finished</b> it yesterday (result)","are reading it <b>now</b>"],"answer":0,"explain":"General-fact imperfective: «чита́ли?» = have you ever. «Прочита́ли?» would ask about a finished result."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which sentence belongs in an objective рефера́т?","opts":["«Мне о́чень понра́вилась статья́».","«А́втор рассма́тривает причи́ны кри́зиса».","«Ну, там про эконо́мику и всё тако́е»."],"answer":1,"explain":"Objective metatext: <b>а́втор рассма́тривает…</b>. The first is opinion; the third is chatty retelling."},{"type":"choice","q":"A reasoning monologue should be built as…","opts":["те́зис → аргуме́нты → вы́вод","a chain of unrelated facts","one long sentence"],"answer":0,"explain":"Thesis, then arguments, then conclusion — the ТРКИ-3 speaking shape."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The literary pole","title":"Reading for the image: <em>мета́фора, эпи́тет, олицетворе́ние</em>","paras":["Week three opened at the literary edge. The <b>худо́жественный</b> style bends every other style on purpose, and it works through <b>тропы</b> (tropes — figurative devices). A <b>мета́фора</b> calls one thing another («го́род — ка́менные джу́нгли»); an <b>эпи́тет</b> is a vivid, charged modifier («золота́я о́сень»).","A <b>сравне́ние</b> compares openly with как («си́льный, как медве́дь»); an <b>олицетворе́ние</b> hands a living action to a lifeless thing («дождь пла́чет»). The point of day 17's close reading was never to translate the image but to ask <b>how</b> it's made and <b>why</b>.","So the literary skill is double vision: you see the plain meaning and, at the same time, the device underneath it doing the emotional work."],"audio":"Week three opened at the literary edge. The khudozhestvenny style bends every other style on purpose, and it works through tropy, tropes, figurative devices. A metafora calls one thing another — gorod, kamennye dzhungli, the city is a stone jungle. An epitet is a vivid, charged modifier — zolotaya osen, golden autumn. A sravneniye compares openly with kak — silny, kak medved, strong as a bear. An olitsetvoreniye hands a living action to a lifeless thing — dozhd plachet, the rain weeps. The point of the close reading was never to translate the image but to ask how it's made and why. So the literary skill is double vision.","table":{"rows":[["мета́фора — «го́род — джу́нгли» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"метафора, город джунгли\">🔊</button>","metaphor — one thing named as another"],["эпи́тет — «золота́я о́сень» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"эпитет, золотая осень\">🔊</button>","epithet — a vivid, charged modifier"],["сравне́ние — «как медве́дь» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"сравнение, как медведь\">🔊</button>","simile — open comparison with «как»"],["олицетворе́ние — «дождь пла́чет» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"олицетворение, дождь плачет\">🔊</button>","personification — life given to a thing"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"Literary: “Autumn has gilded the park.” (metaphor + personification)","en":"Литерату́рно: «О́сень <b>позоло́тила</b> парк».","say":"Литературно: осень позолотила парк."},{"ru":"Literary: “Her voice was soft, like velvet.” (simile)","en":"Литерату́рно: «Её го́лос был мя́гким, <b>как ба́рхат</b>».","say":"Литературно: её голос был мягким, как бархат."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Reading «го́род — ка́менные джу́нгли» as a claim about actual jungles","right":"Reading it as a <b>мета́фора</b>: the city feels wild, crowded, hostile","why":"A trope means more than it says. Take the image for its <b>effect</b>, not its literal denotation — that's the whole art of reading literature."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"«Ста́рый, как мир» — which trope?","opts":["сравне́ние","мета́фора","эпи́тет"],"answer":0,"explain":"Open comparison with «как» → <b>сравне́ние</b> (simile)."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Со́лнце улыбну́лось го́роду» is an example of…","opts":["олицетворе́ние","сравне́ние","эпи́тет"],"answer":0,"explain":"The sun is given a human action (smiling) → <b>олицетворе́ние</b> (personification)."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Verbal nuance","title":"<em>Чита́ли</em> vs <em>прочита́ли</em>: aspect's finest shades","paras":["The one properly grammatical deepening of the week was aspect at C1. The <b>general-fact imperfective</b> asks about experience: «Вы <b>чита́ли</b> Достое́вского?» — “have you ever?” The <b>perfective</b> asks about a finished result: «Вы <b>прочита́ли</b> статью́?» — “did you get it done?”","Aspect also colours commands: a perfective is a one-off request («<b>Закро́й</b> окно́»), while a negated imperfective is a standing prohibition («<b>Не закрыва́й</b> окно́» — leave it). And motion verbs run figurative everywhere: «<b>речь идёт</b> о…», «<b>вре́мя лети́т</b>», «<b>де́ло не дви́жется</b>».","None of this is new machinery — it's the same aspect you've owned since B1, now tuned to its finest distinctions."],"audio":"The one properly grammatical deepening of the week was aspect at C1. The general-fact imperfective asks about experience: Vy chitali Dostoyevskogo, have you ever? The perfective asks about a finished result: Vy prochitali statyu, did you get it done? Aspect also colours commands: a perfective is a one-off request, zakroy okno; a negated imperfective is a standing prohibition, ne zakryvay okno, leave it. And motion verbs run figurative everywhere: rech idyot o, vremya letit, delo ne dvizhetsya. None of this is new machinery — it's the same aspect you've owned since B1, now tuned to its finest distinctions.","table":{"rows":[["Вы чита́ли…? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"вы читали\">🔊</button>","general fact — “ever?” (experience)"],["Вы прочита́ли…? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"вы прочитали\">🔊</button>","perfective — “done?” (result)"],["Не закрыва́й окно́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"не закрывай окно\">🔊</button>","negated imperfective — standing prohibition"],["Речь идёт о… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"речь идёт о\">🔊</button>","figurative motion — “it concerns…”"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"General fact: “Have you (ever) been to the Bolshoi?”","en":"«Вы <b>бы́ли</b> в Большо́м теа́тре?»","say":"Вы были в Большом театре?"},{"ru":"Result: “Have you read the contract (to the end)?”","en":"«Вы <b>прочита́ли</b> догово́р?»","say":"Вы прочитали договор?"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Вы прочита́ли Толсто́го?» meaning “have you ever read any Tolstoy?”","right":"«Вы чита́ли Толсто́го?»","why":"For the general experience — “are you acquainted with his work?” — use the <b>imperfective</b> чита́ли. The perfective прочита́ли asks about finishing one specific text."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"To ask a friend whether they've ever seen a Chekhov play (experience), you say…","opts":["«Ты ви́дел пье́сы Че́хова?»","«Ты уви́дел пье́сы Че́хова?»","«Ты уви́дишь пье́сы Че́хова?»"],"answer":0,"explain":"General-fact imperfective: <b>ви́дел</b> — have you ever. уви́дел is a one-off result; уви́дишь is future."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the missing verb. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"Figurative motion: «Речь … о ва́жном вопро́се» (the matter concerns). Type the verb:","accept":["идёт","идет"],"placeholder":"речь … о…","explain":"Fixed figurative motion: <b>речь идёт о…</b> — “the matter concerns / it's a question of…”."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Report and reason","title":"Shrink it, read it, argue it: <em>рефера́т, зако́н, рассужде́ние</em>","paras":["The back half of the week was output and dense input. The <b>language of law</b> you read, not write: bracket the referential inserts (<b>в соотве́тствии с пу́нктом 3</b>), unfold the nominal chains, and find the spine — <b>кто + что де́лает</b>. Comprehension, not imitation.","A <b>рефера́т</b> compresses someone else's text objectively, through metatext clichés — <b>в статье́ рассма́тривается…, а́втор прихо́дит к вы́воду…</b> — with zero opinion of your own. And a <b>монолог-рассужде́ние</b> is your reasoned voice: <b>те́зис → аргуме́нты → вы́вод</b>, connectors doing the signposting, synonyms instead of repetition, one register held throughout.","Notice the through-line of all six days: at C1 every choice answers two questions at once — is it <b>correct</b>, and is it <b>appropriate</b> to its style?"],"audio":"The back half of the week was output and dense input. The language of law you read, not write: bracket the referential inserts, v sootvetstvii s punktom 3, unfold the nominal chains, and find the spine — kto plus chto delayet. Comprehension, not imitation. A referat compresses someone else's text objectively, through metatext clichés — v statye rassmatrivayetsya, avtor prikhodit k vyvodu — with zero opinion of your own. And a monolog-rassuzhdeniye is your reasoned voice: tezis, argumenty, vyvod, connectors doing the signposting, synonyms instead of repetition, one register held throughout. Notice the through-line of all six days: is it correct, and is it appropriate to its style?","table":{"rows":[["Зако́н: найди́ спину́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"закон, найди спину\">🔊</button>","law → read for the subject + verb"],["Рефера́т: объекти́вный метате́кст <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"реферат, объективный метатекст\">🔊</button>","précis → report, no opinion"],["Рассужде́ние: те́зис → вы́вод <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"рассуждение, тезис, вывод\">🔊</button>","monologue → thesis, arguments, conclusion"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"Précis (objective): “The article examines the causes of the phenomenon.”","en":"Рефера́т: «В статье́ <b>рассма́триваются</b> причи́ны явле́ния».","say":"Реферат: в статье рассматриваются причины явления."},{"ru":"Monologue (reasoned): “Thus, sincerity matters more than comfort.”","en":"Рассужде́ние: «<b>Таки́м о́бразом</b>, и́скренность важне́е комфо́рта».","say":"Рассуждение: таким образом, искренность важнее комфорта."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«В рефера́те: мне ка́жется, а́втор молоде́ц».","right":"«В рефера́те: а́втор прихо́дит к вы́воду, что…».","why":"Opinion («мне ка́жется… молоде́ц») has no place in a рефера́т — that's an о́тзыв. Report the author's position objectively; save the verdict for the genre built to carry it."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"In «В соотве́тствии с пу́нктом 5 сторона́ обя́зуется…», the spine is…","opts":["«в соотве́тствии с пу́нктом 5»","«сторона́ обя́зуется»","«пу́нктом 5»"],"answer":1,"explain":"Bracket the referential insert; the spine is <b>сторона́ обя́зуется</b> — who + does what."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud — even and objective, like a précis.","target":"А́втор прихо́дит к вы́воду, что вопро́с остаётся откры́тым.","sub":"an objective рефера́т closer — flat, no colour","want":"автор приходит к выводу что вопрос остаётся открытым"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"At Ле́на's café, Эмма sums up her week of extremes over coffee. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Ну расскажи́, чем ты занима́лась на э́той неде́ле?","ru":"So tell me, what have you been up to this week?"},{"who":"A","en":"Всем сра́зу. У́тром — Турге́нев с его́ мета́форами, ве́чером — догово́р Макси́ма.","ru":"Everything at once. In the morning — Turgenev with his metaphors, in the evening — Maksim's contract."},{"who":"B","en":"И то, и друго́е — ру́сский?","ru":"And both of those — Russian?"},{"who":"A","en":"Оди́н язы́к, два по́люса. Оди́н поёт о́бразами, друго́й пря́чет смысл в существи́тельные.","ru":"One language, two poles. One sings in images, the other hides its meaning in nouns."},{"who":"B","en":"А что бы́ло тру́днее всего́?","ru":"And what was the hardest?"},{"who":"A","en":"Рефера́т. Сжать чужу́ю статью́ и не сказа́ть ни сло́ва от себя́.","ru":"The précis. To compress someone else's article and not say a single word of my own."},{"who":"B","en":"Зато́ на дискусси́и ты своё мне́ние сказа́ла!","ru":"But at the discussion you did give your opinion!"},{"who":"A","en":"Там мо́жно: те́зис, аргуме́нты, вы́вод — и в одно́м реги́стре. Ка́ждому жа́нру своё.","ru":"There it's allowed: thesis, arguments, conclusion — and in one register. To each genre its own."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve keywords from across week three — the whole range in one deck. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"мета́фора","ru":"metaphor","ex":"«Ка́менные джу́нгли» — мета́фора."},{"en":"эпи́тет","ru":"epithet","ex":"«Золота́я о́сень» — эпи́тет."},{"en":"олицетворе́ние","ru":"personification","ex":"«Дождь пла́чет» — олицетворе́ние."},{"en":"о́браз","ru":"image, figure (literary)","ex":"Э́тот о́браз запомина́ется."},{"en":"вид","ru":"aspect (of a verb)","ex":"Вид меня́ет смысл вопро́са."},{"en":"догово́р","ru":"contract","ex":"Догово́р вступа́ет в си́лу за́втра."},{"en":"рефера́т","ru":"précis, abstract-paper","ex":"Я написа́ла рефера́т статьи́."},{"en":"аннота́ция","ru":"abstract, annotation","ex":"Аннота́ция — три предложе́ния."},{"en":"те́зис","ru":"thesis, central claim","ex":"Мой те́зис прост и я́сен."},{"en":"вы́вод","ru":"conclusion","ex":"В конце́ — коро́ткий вы́вод."},{"en":"рассужде́ние","ru":"reasoning, argument (discourse)","ex":"Э́то стро́йное рассужде́ние."},{"en":"реги́стр","ru":"register (level of speech)","ex":"Держи́ оди́н реги́стр до конца́."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each week-3 term to what it means.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["олицетворе́ние","life given to a lifeless thing"],["согла́сно + дат.","according to (the legal reference)"],["рефера́т","objective compression, no opinion"],["те́зис","the central claim of an argument"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Eight questions — one or two from every day this week. Pass this and week three is yours.","items":[{"q":"«Дождь бараба́нил по кры́ше» gives rain a human action. Which trope?","opts":["эпи́тет","олицетворе́ние","сравне́ние"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Хи́трый, как лиса́» — which trope?","opts":["сравне́ние","мета́фора","эпи́тет"],"answer":0},{"q":"«Вы чита́ли э́тот рома́н?» asks about…","opts":["a finished result today","the general experience (ever)","reading right now"],"answer":1},{"q":"The goal of a close philological reading is to…","opts":["translate the passage word for word","see how the text is made and why","count the sentences"],"answer":1},{"q":"«согла́сно» governs the…","opts":["dative (зако́ну)","genitive (зако́на)","accusative (зако́н)"],"answer":0},{"q":"«неисполне́ние обяза́тельств» means…","opts":["the obligations were met","someone did not fulfil the obligations","a list of obligations"],"answer":1},{"q":"Opinion and “I think” belong in a(n)…, not a рефера́т.","opts":["аннота́ция","те́зисы","о́тзыв"],"answer":2},{"q":"To avoid repeating «ва́жно» in a monologue, reach for…","opts":["«суще́ственно»","«ва́жно» again","«ва́жненько»"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Эмма looks back at her third week — every thread of it hiding in a few lines. Read it aloud.","title":"От о́браза до постановле́ния","sentences":["Э́та неде́ля была́ о кра́йностях языка́.","Я чита́ла Турге́нева — и учи́лась ви́деть мета́фору, эпи́тет, олицетворе́ние.","Пото́м — су́хой язы́к зако́на, где ва́жно найти́ спину́ дли́нного предложе́ния.","Я научи́лась сжима́ть чужо́й текст в рефера́т, не добавля́я своего́ мне́ния.","И, наконе́ц, я вы́ступила: те́зис, аргуме́нты, вы́вод — в одно́м реги́стре.","От о́браза до постановле́ния — тепе́рь я слы́шу ка́ждый стиль."],"translation":"This week was about the extremes of the language. I read Turgenev — and learned to see metaphor, epithet, personification. Then — the dry language of law, where it matters to find the spine of a long sentence. I learned to compress someone else's text into a précis, without adding my own opinion. And, finally, I gave a talk: thesis, arguments, conclusion — in one register. From an image to a decree — now I hear every style."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — show both halves of the week in one go.","prompt":"Pick any text (an article, a book, a film). Write it up TWICE, labelled: first a two-sentence objective <b>аннота́ция</b> (посвящена́…, рассма́тривается…), then a two-sentence <b>рассужде́ние</b> where you take a stand (те́зис + вы́вод, one register). Feel the switch between reporting and reasoning.","hint":"Аннота́ция = the author speaks, you're invisible. Рассужде́ние = you speak, in shape. Same topic, two genres 🙂","example":"Аннота́ция: Статья́ посвящена́ вли́янию смартфо́нов на сон. В рабо́те рассма́триваются причи́ны бессо́нницы и во́зможные реше́ния. Рассужде́ние: Я убеждён, что дисципли́на важне́е техноло́гии. Сле́довательно, не телефо́н винова́т, а на́ши привы́чки."}},"22":{"day":22,"week":"04","themeRu":"A marathon of real text","themeEn":"Марафо́н настоя́щих те́кстов","intro":"Эмма opened a magazine this morning and read four different worlds before her coffee cooled — a hard-news lede, a quoted regulation, a line of near-poetry, and a headline winking at Hamlet. That's a real page: it won't sit still in one style. Today you learn to read it the way she does — skim to map it, study where it counts, and never translate word by word.","introAudio":"Emma opened a magazine this morning and read four different worlds before her coffee cooled — a hard-news lede, a quoted regulation, a line of near-poetry, and a headline winking at Hamlet. That's a real page: it won't sit still in one style. Today you learn to read it the way she does — skim to map it, study where it counts, and never translate word by word.","goals":["Shift between two reading gears: <b>просмо́тр</b> (skim for gist) and <b>изуча́ющее чте́ние</b> (study reading)","Stay steady when a real text switches style and density mid-page","Catch a <b>прецеде́нтная</b> (precedent) phrase — a quote everyone knows — hidden inside a text","Read for the idea and the register, not word by word"],"learned":["Shifted between skimming (просмо́тр) and study reading (изуча́ющее чте́ние)","Held your reading goal as the text switched styles","Spotted precedent phrases hidden in real text","Read for idea and register, not word-by-word"],"review":{"intro":"One minute back through last week — a subtle aspect pair, a trope, a summary opener — then into the reading marathon.","introAudio":"One minute back through last week — a subtle aspect pair, a trope, a summary opener — then into the reading marathon.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer. This is last week — an old friend.","q":"«Вы <b>чита́ли</b> Достое́вского?» vs «Вы <b>прочита́ли</b>?» — which asks about experience (“have you ever”), not a finished result?","opts":["чита́ли","прочита́ли","бу́дете чита́ть"],"answer":0,"explain":"The imperfective <b>чита́ли</b> is the general-fact use — “have you ever had the experience”, not a completed result. <b>прочита́ли</b> asks about finishing."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Го́род спит» — a city is given a human action. This trope is…","opts":["сравне́ние (simile)","олицетворе́ние (personification)","эпи́тет (epithet)"],"answer":1,"explain":"Giving a non-living thing a human action is <b>олицетворе́ние</b> (personification)."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build the рефера́т opener: <b>“The article examines an important question.”</b>","want":"В статье́ рассма́тривается ва́жный вопро́с","answer":"В статье́ рассма́тривается ва́жный вопро́с"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Two reading gears","title":"<em>Просмо́тр</em> or <em>изуча́ющее</em> — pick your reading gear","paras":["A C1 reader has two gears and shifts between them on purpose. <b>Просмо́тр</b> (skimming) — you run your eyes over a page to catch the gist, the topic, whether it's worth reading closely. You don't stop on every word. <b>Изуча́ющее чте́ние</b> (study reading) — you slow right down, weigh each phrase, and don't move on until you've grasped it.","The mistake English speakers make is reading everything in second gear — translating word by word, stalling on the first unknown noun. Real texts are too long and too mixed for that. Skim first to map the terrain; then read closely only where your <b>goal</b> demands it.","Which gear? It depends on your reason for reading. Hunting for one fact — skim. Editing a translation, like Эмма — study. The text doesn't decide; your goal does."],"audio":"A C1 reader has two gears and shifts between them on purpose. Prosmotr, skimming — you run your eyes over a page to catch the gist, whether it's worth reading closely; you don't stop on every word. Izuchayushcheye chteniye, study reading — you slow right down and weigh each phrase. The mistake English speakers make is reading everything in second gear, translating word by word, stalling on the first unknown noun. Real texts are too long and too mixed for that. Skim first to map the terrain; then read closely only where your goal demands. Which gear depends on your reason for reading: hunting one fact, skim; editing a translation like Emma, study.","examples":[{"ru":"Skim reading: “I just ran my eyes over the article.”","en":"Просмо́тр: «Я <b>про́сто пробежа́л глаза́ми</b> статью́».","say":"Просмотр: я просто пробежал глазами статью."},{"ru":"Study reading: “I read the contract closely, line by line.”","en":"Изуча́ющее: «Я <b>внима́тельно вчита́лся</b> в догово́р, стро́чку за стро́чкой».","say":"Изучающее: я внимательно вчитался в договор, строчку за строчкой."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«А воз и ны́не там» — reading it literally as “and the cart is still there”, and missing the point.","right":"«А воз и ны́не там» — узнаём цита́ту: ничего́ не дви́жется, де́ло сто́ит.","why":"This is a precedent phrase (a well-known quote, here from Krylov). Read literally it's a cart on a road; as an allusion it means nothing has budged. Miss the quote, miss the meaning."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"Эмма needs to find one date buried in a long report. Which gear?","opts":["изуча́ющее чте́ние — study every line","просмо́тр — skim for that one fact","translate the whole thing word by word"],"answer":1,"explain":"One fact in a long text → <b>просмо́тр</b> (skim). Study reading is for where you must grasp every phrase."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"Skimming a text for the gist, in one Russian word, is про… . Type it:","accept":["просмотр"],"placeholder":"просмо́тр","explain":"<b>Просмо́тр</b> — a quick look-through for the gist, before you decide what to read closely."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Real text won't hold still","title":"One page, <em>many</em> styles — don't fall off","paras":["A textbook paragraph stays in one style. A real page doesn't. A news article quotes a law (official), then paints a scene (literary), then cites a statistic (scientific), then asks you a question (journalistic) — all in six lines. This is normal, and it's the C1 reading test: four texts, ~2500 words, every register.","When the style shifts, the density and the vocabulary shift with it. The official quote is dense and nominal; the literary line is vivid and loose. Don't read them the same way — and don't be thrown when the ground moves under you.","Your anchor is your <b>reading goal</b>. Whatever the text does, you know why you're reading it — for the gist, for one fact, to edit. Hold the goal and the style-switches become signposts, not traps."],"audio":"A textbook paragraph stays in one style; a real page doesn't. A news article quotes a law, then paints a scene, then cites a statistic, then asks you a question — all in six lines. This is normal, and it's the C1 reading test: four texts, around 2500 words, every register. When the style shifts, the density and the vocabulary shift with it: the official quote is dense and nominal, the literary line vivid and loose. Don't read them the same way, and don't be thrown when the ground moves. Your anchor is your reading goal: whatever the text does, you know why you're reading it. Hold the goal, and the style-switches become signposts, not traps.","table":{"rows":[["«В соотве́тствии с зако́ном…» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в соответствии с законом\">🔊</button>","a switch INTO official"],["«Не́бо темне́ло на глаза́х…» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"небо темнело на глазах\">🔊</button>","a switch INTO literary"],["«Иссле́дование показа́ло, что…» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"исследование показало что\">🔊</button>","a switch INTO scientific"],["«Так кто же в отве́те?» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"так кто же в ответе\">🔊</button>","a switch INTO journalistic (a question to the reader)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"News → law, mid-paragraph.","en":"«Мэ́рия обеща́ет парк, но <b>в соотве́тствии с постановле́нием</b> зе́мля уже́ распределена́».","say":"Мэрия обещает парк, но в соответствии с постановлением земля уже распределена."},{"ru":"Report → image, mid-paragraph.","en":"«Ци́фры расту́т, а за ци́фрами — <b>ти́хие дворы́ и ста́рые о́кна</b>».","say":"Цифры растут, а за цифрами — тихие дворы и старые окна."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«…рост соста́вил четы́ре проце́нта. Но за э́тими проце́нтами — чужа́я боль.» The sentence switches from…","opts":["scientific/report → literary-emotional","official → conversational","literary → scientific"],"answer":0,"explain":"«рост соста́вил…проце́нта» is a dry report (scientific/statistical); «чужа́я боль» is an emotional, literary image. The style shifts inside one sentence."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The quotes hiding in the text","title":"<em>Прецеде́нтные</em> phrases — the text assumes you know them","paras":["Russian writing is stitched with <b>прецеде́нтные тексты</b> (precedent texts) — lines from literature, film and song that every native knows. The writer drops them without warning, often twisted for effect, and assumes you'll catch them. «Быть и́ли не быть» (Hamlet), «А воз и ны́не там» (Krylov — “nothing has moved”), «ли́шний челове́к» (the “superfluous man” of the classics).","Miss the allusion and the sentence still parses — but you lose its point, its irony, its wink. A headline like «Кварти́ра и́ли не кварти́ра» only lands if you hear Hamlet under it.","You don't need to place every quote. You need the reflex: “that phrasing is borrowed — there's a second layer here.” Notice the seam; the meaning lives in it."],"audio":"Russian writing is stitched with pretsedentnye teksty, precedent texts — lines from literature, film and song that every native knows. The writer drops them without warning, often twisted for effect, and assumes you'll catch them. Byt ili ne byt, from Hamlet; a voz i nyne tam, from Krylov, meaning nothing has moved; lishny chelovek, the superfluous man of the classics. Miss the allusion and the sentence still parses, but you lose its point, its irony, its wink. A headline like Kvartira ili ne kvartira only lands if you hear Hamlet under it. You don't need to place every quote; you need the reflex — that phrasing is borrowed, there's a second layer here. Notice the seam; the meaning lives in it.","examples":[{"ru":"A precedent phrase inside the news.","en":"«Обеща́ли отремонти́ровать доро́гу, но <b>а воз и ны́не там</b>».","say":"Обещали отремонтировать дорогу, но а воз и ныне там."},{"ru":"A headline twisting Hamlet.","en":"Заголо́вок: «<b>Стро́ить и́ли не стро́ить</b> — вот в чём вопро́с».","say":"Заголовок: строить или не строить — вот в чём вопрос."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Быть и́ли не быть» — reading it as a real yes/no question about existence.","right":"«Быть и́ли не быть» — узнаём Га́млета: цита́та-си́мвол тру́дного вы́бора.","why":"It's the most famous precedent phrase in the language. In a text it rarely asks literally about existing — it flags a hard, fateful choice. Hear the quote, read the second layer."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«А воз и ны́не там» in an article about road repairs means…","opts":["a cart is literally blocking the road","nothing has actually moved — the problem is stuck","the goods were delivered late"],"answer":1,"explain":"It's a precedent phrase (from Krylov): the situation hasn't budged. Read literally you'd miss the whole point."},{"type":"type","q":"Complete the most famous precedent line (Hamlet, in Russian): «Быть и́ли не …».","accept":["быть"],"placeholder":"…","explain":"«Быть и́ли не быть» — the quote a Russian reader hears instantly under any «X и́ли не X» headline."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Read the idea, not the words","title":"Don't translate word-by-word — and keep the <em>register</em>","paras":["The deepest C1 reading trap is word-by-word translation. You meet «руко́й пода́ть», translate “to hand over with a hand,” and lose “it's a stone's throw away.” Read for the <b>idea</b>; let an unknown word come from context; only reach for the dictionary when the idea won't come.","And read the <b>register</b>, not just the meaning. «Сконча́лся», «у́мер» and «по́мер» all mean “died,” but a text that says «сконча́лся» is telling you it's official or respectful. The word choice is information — it tells you who's speaking and how.","So: skim to map, study where it counts, catch the precedent quotes, and read both the idea and its register. That's reading like a native — and exactly what the exam's four-text marathon asks."],"audio":"The deepest C1 reading trap is word-by-word translation. You meet rukoy podat, translate 'to hand over with a hand', and lose 'it's a stone's throw away'. Read for the idea; let an unknown word come from context; reach for the dictionary only when the idea won't come. And read the register, not just the meaning. Skonchalsya, umer and pomer all mean 'died', but a text that says skonchalsya is telling you it's official or respectful. The word choice is information — it tells you who's speaking and how. So: skim to map, study where it counts, catch the precedent quotes, and read both the idea and its register. That's reading like a native, and exactly what the exam's four-text marathon asks.","examples":[{"ru":"An idiom — don't read it literally.","en":"«До кафе́ <b>руко́й пода́ть</b>» = the café is very close.","say":"До кафе рукой подать."},{"ru":"Register as information: “passed away” signals a respectful text.","en":"«Изве́стный учёный <b>сконча́лся</b>» — сло́во «сконча́лся» выдаёт уважи́тельный, официа́льный тон.","say":"Известный учёный скончался — слово скончался выдаёт уважительный, официальный тон."}],"tasks":[{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it as one idea, evenly.","target":"За э́тими ци́фрами стои́т жива́я исто́рия.","sub":"a journalistic line — read it as a single thought, not word by word","want":"за этими цифрами стоит живая история"},{"type":"choice","q":"You read «по́мер» rather than «у́мер» in a text. That word choice tells you the text is…","opts":["scientific","colloquial / folksy in tone","official"],"answer":1,"explain":"«по́мер» is the colloquial, folksy synonym of «у́мер». The choice itself signals a plain, conversational register."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Эмма has a magazine open; Ива́н leans over to read the same page. Watch the styles change line by line. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Что чита́ешь? Заголо́вок гро́мкий: «Быть и́ли не быть — но́вому мосту́».","ru":"What are you reading? Loud headline: “To be or not to be — for the new bridge.”"},{"who":"A","en":"Ви́дишь? Э́то переде́ланный Га́млет — прецеде́нтная фра́за.","ru":"See? That's a made-over Hamlet — a precedent phrase."},{"who":"B","en":"А да́льше сра́зу су́хо: «В соотве́тствии с реше́нием мэ́рии…». Скучи́ща.","ru":"And right after, all dry: “In accordance with the city's decision…”. So dull."},{"who":"A","en":"Э́то встро́енная официа́льная цита́та. А ни́же — почти́ стихи́.","ru":"That's an embedded official quote. And below it — almost poetry."},{"who":"B","en":"Так э́то же ра́зные сти́ли в одно́й статье́!","ru":"So these are different styles in one article!"},{"who":"A","en":"И́менно. Я не перевожу́ ка́ждое сло́во — ловлю́ смысл и стиль.","ru":"Exactly. I don't translate every word — I catch the meaning and the style."},{"who":"B","en":"А как ты чита́ешь так бы́стро?","ru":"And how do you read so fast?"},{"who":"A","en":"Снача́ла просмо́тр — о чём вообще́, пото́м вчи́тываюсь в ва́жное.","ru":"First a skim — what it's about at all, then I read closely into what matters."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for reading real text — the marathon toolkit. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"отры́вок","ru":"excerpt, passage","ex":"Прочита́й э́тот отры́вок вслух."},{"en":"изда́ние","ru":"edition, publication","ex":"Э́то ре́дкое изда́ние кни́ги."},{"en":"заголо́вок","ru":"headline, title","ex":"Заголо́вок сра́зу привлека́ет внима́ние."},{"en":"абза́ц","ru":"paragraph","ex":"Пе́рвый абза́ц — са́мый ва́жный."},{"en":"содержа́ние","ru":"content, subject matter","ex":"Меня́ интересу́ет содержа́ние, а не фо́рма."},{"en":"суть","ru":"gist, essence","ex":"Схвати́ суть, не тони́ в дета́лях."},{"en":"просмо́тр","ru":"a skim, a quick look-through","ex":"Бе́глый просмо́тр за́нял мину́ту."},{"en":"вчита́ться","ru":"to read closely, get into","ex":"Вчита́йся в э́тот сло́жный абза́ц."},{"en":"конте́кст","ru":"context","ex":"Незнако́мое сло́во ясно́ из конте́кста."},{"en":"аллю́зия","ru":"allusion","ex":"В заголо́вке — аллю́зия на Га́млета."},{"en":"прецеде́нтный","ru":"precedent (well-known, alluded-to)","ex":"«Ли́шний челове́к» — прецеде́нтный о́браз."},{"en":"вы́вод","ru":"conclusion","ex":"К како́му вы́воду прихо́дит а́втор?"}]},"drag":{"intro":"Each fragment comes from a real page — match it to its style.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its style — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["«Статья́ пе́рвая. Настоя́щий зако́н вступа́ет в си́лу…»","official-business"],["«Не́бо бы́ло све́тлым, почти́ прозра́чным.»","literary"],["«Как показа́ло иссле́дование, показа́тель вы́рос.»","scientific"],["«А воз и ны́не там: де́ло не сдви́нулось.»","journalistic (a precedent phrase)"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions on the reading marathon. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"Эмма scans a page just to see what it's about. That gear is…","opts":["изуча́ющее чте́ние","просмо́тр (skimming)","word-by-word translation"],"answer":1},{"q":"A real article quotes a law, then paints a scene, then asks the reader a question. This is…","opts":["a mistake by the writer","normal — real text switches styles","only possible in fiction"],"answer":1},{"q":"«А воз и ны́не там» in a news piece signals…","opts":["a precedent phrase — “nothing has moved”","a literal cart on a road","a scientific term"],"answer":0},{"q":"The safest way to meet an unknown word in a flowing text is to…","opts":["stop and translate it immediately","guess it from context and keep reading for the idea","skip the whole sentence"],"answer":1},{"q":"A text that writes «сконча́лся» rather than «у́мер» is telling you its tone is…","opts":["official / respectful","colloquial","childish"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"One real magazine spread, four styles in a row — a news frame, an official quote, a literary image, a precedent phrase, an evaluative close. Read it as one idea, not word by word.","title":"Оди́н разворо́т, четы́ре ми́ра","sentences":["Го́род сно́ва спо́рит о судьбе́ ста́рого па́рка — и, как изве́стно, э́то далеко́ не пе́рвый тако́й спор.","В официа́льном отве́те ска́зано су́хо: «Террито́рия отно́сится к зо́не озелене́ния».","А ме́жду тем ста́рые ли́пы стоя́т, как молчали́вые свиде́тели, и роня́ют жёлтые ли́стья на скаме́йки.","Го́ды иду́т, коми́ссии заседа́ют, а воз и ны́не там.","Вопро́с остаётся о́стрым: чей же э́то парк — жи́телей и́ли отчёта?"],"translation":"The city is arguing again about the fate of the old park — and, as everyone knows, this is far from the first such dispute. The official reply says drily: “The territory belongs to the green zone.” Meanwhile the old lindens stand like silent witnesses, dropping yellow leaves onto the benches. Years pass, commissions sit, and the cart is still where it was. The question stays sharp: whose park is this — the residents' or the report's?"},"essay":{"intro":"Read like Эмма — first the map, then the detail.","prompt":"Look at today's mixed passage (or any short Russian article). In Russian, do two things: (1) give its <b>main idea in ONE sentence</b> — that's your skim (просмо́тр); (2) point to <b>one line where the style suddenly changes</b> and name the two styles. 2–3 sentences total.","hint":"For the gist, start «В те́ксте говори́тся о том, что…». For the switch, try «Здесь стиль меня́ется с … на …» (e.g. с публицисти́ческого на официа́льный). Don't retell every line — just the idea and one seam 🙂","example":"Гла́вная мысль: в те́ксте говори́тся о спо́ре вокру́г ста́рого па́рка. Стиль меня́ется с публицисти́ческого на официа́льный, когда́ появля́ется цита́та «в соотве́тствии с постановле́нием»."}},"23":{"day":23,"week":"04","themeRu":"Hearing what's meant","themeEn":"Крити́ческое слу́шание","intro":"Yesterday the styles sat still on a page; today they come at you by ear, and moving. On the radio a guest calls a plan «прекра́сный» — but flatly, with a tired little «мол». The words say praise; the voice says the opposite. C1 listening isn't only catching what's said. It's hearing how, and with what stance.","introAudio":"Yesterday the styles sat still on a page; today they come at you by ear, and moving. On the radio a guest calls a plan 'prekrasny' — but flatly, with a tired little 'mol'. The words say praise; the voice says the opposite. C1 listening isn't only catching what's said. It's hearing how, and with what stance.","goals":["Listen on three levels: <b>о́бщий</b> (gist), <b>дета́льный</b> (facts), <b>крити́ческий</b> (the speaker's stance)","Catch <b>how</b> something is said — evaluation and irony, not just the content","Read the reported-speech particles <b>мол</b> and <b>де́скать</b> — “supposedly / he claims”","Tell a speaker's real position from tone and loaded word choice"],"learned":["Listened on three levels, including the critical (stance) level","Caught evaluation and irony behind the words","Read мол / де́скать as signals of distance and doubt","Judged a speaker's stance from tone and word choice"],"review":{"intro":"Yesterday you read across styles. A quick look back — a reading gear, a particle, a well-known line — then we start listening.","introAudio":"Yesterday you read across styles. A quick look back — a reading gear, a particle, a well-known line — then we start listening.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer.","q":"To catch just the gist of a long broadcast, you listen the way you <b>skim</b> a page — that reading gear is…","opts":["изуча́ющее чте́ние","просмо́тр","слова́рь"],"answer":1,"explain":"Skimming for the gist is <b>просмо́тр</b>; study reading (изуча́ющее) is for grasping every phrase."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Ты <b>же</b> обеща́л!» — the particle <b>же</b> here adds…","opts":["a reproach — “you PROMISED, remember”","a polite request","a question about time"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>же</b> here presses on shared knowledge — a reproachful “you did promise, and you know it.”"},{"type":"choice","q":"«Лу́чше по́здно, чем никогда́» dropped into a report is…","opts":["a scientific term","a well-known saying (a precedent phrase)","an official cliché"],"answer":1,"explain":"It's a proverb everyone knows — a precedent phrase carrying a wink, not a term or a formula."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Listen on three levels","title":"<em>О́бщий</em>, <em>дета́льный</em>, <em>крити́ческий</em> — hear all three","paras":["At B2 you listened for two things: the <b>о́бщий</b> level (gist — what's this about?) and the <b>дета́льный</b> level (the facts — names, numbers, when). C1 adds a third, harder one: the <b>крити́ческий</b> level — the speaker's <b>stance</b>. Not what they said, but what they think of it.","Two people can report the same fact and reveal opposite positions. «Це́ны немно́го вы́росли» (mild, neutral) and «Це́ны взлете́ли до небе́с!» (alarmed, negative) carry the same number and a different judgement. The judgement is the message.","English speakers are trained to listen for content and stop there. Add the third channel: as you follow <b>what</b> is said, keep asking — and how does the speaker <b>feel</b> about it?"],"audio":"At B2 you listened for two things: the obshchy level, the gist — what's this about; and the detalny level, the facts — names, numbers, when. C1 adds a third, harder one: the kritichesky level, the speaker's stance. Not what they said, but what they think of it. Two people can report the same fact and reveal opposite positions. Tseny nemnogo vyrosli, mild and neutral, and Tseny vzleteli do nebes, alarmed and negative, carry the same number and a different judgement. The judgement is the message. English speakers are trained to listen for content and stop there. Add the third channel: as you follow what is said, keep asking how the speaker feels about it.","examples":[{"ru":"Same fact, neutral stance.","en":"Нейтра́льно: «Це́ны немно́го вы́росли».","say":"Нейтрально: цены немного выросли."},{"ru":"Same fact, alarmed stance.","en":"С трево́гой: «Це́ны взлете́ли до небе́с!»","say":"С тревогой: цены взлетели до небес!"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"You catch every fact of a speech but miss whether the speaker approves or scorns it. You've heard the … levels but not the critical one.","opts":["general and detailed","only the critical","none of the"],"answer":0,"explain":"Facts = the general and detailed levels. The stance you missed is the <b>critical</b> level, the C1 addition."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Plain letters are fine.","q":"The listening level that catches the speaker's stance and evaluation is the … one. Type the adjective (Russian):","accept":["критический"],"placeholder":"крити́…","explain":"The <b>крити́ческий</b> level — hearing the speaker's position, not just the facts."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The loaded word gives the stance away","title":"<em>Сниже́ние</em> or <em>обва́л</em>? The word carries the verdict","paras":["Stance rides on word choice. For the very same drop, a calm analyst says <b>сниже́ние</b> (a decline) or <b>корректи́ровка</b> (a correction); an alarmist says <b>обва́л</b> (a crash) or <b>катастро́фа</b>. Nothing about the number changed — the speaker's verdict is baked into the noun.","So listen for the marked word. A neutral synonym reports; a loaded synonym judges. «Уча́стники» vs «толпа́», «мероприя́тие» vs «сбо́рище» — each pair is one fact and two attitudes.","This is where your synonym work from Week 1 pays off by ear: the choice between synonyms is the speaker telling you, quietly, whose side they're on."],"audio":"Stance rides on word choice. For the very same drop, a calm analyst says snizheniye, a decline, or korrektirovka, a correction; an alarmist says obval, a crash, or katastrofa. Nothing about the number changed — the speaker's verdict is baked into the noun. So listen for the marked word: a neutral synonym reports, a loaded synonym judges. Uchastniki versus tolpa, meropriyatiye versus sborishche — each pair is one fact and two attitudes. This is where your synonym work from Week 1 pays off by ear: the choice between synonyms is the speaker telling you, quietly, whose side they're on.","table":{"rows":[["сниже́ние — обва́л <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"снижение, обвал\">🔊</button>","a decline (neutral) — a crash (alarmed)"],["уча́стники — толпа́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"участники, толпа\">🔊</button>","participants (neutral) — a mob (hostile)"],["мероприя́тие — сбо́рище <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"мероприятие, сборище\">🔊</button>","an event (neutral) — a gathering (pejorative)"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"Neutral: “Many participants gathered.”","en":"Нейтра́льно: «Собрало́сь мно́го уча́стников».","say":"Нейтрально: собралось много участников."},{"ru":"Hostile: “A mob gathered.” — same people, a verdict.","en":"С неприя́знью: «Собрала́сь толпа́».","say":"С неприязнью: собралась толпа."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Hearing «сбо́рище учёных» and thinking it just means “a meeting of scholars.”","right":"«Сбо́рище» несёт презре́ние — говоря́щий оце́нивает, а не про́сто сообща́ет.","why":"«Сбо́рище» is a loaded, contemptuous synonym of «собра́ние». If you register only the denotation, you miss that the speaker is sneering. The stance is the point."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"On the radio: «На пло́щади собрало́сь <b>сбо́рище</b>». The word «сбо́рище» tells you the speaker is…","opts":["neutral and factual","disapproving / contemptuous","admiring"],"answer":1,"explain":"«сбо́рище» is a pejorative synonym of «собра́ние» — the speaker is judging, not just reporting."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · «Мол», «де́скать» and the ironic wink","title":"<em>Мол</em> / <em>де́скать</em> — “supposedly,” with a raised eyebrow","paras":["Two little words tell you the speaker is quoting someone at arm's length. <b>Мол</b> and <b>де́скать</b> both mean roughly “he says / supposedly,” but they carry doubt: the speaker repeats a claim without owning it. «Он, <b>мол</b>, за́нят» = “he's <i>supposedly</i> busy” — and I'm not convinced.","They're the audible sign of reported speech <i>with distance</i>. Where English would use tone or “air quotes,” Russian has a word for it. Miss «мол» and you hear a plain report; catch it and you hear the doubt.","Irony works the same way, by ear. «Ну коне́чно, гениа́льный план» said flatly means the opposite — a terrible plan. Listen for the mismatch between the praising words and the dry tone: that gap is the irony."],"audio":"Two little words tell you the speaker is quoting someone at arm's length. Mol and deskat both mean roughly 'he says, supposedly', but they carry doubt: the speaker repeats a claim without owning it. On, mol, zanyat — he's supposedly busy, and I'm not convinced. They're the audible sign of reported speech with distance; where English uses tone or air quotes, Russian has a word for it. Miss mol and you hear a plain report; catch it and you hear the doubt. Irony works the same way by ear: Nu konechno, genialny plan, said flatly, means the opposite — a terrible plan. Listen for the mismatch between the praising words and the dry tone: that gap is the irony.","examples":[{"ru":"Reported with doubt: “He's supposedly very busy.”","en":"«Он, <b>мол</b>, о́чень за́нят» — говоря́щий сомнева́ется.","say":"Он, мол, очень занят — говорящий сомневается."},{"ru":"Irony: praising words, opposite meaning.","en":"«Ну коне́чно, <b>гениа́льный</b> план» — ска́зано с ирони́ей.","say":"Ну конечно, гениальный план — сказано с иронией."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Она́, де́скать, всё зна́ет лу́чше всех.» The word «де́скать» adds…","opts":["respect and agreement","the speaker's doubt / distance from the claim","a time reference"],"answer":1,"explain":"«де́скать», like «мол», marks reported speech held at arm's length — the speaker isn't endorsing the claim."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“He's supposedly busy” (with a doubting «мол»).</b>","want":"Он, мол, за́нят","answer":"Он, мол, за́нят"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · The channel English speakers miss","title":"Hear <em>how</em>, not only <em>what</em>","paras":["Critical listening is the least-trained receptive skill for English speakers, because English leans on tone and context where Russian also uses <b>words</b> — particles, loaded synonyms, evaluative markers. If you only decode the content, you'll agree with an ironist and trust a sneer.","So run all three channels at once: the facts (<b>что</b>), the delivery (<b>как</b>), and the verdict (<b>с какой пози́цией</b>). Ask of every speaker: are they approving, doubtful, ironic? Which word gave it away — a «мол», a «сбо́рище», a flat “of course”?","This is the ear the exam's listening subtest checks — general, detailed, and critical. Train it and you stop taking every confident voice at face value."],"audio":"Critical listening is the least-trained receptive skill for English speakers, because English leans on tone and context where Russian also uses words — particles, loaded synonyms, evaluative markers. If you only decode the content, you'll agree with an ironist and trust a sneer. So run all three channels at once: the facts, chto; the delivery, kak; and the verdict, s kakoy pozitsiey. Ask of every speaker: are they approving, doubtful, ironic? Which word gave it away — a mol, a sborishche, a flat 'of course'? This is the ear the exam's listening subtest checks — general, detailed, and critical. Train it and you stop taking every confident voice at face value.","examples":[{"ru":"Read the stance, not just the words.","en":"Ва́жно не то́лько <b>что</b> ска́зано, но и <b>как</b> и с како́й пози́цией.","say":"Важно не только что сказано, но и как и с какой позицией."},{"ru":"A flat “of course” can mean the opposite.","en":"Ро́вное «<b>ну коне́чно</b>» иногда́ зна́чит «вот уж вря́д ли».","say":"Ровное ну конечно иногда значит вот уж вряд ли."}],"tasks":[{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it dry and doubting.","target":"Он, мол, всё сде́лал сам — как же.","sub":"the «мол» and «как же» carry the irony — read it flat, not sincere","want":"он мол всё сделал сам как же"},{"type":"choice","q":"Critical listening means catching, on top of the facts, the speaker's…","opts":["accent","stance and evaluation","speaking speed"],"answer":1,"explain":"The critical level is the speaker's <b>stance and evaluation</b> — approval, doubt, irony — beyond the bare content."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Эмма and Ка́тя are half-listening to a radio talk-show. Эмма keeps hearing a second layer. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Слы́шала? Он сказа́л, что рефо́рма «прошла́ успе́шно».","ru":"Did you hear? He said the reform “went off successfully.”"},{"who":"A","en":"Сказа́ть-то сказа́л. Но ты слы́шала интона́цию?","ru":"Say it he did. But did you hear the tone?"},{"who":"B","en":"А что с интона́цией?","ru":"What about the tone?"},{"who":"A","en":"Су́хо, с усме́шкой. «Успе́шно», мол, — а сам не ве́рит.","ru":"Dry, with a smirk. “Successfully,” supposedly — but he doesn't believe it himself."},{"who":"B","en":"То́чно! И пото́м доба́вил: «э́то, де́скать, вре́менные тру́дности».","ru":"Right! And then he added: “these are, supposedly, temporary difficulties.”"},{"who":"A","en":"Вот-вот. «Де́скать» — зна́чит, он переска́зывает чужи́е слова́ и не согла́сен.","ru":"Exactly. “Deskat” means he's relaying someone else's words and doesn't agree."},{"who":"B","en":"Я слы́шала то́лько фа́кты, а ты — его́ пози́цию.","ru":"I heard only the facts, and you — his stance."},{"who":"A","en":"В э́том и вся суть: не то́лько что, но и как.","ru":"That's the whole point: not only what, but how."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for hearing the stance behind the words. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"слу́шатель","ru":"listener","ex":"Внима́тельный слу́шатель ло́вит подте́кст."},{"en":"оце́нка","ru":"evaluation, verdict","ex":"В его́ слова́х — я́вная оце́нка."},{"en":"пози́ция","ru":"stance, position","ex":"Кака́я у а́втора пози́ция?"},{"en":"ирони́я","ru":"irony","ex":"Э́то ска́зано с ирони́ей."},{"en":"намёк","ru":"hint, insinuation","ex":"Я по́нял намёк без слов."},{"en":"подте́кст","ru":"subtext","ex":"Ва́жен не текст, а подте́кст."},{"en":"интона́ция","ru":"intonation, tone","ex":"Интона́ция вы́дала его́ сомне́ние."},{"en":"я́кобы","ru":"allegedly, supposedly","ex":"Он я́кобы всё уже́ реши́л."},{"en":"одобре́ние","ru":"approval","ex":"В го́лосе слы́шалось одобре́ние."},{"en":"скепти́ческий","ru":"skeptical","ex":"Он говори́л скепти́ческим то́ном."},{"en":"вы́сказывание","ru":"utterance, statement","ex":"Э́то вы́сказывание мо́жно по́нять по-ра́зному."},{"en":"улови́ть","ru":"to catch, perceive","ex":"Постара́йся улови́ть его́ настро́ение."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Each line is heard on air — match it to the speaker's attitude.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto the attitude — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["«Сниже́ние соста́вило три проце́нта.»","a neutral report"],["«Настоя́щий обва́л ры́нка!»","alarm, a negative verdict"],["«Он, мол, о́чень за́нят.»","doubt / keeping distance"],["«Ну коне́чно, гениа́льный план.»","irony — means the opposite"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions on critical listening. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"The third, C1 listening level — beyond gist and detail — is the…","opts":["крити́ческий (stance) level","louder level","faster level"],"answer":0},{"q":"A reporter says «обва́л» instead of «сниже́ние». The word choice signals…","opts":["a neutral fact","alarm / a negative verdict","approval"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Он, мол, о́чень за́нят» tells you the speaker…","opts":["fully agrees he's busy","doubts the claim / keeps distance","is asking a question"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Ну коне́чно, гениа́льный план», said flatly, most likely means…","opts":["genuine praise","the opposite — irony","a real plan proposal"],"answer":1},{"q":"Calling a crowd «толпа́» rather than «уча́стники» shows the speaker's…","opts":["hostility / low opinion","admiration","neutrality"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"A short radio exchange — read it as if you heard it. Follow the facts, then the stance underneath: a proverb, a hedge, a «мол», and a closing verdict on the tone.","title":"На ра́дио: что сказа́л — и что име́л в виду́","sentences":["Веду́щий: Реши́ли постро́ить но́вый мост. Как говори́тся, лу́чше по́здно, чем никогда́.","Гость: Мост, коне́чно, ну́жен. Прое́кт, пра́вда, обеща́ют уже́ деся́тый год.","Веду́щий: То есть вы настро́ены скепти́чески?","Гость: Ска́жем так: обеща́ли, мол, всё гото́во. Посмо́трим.","А в интона́ции — не наде́жда, а уста́лая ирони́я."],"translation":"Host: They've decided to build a new bridge. As they say, better late than never. Guest: The bridge is needed, of course. The project, mind you, has been promised for ten years now. Host: So you're skeptical? Guest: Let's put it this way: they promised, supposedly, it's all ready. We'll see. And in the tone — not hope, but tired irony."},"essay":{"intro":"Now you decode a stance in writing.","prompt":"Think of something you heard recently — a broadcast, a speech, a friend telling a story. In Russian, write 2–3 sentences: (1) <b>what</b> was said, and (2) the speaker's <b>stance</b> — approving, doubtful, ironic — and the word, particle or tone that gave it away.","hint":"Useful frames: «Он/она́ сказа́л(а), что…», then «Но по интона́ции бы́ло я́сно, что…», or «Сло́во “…” вы́дало его́ иро́нию». Watch for a «мол» or a loaded synonym 🙂","example":"Веду́щий сказа́л, что план «прекра́сный». Но по его́ уста́лой интона́ции бы́ло я́сно, что он не ве́рит в успе́х: э́то «прекра́сный», мол, — с ирони́ей."}},"24":{"day":24,"week":"04","themeRu":"The C1 essay","themeEn":"Эссе́ уровня́ C1","intro":"You've read across styles and listened for stance. Now you produce — and the hardest thing to produce in writing is the essay. Not a warm stream of opinion, but an argued case: a claim, reasons with facts, bookish connectors, one steady register, a conclusion that lands. Ка́тя has one due this week and no idea where to start. By the end of today, you could show her.","introAudio":"You've read across styles and listened for stance. Now you produce — and the hardest thing to produce in writing is the essay. Not a warm stream of opinion, but an argued case: a claim, reasons with facts, bookish connectors, one steady register, a conclusion that lands. Katya has one due this week and no idea where to start. By the end of today, you could show her.","goals":["Build a C1 essay: <b>те́зис</b> (thesis) → arguments with facts → <b>вы́вод</b> (conclusion)","Hold ONE register throughout — bookish-neutral, no chat, no slang, no dead officialese","Link the argument with bookish connectors (<b>тем не ме́нее, сле́довательно, что каса́ется</b>)","State the objective content <b>and</b> your own position — and argue it"],"learned":["Structured an essay as thesis → arguments → conclusion","Held one bookish-neutral register from start to finish","Connected ideas with bookish connectors","Separated objective content from your own argued position"],"review":{"intro":"One look back — a doubting particle, a connector, a claim — and then the flagship written genre of C1: the essay.","introAudio":"One look back — a doubting particle, a connector, a claim — and then the flagship written genre of C1: the essay.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer.","q":"«Он, мол, всё сде́лал сам.» The «мол» tells you the speaker…","opts":["fully believes it","doubts it / keeps distance","is giving an order"],"answer":1,"explain":"«мол» marks reported speech held at a distance — the speaker isn't endorsing the claim."},{"type":"choice","q":"“The plan is risky. …, it may still work.” Which connector fits the contrast?","opts":["Сле́довательно","Тем не ме́нее","Наприме́р"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>Тем не ме́нее</b> (nevertheless) marks contrast. Сле́довательно = therefore; наприме́р = for example."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build a thesis line: <b>“In my view, the main thing is not talent, but work.”</b>","want":"На мой взгля́д, гла́вное — не тала́нт, а труд","answer":"На мой взгля́д, гла́вное — не тала́нт, а труд"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Thesis → arguments → conclusion","title":"An essay is an <em>argued case</em>, not a stream of opinion","paras":["The C1 essay has a spine. It opens with a <b>те́зис</b> — one clear claim you'll defend. It develops through <b>аргуме́нты</b> — reasons backed by facts or examples, one idea per paragraph. It closes with a <b>вы́вод</b> — a conclusion that follows from the arguments, not a fresh thought.","What it is <i>not</i> is a stream of feelings — «мне ка́жется… а ещё… ну и вообще́…». That reads as a chat, not an essay. The examiner is looking for a built structure: claim, support, close.","So before you write, decide your thesis in one sentence. Everything after it either supports or qualifies that sentence. If a line doesn't, it doesn't belong."],"audio":"The C1 essay has a spine. It opens with a tezis, one clear claim you'll defend. It develops through argumenty, reasons backed by facts or examples, one idea per paragraph. It closes with a vyvod, a conclusion that follows from the arguments, not a fresh thought. What it is not is a stream of feelings — mne kazhetsya, a yeshchyo, nu i voobshche. That reads as a chat, not an essay. The examiner is looking for a built structure: claim, support, close. So before you write, decide your thesis in one sentence. Everything after it either supports or qualifies that sentence; if a line doesn't, it doesn't belong.","examples":[{"ru":"A thesis — one defensible claim.","en":"Те́зис: «Чте́ние остаётся ва́жным да́же в эпо́ху ви́део».","say":"Тезис: чтение остаётся важным даже в эпоху видео."},{"ru":"A conclusion — it follows from the arguments.","en":"Вы́вод: «Сле́довательно, отка́зываться от книг ра́но».","say":"Вывод: следовательно, отказываться от книг рано."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"Which is the <b>essay</b>, not a chat?","opts":["«Ну, мне ка́жется, кни́ги — э́то кру́то, и вообще́…»","«Чте́ние развива́ет мышле́ние; сле́довательно, оно́ ва́жно.»","«Да ла́дно, кто сейча́с чита́ет!»"],"answer":1,"explain":"The middle line has a claim and a conclusion in an even register. The others are conversational — fine with friends, wrong for an essay."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Plain letters are fine.","q":"The essay's opening claim — the one sentence you set out to defend — is the … . Type the Russian noun:","accept":["тезис"],"placeholder":"те́зис","explain":"The <b>те́зис</b> — your main claim; everything else in the essay supports it."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · One register, held to the end","title":"An essay stays in <em>one</em> voice","paras":["The essay lives in a <b>bookish-neutral</b> register — educated, even, unemotional but not dead. One slangy word («кру́то», «жесть», «бе́сит») or one chat particle («ну», «вот», «да ла́дно») punctures it instantly. So does the opposite extreme: piling on officialese («в связи́ с вышеизло́женным») until it reads like a form.","The test is consistency. A reader should not be able to tell, from register, where in the essay they are — the first line and the last sit in the same voice. This is the single most common place C1 writers slip: a perfect argument spoiled by one out-of-register word.","When in doubt, choose the neutral synonym you drilled in Week 1: «мно́го», not «до́фига»; «о́чень хорошо́», not «класс»; «по-мо́ему», not «я тебе́ говорю́»."],"audio":"The essay lives in a bookish-neutral register — educated, even, unemotional but not dead. One slangy word — kruto, zhest, besit — or one chat particle — nu, vot, da ladno — punctures it instantly. So does the opposite extreme: piling on officialese, v svyazi s vysheizlozhennym, until it reads like a form. The test is consistency: a reader should not be able to tell, from register, where in the essay they are. This is the single most common place C1 writers slip — a perfect argument spoiled by one out-of-register word. When in doubt, choose the neutral synonym you drilled in Week 1: mnogo, not dofiga; ochen khorosho, not klass.","examples":[{"ru":"In register: even and bookish.","en":"«Э́тот до́вод представля́ется убеди́тельным».","say":"Этот довод представляется убедительным."},{"ru":"Out of register in an essay: too chatty.","en":"«Э́тот до́вод — про́сто <b>о́гонь</b>, че́стно».","say":"Этот довод — просто огонь, честно."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«А́втор прав, и вообще́ его́ иде́я — про́сто бо́мба.»","right":"«А́втор прав, и его́ иде́я представля́ется весьма́ уда́чной.»","why":"«Про́сто бо́мба» is colloquial slang — it shatters the essay's even, bookish register. The neutral-bookish «представля́ется весьма́ уда́чной» holds the voice. One slangy word is enough to break an otherwise strong essay."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"In an essay, “this argument is very convincing” is best as…","opts":["«э́тот до́вод — про́сто бо́мба»","«э́тот до́вод весьма́ убеди́телен»","«э́тот аргуме́нт — о́гонь»"],"answer":1,"explain":"Bookish-neutral: <b>весьма́ убеди́телен</b>. The others are slang — wrong register for an essay."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The connectors that hold it together","title":"Bookish connectors: the <em>skeleton</em> of the essay","paras":["Cohesion is visible. A C1 essay is laced with <b>bookish connectors</b> that show the reader the logic: <b>во-пе́рвых… во-вторы́х…</b> (firstly… secondly), <b>с одно́й стороны́… с друго́й стороны́…</b> (on one hand… on the other), <b>тем не ме́нее</b> (nevertheless), <b>сле́довательно / таки́м о́бразом</b> (therefore / thus), <b>что каса́ется…</b> (as for…), <b>в заключе́ние</b> (in conclusion).","These aren't decoration — they mark the moves of the argument: adding, contrasting, concluding, changing topic. English speakers tend to just <i>list</i> («and… and… also…»); Russian expects the logical relation named.","Place them where the logic turns, not in every line. One connector per move: a contrast gets a «тем не ме́нее», a conclusion gets a «сле́довательно». Overused, they clog; placed well, they carry the reader."],"audio":"Cohesion is visible. A C1 essay is laced with bookish connectors that show the reader the logic: vo-pervykh, vo-vtorykh — firstly, secondly; s odnoy storony, s drugoy storony — on one hand, on the other; tem ne meneye — nevertheless; sledovatelno, takim obrazom — therefore, thus; chto kasayetsya — as for; v zaklyucheniye — in conclusion. These aren't decoration — they mark the moves of the argument: adding, contrasting, concluding, changing topic. English speakers tend to just list — and, and, also; Russian expects the logical relation named. Place them where the logic turns, not in every line. One connector per move; overused they clog, placed well they carry the reader.","table":{"rows":[["во-пе́рвых… во-вторы́х… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"во-первых, во-вторых\">🔊</button>","firstly… secondly… (listing arguments)"],["тем не ме́нее <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"тем не менее\">🔊</button>","nevertheless (contrast)"],["сле́довательно <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"следовательно\">🔊</button>","therefore (a conclusion)"],["что каса́ется… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"что касается\">🔊</button>","as for… (a new sub-topic)"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"Contrast, marked.","en":"«План рискова́н. <b>Тем не ме́нее</b> он мо́жет срабо́тать».","say":"План рискован. Тем не менее он может сработать."},{"ru":"Conclusion, marked.","en":"«Фа́кты схо́дятся. <b>Сле́довательно</b>, вы́вод очеви́ден».","say":"Факты сходятся. Следовательно, вывод очевиден."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“The idea is appealing. …, it has one serious flaw.” The connector should be…","opts":["Сле́довательно","Одна́ко / тем не ме́нее","Наприме́р"],"answer":1,"explain":"A contrast needs <b>одна́ко / тем не ме́нее</b>. Сле́довательно marks a conclusion; наприме́р introduces an example."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build a conclusion line: <b>“Therefore, the question remains open.”</b>","want":"Сле́довательно, вопро́с остаётся откры́тым","answer":"Сле́довательно, вопро́с остаётся откры́тым"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Objective content + your own position","title":"State the <em>facts</em> — and take a <em>stand</em>","paras":["A C1 essay is not pure opinion and not a neutral summary — it's both, in order. First establish the <b>objective</b> side: what's known, what the facts show («изве́стно, что…», «фа́кты пока́зывают, что…», «неоспори́мо, что…»). Then stake your <b>own position</b> clearly («на мой взгля́д…», «я убеждён / убеждена́, что…», «представля́ется, что…») and defend it.","And write for someone. Every essay has an addressee and a purpose — to persuade a reader who may disagree. That's why the objective ground matters: you're not just asserting a feeling, you're building a case a sceptic would have to engage with.","So the shape of the whole thing: an objective opening, an argued middle in one register with clear connectors, a personal but reasoned stand, and a conclusion that lands. That is Writing Task 3 of the exam — and the essay a native would respect."],"audio":"A C1 essay is not pure opinion and not a neutral summary — it's both, in order. First establish the objective side: what's known, what the facts show — izvestno chto, fakty pokazyvayut chto, neosporimo chto. Then stake your own position clearly — na moy vzglyad, ya ubezhdyon, predstavlyayetsya chto — and defend it. And write for someone: every essay has an addressee and a purpose, to persuade a reader who may disagree. That's why the objective ground matters — you're not asserting a feeling, you're building a case a sceptic would have to engage with. So the shape of the whole thing: an objective opening, an argued middle in one register with clear connectors, a personal but reasoned stand, and a conclusion that lands. That is Writing Task 3 of the exam.","examples":[{"ru":"Objective ground, then position.","en":"«Изве́стно, что чте́ние развива́ет мышле́ние. На мой взгля́д, э́то ва́жно с де́тства».","say":"Известно, что чтение развивает мышление. На мой взгляд, это важно с детства."},{"ru":"A reasoned stand, not a bare feeling.","en":"«Я убеждён, что <b>э́то так</b>, и вот почему́…».","say":"Я убеждён, что это так, и вот почему."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"A C1 essay differs from a bare opinion because it also…","opts":["avoids any personal view","grounds the view in facts and arguments","uses slang for effect"],"answer":1,"explain":"The essay pairs your position with objective facts and reasoning — that's what makes it a case, not just a feeling."},{"type":"type","q":"To open the objective side of an essay, a common frame is «… , что…» — “it is known that…”. Type the missing adverb (Russian, one word):","accept":["известно"],"placeholder":"Изве́…","explain":"«Изве́стно, что…» sets the objective ground before you state your own position."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Ка́тя has to write an essay for a course and can't get started. Эмма walks her through the spine. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Э́мма, у меня́ эссе́, а вы́ходит како́й-то пото́к мы́слей.","ru":"Emma, I have an essay, and what comes out is some stream of thoughts."},{"who":"A","en":"Пе́рвый вопро́с: како́й у тебя́ те́зис? Одно́ предложе́ние.","ru":"First question: what's your thesis? One sentence."},{"who":"B","en":"Ну… что чте́ние ва́жно. Наве́рное.","ru":"Well… that reading matters. Probably."},{"who":"A","en":"Отли́чно. Тепе́рь два до́вода — и к ка́ждому факт и́ли приме́р.","ru":"Great. Now two arguments — and a fact or example for each."},{"who":"B","en":"А мо́жно написа́ть «кни́ги — э́то про́сто кру́то»?","ru":"Can I write “books are just awesome”?"},{"who":"A","en":"В эссе́ — нет. Оди́н реги́стр, кни́жно-нейтра́льный. «Кру́то» его́ ло́мает.","ru":"In an essay — no. One register, bookish-neutral. “Awesome” breaks it."},{"who":"B","en":"А в конце́?","ru":"And at the end?"},{"who":"A","en":"Вы́вод со свя́зкой: «сле́довательно…». И держи́ свою́ пози́цию.","ru":"A conclusion with a connector: “therefore…”. And hold your position."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for building an essay. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"эссе́","ru":"essay","ex":"Мне ну́жно написа́ть эссе́."},{"en":"те́зис","ru":"thesis, main claim","ex":"Сформули́руй те́зис в одно́м предложе́нии."},{"en":"аргуме́нт","ru":"argument, reasoned point (not a quarrel)","ex":"Ка́ждый аргуме́нт ну́жно обоснова́ть."},{"en":"до́вод","ru":"argument, point","ex":"Э́то си́льный до́вод в по́льзу иде́и."},{"en":"пози́ция","ru":"position, stance","ex":"В эссе́ ва́жно вы́разить свою́ пози́цию."},{"en":"обоснова́ть","ru":"to substantiate, justify","ex":"Мне́ние на́до обоснова́ть фа́ктами."},{"en":"убеди́тельный","ru":"convincing","ex":"Его́ до́воды звуча́т убеди́тельно."},{"en":"свя́зность","ru":"cohesion","ex":"Свя́зность де́ржит текст воеди́но."},{"en":"вы́вод","ru":"conclusion","ex":"Вы́вод сле́дует из аргуме́нтов."},{"en":"сле́довательно","ru":"therefore, consequently","ex":"Фа́кты схо́дятся, сле́довательно, я прав."},{"en":"реги́стр","ru":"register (level of speech)","ex":"Держи́ оди́н реги́стр во всём эссе́."},{"en":"в заключе́ние","ru":"in conclusion","ex":"В заключе́ние подведём ито́г."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each bookish connector to the logical move it makes.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its function — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["сле́довательно","therefore (a conclusion)"],["тем не ме́нее","nevertheless (a contrast)"],["что каса́ется…","as for… (a new sub-topic)"],["во-пе́рвых","firstly (the first argument)"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions on the C1 essay. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"The backbone of a C1 essay is…","opts":["thesis → arguments → conclusion","a stream of feelings","a list of facts only"],"answer":0},{"q":"Which word would break an essay's register?","opts":["сле́довательно","бо́мба (slang)","тем не ме́нее"],"answer":1},{"q":"“The plan is bold; …, it is risky.” The right connector is…","opts":["тем не ме́нее (contrast)","наприме́р","во-пе́рвых"],"answer":0},{"q":"«На мой взгля́д…» in an essay introduces…","opts":["an objective fact","the writer's own position","a quotation"],"answer":1},{"q":"Besides your position, a C1 essay must offer…","opts":["only emotions","only clichés","objective content and arguments"],"answer":2}]},"reading":{"intro":"A model mini-essay — thesis, one side, a contrast, a fact, your own position, a conclusion. Notice the even bookish register and the connectors doing the work.","title":"Эссе́: как оно́ звучи́т","sentences":["Вопро́с о том, ну́жно ли сохраня́ть ста́рые зда́ния, не так прост, как ка́жется.","С одно́й стороны́, го́роду нужны́ но́вые дома́ и совреме́нное жильё.","Тем не ме́нее ста́рая архитекту́ра храни́т па́мять и о́блик го́рода.","Фа́кты пока́зывают, что реставра́ция ча́сто деше́вле сно́са.","На мой взгля́д, разу́мнее не сноси́ть, а бе́режно обновля́ть.","Сле́довательно, го́род мо́жет расти́, не теря́я своего́ лица́."],"translation":"The question of whether to preserve old buildings is not as simple as it seems. On the one hand, the city needs new houses and modern housing. Nevertheless, old architecture keeps the memory and the face of the city. Facts show that restoration is often cheaper than demolition. In my view, it is wiser not to demolish but to renovate carefully. Consequently, the city can grow without losing its face."},"essay":{"intro":"Now write one yourself — the flagship task of C1.","prompt":"Write a short <b>C1 essay</b> (4–6 sentences) on ONE question, e.g. «Что важне́е — тала́нт и́ли труд?» or «Ну́жен ли иностра́нный язы́к ка́ждому?». Structure it: a <b>thesis</b>, at least two <b>arguments</b> (with a connector between them), your <b>own position</b>, and a <b>conclusion</b>. Hold ONE bookish-neutral register from first word to last.","hint":"Skeleton: те́зис → «Во-пе́рвых… Во-вторы́х…» / «С одно́й стороны́… Тем не ме́нее…» → «На мой взгля́д…» → «Сле́довательно, …». No slang, no «ну / вот» — even, bookish, argued 🙂","example":"Что важне́е — тала́нт и́ли труд? На мой взгля́д, реша́ющую роль игра́ет труд. Тала́нт даёт лишь возмо́жность, тогда́ как результа́т рожда́ется из уси́лий. Тем не ме́нее без тала́нта труд ча́сто остаётся незаме́ченным. Сле́довательно, ва́жно не противопоставля́ть их, а сочета́ть."}},"25":{"day":25,"week":"04","themeRu":"A response and a summary","themeEn":"О́тзыв и пробле́мный конспе́кт","intro":"An essay argues your own case from the ground up. But two other written genres react to someone else's text — and they pull in opposite directions. A рефера́т only summarises; an о́тзыв judges. A конспе́кт compresses — and a good one circles the source's real problem. Эмма does all three on the manuscript on her desk. Today, so do you.","introAudio":"An essay argues your own case from the ground up. But two other written genres react to someone else's text — and they pull in opposite directions. A referat only summarises; an otzyv judges. A konspekt compresses — and a good one circles the source's real problem. Emma does all three on the manuscript on her desk. Today, so do you.","goals":["Write an <b>о́тзыв</b> — a response that <b>evaluates</b>: strong or weak, convincing or not, and why","Tell an о́тзыв from a <b>рефера́т</b> — one judges, the other only summarises","Write a <b>problem конспе́кт</b> — compress a source <b>and</b> flag its central problem","Use each genre's set clichés and keep a bookish-neutral register"],"learned":["Wrote an о́тзыв carrying real evaluation","Distinguished о́тзыв (judges) from рефера́т (only summarises)","Wrote a problem-focused конспе́кт that flags the central problem","Used each genre's clichés in an even register"],"review":{"intro":"A glance back — an essay's own-position frame, a summary cliché, a bit of bookish compression — then two more written genres.","introAudio":"A glance back — an essay's own-position frame, a summary cliche, a bit of bookish compression — then two more written genres.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer.","q":"In an essay, «на мой взгля́д» introduces…","opts":["an objective fact","the writer's own position","a quotation"],"answer":1,"explain":"«на мой взгля́д» flags the writer's own position — the personal stand the essay then defends."},{"type":"choice","q":"A neutral summary (рефера́т) of an article opens with…","opts":["«В статье́ рассма́тривается…»","«Че́стно, статья́ — бо́мба…»","«Я ненави́жу э́ту статью́…»"],"answer":0,"explain":"The рефера́т is objective: «В статье́ рассма́тривается…». The others smuggle in a verdict and a slangy register."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Compress to a bookish nominal: <b>“after he returned”</b> → …","want":"по возвраще́нии","answer":"по возвраще́нии"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The о́тзыв judges","title":"An <em>о́тзыв</em> is a reaction — it says what you <em>think</em>","paras":["An <b>о́тзыв</b> (a response / review) is your reasoned reaction to a text, a book, a film. Its whole job is <b>evaluation</b>: was it strong or weak, convincing or thin, moving or flat — and <i>why</i>. Without a verdict, it isn't an о́тзыв.","It's built on evaluative formulas: «текст произво́дит си́льное впечатле́ние» (the text makes a strong impression), «а́втор убеди́тельно пока́зывает…», «вы́вод ка́жется спо́рным», «нельзя́ не согласи́ться с тем, что…». The register stays bookish-neutral — you judge, but you don't gush or sneer.","Think of Эмма writing an о́тзыв on a manuscript she edited: she names what works, what doesn't, and gives her overall verdict. Opinion — but argued and in register."],"audio":"An otzyv, a response or review, is your reasoned reaction to a text, a book, a film. Its whole job is evaluation: was it strong or weak, convincing or thin, moving or flat — and why. Without a verdict, it isn't an otzyv. It's built on evaluative formulas: tekst proizvodit silnoye vpechatleniye, the text makes a strong impression; avtor ubeditelno pokazyvayet; vyvod kazhetsya spornym; nelzya ne soglasitsya s tem chto. The register stays bookish-neutral — you judge, but you don't gush or sneer. Think of Emma writing an otzyv on a manuscript she edited: she names what works, what doesn't, and gives her overall verdict. Opinion, but argued and in register.","examples":[{"ru":"A positive evaluation.","en":"«Кни́га произво́дит си́льное впечатле́ние и чита́ется легко́».","say":"Книга производит сильное впечатление и читается легко."},{"ru":"A measured criticism.","en":"«Одна́ко фина́л ка́жется мне сли́шком поспе́шным».","say":"Однако финал кажется мне слишком поспешным."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"What must an о́тзыв contain that a plain summary need not?","opts":["a full retelling","an evaluation / verdict","a list of dates"],"answer":1,"explain":"The о́тзыв's defining feature is <b>evaluation</b> — a reasoned verdict a рефера́т deliberately leaves out."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Plain letters are fine.","q":"A response that reacts to and evaluates a text is called an … (Russian noun). Type it:","accept":["отзыв"],"placeholder":"о́тзыв","explain":"An <b>о́тзыв</b> carries evaluation — that's what sets it apart from a рефера́т."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · О́тзыв vs рефера́т — verdict or no verdict","title":"Same text, two genres: one <em>judges</em>, one only <em>summarises</em>","paras":["The cleanest way to fix the о́тзыв is against the <b>рефера́т</b> you met earlier. A рефера́т <b>summarises</b> a source objectively, no opinion: «в статье́ рассма́тривается…», «а́втор прихо́дит к вы́воду, что…». Insert your judgement and it stops being a рефера́т.","An о́тзыв does the opposite: it foregrounds the <b>judgement</b>. «Статья́ убеди́тельна», «вы́вод спо́рен», «текст глубо́к, хотя́ и не бесспо́рен». The facts of the source are there only to support your verdict.","So the test question for any sentence: is this <i>reporting</i> the source (рефера́т) or <i>rating</i> it (о́тзыв)? Mixing them by accident — sliding an opinion into a рефера́т — is the classic slip."],"audio":"The cleanest way to fix the otzyv is against the referat you met earlier. A referat summarises a source objectively, no opinion: v state rassmatrivayetsya, avtor prikhodit k vyvodu chto. Insert your judgement and it stops being a referat. An otzyv does the opposite: it foregrounds the judgement — statya ubeditelna, vyvod sporen, tekst glubok khotya i ne bessporen. The facts of the source are there only to support your verdict. So the test question for any sentence: is this reporting the source, a referat, or rating it, an otzyv? Mixing them by accident, sliding an opinion into a referat, is the classic slip.","table":{"rows":[["В статье́ рассма́тривается… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в статье рассматривается\">🔊</button>","рефера́т — reports, no verdict"],["А́втор прихо́дит к вы́воду… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"автор приходит к выводу\">🔊</button>","рефера́т — reports the conclusion"],["Статья́ убеди́тельна, но вы́вод спо́рен. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"статья убедительна, но вывод спорен\">🔊</button>","о́тзыв — gives a verdict"],["Текст произво́дит си́льное впечатле́ние. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"текст производит сильное впечатление\">🔊</button>","о́тзыв — an evaluation"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"Рефера́т — no opinion.","en":"«В статье́ рассма́тривается вопро́с ми́грации».","say":"В статье рассматривается вопрос миграции."},{"ru":"О́тзыв — a clear verdict.","en":"«Статья́ убеди́тельна, но вы́вод ка́жется спо́рным».","say":"Статья убедительна, но вывод кажется спорным."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"В рефера́те: «В статье́ рассма́тривается вопро́с, и, че́стно, а́втор кру́то прав».","right":"В рефера́те: «В статье́ рассма́тривается вопро́с; а́втор прихо́дит к определённому вы́воду».","why":"A рефера́т must stay neutral — «че́стно… кру́то прав» smuggles in a verdict and a slangy register. Save the evaluation for an о́тзыв; keep the рефера́т objective."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«А́втор прихо́дит к вы́воду, что рефо́рма прова́лилась.» This sentence belongs to a…","opts":["рефера́т — it reports the conclusion","о́тзыв — it gives a verdict","chat message"],"answer":0,"explain":"It only <i>reports</i> the source's conclusion — no evaluation of its own. That's a <b>рефера́т</b>. An о́тзыв would add a judgement."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The конспе́кт that flags the problem","title":"A <em>problem конспе́кт</em>: compress — and name the tension","paras":["A <b>конспе́кт</b> is compressed notes on a source — shorter than the original, keeping its logic. A <b>problem-focused</b> конспе́кт does one thing more: it <b>names the central problem</b> — the question or tension the source is really about — instead of just listing points.","Its markers foreground that problem: «в осно́ве те́кста — вопро́с о том, …», «а́втор ста́вит пробле́му…», «гла́вное противоре́чие состои́т в том, что…». You compress the content, but you organise it around the problem, not around the paragraph order.","It stays objective — no verdict, that's the о́тзыв's job — but it's not a flat list either. It's a map of the source's argument with the key question circled."],"audio":"A konspekt is compressed notes on a source — shorter than the original, keeping its logic. A problem-focused konspekt does one thing more: it names the central problem, the question or tension the source is really about, instead of just listing points. Its markers foreground that problem: v osnove teksta vopros o tom, avtor stavit problemu, glavnoye protivorechiye sostoit v tom chto. You compress the content, but you organise it around the problem, not around the paragraph order. It stays objective — no verdict, that's the otzyv's job — but it's not a flat list either. It's a map of the source's argument with the key question circled.","examples":[{"ru":"Flag the central problem.","en":"«В осно́ве те́кста — вопро́с о том, ну́жен ли прогре́ссу преде́л».","say":"В основе текста — вопрос о том, нужен ли прогрессу предел."},{"ru":"Name the tension.","en":"«Гла́вное противоре́чие: свобо́да ли́чности про́тив о́бщего бла́га».","say":"Главное противоречие: свобода личности против общего блага."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"What makes a конспе́кт a <b>problem</b> конспе́кт?","opts":["it is longer than the source","it names the source's central problem","it adds your own opinion"],"answer":1,"explain":"A problem конспе́кт compresses the source <i>and</i> names its central problem — without adding a verdict."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build the problem-flag opener: <b>“At the heart of the text is the question of freedom.”</b>","want":"В осно́ве те́кста — вопро́с о свобо́де","answer":"В осно́ве те́кста — вопро́с о свобо́де"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Right clichés, even register","title":"Each genre has its <em>formulas</em> — and one steady voice","paras":["All three genres — рефера́т, о́тзыв, конспе́кт — are built from <b>set formulas</b>, not from “your own words” invented on the spot. The skill is reaching for the right formula: reporting phrases for a рефера́т, evaluative phrases for an о́тзыв, problem-phrases for a конспе́кт.","And all three sit in a <b>bookish-neutral register</b>. The о́тзыв judges but never gushes («про́сто отпа́д») or sneers («по́лная ерунда́»); it says «весьма́ уда́чно» or «недоста́точно обосно́вано». Even when you're critical, the voice stays measured.","Hold two things at once, then: the genre's job (report? judge? flag the problem?) and one steady, educated register. That's exactly what the exam's writing genres test — and what makes Эмма's editorial notes read like a professional's."],"audio":"All three genres — referat, otzyv, konspekt — are built from set formulas, not from your own words invented on the spot. The skill is reaching for the right formula: reporting phrases for a referat, evaluative phrases for an otzyv, problem-phrases for a konspekt. And all three sit in a bookish-neutral register. The otzyv judges but never gushes, prosto otpad, or sneers, polnaya erunda; it says vesma udachno or nedostatochno obosnovano. Even when you're critical, the voice stays measured. Hold two things at once: the genre's job — report, judge, or flag the problem — and one steady, educated register. That's exactly what the exam's writing genres test.","examples":[{"ru":"Measured criticism, in register.","en":"«До́вод недоста́точно обосно́ван» — кри́тика без гру́бости.","say":"Довод недостаточно обоснован — критика без грубости."},{"ru":"Out of register: too crude for an о́тзыв.","en":"«По́лная ерунда́» — сли́шком гру́бо для о́тзыва.","say":"Полная ерунда — слишком грубо для отзыва."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"A critical point in an о́тзыв is best phrased…","opts":["«по́лная ерунда́»","«до́вод недоста́точно обосно́ван»","«а́втор — бе́здарь»"],"answer":1,"explain":"Bookish-neutral criticism: <b>недоста́точно обосно́ван</b>. The others are rude — wrong register even for a negative о́тзыв."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and read it evenly.","target":"В осно́ве те́кста лежи́т о́стрый нра́вственный вопро́с.","sub":"a problem-flag line — read it like written notes read aloud","want":"в основе текста лежит острый нравственный вопрос"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"At the café, Ле́на asks Эмма about the manuscript she's been editing. Эмма slips between summary and verdict. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Ну как кни́га, кото́рую ты реда́ктируешь? Сто́ит чита́ть?","ru":"So how's the book you're editing? Worth reading?"},{"who":"A","en":"Е́сли ко́ротко о содержа́нии — э́то рома́н о вы́боре и со́вести.","ru":"If I'm brief about the content — it's a novel about choice and conscience."},{"who":"B","en":"Э́то ты переска́зываешь. А тебе́-то сама́ она́ как?","ru":"That's you retelling it. But how is it to you yourself?"},{"who":"A","en":"Э́то уже́ друго́е. Переска́з — рефера́т, а моя́ оце́нка — о́тзыв.","ru":"That's a different thing. A retelling is a summary; my evaluation is a response."},{"who":"B","en":"Дава́й о́тзыв!","ru":"Give me the response, then!"},{"who":"A","en":"Кни́га си́льная, чита́ется легко́, но фина́л ка́жется мне поспе́шным.","ru":"The book is strong, an easy read, but the ending feels rushed to me."},{"who":"B","en":"А в чём её гла́вная мысль?","ru":"And what's its main idea?"},{"who":"A","en":"В осно́ве — оди́н вопро́с: где грани́ца компроми́сса.","ru":"At its heart — one question: where the limit of compromise lies."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for responding to and boiling down a text. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"о́тзыв","ru":"response, review (evaluative)","ex":"Я написа́ла о́тзыв на статью́."},{"en":"конспе́кт","ru":"summary notes, précis","ex":"Я сде́лала конспе́кт ле́кции."},{"en":"оце́нка","ru":"evaluation, judgement","ex":"В о́тзыве всегда́ есть оце́нка."},{"en":"впечатле́ние","ru":"impression","ex":"Кни́га оста́вила си́льное впечатле́ние."},{"en":"досто́инство","ru":"merit, strength","ex":"Гла́вное досто́инство те́кста — я́сность."},{"en":"недоста́ток","ru":"shortcoming, flaw","ex":"Еди́нственный недоста́ток — сла́бый фина́л."},{"en":"спо́рный","ru":"debatable, contentious","ex":"Э́тот вы́вод дово́льно спо́рный."},{"en":"убеди́тельный","ru":"convincing","ex":"А́втор приво́дит убеди́тельные до́воды."},{"en":"изложи́ть","ru":"to set out, present","ex":"Постара́йся изложи́ть суть кра́тко."},{"en":"сжа́то","ru":"concisely","ex":"В конспе́кте пиши́ сжа́то."},{"en":"пробле́ма","ru":"problem, central issue","ex":"В це́нтре те́кста — нра́вственная пробле́ма."},{"en":"подчеркну́ть","ru":"to emphasize","ex":"Хочу́ подчеркну́ть одну́ мысль."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Each line does a different job — match it to what it does.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its function — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["«А́втор прихо́дит к вы́воду, что…»","states the source's conclusion"],["«Гла́вная пробле́ма те́кста — свобо́да.»","flags the central problem"],["«Текст глубо́к и убеди́телен.»","gives a positive evaluation"],["«Вы́вод, одна́ко, спо́рен.»","gives a critical evaluation"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions on the response and the summary. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"The genre that must carry an <b>evaluation</b> is the…","opts":["рефера́т","о́тзыв","конспе́кт"],"answer":1},{"q":"«В статье́ рассма́тривается…, а́втор прихо́дит к вы́воду…» is the voice of a…","opts":["рефера́т (neutral summary)","о́тзыв (a verdict)","chat message"],"answer":0},{"q":"A <b>problem</b> конспе́кт adds, to plain notes,…","opts":["your personal verdict","a naming of the central problem","extra length"],"answer":1},{"q":"A negative point in an о́тзыв should sound…","opts":["rude and blunt («ерунда́»)","measured («недоста́точно обосно́вано»)","slangy («отпа́д»)"],"answer":1},{"q":"All three genres — рефера́т, о́тзыв, конспе́кт — share one…","opts":["bookish-neutral register","slangy tone","set of jokes"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"The same source, two genres side by side. First a problem конспе́кт — compress and flag the question. Then an о́тзыв — the same reader now judges. Notice where the verdict begins.","title":"О́тзыв и конспе́кт: две ре́чи об одно́м те́ксте","sentences":["Конспе́кт: В статье́ рассма́тривается роль чте́ния в эпо́ху экра́нов.","А́втор ста́вит вопро́с: спосо́бен ли экра́н замени́ть кни́гу?","Гла́вная мысль — чте́ние формиру́ет глубину́ мышле́ния.","О́тзыв: Ста́тья напи́сана жи́во и убеди́тельно.","Одна́ко не́которые до́воды ка́жутся спо́рными.","В це́лом текст заставля́ет заду́маться — и в э́том его́ гла́вное досто́инство."],"translation":"Summary: The article examines the role of reading in the age of screens. The author poses a question: can a screen replace a book? The main idea — reading shapes depth of thinking. Response: The article is written vividly and convincingly. However, some of its arguments seem debatable. On the whole, the text makes you think — and that is its main merit."},"essay":{"intro":"Two short genres, side by side — compress, then judge.","prompt":"Pick a short text, article or book you know. In Russian, write TWO labelled pieces: (1) a <b>problem конспе́кт</b> — 1–2 sentences that compress it <b>and</b> name its central problem; (2) an <b>о́тзыв</b> — 1–2 sentences that <b>evaluate</b> it (a strength, a weakness, your overall impression). Label each «Конспе́кт:» and «О́тзыв:».","hint":"Конспе́кт frames: «В те́ксте рассма́тривается…», «в осно́ве — вопро́с о…». О́тзыв frames: «текст произво́дит впечатле́ние…», «до́вод убеди́телен / спо́рен». Keep both bookish-neutral — judge without gushing 🙂","example":"Конспе́кт: В статье́ рассма́тривается бу́дущее бума́жной кни́ги; в осно́ве те́кста — вопро́с, вы́теснит ли экра́н бума́гу. О́тзыв: Статья́ убеди́тельна и напи́сана жи́во, хотя́ её вы́вод ка́жется мне сли́шком категори́чным."}},"26":{"day":26,"week":"04","themeRu":"Professional writing","themeEn":"Делово́е письмо́ и жа́нры","intro":"Эмма opens a blank message to a publishing house and freezes — not for lack of words, but because a business letter isn't written the way you talk. It's assembled from ready-made blocks: a fixed greeting, a fixed opening line, a fixed sign-off. Yesterday you learned to respond to a text and sum it up; today you produce the most formulaic register there is. Here, sounding «original» is exactly the wrong move — the pros reach for the cliché.","introAudio":"Emma opens a blank message to a publishing house and freezes — not for lack of words, but because a business letter isn't written the way you talk. It's assembled from ready-made blocks: a fixed greeting, a fixed opening line, a fixed sign-off. Yesterday you learned to respond to a text and sum it up; today you produce the most formulaic register there is. Here, sounding original is exactly the wrong move — the pros reach for the cliché.","goals":["Open and close a business letter with the fixed frame: <b>Уважа́емый…!</b> … <b>С уваже́нием,</b>","Reach for ready-made clichés, not «your own words»: <b>В отве́т на Ваш запро́с</b>, <b>Дово́жу до Ва́шего све́дения</b>","Tell the service genres apart: <b>письмо́</b>, <b>служе́бная запи́ска</b>, <b>заявле́ние</b>","Hold one official register from greeting to signature — no slips into the conversational"],"learned":["Opened and closed a business letter with the fixed frame: <b>Уважа́емый…!</b> … <b>С уваже́нием,</b>","Reached for ready-made clichés, not «your own words»","Told the service genres apart: <b>письмо́</b>, <b>служе́бная запи́ска</b>, <b>заявле́ние</b>","Held one official register from greeting to signature"],"review":{"intro":"A one-minute lap over the writing week — the difference an отзыв makes, an official cliché, and a formal request — then we produce a business letter of our own.","introAudio":"A one-minute lap over the writing week — the difference an otzyv makes, an official cliché, and a formal request — then we produce a business letter of our own.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer — a quick pull from the week.","q":"An <b>о́тзыв</b> (a response) differs from a <b>рефера́т</b> because it…","opts":["carries the writer's evaluation","only neutrally summarises the source","just copies the original"],"answer":0,"explain":"An <b>о́тзыв</b> adds <b>evaluation</b>; a <b>рефера́т</b> only condenses the source neutrally."},{"type":"choice","q":"«В связи́ с приня́тием ново́го зако́на…» — which register?","opts":["разгово́рный","официа́льно-делово́й","худо́жественный"],"answer":1,"explain":"The cliché <b>в связи́ с</b> + a verbal noun is a classic <b>official</b> marker."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build a formal request: <b>“I ask you to consider my application.”</b>","want":"Прошу́ рассмотре́ть моё заявле́ние","answer":"Прошу́ рассмотре́ть моё заявле́ние"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The frame of a business letter","title":"<em>Уважа́емый… С уваже́нием</em> — the fixed frame","paras":["A Russian business letter runs on a rigid frame. It opens with <b>Уважа́емый</b> + name and patronymic — <b>Уважа́емый Ива́н Петро́вич!</b> — or, to a group, <b>Уважа́емые колле́ги!</b> Note the exclamation mark, not a comma: that's the Russian norm.","It closes just as fixedly: <b>С уваже́нием,</b> and your name. Between the opening and the sign-off go ready-made clichés, not chit-chat.","One more mark of respect: the formal <b>Вы</b> and <b>Ваш</b> are capitalised throughout a business letter. The frame is not yours to reinvent — it's a form you fill in."],"audio":"A Russian business letter runs on a rigid frame. It opens with Uvazhayemy plus a name and patronymic — Uvazhayemy Ivan Petrovich! — or, to a group, Uvazhayemye kollegi! Note the exclamation mark, not a comma: that's the Russian norm. It closes just as fixedly: S uvazheniyem, and your name. Between the opening and the sign-off go ready-made clichés, not chit-chat. One more mark of respect: the formal Vy and Vash are capitalised throughout. The frame is not yours to reinvent — it's a form you fill in.","table":{"rows":[["Уважа́емый Ива́н Петро́вич! <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"уважаемый Иван Петрович\">🔊</button>","Dear Ivan Petrovich! (opening + patronymic)"],["Уважа́емые колле́ги! <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"уважаемые коллеги\">🔊</button>","Dear colleagues! (to a group)"],["С уваже́нием, Э́мма Смит <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"с уважением, Эмма Смит\">🔊</button>","Yours sincerely, Emma Smith (sign-off)"],["Зара́нее благодарю́ за отве́т. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"заранее благодарю за ответ\">🔊</button>","Thank you in advance for a reply."]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"In a letter: “Dear colleagues! We inform you that the meeting has been rescheduled.”","en":"В письме́: «Уважа́емые колле́ги! Сообща́ем, что совеща́ние перенесено́».","say":"В письме: уважаемые коллеги! Сообщаем, что совещание перенесено."},{"ru":"To a friend — the same news, another world.","en":"Дру́гу: «Слу́шай, совеща́ние перенесли́».","say":"Другу: слушай, совещание перенесли."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Приве́т, Ива́н Петро́вич! Как дела́?» (business letter)","right":"«Уважа́емый Ива́н Петро́вич!»","why":"Greeting a business partner like a friend breaks the register in the very first line. The letter opens with <b>Уважа́емый…</b>, never «Приве́т»."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"You're opening a formal letter to Mr Petrov. Pick the right first line:","opts":["Приве́т, Петро́в!","Уважа́емый господи́н Петро́в!","Здоро́во, Петро́в!"],"answer":1,"explain":"A business letter opens with <b>Уважа́емый…</b> + name. «Приве́т» / «Здоро́во» are for friends."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the phrase. Stress marks are just for you — plain letters are fine.","q":"Type the standard sign-off of a business letter (“Yours sincerely,”):","accept":["с уважением","с уважением,"],"placeholder":"с …","explain":"Every business letter closes with <b>С уваже́нием,</b> and your name."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Clichés do the work","title":"<em>В отве́т на Ваш запро́с…</em> — reach for the ready block","paras":["The heart of officialese is counter-intuitive: you don't invent phrasing, you <b>select a standard formula</b>. To reply — <b>В отве́т на Ваш запро́с сообща́ем…</b> To inform — <b>Дово́жу до Ва́шего све́дения, что…</b> or <b>Настоя́щим уведомля́ем…</b> To request — <b>Про́сим предоста́вить…</b>, <b>Прошу́ рассмотре́ть…</b>","To English ears these sound stiff and impersonal. In Russian officialese that is precisely the point — they are simply <b>correct</b>. Trying to warm them up or make them «your own» reads as unprofessional.","Learn the formulas as fixed blocks and you can build a whole letter by choosing among them, like parts from a shelf."],"audio":"The heart of officialese is counter-intuitive: you don't invent phrasing, you select a standard formula. To reply — V otvet na Vash zapros soobshchayem. To inform — Dovozhu do Vashego svedeniya chto, or Nastoyashchim uvedomlyayem. To request — Prosim predostavit, Proshu rassmotret. To English ears these sound stiff and impersonal; in Russian officialese that is precisely the point — they are simply correct. Trying to warm them up or make them your own reads as unprofessional. Learn the formulas as fixed blocks and you can build a whole letter by choosing among them, like parts from a shelf.","table":{"rows":[["В отве́т на Ваш запро́с… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в ответ на ваш запрос\">🔊</button>","In reply to your inquiry…"],["Дово́жу до Ва́шего све́дения, что… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"довожу до вашего сведения, что\">🔊</button>","I hereby bring to your attention that…"],["Про́сим предоста́вить докуме́нты. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"просим предоставить документы\">🔊</button>","We request that you provide the documents."],["В приложе́нии направля́ем догово́р. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в приложении направляем договор\">🔊</button>","Please find the contract attached."]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"Official reply: “In reply to your inquiry, we inform you that the translation is ready.”","en":"В отве́т на Ваш запро́с сообща́ем, что перево́д гото́в.","say":"В ответ на ваш запрос сообщаем, что перевод готов."},{"ru":"The same, to a friend — no formula needed.","en":"Дру́гу: «Слу́шай, насчёт кни́ги — гото́во!»","say":"Другу: слушай, насчёт книги — готово!"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Напиши́те, е́сли что» (in a business letter)","right":"«При возникнове́нии вопро́сов про́сим обраща́ться».","why":"Same meaning — “get in touch if anything comes up” — but the letter needs the standard formula, not the casual line."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"In a business reply, “in reply to your inquiry” is:","opts":["В отве́т на Ваш запро́с","Ну, ты спра́шивал","Насчёт твоего́ вопро́са"],"answer":0,"explain":"The official formula is <b>В отве́т на Ваш запро́с</b>. The other two are conversational."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“We request that you provide the documents.”</b>","want":"Про́сим предоста́вить докуме́нты","answer":"Про́сим предоста́вить докуме́нты"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The service genres","title":"<em>Письмо́, служе́бная запи́ска, заявле́ние</em> — one register, three jobs","paras":["Officialese isn't one document but a small family, each with its own address. A <b>делово́е письмо́</b> goes <b>outward</b> — to a partner, a client, another organisation. A <b>служе́бная запи́ска</b> (a memo) stays <b>inside</b> — usually from you to your manager.","A <b>заявле́ние</b> (an application/statement) is you asking the organisation for something in your own name: <b>Прошу́ предоста́вить о́тпуск…</b> A <b>докладна́я запи́ска</b> reports a fact or an incident upward.","Different openings, one shared register: impersonal, cliché-built, unemotional. Pick the genre, and its formulas come with it."],"audio":"Officialese isn't one document but a small family, each with its own address. A delovoye pismo goes outward — to a partner, a client, another organisation. A sluzhebnaya zapiska, a memo, stays inside — usually from you to your manager. A zayavleniye, an application, is you asking the organisation for something in your own name: Proshu predostavit otpusk. A dokladnaya zapiska reports a fact or an incident upward. Different openings, one shared register: impersonal, cliché-built, unemotional. Pick the genre, and its formulas come with it.","table":{"rows":[["делово́е письмо́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"деловое письмо\">🔊</button>","a business letter (outward: partner, client)"],["служе́бная запи́ска <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"служебная записка\">🔊</button>","an internal memo (to your manager)"],["заявле́ние <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"заявление\">🔊</button>","an application/statement (a request in your own name)"],["докладна́я запи́ска <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"докладная записка\">🔊</button>","a report memo (reporting a fact upward)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"An application: “I request a week's leave.”","en":"Заявле́ние: «Прошу́ предоста́вить о́тпуск на неде́лю».","say":"Заявление: прошу предоставить отпуск на неделю."},{"ru":"A memo: “I hereby report the completion of the project.”","en":"Служе́бная запи́ска: «Сообща́ю о заверше́нии прое́кта».","say":"Служебная записка: сообщаю о завершении проекта."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Заявле́ние: «Хочу́ в о́тпуск, отпусти́те уже́».","right":"Заявле́ние: «Прошу́ предоста́вить о́тпуск».","why":"An application is built on <b>Прошу́…</b>, not on a casual «хочу́». The genre carries its own formula."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"A formal letter to an outside client is a…","opts":["служе́бная запи́ска","делово́е письмо́","заявле́ние"],"answer":1,"explain":"Outward, to a client → a <b>делово́е письмо́</b>. A memo and an application stay inside the organisation."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Прошу́ предоста́вить о́тпуск на неде́лю.","sub":"an application line — read it in a flat, official tone","want":"прошу предоставить отпуск на неделю"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Hold the register to the last line","title":"One slip and the whole letter <em>breaks</em>","paras":["The commonest C1 error here isn't a wrong word — it's a register slip mid-letter. You open «Уважа́емый…», hold formal for three sentences, then sign off «Ну, жду, пока́!» — and it all collapses. A clumsy formula is forgiven; a friendly «пока́» in a business letter never is.","So decide the register at «Уважа́емый» and hold it to «С уваже́нием». Keep the capital <b>Вы/Ваш</b>, prefer the nominal, impersonal turn (<b>сообща́ем о заверше́нии</b>, not «мы всё зако́нчили»), and leave emotion out.","Consistency is the whole game. The letter should read as if one calm, formal voice wrote every line."],"audio":"The commonest C1 error here isn't a wrong word — it's a register slip mid-letter. You open Uvazhayemy, hold formal for three sentences, then sign off Nu, zhdu, poka! — and it all collapses. A clumsy formula is forgiven; a friendly poka in a business letter never is. So decide the register at Uvazhayemy and hold it to S uvazheniyem. Keep the capital Vy and Vash, prefer the nominal impersonal turn — soobshchayem o zavershenii, not my vsyo zakonchili — and leave emotion out. Consistency is the whole game: the letter should read as if one calm, formal voice wrote every line.","examples":[{"ru":"A formal close: “We would be glad to cooperate. Yours sincerely,”","en":"Официа́льно: «Бу́дем ра́ды сотру́дничеству. С уваже́нием,»","say":"Будем рады сотрудничеству. С уважением."},{"ru":"The register held from first line to last — that's a professional letter.","en":"Оди́н реги́стр от пе́рвой строки́ до по́дписи — вот при́знак профессиона́ла.","say":"Один регистр от первой строки до подписи — вот признак профессионала."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«…С уваже́нием, Э́мма. P.S. Ну ты по́нял 😉»","right":"«…С уваже́нием, Э́мма Смит.»","why":"A winking, chatty postscript detonates the formal register the whole letter built. End clean: <b>С уваже́нием</b> + your name, nothing more."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which ending keeps the register of a business letter?","opts":["Ну всё, пока́!","С уваже́нием, Э́мма Смит.","Целу́ю, до встре́чи!"],"answer":1,"explain":"Only <b>С уваже́нием, …</b> holds the formal register. «Пока́» and «целу́ю» belong with friends."},{"type":"type","q":"An official letter stays impersonal. Fill the formula “We inform you that…”: «… Вам, что…»","accept":["сообщаем","сообщаем,"],"placeholder":"…","explain":"<b>Сообща́ем Вам, что…</b> — the neutral, impersonal official formula."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Эмма is drafting a letter to a publishing house; Ива́н, who now works in an office, reads over her shoulder. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Кому́ э́то ты так серьёзно пи́шешь?","ru":"Who are you writing to so seriously?"},{"who":"A","en":"В изда́тельство. Делово́е письмо́ — э́то не разгово́р, а фо́рмулы.","ru":"To a publishing house. A business letter isn't a conversation — it's formulas."},{"who":"B","en":"Начала́ с «Уважа́емый»?","ru":"Did you start with «Dear»?"},{"who":"A","en":"Коне́чно. «Уважа́емый Ива́н Петро́вич!» — и да́льше по клише́.","ru":"Of course. «Dear Ivan Petrovich!» — and then by the clichés."},{"who":"B","en":"А зако́нчишь «пока́»?","ru":"And will you end with «bye»?"},{"who":"A","en":"Ни в ко́ем слу́чае. То́лько «С уваже́нием». Одна́ оши́бка в то́не — и письмо́ разва́лится.","ru":"Absolutely not. Only «Yours sincerely». One mistake in the tone and the letter falls apart."},{"who":"B","en":"Ра́ньше ты писа́ла про́ще.","ru":"You used to write more simply."},{"who":"A","en":"Ра́ньше — как уме́ла. Тепе́рь — как ну́жно.","ru":"Before — as best I could. Now — as it should be."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the world of business writing — the formulas and the genres. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"делово́е письмо́","ru":"a business letter","ex":"Я пишу́ делово́е письмо́."},{"en":"служе́бная запи́ска","ru":"a memo (internal)","ex":"Он оста́вил служе́бную запи́ску."},{"en":"заявле́ние","ru":"an application, a statement","ex":"Я по́дал заявле́ние на о́тпуск."},{"en":"запро́с","ru":"an inquiry, a request","ex":"Мы получи́ли Ваш запро́с."},{"en":"уваже́ние","ru":"respect","ex":"Письмо́ зака́нчивают слова́ми «с уваже́нием»."},{"en":"клише́","ru":"a cliché, a set formula","ex":"Официа́льный стиль по́лон клише́."},{"en":"форму́ла","ru":"a formula, a fixed phrase","ex":"У ка́ждого письма́ свои́ фо́рмулы."},{"en":"приложе́ние","ru":"an attachment, an enclosure","ex":"Догово́р — в приложе́нии."},{"en":"адреса́т","ru":"the addressee","ex":"Укажи́те адреса́та письма́."},{"en":"канцеляри́т","ru":"bureaucratese (pejorative)","ex":"Не пиши́ канцеляри́том дру́гу."},{"en":"уведомля́ть","ru":"to notify (formally)","ex":"Настоя́щим уведомля́ем Вас."},{"en":"предоста́вить","ru":"to provide, to grant","ex":"Про́сим предоста́вить докуме́нты."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each business-letter block to what it does.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Уважа́емый Ива́н Петро́вич!","the opening of a formal letter"],["В отве́т на Ваш запро́с…","in reply to your inquiry"],["С уваже́нием,","the formal sign-off"],["Про́сим предоста́вить докуме́нты.","we request the documents"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"The opening of a formal business letter is…","opts":["Приве́т, Ива́н!","Уважа́емый Ива́н Петро́вич!","Здоро́во!"],"answer":1},{"q":"“In reply to your inquiry” — the official cliché is…","opts":["В отве́т на Ваш запро́с","Ну, ты спра́шивал","Насчёт вопро́са"],"answer":0},{"q":"A formal letter to an outside partner is a…","opts":["заявле́ние","служе́бная запи́ска","делово́е письмо́"],"answer":2},{"q":"A memo to your own manager is a…","opts":["служе́бная запи́ска","делово́е письмо́","публицисти́ческая статья́"],"answer":0},{"q":"Ending a business letter with «Ну всё, пока́!» is…","opts":["perfectly fine","a register slip — a mistake","too formal"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Эмма's finished letter to the publishing house — every line a ready formula, one calm register throughout. Read it aloud.","title":"Делово́е письмо́","sentences":["Уважа́емый Ива́н Петро́вич!","В отве́т на Ваш запро́с сообща́ем, что перево́д кни́ги завершён.","Гото́вый текст мы направля́ем Вам в приложе́нии.","При возникнове́нии вопро́сов про́сим обраща́ться к реда́ктору.","С уваже́нием, Э́мма Смит."],"translation":"Dear Ivan Petrovich! In reply to your inquiry, we inform you that the translation of the book is finished. We are sending you the finished text as an attachment. Should any questions arise, please contact the editor. Yours sincerely, Emma Smith."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn to write like a professional.","prompt":"Write a short business letter or memo (3–5 sentences) using at least three ready formulas: a greeting (<b>Уважа́емый…!</b>), one body cliché (<b>В отве́т на Ваш запро́с…</b> / <b>Дово́жу до Ва́шего све́дения…</b> / <b>Про́сим предоста́вить…</b>), and the sign-off (<b>С уваже́нием,</b>). Hold one official register from first line to last.","hint":"Don't invent phrasing — reach for the formula. Keep <b>Вы/Ваш</b> capital, stay impersonal, and never slip into «пока́» 🙂","example":"Уважа́емый Ива́н Петро́вич! Дово́жу до Ва́шего све́дения, что рабо́та над перево́дом завершена́. Гото́вые материа́лы про́сим приня́ть в приложе́нии. При возникнове́нии вопро́сов гото́вы предоста́вить поясне́ния. С уваже́нием, Э́мма Смит."}},"27":{"day":27,"week":"04","themeRu":"Debate and negotiate","themeEn":"Спор и перегово́ры","intro":"A letter you can redraft ten times; a negotiation happens live. Эмма is at the publishing house, facing a senior editor who wants a translation done in a week — and she has to disagree. Out loud, with reasons, without backing down and without a single rude word. This is the ТРКИ-3 speaking task: steer a business dialogue, argue your case, and paraphrase instead of hammering the same word. Firm on the point, soft on the person.","introAudio":"A letter you can redraft ten times; a negotiation happens live. Emma is at the publishing house, facing a senior editor who wants a translation done in a week — and she has to disagree. Out loud, with reasons, without backing down and without a single rude word. This is the TORFL-three speaking task: steer a business dialogue, argue your case, and paraphrase instead of hammering the same word. Firm on the point, soft on the person.","goals":["Disagree politely: <b>Позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться</b>, <b>Бою́сь, я не могу́ согласи́ться</b>","Weigh two sides, then land on yours: <b>С одно́й стороны́… но с друго́й…</b>","Argue with reasons and <b>paraphrase</b> — say the point a new way, don't repeat it","Steer a negotiation toward a <b>компроми́сс</b> while holding a polite register"],"learned":["Disagreed politely: <b>Позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться</b>, <b>Бою́сь, я не могу́ согласи́ться</b>","Weighed two sides, then landed on my own: <b>С одно́й стороны́… но с друго́й…</b>","Argued with reasons and <b>paraphrased</b> instead of repeating","Steered a negotiation toward a <b>компроми́сс</b> in a polite register"],"review":{"intro":"A quick warm-up from the writing week and beyond — a business line, a particle's colour, an opinion opener — then into the negotiation.","introAudio":"A quick warm-up from the writing week and beyond — a business line, a particle's colour, an opinion opener — then into the negotiation.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer.","q":"Which line belongs in a business letter (not a chat)?","opts":["Слу́шай, насчёт кни́ги…","В отве́т на Ваш запро́с сообща́ем…","Ну что, договори́лись?"],"answer":1,"explain":"The official cliché <b>В отве́т на Ваш запро́с сообща́ем…</b> is the business register; the others are conversational."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Ты же обеща́л!» — the particle «же» here adds…","opts":["a reproachful reminder","a neutral question","a polite request"],"answer":0,"explain":"«же» presses on shared knowledge — here a reproachful “but you promised!”"},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“In my opinion, the question is more complex.”</b>","want":"По-мо́ему, вопро́с сложне́е","answer":"По-мо́ему, вопро́с сложне́е"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Disagreeing without a fight","title":"<em>Позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться</em> — soft words, firm point","paras":["In Russian debate, bluntness — «Вы непра́вы», «Э́то неве́рно» — reads as aggression. The polite formulas cushion the disagreement while keeping your position intact: <b>Позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться</b> (allow me to disagree), <b>Бою́сь, что не могу́ с Ва́ми согласи́ться</b>, <b>Не совсе́м так</b> (not quite so).","You soften the wrapper, never the argument. A half-agreement is stronger still: <b>Вы отча́сти пра́вы, но…</b> — concede a little, then turn.","The rule of thumb: attack the idea, never the person. Keep «Вы» and a level tone, and the disagreement stays a discussion, not a quarrel."],"audio":"In Russian debate, bluntness — Vy nepravy, eto neverno — reads as aggression. The polite formulas cushion the disagreement while keeping your position intact: Pozvolyu sebe ne soglasitsya, allow me to disagree; Boyus chto ne mogu s Vami soglasitsya; Ne sovsem tak, not quite so. You soften the wrapper, never the argument. A half-agreement is stronger still: Vy otchasti pravy, no — concede a little, then turn. Attack the idea, never the person; keep Vy and a level tone, and the disagreement stays a discussion, not a quarrel.","table":{"rows":[["Позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"позволю себе не согласиться\">🔊</button>","Allow me to disagree (polite)."],["Бою́сь, я не могу́ с Ва́ми согласи́ться. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"боюсь, я не могу с вами согласиться\">🔊</button>","I'm afraid I can't agree with you."],["Вы отча́сти пра́вы, но… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"вы отчасти правы, но\">🔊</button>","You're partly right, but…"],["Э́то спо́рный вопро́с. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"это спорный вопрос\">🔊</button>","That's a debatable point."]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Polite: “Allow me to disagree: the deadline is unrealistic.”","en":"Ве́жливо: «Позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться: срок нереа́лен».","say":"Позволю себе не согласиться: срок нереален."},{"ru":"Half-agreement: “You're partly right, but the quality will suffer.”","en":"«Вы отча́сти пра́вы, но ка́чество пострада́ет».","say":"Вы отчасти правы, но качество пострадает."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Вы говори́те ерунду́.» (in a negotiation)","right":"«Бою́сь, здесь я с Ва́ми не соглашу́сь».","why":"Same meaning — “I disagree” — but the rude version ends the negotiation; the polite formula keeps it alive. Register decides whether the deal survives."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"You disagree in a negotiation. The polite, register-appropriate opener is:","opts":["Вы говори́те ерунду́.","Позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться.","Да бро́сьте, э́то чушь."],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>Позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться</b> keeps it polite. The other two are rude and end the talk."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the hedge. Stress marks are optional.","q":"Soften “that's not quite right” — type the three-word hedge «не … так»:","accept":["не совсем так","не совсем"],"placeholder":"не … так","explain":"<b>Не совсе́м так</b> — “not quite so”: you disagree without a head-on «Вы непра́вы»."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Two hands","title":"<em>С одно́й стороны́… но с друго́й…</em> — argue in the round","paras":["A C1 arguer doesn't just assert — you show you've weighed both sides, then land on yours. The frame is <b>С одно́й стороны́…, но с друго́й стороны́…</b> It signals fairness and makes your conclusion sound reasoned, not stubborn.","Pair it with the concessive connectors you already own: <b>тем не ме́нее</b> (nevertheless), <b>одна́ко</b> (however, bookish), <b>всё же</b> (still). Concede the small point to win the big one.","Weighing both sides out loud is also tactical: it disarms the other person, because they hear their own view named before you overturn it."],"audio":"A C1 arguer doesn't just assert — you show you've weighed both sides, then land on yours. The frame is S odnoy storony, no s drugoy storony. It signals fairness and makes your conclusion sound reasoned, not stubborn. Pair it with the concessive connectors you already own: tem ne meneye, nevertheless; odnako, however, bookish; vsyo zhe, still. Concede the small point to win the big one. Weighing both sides out loud is tactical too: it disarms the other person, because they hear their own view named before you overturn it.","table":{"rows":[["С одно́й стороны́… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"с одной стороны\">🔊</button>","On one hand…"],["…но с друго́й стороны́… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"но с другой стороны\">🔊</button>","…but on the other hand…"],["тем не ме́нее <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"тем не менее\">🔊</button>","nevertheless"],["одна́ко <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"однако\">🔊</button>","however (bookish)"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"“On one hand it's expensive, but on the other it saves time.”","en":"С одно́й стороны́, э́то до́рого, но с друго́й — эконо́мит вре́мя.","say":"С одной стороны, это дорого, но с другой — экономит время."},{"ru":"“The plan is risky; nevertheless, I support it.”","en":"План риско́ванный; тем не ме́нее я его́ подде́рживаю.","say":"План рискованный; тем не менее я его поддерживаю."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Э́то до́рого. Э́то до́лго. Э́то пло́хо».","right":"«С одно́й стороны́, э́то до́рого, но с друго́й — надёжно».","why":"A pile of flat assertions sounds stubborn. Weighing two sides — concede, then turn — sounds reasoned, and it's harder to argue with."}],"tasks":[{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“On one hand it's expensive, but on the other — it saves time.”</b>","want":"С одно́й стороны́, э́то до́рого, но с друго́й — эконо́мит вре́мя","answer":"С одно́й стороны́, э́то до́рого, но с друго́й — эконо́мит вре́мя"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which connector means “nevertheless”, conceding then pushing back?","opts":["тем не ме́нее","потому́ что","наприме́р"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>Тем не ме́нее</b> = nevertheless. «потому́ что» gives a cause; «наприме́р» gives an example."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Say it a new way","title":"<em>Ины́ми слова́ми…</em> — paraphrase, not repetition","paras":["A native ear (and an examiner's) tires of one word repeated. C1 argument re-says the key idea with synonyms and re-phrasings: <b>вы́годно → целесообра́зно → в на́ших интере́сах</b>. Bridge with <b>Ина́че говоря́…</b> (in other words), <b>Ины́ми слова́ми…</b>, <b>То есть…</b>","Paraphrase isn't just polish — it buys you a second angle on the same point, which makes an argument feel fuller and more considered.","This is a named ТРКИ-3 skill: reformulate, don't repeat. When you feel a word coming back for the third time, reach for its synonym instead."],"audio":"A native ear, and an examiner's, tires of one word repeated. C1 argument re-says the key idea with synonyms and re-phrasings: vygodno, tselesoobrazno, v nashikh interesakh. Bridge with Inache govorya, in other words; Inymi slovami; To yest. Paraphrase isn't just polish — it buys you a second angle on the same point, which makes an argument feel fuller and more considered. This is a named TORFL-three skill: reformulate, don't repeat. When you feel a word coming back for the third time, reach for its synonym instead.","table":{"rows":[["Ина́че говоря́,… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"иначе говоря\">🔊</button>","In other words,…"],["Ины́ми слова́ми,… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"иными словами\">🔊</button>","Put another way,…"],["То есть… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"то есть\">🔊</button>","That is,… / I mean,…"],["вы́годно → целесообра́зно → в на́ших интере́сах <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"выгодно, целесообразно, в наших интересах\">🔊</button>","profitable → advisable → in our interest (one idea, three ways)"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"“The project is profitable — in other words, it's in our interest.”","en":"Прое́кт при́былен — ины́ми слова́ми, э́то в на́ших интере́сах.","say":"Проект прибылен — иными словами, это в наших интересах."},{"ru":"“It matters — I'd even say it's essential.”","en":"Э́то ва́жно — я бы да́же сказа́л, суще́ственно.","say":"Это важно — я бы даже сказал, существенно."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Э́то ва́жно… ва́жно… о́чень ва́жно».","right":"«Э́то ва́жно… суще́ственно… нельзя́ недооце́нивать».","why":"Repeating one word sounds poor and pleading. Re-saying it with synonyms — <b>ва́жно / суще́ственно / нельзя́ недооце́нивать</b> — is the C1 mark of a full argument."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Instead of repeating «ва́жно… ва́жно… ва́жно», a C1 speaker…","opts":["says it louder each time","paraphrases: «ва́жно… суще́ственно… нельзя́ недооце́нивать»","stops talking"],"answer":1,"explain":"C1 argument re-says the idea with synonyms — <b>paraphrase</b>, not repetition."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Ины́ми слова́ми, э́то в на́ших интере́сах.","sub":"a paraphrase move — “put another way, it's in our interest”","want":"иными словами, это в наших интересах"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Steering to a deal","title":"Lead the room to a <em>компроми́сс</em>","paras":["The speaking task isn't only to defend — it's to <b>move</b> the dialogue: open it, invite the other side, propose, and close on a compromise. Open: <b>Дава́йте обсу́дим…</b>, <b>Предлага́ю нача́ть с…</b> Invite: <b>Что Вы об э́том ду́маете?</b>","Propose a concession: <b>Гото́вы пойти́ на усту́пки, е́сли…</b> Close: <b>Ду́маю, мы нашли́ компроми́сс.</b> Throughout — «Вы», no raised voice, no sarcasm.","Indifference kills a negotiation as surely as rudeness. To stay in control, you keep it moving toward a solution both sides can accept."],"audio":"The speaking task isn't only to defend — it's to move the dialogue: open it, invite the other side, propose, and close on a compromise. Open: Davayte obsudim; Predlagayu nachat s. Invite: Chto Vy ob etom dumayete? Propose a concession: Gotovy poyti na ustupki yesli. Close: Dumayu, my nashli kompromiss. Throughout — Vy, no raised voice, no sarcasm. Indifference kills a negotiation as surely as rudeness. To stay in control, you keep it moving toward a solution both sides can accept.","table":{"rows":[["Дава́йте обсу́дим усло́вия. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"давайте обсудим условия\">🔊</button>","Let's discuss the terms."],["Что Вы об э́том ду́маете? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"что вы об этом думаете\">🔊</button>","What do you think about this?"],["Гото́вы пойти́ на усту́пки. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"готовы пойти на уступки\">🔊</button>","We're ready to make concessions."],["Ду́маю, мы нашли́ компроми́сс. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"думаю, мы нашли компромисс\">🔊</button>","I think we've found a compromise."]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"“Let's start with the deadline — what do you suggest?”","en":"Дава́йте начнём со сро́ка — что Вы предлага́ете?","say":"Давайте начнём со срока — что вы предлагаете?"},{"ru":"“We're ready to concede on price if you agree on the deadline.”","en":"Мы гото́вы уступи́ть в цене́, е́сли Вы согласи́тесь на срок.","say":"Мы готовы уступить в цене, если вы согласитесь на срок."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Как хоти́те, мне всё равно́».","right":"«Дава́йте найдём реше́ние, кото́рое устро́ит о́бе стороны́».","why":"Indifference is as fatal to a negotiation as rudeness. Steering it — proposing, inviting — is what keeps you in control."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"To steer a negotiation toward agreement, you…","opts":["say «мне всё равно́»","propose «дава́йте найдём компроми́сс»","raise your voice"],"answer":1,"explain":"You <b>steer</b>: invite, propose, aim for a <b>компроми́сс</b>. Indifference or shouting kills the talk."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks are optional.","q":"The goal of a good negotiation — a solution both sides accept. Type the word (a … ):","accept":["компромисс"],"placeholder":"компро…","explain":"A <b>компроми́сс</b> — a middle ground that suits both sides."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Эмма is at the publishing house, negotiating a translation deadline with a senior editor. Watch her disagree — firmly, and without a single rude word. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Нам ну́жен перево́д че́рез неде́лю. Э́то оконча́тельно.","ru":"We need the translation in a week. That's final."},{"who":"A","en":"Позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться: за неде́лю ка́чество пострада́ет.","ru":"Allow me to disagree: in a week the quality will suffer."},{"who":"B","en":"Но у нас сро́ки.","ru":"But we have deadlines."},{"who":"A","en":"Я понима́ю. С одно́й стороны́, сро́ки, но с друго́й — кни́гу бу́дут чита́ть го́ды.","ru":"I understand. On one hand, deadlines — but on the other, people will read the book for years."},{"who":"B","en":"И что Вы предлага́ете?","ru":"And what do you propose?"},{"who":"A","en":"Дава́йте компроми́сс: две неде́ли, но пе́рвую полови́ну я сдам уже́ в пя́тницу.","ru":"Let's find a compromise: two weeks, but I'll hand in the first half this Friday."},{"who":"B","en":"Ины́ми слова́ми, мы ничего́ не теря́ем?","ru":"In other words, we lose nothing?"},{"who":"A","en":"И́менно. Реше́ние, кото́рое устра́ивает о́бе стороны́.","ru":"Exactly. A solution that suits both sides."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the table where things get decided — disagreement, argument, compromise. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"перегово́ры","ru":"negotiations","ex":"Перегово́ры прошли́ успе́шно."},{"en":"спор","ru":"a dispute, an argument","ex":"Э́то ста́рый спор."},{"en":"компроми́сс","ru":"a compromise","ex":"Мы нашли́ компроми́сс."},{"en":"усту́пка","ru":"a concession","ex":"Я пошёл на усту́пку."},{"en":"аргуме́нт","ru":"an argument (a reasoned point, not a quarrel)","ex":"У Вас си́льный аргуме́нт."},{"en":"до́вод","ru":"a reason, a point","ex":"Приведи́те ещё оди́н до́вод."},{"en":"убеди́ть","ru":"to convince","ex":"Он суме́л меня́ убеди́ть."},{"en":"возража́ть","ru":"to object","ex":"Не бу́ду возража́ть."},{"en":"согласи́ться","ru":"to agree","ex":"Я гото́в согласи́ться."},{"en":"вежли́вый","ru":"polite","ex":"Держи́те вежли́вый тон."},{"en":"взаимовы́годный","ru":"mutually beneficial","ex":"Э́то взаимовы́годное реше́ние."},{"en":"то́чка зре́ния","ru":"a point of view","ex":"У ка́ждого своя́ то́чка зре́ния."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each negotiation move to what it does.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться.","allow me to disagree (polite)"],["С одно́й стороны́…","on one hand…"],["Ины́ми слова́ми,…","in other words,…"],["Мы нашли́ компроми́сс.","we found a compromise"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"The polite way to disagree in a negotiation is…","opts":["Вы говори́те ерунду́.","Позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться.","Да ла́дно, чушь."],"answer":1},{"q":"“On one hand… on the other…” is…","opts":["С одно́й стороны́… с друго́й…","Потому́ что… поэ́тому…","Во-пе́рвых… во-вторы́х…"],"answer":0},{"q":"Instead of repeating one word, a C1 speaker…","opts":["says it louder","paraphrases with synonyms","repeats it exactly"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Вы говори́те ерунду́» in a negotiation is…","opts":["polite","too rude — wrong register","very formal"],"answer":1},{"q":"A good negotiation aims to reach a…","opts":["компроми́сс","ссо́ру","монопо́лию"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Эмма on how she learned to argue at the table. Read it aloud — it's the whole day in five lines.","title":"Спор без ссо́ры","sentences":["На перегово́рах я научи́лась спо́рить, не ссо́рясь.","Ме́сто «Вы непра́вы» за́няло «позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться».","Я всегда́ взве́шиваю о́бе стороны́: с одно́й стороны́ так, но с друго́й — ина́че.","Оди́н и тот же до́вод я повторя́ю не сло́во в сло́во, а ины́ми слова́ми.","И почти́ всегда́ мы нахо́дим компроми́сс, кото́рый устра́ивает всех."],"translation":"In negotiations I've learned to argue without quarrelling. In place of «you're wrong» came «allow me to disagree». I always weigh both sides: on one hand it's this way, but on the other — otherwise. The same argument I repeat not word for word, but in other words. And almost always we find a compromise that suits everyone."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn to hold your ground — politely.","prompt":"Someone proposes something you don't fully agree with (a deadline, a plan, a price). In 3–4 Russian sentences, disagree politely (<b>Позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться</b> / <b>Вы отча́сти пра́вы, но…</b>), weigh both sides (<b>С одно́й стороны́… но с друго́й…</b>), and propose a compromise. Say your key point once, then <b>paraphrase</b> it — don't repeat it word for word.","hint":"Soften the wrapper, keep the point. «Вы» throughout, no sarcasm — firm on the issue, warm to the person 🙂","example":"Позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться с таки́м сро́ком. С одно́й стороны́, я понима́ю спе́шку, но с друго́й — сли́шком бы́стрый перево́д поте́ряет в ка́честве. Ины́ми слова́ми, лу́чше сде́лать чуть по́зже, но хорошо́. Предлага́ю компроми́сс: я сдам рабо́ту по частя́м, и Вы ничего́ не потеря́ете."}},"28":{"day":28,"week":"04","themeRu":"Turns of phrase, in place","themeEn":"Идио́мы к ме́сту: без кальки́","intro":"«It's raining cats and dogs.» Translate that word for word — «дождь из ко́шек и соба́к» — and a Russian just blinks. The idiom is real, but it isn't <b>theirs</b>. Today is a consolidation and a clean-up: the three bruises Эмма finds most in a rough translation — an idiom copied literally from English, a right idiom in the wrong register, and a suffix that leaked an attitude nobody meant. Find them, fix them.","introAudio":"It's raining cats and dogs. Translate that word for word — dozhd iz koshek i sobak — and a Russian just blinks. The idiom is real, but it isn't theirs. Today is a consolidation and a clean-up: the three bruises Emma finds most in a rough translation — an idiom copied literally from English, a right idiom in the wrong register, and a suffix that leaked an attitude nobody meant. Find them, fix them.","goals":["Kill the <b>calque</b>: swap a word-for-word English idiom for the real Russian one","Match idiom to <b>register</b>: bookish where it's bookish, colloquial where it's colloquial","Catch a stray <b>suffix connotation</b> (работёнка, городи́шко) that leaks scorn","Repair a register mismatch — the right meaning in the wrong world"],"learned":["Killed the <b>calque</b>: swapped a word-for-word English idiom for the real Russian one","Matched idiom to <b>register</b>: bookish for bookish, colloquial for colloquial","Caught a stray <b>suffix connotation</b> (работёнка, городи́шко) leaking scorn","Repaired a register mismatch — the right meaning in the wrong world"],"review":{"intro":"A quick clean-up warm-up — a polite disagreement, an idiom's register, a suffix's sting — then today's three fixes.","introAudio":"A quick clean-up warm-up — a polite disagreement, an idiom's register, a suffix's sting — then today's three fixes.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer.","q":"«Позво́лю себе́ не согласи́ться» is a way to…","opts":["disagree politely","agree warmly","end the talk rudely"],"answer":0,"explain":"It's the polite disagreement from yesterday — soft wrapper, firm point."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Бить баклу́ши» (to laze about) belongs to which register?","opts":["нау́чный","разгово́рный","официа́льно-делово́й"],"answer":1,"explain":"«Бить баклу́ши» is <b>colloquial</b> — fine with friends, wrong in a report."},{"type":"choice","q":"«городи́шко» means…","opts":["simply a small town","a town spoken of with contempt","a big city"],"answer":1,"explain":"The suffix -и́шк- adds <b>scorn</b>, not size: a town said with contempt."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The calque trap","title":"<em>Не «из ко́шек и соба́к»</em> — the real Russian idiom","paras":["An idiom is a whole; translate its parts and you get nonsense. English idioms almost never map onto Russian ones literally. «It's raining cats and dogs» → not «ко́шки и соба́ки», but <b>льёт как из ведра́</b> (it pours as from a bucket). «When pigs fly» → <b>когда́ рак на горе́ сви́стнет</b> (when the crayfish whistles on the mountain).","«To pull someone's leg» → <b>разы́грывать</b> / <b>подшу́чивать</b>. «Like two peas in a pod» → <b>как две ка́пли воды́</b> (like two drops of water). «The odd one out» → <b>бе́лая воро́на</b> (a white crow, not a “black sheep”).","Learn the Russian idiom as one fixed block, and never build it from English parts — that's the single commonest C1 idiom error."],"audio":"An idiom is a whole; translate its parts and you get nonsense. English idioms almost never map onto Russian ones literally. It's raining cats and dogs — not koshki i sobaki, but lyot kak iz vedra, it pours as from a bucket. When pigs fly — kogda rak na gore svistnet, when the crayfish whistles on the mountain. To pull someone's leg — razygryvat, podshuchivat. Like two peas in a pod — kak dve kapli vody, like two drops of water. The odd one out — belaya vorona, a white crow, not a black sheep. Learn the Russian idiom as one fixed block, and never build it from English parts.","table":{"rows":[["льёт как из ведра́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"льёт как из ведра\">🔊</button>","it's pouring (not «cats and dogs»)"],["когда́ рак на горе́ сви́стнет <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"когда рак на горе свистнет\">🔊</button>","when pigs fly — i.e. never (lit. when the crayfish whistles)"],["как две ка́пли воды́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"как две капли воды\">🔊</button>","like two peas in a pod (lit. like two drops of water)"],["бе́лая воро́на <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"белая ворона\">🔊</button>","the odd one out (lit. a white crow)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"“It poured all day.”","en":"Весь день лило́ как из ведра́.","say":"Весь день лило как из ведра."},{"ru":"“They're as alike as two peas in a pod.”","en":"Они́ похо́жи как две ка́пли воды́.","say":"Они похожи как две капли воды."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Идёт дождь из ко́шек и соба́к».","right":"«Льёт как из ведра́».","why":"A literal calque of an English idiom means nothing to a Russian. Reach for the whole Russian idiom, not the English parts."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"“It's raining cats and dogs” in real Russian is…","opts":["льёт как из ведра́","дождь из ко́шек и соба́к","мо́края пого́да"],"answer":0,"explain":"The idiom is a whole: <b>льёт как из ведра́</b>. The literal calque means nothing to a Russian."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the verb. Stress marks are optional.","q":"Replace the calque with the real idiom (it's pouring): «… как из ведра́»","accept":["льёт","льет","лил"],"placeholder":"… как из ведра́","explain":"<b>Льёт как из ведра́</b> — the Russian says “it pours as from a bucket”."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Right idiom, wrong room","title":"<em>Ка́нуть в Ле́ту</em> vs <em>бить баклу́ши</em> — pick by register","paras":["Every idiom carries a register stamp. <b>Ка́нуть в Ле́ту</b> (to sink into oblivion) is bookish — perfect in an essay, absurd over coffee. <b>Бить баклу́ши</b> (to twiddle your thumbs) is colloquial — perfect with friends, wrong in a report.","The meaning can be right and the idiom still wrong, because its register clashes with the text around it. <b>Ва́лять дурака́</b> is colloquial too; <b>оставля́ть жела́ть лу́чшего</b> (to leave much to be desired) is neutral-to-bookish.","Before you use an idiom, ask not only “does it mean this?” but “does it belong in this style?” That second question is the whole of C1."],"audio":"Every idiom carries a register stamp. Kanut v Letu, to sink into oblivion, is bookish — perfect in an essay, absurd over coffee. Bit baklushi, to twiddle your thumbs, is colloquial — perfect with friends, wrong in a report. The meaning can be right and the idiom still wrong, because its register clashes with the text around it. Valyat duraka is colloquial too; ostavlyat zhelat luchshego, to leave much to be desired, is neutral-to-bookish. Before you use an idiom, ask not only does it mean this, but does it belong in this style. That second question is the whole of C1.","table":{"rows":[["ка́нуть в Ле́ту <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"кануть в Лету\">🔊</button>","to sink into oblivion (bookish)"],["бить баклу́ши <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"бить баклуши\">🔊</button>","to laze about (colloquial)"],["ва́лять дурака́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"валять дурака\">🔊</button>","to mess around (colloquial)"],["оставля́ть жела́ть лу́чшего <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"оставлять желать лучшего\">🔊</button>","to leave much to be desired (neutral/bookish)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Bookish (in an essay): “These disputes have sunk into oblivion.”","en":"Кни́жно: «Э́ти спо́ры ка́нули в Ле́ту».","say":"Эти споры канули в Лету."},{"ru":"Colloquial (with friends): “Stop lazing about!”","en":"Разг.: «Хва́тит бить баклу́ши!»","say":"Хватит бить баклуши!"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"В нау́чной статье́: «А́втор це́лый год бил баклу́ши».","right":"В нау́чной статье́: «А́втор до́лго не занима́лся э́тим вопро́сом».","why":"A colloquial idiom crashing a scientific text is a register error. The meaning is fine; the idiom's register isn't — replace it with the neutral phrasing."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which idiom fits a bookish essay (not a chat)?","opts":["бить баклу́ши","ка́нуть в Ле́ту","ва́лять дурака́"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>Ка́нуть в Ле́ту</b> is bookish. The other two are colloquial."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Э́ти спо́ры давно́ ка́нули в Ле́ту.","sub":"a bookish idiom — read it evenly, like a line in an essay","want":"эти споры давно канули в Лету"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The suffix that leaked an attitude","title":"<em>Работёнка, городи́шко</em> — the accidental sneer","paras":["Back on Day 3 you met the suffixes of attitude. They're a C1 trap because they slip in by accident. <b>работёнка</b> isn't just “a job” — it's a job spoken of dismissively. <b>городи́шко</b> isn't “a small town” — it's a town said with contempt. <b>люди́шки</b> = “wretched little people”.","Use them on purpose for irony; use them by accident and you've insulted something without meaning to. If you love your small hometown, it's a warm <b>городо́к</b> — never a scornful городи́шко.","In a neutral or official text, wipe them out: reach for the plain word (рабо́та, го́род, лю́ди) and let the sentence stay unmarked."],"audio":"Back on Day three you met the suffixes of attitude. They're a C1 trap because they slip in by accident. Rabotyonka isn't just a job — it's a job spoken of dismissively. Gorodishko isn't a small town — it's a town said with contempt. Lyudishki means wretched little people. Use them on purpose for irony; use them by accident and you've insulted something without meaning to. If you love your small hometown, it's a warm gorodok — never a scornful gorodishko. In a neutral or official text, wipe them out: reach for the plain word — rabota, gorod, lyudi — and let the sentence stay unmarked.","table":{"rows":[["рабо́та → работёнка <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"работа, работёнка\">🔊</button>","a job → a measly little job (dismissive)"],["го́род → городи́шко <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"город, городишко\">🔊</button>","a town → a miserable little town (contempt)"],["го́род → городо́к <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"город, городок\">🔊</button>","a town → a nice small town (warm, neutral)"],["лю́ди → люди́шки <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"люди, людишки\">🔊</button>","people → wretched little people (scorn)"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"Neutral (correct): “He found a good job.”","en":"Нейтр.: «Он нашёл хоро́шую рабо́ту».","say":"Он нашёл хорошую работу."},{"ru":"Dismissive (on purpose, ironic): “Just a little job, nothing serious.”","en":"Иро́ния: «Так, работёнка, ничего́ серьёзного».","say":"Так, работёнка, ничего серьёзного."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Я вы́росла в ру́сском городи́шке» (about a town you love)","right":"«Я вы́росла в ру́сском городке́».","why":"городи́шко sneers. If you mean a small town warmly, it's <b>городо́к</b>. The suffix, not the root, carries the insult."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"You love your small hometown. The neutral, warm word is…","opts":["городи́шко","городо́к","городи́ще"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>городо́к</b> is a warm, neutral “small town”. городи́шко sneers; городи́ще means “huge”."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the plain word. Stress marks are optional.","q":"Neutralise the sneer: rewrite «работёнка» as the plain word for a job/work:","accept":["работа"],"placeholder":"рабо́…","explain":"<b>рабо́та</b> is neutral; <b>работёнка</b> adds a dismissive shrug."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Repairing a mismatch","title":"Find the clash, <em>fix</em> it","paras":["Consolidation. The C1 editor's job is to spot where a word or idiom fights its surroundings and swap it for one that fits. A slangy idiom in a formal letter, a lofty word in a chat, a scornful suffix in a neutral line — each is repaired by moving to the neutral or the register-appropriate form.","The meaning stays; only the register changes. «Мы бро́сили э́ту зате́ю» (colloquial) becomes «Мы отказа́лись от э́того пла́на» in a letter; «Скорблю́ о случи́вшемся» (lofty) becomes «Жаль, что так вы́шло» in a chat.","This is exactly what Эмма does all day: not «is it correct?» but «does it belong here?» — and if it doesn't, she moves it home."],"audio":"Consolidation. The C1 editor's job is to spot where a word or idiom fights its surroundings and swap it for one that fits. A slangy idiom in a formal letter, a lofty word in a chat, a scornful suffix in a neutral line — each is repaired by moving to the neutral or the register-appropriate form. The meaning stays; only the register changes. My brosili etu zateyu, colloquial, becomes my otkazalis ot etogo plana in a letter; skorblyu o sluchivshemsya, lofty, becomes zhal chto tak vyshlo in a chat. This is exactly what Emma does all day: not is it correct, but does it belong here — and if it doesn't, she moves it home.","table":{"rows":[["«бро́сили зате́ю» → «отказа́лись от пла́на» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"бросили затею, отказались от плана\">🔊</button>","colloquial → neutral (for a letter)"],["«Скорблю́» → «Жаль» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"скорблю, жаль\">🔊</button>","lofty → colloquial (for a chat)"],["ка́лька → ру́сская идио́ма <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"калька, русская идиома\">🔊</button>","calque → the real Russian idiom"],["«работёнка» → «рабо́та» <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"работёнка, работа\">🔊</button>","scornful suffix → neutral form"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"Fix for a letter: neutral instead of colloquial.","en":"В письме́: «Мы отказа́лись от э́того пла́на» (не «бро́сили зате́ю»).","say":"Мы отказались от этого плана."},{"ru":"Fix for a chat: colloquial instead of lofty.","en":"В ча́те: «Жаль, что так вы́шло» (не «Скорблю́ о случи́вшемся»).","say":"Жаль, что так вышло."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Дру́гу: «Приношу́ Вам глубоча́йшие извине́ния».","right":"Дру́гу: «Извини́, я не хоте́л».","why":"A lofty official apology to a friend is a mismatch. Repair it to the neutral/colloquial — the meaning is identical, the register finally fits."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"A slangy idiom lands in a formal letter. The C1 fix is to…","opts":["leave it — idioms are always good","swap it for a neutral, register-fitting phrase","add three more idioms"],"answer":1,"explain":"Repair by moving to the register-appropriate form. Meaning stays; register changes."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the repair. Stress marks are optional.","q":"A friend apologises with the lofty «Приношу́ глубоча́йшие извине́ния». Repair to the neutral word (one word) a friend would use:","accept":["извини","извини,","прости","прости,"],"placeholder":"…","explain":"To a friend, <b>«Извини́»</b> fits; the lofty apology is a register mismatch."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Ка́тя has started learning English idioms and keeps translating them straight into Russian — Эмма, the editor, can't stop smiling. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Слу́шай, на у́лице льёт как из ведра́. По-англи́йски говоря́т «ко́шки и соба́ки», да?","ru":"Listen, it's pouring outside. In English they say «cats and dogs», right?"},{"who":"A","en":"«It's raining cats and dogs», да. Но по-ру́сски «дождь из ко́шек» — э́то смешна́я ка́лька.","ru":"«It's raining cats and dogs», yes. But in Russian «rain of cats» is a funny calque."},{"who":"B","en":"А как пра́вильно?","ru":"And how do you say it properly?"},{"who":"A","en":"Как ты и сказа́ла — «льёт как из ведра́». Идио́му нельзя́ переводи́ть по частя́м.","ru":"Just as you said — «it pours as from a bucket». You can't translate an idiom piece by piece."},{"who":"B","en":"Поняла́. А «бить баклу́ши» мо́жно в докла́де?","ru":"Got it. And can I use «бить баклу́ши» in a report?"},{"who":"A","en":"Ни в ко́ем слу́чае — э́то разгово́рное. В докла́де — «безде́йствовать».","ru":"Absolutely not — that's colloquial. In a report — «to be idle»."},{"who":"B","en":"Сло́жно у вас, реда́кторов.","ru":"It's complicated with you editors."},{"who":"A","en":"Не сло́жно — про́сто ка́ждое сло́во зна́ет своё ме́сто.","ru":"Not complicated — every word just knows its place."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for idiom, calque and connotation — the C1 editor's toolkit. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"идио́ма","ru":"an idiom","ex":"У ка́ждого языка́ свои́ идио́мы."},{"en":"ка́лька","ru":"a calque, a loan translation","ex":"Э́то ка́лька с англи́йского."},{"en":"фразеологи́зм","ru":"a set phrase, an idiom","ex":"Фразеологи́зм нельзя́ переводи́ть досло́вно."},{"en":"коннота́ция","ru":"a connotation","ex":"У сло́ва есть коннота́ция."},{"en":"досло́вно","ru":"word for word, literally","ex":"Не переводи́ досло́вно."},{"en":"уме́стный","ru":"appropriate, fitting","ex":"Идио́ма должна́ быть уме́стной."},{"en":"льёт как из ведра́","ru":"it's pouring (idiom)","ex":"На у́лице льёт как из ведра́."},{"en":"бить баклу́ши","ru":"to laze about (colloquial)","ex":"Хва́тит бить баклу́ши!"},{"en":"ка́нуть в Ле́ту","ru":"to sink into oblivion (bookish)","ex":"Э́ти спо́ры ка́нули в Ле́ту."},{"en":"презри́тельный","ru":"contemptuous, scornful","ex":"У су́ффикса презри́тельный отте́нок."},{"en":"рассогласова́ние","ru":"a mismatch (of register)","ex":"Я чиню́ стилево́е рассогласова́ние."},{"en":"отте́нок","ru":"a shade, a nuance","ex":"Ва́жен ка́ждый отте́нок."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each idiom or word to what it really is.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["льёт как из ведра́","it's pouring (not «cats and dogs»)"],["ка́нуть в Ле́ту","to sink into oblivion (bookish)"],["бить баклу́ши","to laze about (colloquial)"],["городи́шко","a miserable little town (contempt)"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"“It's raining cats and dogs” in real Russian is…","opts":["дождь из ко́шек и соба́к","льёт как из ведра́","о́чень мо́кро"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Ка́нуть в Ле́ту» belongs in…","opts":["a bookish, written text","a chat with friends","a shopping list"],"answer":0},{"q":"«городи́шко» carries…","opts":["just smallness","contempt","affection"],"answer":1},{"q":"A colloquial idiom in a scientific article is…","opts":["a nice touch","a register mistake","required"],"answer":1},{"q":"To fix a calque you…","opts":["use the real Russian idiom","translate even more literally","add English words"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Эмма names the three bruises she fixes in almost every rough text. Read it aloud.","title":"Три ло́вушки","sentences":["Ка́ждый день я чиню́ в те́кстах одни́ и те же три оши́бки.","Пе́рвая — ка́лька: англи́йскую идио́му перевели́ сло́во в сло́во.","Втора́я — идио́ма не в своём реги́стре: разгово́рная фра́за в делово́м письме́.","Тре́тья — су́ффикс, кото́рый неча́янно приба́вил презре́ние.","Хоро́ший реда́ктор слы́шит не то́лько смысл, но и его́ отте́нок."],"translation":"Every day I fix the same three mistakes in texts. The first — a calque: an English idiom translated word for word. The second — an idiom out of its register: a colloquial phrase in a business letter. The third — a suffix that accidentally added contempt. A good editor hears not only the meaning, but its shade."},"essay":{"intro":"Now you be the editor.","prompt":"Here are three flawed lines — repair each so the meaning stays but the register fits: (1) a literal calque «Идёт дождь из ко́шек и соба́к»; (2) a colloquial idiom in a formal reply «В отве́т на Ваш запро́с сообща́ем, что мы не бу́дем бить баклу́ши»; (3) an accidental scornful suffix «Я нашла́ хоро́шую работёнку в изда́тельстве». Rewrite all three.","hint":"Calque → the real Russian idiom (<b>льёт как из ведра́</b>). Colloquial-in-formal → a neutral verb (<b>рабо́тать в срок</b>). Scornful suffix → the plain word (<b>рабо́та</b>) 🙂","example":"1. На у́лице льёт как из ведра́. 2. В отве́т на Ваш запро́с сообща́ем, что рабо́та бу́дет вы́полнена в срок. 3. Я нашла́ хоро́шую рабо́ту в изда́тельстве."}},"29":{"day":29,"week":"04","themeRu":"Putting it all together","themeEn":"Всё вме́сте: те́кст уровня́ C1","intro":"No new tool today — every one is already on your bench. Эмма doesn't learn a word; she sits down and writes a whole piece, and every C1 muscle shows up at once: she picks a style and holds it, drops an idiom exactly where it lands, colours a line with «же», folds two clauses into one nominal phrase, and lets a participle carry the weight. Assembly, not acquisition. This is what fluency looks like from the inside.","introAudio":"No new tool today — every one is already on your bench. Emma doesn't learn a word; she sits down and writes a whole piece, and every C1 muscle shows up at once: she picks a style and holds it, drops an idiom exactly where it lands, colours a line with zhe, folds two clauses into one nominal phrase, and lets a participle carry the weight. Assembly, not acquisition. This is what fluency looks like from the inside.","goals":["Choose one <b>style</b> for a whole text and hold it start to finish","Weave the level together: idiom + particle + compression + expressive syntax, each to the point","Keep B2 grammar (participles, government, clauses) running as the invisible base","Read a paragraph as a native would — for register and effect, not just meaning"],"learned":["Chose one <b>style</b> for a whole text and held it start to finish","Wove the level together: idiom + particle + compression + expressive syntax, each to the point","Kept B2 grammar running as the invisible base","Read a paragraph for register and effect, not just meaning"],"review":{"intro":"A lap around the whole level before the final assembly — a style marker, a particle's colour, a compression, a bookish connector.","introAudio":"A lap around the whole level before the final assembly — a style marker, a particle's colour, a compression, a bookish connector.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer.","q":"«Как изве́стно, э́то о́стрый вопро́с» — which style?","opts":["публицисти́ческий","нау́чный","разгово́рный"],"answer":0,"explain":"«Как изве́стно» + evaluation is a <b>journalistic</b> marker."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Ты же зна́ешь э́то.» — «же» here…","opts":["asks a question","appeals to shared knowledge","gives an order"],"answer":1,"explain":"«же» leans on what you both know — “you know this, after all”."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build the bookish compression of “after he returned home”:","want":"по возвраще́нии домо́й","answer":"по возвраще́нии домо́й"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the connector. Stress marks are optional.","q":"Type the bookish connector for “nevertheless” (3 words):","accept":["тем не менее"],"placeholder":"тем …","explain":"<b>Тем не ме́нее</b> concedes, then turns."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Pick a style and hold it","title":"One <em>text</em>, one style — chosen and held","paras":["The first act of a C1 text is a decision: which of the five styles am I in? Everything downstream — word choice, sentence length, whether an idiom is even allowed — flows from it. A publicistic piece welcomes an image and a rhetorical question; a scientific one wants nominal phrases and impersonals; a conversational note wants particles and short lines.","The skill isn't knowing the styles — it's <b>committing</b> to one and not leaking another in. The moment «по́лная жесть» drops into a scientific sentence, the whole thing wobbles.","Choose the register at the first word and hold it to the last. Consistency is what makes a text sound native."],"audio":"The first act of a C1 text is a decision: which of the five styles am I in? Everything downstream — word choice, sentence length, whether an idiom is even allowed — flows from it. A publicistic piece welcomes an image and a rhetorical question; a scientific one wants nominal phrases and impersonals; a conversational note wants particles and short lines. The skill isn't knowing the styles — it's committing to one and not leaking another in. The moment polnaya zhest drops into a scientific sentence, the whole thing wobbles. Choose the register at the first word and hold it to the last.","table":{"rows":[["нау́чный: сле́дует отме́тить, что… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"следует отметить, что\">🔊</button>","scientific: it should be noted that…"],["публицисти́ческий: как изве́стно, вопро́с о́стрый <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"как известно, вопрос острый\">🔊</button>","journalistic: as we know, the issue is a burning one"],["разгово́рный: ну, э́то же очеви́дно <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ну, это же очевидно\">🔊</button>","conversational: well, it's obvious, isn't it"],["официа́льно-делово́й: в соотве́тствии с… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в соответствии с\">🔊</button>","official: in accordance with…"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Journalistic, held throughout: “As we know, the question of translation is more acute than ever.”","en":"В газе́те: «Как изве́стно, вопро́с перево́да сего́дня остёр как никогда́».","say":"В газете: как известно, вопрос перевода сегодня остёр как никогда."},{"ru":"Scientific, held throughout: “It should be noted that the problem remains open.”","en":"Нау́чно: «Сле́дует отме́тить, что пробле́ма остаётся откры́той».","say":"Следует отметить, что проблема остаётся открытой."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Иссле́дование пока́зывает, что э́то по́лная жесть».","right":"«Иссле́дование пока́зывает, что пробле́ма серьёзна».","why":"A slangy «по́лная жесть» crashing a scientific frame is the classic leak. Hold the style you chose — one register, all the way through."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"A slangy word crashes into a scientific sentence. That is…","opts":["a stylistic strength","a register slip — a mistake","impossible"],"answer":1,"explain":"One style leaking into another is the giveaway of a non-native. Hold the style you chose."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks are optional.","q":"A scientific text opens impersonally. Fill “it should be noted that”: «… отме́тить, что…»","accept":["следует","следует,"],"placeholder":"…","explain":"<b>Сле́дует отме́тить, что…</b> — the impersonal scientific opener."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Season, don't drown","title":"<em>Же, ведь</em> and an idiom — measured, to the point","paras":["A native text isn't packed with idioms and particles — it <b>places</b> them. One well-set idiom lands; three in a paragraph is showing off. A single «же» or «ведь» colours a sentence with reproach, shared knowledge or insistence; sprinkled everywhere they turn to noise.","The C1 mark is restraint: the right colour in the right spot, then back to plain. «Ты же сам э́то зна́ешь» presses gently; «Де́ло сдви́нулось с мёртвой то́чки» lands one vivid idiom and moves on.","Think of them as seasoning. A pinch makes the dish; a handful ruins it."],"audio":"A native text isn't packed with idioms and particles — it places them. One well-set idiom lands; three in a paragraph is showing off. A single zhe or ved colours a sentence with reproach, shared knowledge or insistence; sprinkled everywhere they turn to noise. The C1 mark is restraint: the right colour in the right spot, then back to plain. Ty zhe sam eto znayesh presses gently; delo sdvinulos s myortvoy tochki lands one vivid idiom and moves on. Think of them as seasoning: a pinch makes the dish, a handful ruins it.","table":{"rows":[["Ты же обеща́л. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ты же обещал\">🔊</button>","You promised, though (reproach — «же»)"],["Э́то ведь очеви́дно. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"это ведь очевидно\">🔊</button>","It's obvious, after all (shared knowledge — «ведь»)"],["Де́ло сдви́нулось с мёртвой то́чки. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"дело сдвинулось с мёртвой точки\">🔊</button>","Things finally got moving (one apt idiom)"],["Вот и всё. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"вот и всё\">🔊</button>","And that's that. (plain close)"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"One particle, to the point: “You know that yourself, though.”","en":"Ты же сам э́то прекра́сно зна́ешь.","say":"Ты же сам это прекрасно знаешь."},{"ru":"One idiom, to the point: “At last things got moving.”","en":"Наконе́ц де́ло сдви́нулось с мёртвой то́чки.","say":"Наконец дело сдвинулось с мёртвой точки."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Ну ведь же вот э́то же про́сто-таки…»","right":"«Э́то же очеви́дно».","why":"A pile-up of particles isn't “very Russian” — it's noise. One «же», placed on purpose, does the whole job."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Three idioms packed into one paragraph is…","opts":["very Russian","showing off — too much","required at C1"],"answer":1,"explain":"C1 restraint: one idiom lands, three is noise. Place them, don't pile them."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"Ты же сам э́то прекра́сно зна́ешь.","sub":"colour it with «же» — a gentle reproach, “you know this perfectly well yourself”","want":"ты же сам это прекрасно знаешь"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Compress","title":"<em>По возвраще́нии</em> — say more in fewer words","paras":["The structural signature of a C1 written text is compression: a subordinate clause folded into a participle, a gerund, or a verbal noun. «По́сле того́ как зако́н при́няли» becomes <b>по́сле приня́тия зако́на</b>. «Когда́ он верну́лся» → <b>по возвраще́нии</b>. «Так как шёл дождь» → <b>из-за дождя́</b>.","It's the move English speakers resist most — you keep the verbal clause where the bookish Russian norm folds it into a noun. In a scientific or official text, the tighter nominal version is the native default.","One caution: don't over-compress a chatty note. Bookish density belongs in bookish text; dumped into casual speech it sounds absurd."],"audio":"The structural signature of a C1 written text is compression: a subordinate clause folded into a participle, a gerund, or a verbal noun. Posle togo kak zakon prinyali becomes posle prinyatiya zakona. Kogda on vernulsya becomes po vozvrashchenii. Tak kak shyol dozhd becomes iz-za dozhdya. It's the move English speakers resist most — you keep the verbal clause where the bookish Russian norm folds it into a noun. In a scientific or official text, the tighter nominal version is the native default. One caution: don't over-compress a chatty note. Bookish density belongs in bookish text; dumped into casual speech it sounds absurd.","table":{"rows":[["когда́ он верну́лся → по возвраще́нии <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"когда он вернулся, по возвращении\">🔊</button>","when he returned → on his return"],["по́сле того́ как зако́н при́няли → по́сле приня́тия зако́на <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"после того как закон приняли, после принятия закона\">🔊</button>","when the law was passed → after the passing of the law"],["так как шёл дождь → из-за дождя́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"так как шёл дождь, из-за дождя\">🔊</button>","because it was raining → because of the rain"],["челове́к, кото́рый чита́ет → чита́ющий челове́к <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"человек, который читает, читающий человек\">🔊</button>","a person who reads → a reading person (participle)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Compressed: “On returning to Moscow, she got back to work.”","en":"По возвраще́нии в Москву́ она́ верну́лась к рабо́те.","say":"По возвращении в Москву она вернулась к работе."},{"ru":"Compressed: “After the contract was signed, work began.”","en":"По́сле подписа́ния догово́ра начала́сь рабо́та.","say":"После подписания договора началась работа."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Дру́гу: «По оконча́нии просмо́тра фи́льма предлага́ю осуществи́ть чаепи́тие».","right":"Дру́гу: «Досмо́трим фильм — и попьём ча́ю».","why":"Bookish compression dumped into a friendly line is comic. Compress the bookish text, not the chat — density is a tool with an address."}],"tasks":[{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build the compression of “when he returned to Moscow”:","want":"по возвраще́нии в Москву́","answer":"по возвраще́нии в Москву́"},{"type":"choice","q":"«По́сле того́ как зако́н при́няли» compresses to…","opts":["по́сле приня́тия зако́на","когда́ зако́н при́няли","зако́н был при́нят"],"answer":0,"explain":"Fold the clause into a verbal noun: <b>по́сле приня́тия зако́на</b>."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · The invisible base","title":"Participles, government, clauses — <em>owned</em>, running underneath","paras":["None of this floats free. Under every C1 sentence the B2 machine still runs: participles and gerunds agree and attach, verbs demand their cases (<b>зави́сеть от</b>+gen, <b>интересова́ться</b>+instr), the passive turns on cleanly, complex clauses hold their shape.","At C1 you no longer think about them — they're the base the style sits on. A single sentence now carries a chosen register, a placed particle, a compression, and flawless grammar, all at once. That's the level.","Which is why a slip in the base still fails, however stylish the surface: «зави́сит на» instead of «зави́сит от» sinks the whole fine sentence. Keep the machine clean, and let the style shine on top."],"audio":"None of this floats free. Under every C1 sentence the B2 machine still runs: participles and gerunds agree and attach, verbs demand their cases — zaviset ot plus genitive, interesovatsya plus instrumental — the passive turns on cleanly, complex clauses hold their shape. At C1 you no longer think about them — they're the base the style sits on. A single sentence now carries a chosen register, a placed particle, a compression, and flawless grammar, all at once. That's the level. Which is why a slip in the base still fails, however stylish the surface: zavisit na instead of zavisit ot sinks the whole fine sentence. Keep the machine clean, and let the style shine on top.","table":{"rows":[["Статья́, напи́санная колле́гой, вы́звала спор. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"статья, написанная коллегой, вызвала спор\">🔊</button>","participle + instrumental agent, in one line"],["Всё зави́сит от конте́кста. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"всё зависит от контекста\">🔊</button>","verb government (зави́сеть от + gen)"],["Речь идёт о ва́жном вопро́се. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"речь идёт о важном вопросе\">🔊</button>","figurative movement (речь идёт о…)"],["Проанализи́ровав текст, она́ сде́лала вы́вод. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"проанализировав текст, она сделала вывод\">🔊</button>","gerund, compressed"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"All at once: “Having analysed the text written by the author, the editor drew a conclusion.”","en":"Проанализи́ровав текст, напи́санный а́втором, реда́ктор сде́лал вы́вод.","say":"Проанализировав текст, написанный автором, редактор сделал вывод."},{"ru":"Style on a clean base: “Everything depends on the context, of course.”","en":"Всё, разуме́ется, зави́сит от конте́кста.","say":"Всё, разумеется, зависит от контекста."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Э́то зави́сит на мно́гих фа́кторов».","right":"«Э́то зави́сит от мно́гих фа́кторов».","why":"A broken base sinks a stylish surface. <b>зави́сеть</b> governs <b>от</b> + genitive — get the government wrong and no amount of style saves the sentence."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Under every polished C1 sentence still runs…","opts":["nothing — style replaces grammar","the B2 grammar base (participles, government, clauses)","only vocabulary"],"answer":1,"explain":"Style sits <b>on</b> the grammar; the B2 machine never stops running underneath."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the preposition. Stress marks are optional.","q":"Government still rules. Fill: “It depends on many factors.” — «Э́то зави́сит … мно́гих фа́кторов».","accept":["от"],"placeholder":"…","explain":"<b>зави́сеть от</b> + genitive: зави́сит <b>от</b> мно́гих фа́кторов."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Эмма shows Макси́м — Ива́н's brother, an engineer who likes precision — a paragraph she's just written. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Дай почита́ть. Ты же над статьёй сиде́ла.","ru":"Let me read it. You were working on that article."},{"who":"A","en":"Вот абза́ц. Оди́н стиль, оди́н оборо́т, одна́ части́ца — и всё на ме́сте.","ru":"Here's the paragraph. One style, one turn of phrase, one particle — and everything's in place."},{"who":"B","en":"«По возвраще́нии в Москву́…» — как сжа́то. У нас в чертежа́х то́же так лю́бят.","ru":"«On returning to Moscow…» — how compact. We like it that way in blueprints too."},{"who":"A","en":"И́менно. Кни́жный текст сжима́ет, как хоро́ший инжене́р.","ru":"Exactly. A bookish text compresses, like a good engineer."},{"who":"B","en":"А э́то «же» заче́м?","ru":"And what's that «же» for?"},{"who":"A","en":"Отте́нок. Без него́ фра́за пра́вильная, но пло́ская.","ru":"A shade. Without it the phrase is correct, but flat."},{"who":"B","en":"Слу́шай, э́то уже́ не учёба. Э́то мастерство́.","ru":"Listen, this isn't studying any more. This is mastery."},{"who":"A","en":"Про́сто всё, что учи́ла, наконе́ц зарабо́тало вме́сте.","ru":"Everything I learned finally started working together."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for the whole toolkit, assembled — style, compression, particle, command. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"стиль","ru":"a style","ex":"Вы́бери оди́н стиль."},{"en":"реги́стр","ru":"register (level of speech)","ex":"Держи́ оди́н реги́стр."},{"en":"свя́зка","ru":"a connector, a linking word","ex":"Свя́зка соединя́ет мы́сли."},{"en":"сжа́тие","ru":"compression, condensation","ex":"Кни́жное сжа́тие — при́знак C1."},{"en":"отглаго́льное существи́тельное","ru":"a verbal noun (deverbal)","ex":"«Реше́ние» — отглаго́льное существи́тельное."},{"en":"части́ца","ru":"a particle (же, ведь, -то)","ex":"Части́ца «же» несёт отте́нок."},{"en":"идио́ма","ru":"an idiom","ex":"Идио́ма к ме́сту украша́ет речь."},{"en":"инве́рсия","ru":"inversion (marked word order)","ex":"Инве́рсия де́лает акце́нт."},{"en":"пло́тный","ru":"dense (of a text)","ex":"Нау́чный текст о́чень пло́тный."},{"en":"це́лостность","ru":"coherence, wholeness","ex":"В те́ксте важна́ це́лостность."},{"en":"уме́стно","ru":"appropriately, fittingly","ex":"Идио́ма стои́т уме́стно."},{"en":"владе́ние","ru":"command, mastery","ex":"Э́то настоя́щее владе́ние языко́м."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each piece of the toolkit to what it is.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["по возвраще́нии","on returning (compression)"],["Ты же обеща́л.","you promised, though (particle «же»)"],["сле́дует отме́тить, что…","it should be noted that… (scientific)"],["как изве́стно","as is well known (journalistic)"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today. If one bites, glance back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"The first decision when writing a C1 text is…","opts":["which style to choose and hold","how many idioms to use","how long to make it"],"answer":0},{"q":"«По́сле того́ как зако́н при́няли» compresses to…","opts":["по́сле приня́тия зако́на","зако́н при́няли по́сле","когда́ при́мут зако́н"],"answer":0},{"q":"Three idioms in a single paragraph is…","opts":["ideal","too much — showing off","the C1 minimum"],"answer":1},{"q":"Under every C1 sentence still runs…","opts":["the B2 grammar base","nothing at all","only slang"],"answer":0},{"q":"«Иссле́дование пока́зывает, что э́то по́лная жесть» — the error is…","opts":["a spelling slip","slang crashing a scientific frame","too many particles"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"One paragraph Эмма wrote — style, idiom, particle and compression, all pulling together. Read it aloud.","title":"Оди́н абза́ц — весь у́ровень","sentences":["Как изве́стно, хоро́ший перево́д не заме́тен: он не кричи́т о себе́.","По́сле до́лгих лет рабо́ты Э́мма поняла́ э́то как никто́.","Оди́н уда́чный оборо́т — и фра́за встаёт на своё ме́сто.","Здесь ва́жен, разуме́ется, не то́лько смысл, но и его́ отте́нок.","Стиль, идио́ма, части́ца, сжа́тие — всё должно́ рабо́тать вме́сте.","И тогда́ текст звучи́т так, сло́вно его́ написа́л носи́тель."],"translation":"As is well known, a good translation is invisible: it doesn't shout about itself. After long years of work Emma understood this like no one else. One well-chosen turn of phrase — and the sentence falls into place. Here what matters, of course, is not only the meaning, but its shade. Style, idiom, particle, compression — everything has to work together. And then the text sounds as if a native wrote it."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — assemble the level.","prompt":"Write one coherent paragraph (4–6 Russian sentences) in a single chosen style (journalistic, scientific, or conversational — you pick). Deploy, to the point, at least: one <b>idiom</b>, one <b>particle</b> (же / ведь / -то), and one <b>compression</b> (a participle, a gerund, or a «по возвраще́нии»-style verbal noun). Hold the style from the first word to the last.","hint":"Choose the style first, then let everything obey it. Idiom and particle to the point, not for show; compress the bookish sentence, keep the base grammar clean 🙂","example":"Как изве́стно, вы́учить язы́к до конца́ невозмо́жно — и всё же грани́ца отодвига́ется ка́ждый день. По возвраще́нии из Ло́ндона Э́мма сно́ва взяла́сь за перево́д, кото́рый до́лго не дви́гался с мёртвой то́чки. Тепе́рь, разобра́вшись в сти́ле а́втора, она́ рабо́тает бы́стро и то́чно. Де́ло, что называ́ется, пошло́ на лад. Ведь язы́к — э́то не слова́рь, а слух, кото́рый воспи́тывают года́ми."}},"30":{"day":30,"week":"04","themeRu":"About me (C1 capstone)","themeEn":"О себе́: мастерство́ и стиль","intro":"Thirty days ago you learned that Russian has five styles — and you couldn't yet hear them. Today you write your own <b>«О себе́»</b>, and it's a different creature from the one you'd have written at B2. Back then the pride was in the grammar: cases in place, clauses linked. Now the grammar is silent, assumed — the pride is in the <b>style</b>. You don't just say who you are; you choose how to say it, and you could say it five ways. This is the last page. Let's make it beautiful.","introAudio":"Thirty days ago you learned that Russian has five styles — and you couldn't yet hear them. Today you write your own about-me, and it's a different creature from the one you'd have written at B2. Back then the pride was in the grammar: cases in place, clauses linked. Now the grammar is silent, assumed — the pride is in the style. You don't just say who you are; you choose how to say it, and you could say it five ways. This is the last page. Let's make it beautiful.","goals":["Write your <b>«О себе́»</b> as a C1 speaker — command of <b>style</b>, not just correct grammar","Build a small <b>portfolio</b>: an analytical эссе́, a spoken рассужде́ние, an аннота́ция","Hold and switch register on purpose across three genres on one theme","Look back at the whole road — from a first plural to a native's ear"],"learned":["Wrote your <b>«О себе́»</b> as a C1 speaker — command of <b>style</b>, not just grammar","Built a small <b>portfolio</b>: an analytical эссе́, a spoken рассужде́ние, an аннота́ция","Held and switched register on purpose across three genres on one theme","Looked back at the whole road — from a first plural to a native's ear"],"review":{"intro":"One last lap around the whole level — a register-choice, a bookish idiom, a compression, and one B2 participle — then your capstone.","introAudio":"One last lap around the whole level — a register-choice, a bookish idiom, a compression, and one B2 participle — then your capstone.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer — a tour of everything you've built.","q":"In an official report, “money” should be…","opts":["деньжа́та","де́нежные сре́дства","ба́бки"],"answer":1,"explain":"Official register → <b>де́нежные сре́дства</b>. деньжа́та is colloquial, ба́бки is slang."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which idiom is bookish — right for an essay?","opts":["бить баклу́ши","ка́нуть в Ле́ту","ва́лять дурака́"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>Ка́нуть в Ле́ту</b> is bookish; the others are colloquial."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Когда́ он верну́лся» compresses to…","opts":["по возвраще́нии","верну́вшись домо́й когда́","он верну́лся тогда́"],"answer":0,"explain":"Fold it into a verbal noun: <b>по возвраще́нии</b>."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build: <b>“an article written by an editor”</b> (passive participle)","want":"статья́, напи́санная реда́ктором","answer":"статья́, напи́санная реда́ктором"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · О себе́, C1","title":"<em>Не про́сто пра́вильно — уме́стно</em>","paras":["At B2 your «О себе́» was a victory of grammar: every case in place, every clause linked. At C1 that's assumed — invisible. The new pride is that you now <b>choose the register</b>. You can introduce yourself to a professor and to a friend, and the two sound nothing alike, both perfect.","«Я перево́дчик» stays the fact; how you frame it is the C1 command. Bookish: <b>занима́юсь литерату́рным перево́дом и реда́ктурой</b>. Colloquial: <b>да я так, кни́жки перевожу́</b>. One fact, three registers.","So this last «О себе́» isn't a list of facts — it's a demonstration that you can dress one truth in whatever style the moment asks for."],"audio":"At B2 your about-me was a victory of grammar: every case in place, every clause linked. At C1 that's assumed — invisible. The new pride is that you now choose the register. You can introduce yourself to a professor and to a friend, and the two sound nothing alike, both perfect. Ya perevodchik stays the fact; how you frame it is the C1 command. Bookish: zanimayus literaturnym perevodom i redakturoy. Colloquial: da ya tak, knizhki perevozhu. One fact, three registers. So this last about-me isn't a list of facts — it's a demonstration that you can dress one truth in whatever style the moment asks for.","table":{"rows":[["нейтр.: Я рабо́таю перево́дчиком. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"я работаю переводчиком\">🔊</button>","neutral: I work as a translator."],["кни́жн.: Занима́юсь литерату́рным перево́дом. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"занимаюсь литературным переводом\">🔊</button>","bookish: I do literary translation."],["разг.: Да я так, кни́жки перевожу́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"да я так, книжки перевожу\">🔊</button>","colloquial: Oh, I just translate books, you know."],["Оди́н факт — три реги́стра. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"один факт — три регистра\">🔊</button>","One fact, three registers."]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"“I've come to hear register the way natives do.”","en":"Я научи́лась слы́шать реги́стр так, как слы́шат носи́тели.","say":"Я научилась слышать регистр так, как слышат носители."},{"ru":"“I don't just choose the correct word now — I choose the fitting one.”","en":"Тепе́рь я выбира́ю не про́сто пра́вильное сло́во, а уме́стное.","say":"Теперь я выбираю не просто правильное слово, а уместное."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Я перево́дчик, я де́лаю перево́д, перево́д — моя́ рабо́та».","right":"«Я перево́дчик: занима́юсь перево́дом и реда́ктурой».","why":"Repeating one word is the opposite of C1. Vary and choose — that flat repetition is exactly what a year ago you'd now outgrow."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the answer.","q":"At C1, your «О себе́» shows command because you now…","opts":["never make a single grammar mistake","choose the register on purpose","use the longest words you can"],"answer":1,"explain":"Grammar is assumed now; the new command is <b>choosing the register</b> — you could say it five ways."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the verb. Stress marks are optional.","q":"One fact, three registers. Neutral “I work as a translator”: «Я … перево́дчиком».","accept":["работаю"],"placeholder":"я …","explain":"Neutral: <b>Я рабо́таю перево́дчиком</b>. Bookish: «занима́юсь перево́дом»; colloquial: «перевожу́ кни́жки»."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The portfolio","title":"<em>Эссе́, рассужде́ние, аннота́ция</em> — one theme, three registers","paras":["Your capstone isn't one text but three, on a single theme (say, “how a language changes who you are”). An <b>аналити́ческое эссе́</b> — written, bookish, a thesis with arguments and a conclusion, connectors doing the linking.","A <b>у́стное рассужде́ние</b> — spoken, still structured but lighter, with a particle or two and a natural rhythm. An <b>аннота́ция</b> — a dry, impersonal two-sentence summary in the clichés of the scientific style: «В эссе́ рассма́тривается…, а́втор прихо́дит к вы́воду, что…»","Same theme; three worlds. Switching between them cleanly — and never leaking one into another — is the whole proof of command."],"audio":"Your capstone isn't one text but three, on a single theme, say, how a language changes who you are. An analiticheskoye esse — written, bookish, a thesis with arguments and a conclusion, connectors doing the linking. An ustnoye rassuzhdeniye — spoken, still structured but lighter, with a particle or two and a natural rhythm. An annotatsiya — a dry, impersonal two-sentence summary in the clichés of the scientific style: v esse rassmatrivayetsya, avtor prikhodit k vyvodu chto. Same theme; three worlds. Switching between them cleanly — and never leaking one into another — is the whole proof of command.","table":{"rows":[["аналити́ческое эссе́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"аналитическое эссе\">🔊</button>","analytical essay (written, bookish, argued)"],["у́стное рассужде́ние <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"устное рассуждение\">🔊</button>","spoken reasoning (structured, lighter)"],["аннота́ция <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"аннотация\">🔊</button>","an abstract (dry, cliché-built)"],["В статье́ рассма́тривается вопро́с… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в статье рассматривается вопрос\">🔊</button>","The article examines the question… (abstract cliché)"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"Essay opener (bookish): “The question of how a language shapes a person deserves attention.”","en":"Эссе́: «Вопро́с о том, как язы́к формиру́ет челове́ка, заслу́живает внима́ния».","say":"Вопрос о том, как язык формирует человека, заслуживает внимания."},{"ru":"Abstract (dry cliché): “The essay examines the influence of language on identity. The author concludes that…”","en":"Аннота́ция: «В эссе́ рассма́тривается влия́ние языка́ на ли́чность. А́втор прихо́дит к вы́воду, что…»","say":"В эссе рассматривается влияние языка на личность. Автор приходит к выводу, что…"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Как аннота́ция: «Э́то потряса́ющее эссе́ о том, как я полюби́ла ру́сский!»","right":"Как аннота́ция: «В эссе́ рассма́тривается связь языка́ и ли́чности».","why":"An abstract carries no emotion or opinion — it's dry scientific cliché. The excited version is fine for a chat, wrong for an аннота́ция."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"An <b>аннота́ция</b> (abstract) should be…","opts":["emotional and personal","dry, impersonal, cliché-built","full of idioms"],"answer":1,"explain":"The abstract is dry scientific cliché: «В статье́ рассма́тривается…, а́втор прихо́дит к вы́воду…» — no opinion, no feeling."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud.","target":"В эссе́ рассма́тривается влия́ние языка́ на ли́чность.","sub":"an abstract line — read it flat and impersonal, like a summary","want":"в эссе рассматривается влияние языка на личность"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The whole road","title":"From a first plural to a <em>native's ear</em>","paras":["Look back. A2 gave you the cases; B1 gave you the clauses and the conditional; B2 gave you participles, government, the passive — the whole grammar. C1 gave you the thing grammar can't: the <b>ear</b>.","You hear the quotation behind a headline, you feel a suffix sneer, you catch a register slip at ten paces, you colour a line with «же» and mean exactly one thing by it. What's left isn't another level — it's living in the language, which you already do.","This «О себе́» is your signature on all of it. Two years ago you couldn't hear the difference; now you can't <b>not</b> hear it. Sign the page."],"audio":"Look back. A2 gave you the cases; B1 gave you the clauses and the conditional; B2 gave you participles, government, the passive — the whole grammar. C1 gave you the thing grammar can't: the ear. You hear the quotation behind a headline, you feel a suffix sneer, you catch a register slip at ten paces, you colour a line with zhe and mean exactly one thing by it. What's left isn't another level — it's living in the language, which you already do. This about-me is your signature on all of it. Two years ago you couldn't hear the difference; now you can't not hear it. Sign the page.","table":{"rows":[["A2–B1: паде́жи, прида́точные, усло́вие <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"падежи, придаточные, условие\">🔊</button>","cases, clauses, the conditional"],["B2: прича́стия, управле́ние, зало́г <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"причастия, управление, залог\">🔊</button>","participles, government, voice"],["C1: стиль, реги́стр, идио́ма, слух <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"стиль, регистр, идиома, слух\">🔊</button>","style, register, idiom, an ear"],["Да́льше — жизнь в языке́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"дальше — жизнь в языке\">🔊</button>","What's next is living in the language."]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"“Two years ago I couldn't hear the difference; now I can't not hear it.”","en":"Два го́да наза́д я не слы́шала ра́зницы; тепе́рь не слы́шать её не могу́.","say":"Два года назад я не слышала разницы; теперь не слышать её не могу."},{"ru":"“The grammar was the first half of the road; the second is style.”","en":"Грамма́тика была́ пе́рвой полови́ной пути́; втора́я — э́то стиль.","say":"Грамматика была первой половиной пути; вторая — это стиль."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"C1 gave you what grammar alone couldn't. It gave you…","opts":["a bigger dictionary only","an ear for style and register","perfect spelling"],"answer":1,"explain":"The ear: you hear a quotation behind a headline, feel a suffix sneer, catch a register slip — style, not just grammar."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the word. Stress marks are optional.","q":"The grammar was the first half; the second half of the journey has one name. Type the Russian for “style”:","accept":["стиль"],"placeholder":"…","explain":"The grammar was the first half; <b>стиль</b> (style) is the second — and it's yours."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"The evening before C1 is behind her, Эмма and Ива́н sit by the window and she reads him a line from her portfolio. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Ну что, дописа́ла портфо́лио?","ru":"So, have you finished the portfolio?"},{"who":"A","en":"Почти́. Одна́ мысль — в трёх стиля́х сра́зу.","ru":"Almost. One thought — in three styles at once."},{"who":"B","en":"Прочита́й кусо́чек.","ru":"Read me a little piece."},{"who":"A","en":"«В эссе́ рассма́тривается вопро́с о том, как язы́к меня́ет челове́ка».","ru":"“The essay examines the question of how a language changes a person.”"},{"who":"B","en":"Су́хо, как в нау́чной статье́.","ru":"Dry, like in a scientific article."},{"who":"A","en":"И должно́ быть су́хо — э́то аннота́ция. В са́мом эссе́ я пишу́ ина́че, с ду́шой.","ru":"And it should be dry — that's the abstract. In the essay itself I write differently, with feeling."},{"who":"B","en":"Ра́ньше ты иска́ла пра́вильное сло́во. А тепе́рь?","ru":"You used to search for the correct word. And now?"},{"who":"A","en":"Тепе́рь выбира́ю уме́стное. Грамма́тика была́ полдоро́ги — остально́е стиль.","ru":"Now I choose the fitting one. Grammar was half the road — the rest is style."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for looking back with a native's ear — mastery, register, the three genres. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"о себе́","ru":"about oneself","ex":"Я пишу́ о себе́ ина́че, чем ра́ньше."},{"en":"мастерство́","ru":"mastery, craft","ex":"Э́то уже́ мастерство́, а не учёба."},{"en":"стиль","ru":"style","ex":"Я выбира́ю стиль по ситуа́ции."},{"en":"реги́стр","ru":"register","ex":"Я переключа́ю реги́стр без уси́лий."},{"en":"эссе́","ru":"an essay (analytical)","ex":"В эссе́ я привожу́ аргуме́нты."},{"en":"рассужде́ние","ru":"reasoning (a spoken argument)","ex":"У́стное рассужде́ние звучи́т живе́е."},{"en":"аннота́ция","ru":"an abstract, an annotation","ex":"Аннота́ция — су́хо и ко́ротко."},{"en":"уме́стный","ru":"appropriate, fitting","ex":"Гла́вное — не пра́вильное, а уме́стное сло́во."},{"en":"отте́нок","ru":"a shade, a nuance","ex":"Тепе́рь я слы́шу ка́ждый отте́нок."},{"en":"слух","ru":"an ear (for language)","ex":"Язы́к — э́то не слова́рь, а слух."},{"en":"владе́ние языко́м","ru":"command of a language","ex":"Э́то и есть владе́ние языко́м."},{"en":"го́рдость","ru":"pride","ex":"Ру́сский — моя́ ти́хая го́рдость."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match each genre of the portfolio to what it is.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["аналити́ческое эссе́","an analytical essay (bookish, argued)"],["у́стное рассужде́ние","spoken reasoning (lighter, structured)"],["аннота́ция","a dry abstract (scientific clichés)"],["о себе́","about oneself"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Eight questions — the whole of C1 in miniature. Take your time; you've got all of this.","items":[{"q":"At C1, your «О себе́» shows mastery of…","opts":["grammar only","style and register","spelling"],"answer":1},{"q":"An <b>аннота́ция</b> is written in a register that is…","opts":["dry and impersonal","emotional","slangy"],"answer":0},{"q":"For an official report, “money” is…","opts":["деньжа́та","де́нежные сре́дства","ба́бки"],"answer":1},{"q":"Which idiom is bookish (right for an essay)?","opts":["ка́нуть в Ле́ту","бить баклу́ши","ва́лять дурака́"],"answer":0},{"q":"«Когда́ он верну́лся» compresses to…","opts":["по возвраще́нии","верну́лся когда́","тогда́ верну́лся"],"answer":0},{"q":"“It's raining cats and dogs” in real Russian is…","opts":["дождь из ко́шек и соба́к","льёт как из ведра́","мо́крый день"],"answer":1},{"q":"A friendly «пока́!» ending a business letter is…","opts":["perfect","a register slip","too formal"],"answer":1},{"q":"The portfolio's three genres — эссе́, рассужде́ние, аннота́ция — share…","opts":["one theme, three registers","one register, three themes","nothing at all"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Эмма's «О себе́» at C1 — not a list of facts, but the sound of a mastered language. Everything in it is yours now. Read it aloud, and hear how far the road has come.","title":"О себе́: мастерство́ и стиль","sentences":["Три го́да наза́д я слы́шала слова́, а тепе́рь слы́шу их отте́нки.","Я перево́дчик и реда́ктор: одну́ и ту же мысль я уме́ю сказа́ть нау́чно, официа́льно, легко́ и́ли о́бразно.","Гла́вным стал не пра́вильный отве́т, а уме́стный.","Я слы́шу цита́ту за заголо́вком и чу́вствую, как су́ффикс мо́жет уколо́ть.","«Же» и «ведь» тепе́рь кра́сят мою́ речь так, как я хочу́.","Грамма́тика была́ пе́рвой полови́ной пути́; втора́я — э́то стиль.","И вот я до́ма — не в го́роде, а в са́мом языке́."],"translation":"Three years ago I heard words; now I hear their shades. I'm a translator and an editor: the same thought I can say scientifically, officially, lightly or with imagery. What matters became not the correct answer, but the fitting one. I hear the quotation behind a headline and feel how a suffix can sting. «Же» and «ведь» now colour my speech exactly as I want. Grammar was the first half of the road; the second is style. And here I am, at home — not in the city, but in the language itself."},"essay":{"intro":"This is it — the capstone. Take your time.","prompt":"Pick ONE theme (for example, “how Russian changed who I am”). Write it three ways: (1) an <b>аналити́ческое эссе́</b> of 5–7 sentences — bookish register, a thesis, arguments, a book connector or two, one apt idiom; (2) a 2–3-sentence <b>у́стное рассужде́ние</b> — the same theme, lighter and spoken, with a particle («же» / «ведь»); (3) a dry 2-sentence <b>аннота́ция</b> in scientific clichés («В эссе́ рассма́тривается…, а́втор прихо́дит к вы́воду, что…»). Same idea, three registers. This is your signature.","hint":"The essay argues; the рассужде́ние talks; the аннота́ция summarises without a drop of emotion. Switching register on purpose is the whole of C1 — and it's yours now 🙂","example":"Эссе́: Вопро́с о том, как чужо́й язы́к меня́ет челове́ка, ка́жется мне одни́м из са́мых глубо́ких. С одно́й стороны́, язы́к — то́лько сре́дство; но с друго́й, вме́сте с ним прихо́дит но́вый взгляд на мир. Изучи́в ру́сский, я ста́ла ина́че ду́мать и да́же ина́че шути́ть. Ста́рые стра́хи ка́нули в Ле́ту, а на их ме́сто пришла́ свобо́да. Сле́довательно, язы́к — э́то не про́сто слова́, а втора́я ли́чность. Рассужде́ние: Зна́ете, я ведь и впрямь измени́лась. Ра́ньше я про́сто переводи́ла слова́, а тепе́рь ду́маю на ру́сском — э́то же совсе́м друго́е де́ло. Аннота́ция: В эссе́ рассма́тривается влия́ние иностра́нного языка́ на ли́чность челове́ка. А́втор прихо́дит к вы́воду, что владе́ние языко́м формиру́ет но́вый взгляд на мир."}}};
